research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-21.coverage.json:185:          "actual_read_extent": "Retrieved the complete three-page paper and inspected all pages, including the finite-action boundary condition, charge formula and mapping-degree discussion, action inequality, explicit SU(2) charge-one self-dual solution, and the authors' stated historical limitation on higher charge.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-25.pages.json:47:        "statement": "Use four-dimensional Minkowski space $M=\\mathbb R^{1,3}$, coordinates $x^0=ct$ and metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, and natural units $c=\\hbar=1$ for mass-dimension counting. A **physical EFT operator contract** is a record of the following selected model data; none of the selections is inferred from an operator's name.\n\nHere an EFT is an adopted low-energy model whose local Lagrangian is organized by specified fields, symmetries, coefficients, and a finite retained order; this definition implies no ultraviolet completion or physical realization. First choose a finite set $I$ of field species. For each $i\\in I$, specify a finite-dimensional complex graded space $\\Phi_i=\\Phi_i^{\\bar 0}\\oplus\\Phi_i^{\\bar 1}$, its bosonic or fermionic parity, its smooth field domain on $M$, any reality condition, and its assigned rational engineering weight $\\Delta_i$. Even fields are smooth maps $M\\to\\Phi_i^{\\bar 0}$; odd field components take values in a fixed Grassmann algebra with smooth coefficient functions. A spurion is a fixed, non-dynamical parameter assigned a representation and engineering weight, used to record a declared symmetry-breaking insertion. The proper orthochronous Lorentz group is $SO^+(1,3)=\\{L\\in GL_4(\\mathbb R):L^T\\eta L=\\eta,\\ \\det L=1,\\ L^0{}_0\\ge1\\}$; specify its finite-dimensional action on all spacetime indices and require each Lagrangian density to be a Lorentz scalar. State the full gauge and flavor actions on every field, index, and spurion. A flavor symmetry that is absent is recorded as trivial; an approximate or explicitly broken symmetry requires its breaking spurions and weights to be listed.\n\nFor a non-Abelian gauge sector, specify the compact real gauge group with its actual global form, smooth right principal bundle, admitted bundle sectors, unitary representations, and a smooth principal connection. The connection is a Lie-algebra-valued one-form reproducing fundamental vertical vectors and transforming by the adjoint action; its local curvature is $F_A=dA+\\tfrac12[A\\wedge A]$, and the covariant derivative in a representation $\\rho$ is $D_\\mu\\psi=\\partial_\\mu\\psi+\\rho_*(A_\\mu)\\psi$. The field and connection data must include their regularity and spacetime and internal index representations. These formulas use the right-principal-bundle convention fixed by the supplied gauge-geometry definitions.\n\nThe local operator algebra uses a separate finite-dimensional rational representation of a complex reductive affine algebraic group $G_{\\mathrm{alg}}$ on the finite jet generators. A contract must supply the maps and representations relating $G_{\\mathrm{alg}}$ to the selected compact gauge group and flavor group. It must either verify, on each retained finite jet space, that the algebraic invariant subspace is exactly the intended physical invariant space, or label the quotient as a formal algebraic selection only. Compactness of a real gauge group alone does not identify its invariant polynomials with those of an algebraic complexification.\n\nChoose a derivative cutoff $r$, maximum polynomial field degree $q$, finite coefficient field and conjugation data, and a finite maximum engineering dimension $D_{\\max}$. With derivatives assigned weight one, the local field-jet definition supplies the supercommutative polynomial module $P_r=\\operatorname{Sym}(J_{r,\\bar 0}^*)\\otimes\\Lambda(J_{r,\\bar 1}^*)$ and its fixed-weight subspaces. For each weight $D$, let $W_D$ be the finite raw polynomial space, $V_D=W_D^{G_{\\mathrm{alg}}}$ its invariant scalar subspace, and $\\widetilde R_D$ the explicitly declared $G_{\\mathrm{alg}}$-stable linear relation subspace. The selected operator quotient is $\\mathcal Q_D=V_D/(\\widetilde R_D\\cap V_D)$: it identifies exactly the declared relations and makes no completeness claim about all EFT redundancies. The finite jet, field-degree, and dimension cutoffs give a finite-dimensional retained space.\n\nFor each $\\widetilde R_D$, record each generator as a polynomial or Fierz identity, a total divergence under compact-support or stated boundary conditions, or a leading-equation or field-redefinition relation tied to an explicit base action and retained order. An equation-of-motion entry records its variational derivative and field multiplier; a field-redefinition entry records the local map, its inverse through the retained order, and all induced higher-order terms. The quotient is a definition of the declared relation space; its existence alone does not establish action, off-shell Green-function, quantum-measure, or scattering equivalence.\n\nChoose the canonical-dimension power-counting scheme, coefficient truncation, and a finite loop order $L_{\\max}$. The formal loop label $\\zeta$ is dimensionless bookkeeping and is not a convergence claim. If $\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{op}}>0$ is the coefficient-suppression scale, the retained local Lagrangian has the convention\n\n$$\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{tr}}=\\sum_{\\Delta_i\\le D_{\\max}}\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{L_{\\max}}\\zeta^\\ell C_{i,\\ell}(\\mu_W)\\frac{\\mathcal O_i}{\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{op}}^{\\Delta_i-4}},$$\n\nwhere each $\\mathcal O_i$ has engineering weight $\\Delta_i$, each $C_{i,\\ell}$ is dimensionless in the stated natural units, and the chosen reality conditions make $\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{tr}}$ real. This is canonical-dimension counting for the selected linear EFT; HEFT chiral counting and multi-scale SCET counting are different schemes and are not implied. A truncation order is not a numerical error bound, a coefficient prediction, or a derived renormalization-group flow.\n\nIf the same application also uses a finite-regulator data record, retain its graph variables, measure, $\\mu_W$, $M$, $E$, and regulator cutoff $\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{reg}}$ with their supplied meanings. $\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{op}}$, $\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{reg}}$, $M$, $E$, and $\\mu_W$ are distinct parameters unless an additional model input identifies them. A map from finite-graph variables to the continuum finite-jet operator data is also additional input; the finite-regulator definition supplies no such map or continuum limit.",
