research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-13.pages.json:194:        "statement": "Let $K$ be the ordered tetrahedron $0<1<2<3$, let $G=\\mathbb Z_2$ under addition, and let $A=\\mathbb Z_3$ under addition. Define the action by $\\rho_0(t)=t$ and $\\rho_1(t)=-t$, and take $\\beta=0$. From vertex labels $x_0=0,x_1=1,x_2=0,x_3=1$ in $\\mathbb Z_2$, set $a_{ij}=x_j-x_i$ and $B_{ijk}=0$. These data form a finite 2-group background. For the 1-form gauge parameter with $\\lambda_{01}=1$ and all other edge labels zero, and with all vertex labels $g_i=0$, the transformed fields satisfy $a'=a$, $B'_{012}=B'_{013}=1$, and $B'_{023}=B'_{123}=0$; they form a gauge-equivalent background.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-13.pages.json:209:        "id": "ex-aqft-two-group-postnikov-background",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-13.pages.json:212:        "title": "A nonzero finite Postnikov cocycle background",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-13.pages.json:213:        "reference": "items/ex-aqft-two-group-postnikov-background.md",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-13.pages.json:217:          "def-aqft-two-group-background"
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-13.pages.json:220:          "def-aqft-two-group-background": "mathematical-premise"
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-13.pages.json:223:        "statement": "Let $G=A=\\mathbb Z_2$ under addition with trivial action, and let $\\beta(g,h,k)=ghk$ in $\\mathbb Z_2$. On the ordered tetrahedron $K=\\Delta^3$ with vertices $0<1<2<3$, take vertex labels $x_0=0,x_1=1,x_2=0,x_3=1$ and edges $a_{ij}=x_j-x_i$. Put $B_{012}=1$ and $B_{013}=B_{023}=B_{123}=0$. Then $\\beta$ is a normalized $3$-cocycle representing a nonzero class, and $(a,B)$ is a finite $2$-group background. Under the $0$-form gauge labels $g_0=1$ and $g_1=g_2=g_3=0$, with all $1$-form labels zero, the transformed edge labels are $a'_{01}=0,a'_{12}=1,a'_{23}=1,a'_{02}=1,a'_{13}=0,a'_{03}=0$ and all four transformed face labels vanish; these transformed data also form a background.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-16.proof-contracts.json:78:          "quote": "Let $G$ be a complex affine algebraic group\n([[def-rational-action-on-affine-variety]]).\n\n**Unipotent subgroups.** A closed subgroup $U\\subseteq G$ is **unipotent** if\nevery non-zero finite-dimensional rational $U$-module has a non-zero $U$-fixed\nvector. This fixed-vector condition is the characterization used in Brion's\nExample 1.22 (printed p. 8), and it is\nthe only form of the notion used in this pair. Equivalently, by the Lie–Kolchin\ntheorem, $U$ is unipotent in the standard sense that it admits no non-trivial\nrational characters and all its elements are unipotent; this equivalence is\nrecorded as a sourced parenthetical companion to the definition (Brion Example\n1.22 cites Lie–Kolchin) and is not used as a supplier anywhere in this pair, so\nno edge to the higher-order unipotent/solvable page is introduced.\n\n**Reductive and linearly reductive groups.** The group $G$ is **reductive** if\nit has no non-trivial closed normal unipotent subgroup, and **linearly\nreductive** if every finite-dimensional rational $G$-module is completely\nreducible, i.e. a direct sum of simple $G$-submodules\n([[def-completely-reducible-representation]], [[def-semisimple-module]]).\n\nOver $\\mathbf C$ the two notions coincide: every complex\nreductive affine algebraic group is linearly reductive, and conversely a\nlinearly reductive $G$ has no non-trivial closed normal unipotent subgroup.\nBoth implications, together with the identity-component reduction below, are\nproved in\n[[thm-complete-reducibility-and-reynolds-operator-for-complex-reductive-group]];\nthis definition only records them for consumers of that theorem.\n\nBoth notions depend only on the identity component, in the sense that $G$ is\nreductive if and only if $G^\\circ$ is, and $G$ is linearly reductive if and\nonly if $G^\\circ$ is; the passage from $G^\\circ$ to the finite component group\nfor both notions is carried out in the same theorem.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-16.proof-contracts.json:461:          "claim": "If the order-$k$ action contains $+\\epsilon^k\\mathcal I(F^a\\mathcal E_a^{(0)})$, the negative term in step 2.2 cancels it, while the substitutions in all $S_j$ with $j\\geq1$ begin above order $k$. Step 1.4 makes this an allowed invertible local field redefinition; its induced higher-order terms remain part of the transformed truncated action. No off-shell or quantum-measure conclusion follows from this action-level calculation.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-16.proof-contracts.json:564:          "quote": "Let $G$ be a complex affine algebraic group\n([[def-rational-action-on-affine-variety]]).\n\n**Unipotent subgroups.** A closed subgroup $U\\subseteq G$ is **unipotent** if\nevery non-zero finite-dimensional rational $U$-module has a non-zero $U$-fixed\nvector. This fixed-vector condition is the characterization used in Brion's\nExample 1.22 (printed p. 8), and it is\nthe only form of the notion used in this pair. Equivalently, by the Lie–Kolchin\ntheorem, $U$ is unipotent in the standard sense that it admits no non-trivial\nrational characters and all its elements are unipotent; this equivalence is\nrecorded as a sourced parenthetical companion to the definition (Brion Example\n1.22 cites Lie–Kolchin) and is not used as a supplier anywhere in this pair, so\nno edge to the higher-order unipotent/solvable page is introduced.\n\n**Reductive and linearly reductive groups.** The group $G$ is **reductive** if\nit has no non-trivial closed normal unipotent subgroup, and **linearly\nreductive** if every finite-dimensional rational $G$-module is completely\nreducible, i.e. a direct sum of simple $G$-submodules\n([[def-completely-reducible-representation]], [[def-semisimple-module]]).\n\nOver $\\mathbf C$ the two notions coincide: every complex\nreductive affine algebraic group is linearly reductive, and conversely a\nlinearly reductive $G$ has no non-trivial closed normal unipotent subgroup.\nBoth implications, together with the identity-component reduction below, are\nproved in\n[[thm-complete-reducibility-and-reynolds-operator-for-complex-reductive-group]];\nthis definition only records them for consumers of that theorem.\n\nBoth notions depend only on the identity component, in the sense that $G$ is\nreductive if and only if $G^\\circ$ is, and $G$ is linearly reductive if and\nonly if $G^\\circ$ is; the passage from $G^\\circ$ to the finite component group\nfor both notions is carried out in the same theorem.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-16.proof-contracts.json:573:          "claim": "By F2 and A4, the admitted order-$k$ relation is implemented by an actual local invertible coordinate map $F_\\epsilon$; the reductive-group condition F4 is the hypothesis carried by the quotient proposition. Any boundary relation has zero action by its boundary hypothesis, and any declared polynomial identity vanishes identically. All higher-order terms generated by the full pullback are retained.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-alpha-3a-adjudicate.task.md:31:- Follow `research/<run>-alpha-G-3a-order.task.md` within your dispatched batches. Adjudicate routed items from lowest to highest in-run dependency level, complete each item's risk review before moving higher, and keep all findings for one item together. Page-only obligations retain their own scope. Escalate affected consumers outside your dispatched batches to the owner for resolution before closure.
