research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-step1-def-aqft-two-group-background.json:7:  "reason": "Complete finite skeletal 2-group and simplicial background definition is placed on the assigned A page in the mathematics library at dependency level 0 with no dependencies. The normalized-cocycle equivalence relation, associator direction, local coefficients, background equation, gauge formulas, and representative-change calculation are explicit. Three full-text treatments were inspected and fetch-stamped; source coverage and definition contract are recorded. Scoped content, manifest, layout, render, and dependency checks pass.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-14.proof-contracts.json:7:    "def-aqft-background-gauging-data",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-14.proof-contracts.json:14:    "def-aqft-background-gauging-data": {
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-14.proof-contracts.json:18:          "source": "def-aqft-two-group-background",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-14.proof-contracts.json:20:          "quote": "Let $G$ be a finite group with identity $e$, let $A$ be a finite abelian group written additively, and let $\\rho:G\\to\\operatorname{Aut}(A)$ be a left action, so $\\rho_{gh}=\\rho_g\\rho_h$ and $\\rho_e=\\operatorname{id}_A$. A group $2$-cochain is a function $c:G^2\\to A$; it is normalized when it vanishes if either input is $e$. Its coboundary is\n\n$$\n(\\delta_\\rho c)(g,h,k)=\\rho_g c(h,k)-c(gh,k)+c(g,hk)-c(g,h).\n$$\n\nA normalized group $3$-cocycle is a function $\\beta:G^3\\to A$ that vanishes whenever any argument is $e$ and satisfies\n\n$$\n\\rho_g\\beta(h,k,l)-\\beta(gh,k,l)+\\beta(g,hk,l)-\\beta(g,h,kl)+\\beta(g,h,k)=0\n$$\n\nfor all $g,h,k,l\\in G$. Two normalized $3$-cocycles represent the same class when their difference is $\\delta_\\rho c$ for a normalized $2$-cochain $c$; this is an equivalence relation by additivity of $\\delta_\\rho$. The class $[\\beta]$ is the Postnikov class in this skeletal model.\n\nThe corresponding skeletal categorical $2$-group has objects $g\\in G$ and, for each $g$, one automorphism labelled by each $u\\in A$; there are no morphisms between distinct objects. Composition of automorphisms adds their labels, tensor product is\n\n$$\n(g,u)\\otimes(h,v)=(gh,u+\\rho_gv),\n$$\n\nand the associator from $(gh)k$ to $g(hk)$ has label $\\beta(g,h,k)$. The two pentagon path labels are $\\beta(gh,k,l)+\\beta(g,h,kl)$ and $\\rho_g\\beta(h,k,l)+\\beta(g,hk,l)+\\beta(g,h,k)$; equality of these labels is exactly the displayed $3$-cocycle equation.\n\nA finite ordered simplicial complex $K$ is a finite vertex set with a total order and a collection of nonempty finite vertex subsets, called simplices, closed under taking nonempty subsets. Write each simplex in increasing order. A $q$-cochain with values in a local coefficient system assigns an element to each ordered $q$-simplex at its first vertex. A flat $G$-valued edge cochain is a family $a_{ij}\\in G$ for each edge $i<j$ such that $a_{ij}a_{jk}=a_{ik}$ on every triangle. It defines the local coefficient system with fiber $A$ at each vertex and transport $\\rho_{a_{ij}}$ from vertex $j$ to vertex $i$.\n\nFor each integer $q\\geq0$ and local-coefficient $q$-cochain $c$, define the twisted coboundary on each ordered $(q+1)$-simplex by\n\n$$\n(\\delta_a c)_{i_0\\ldots i_{q+1}}=\\rho_{a_{i_0i_1}}c_{i_1\\ldots i_{q+1}}+\\sum_{r=1}^{q+1}(-1)^r c_{i_0\\ldots\\widehat{i_r}\\ldots i_{q+1}}.\n$$\n\nIn particular, for an $A$-valued $2$-cochain $B$,\n\n$$\n(\\delta_aB)_{ijkl}=\\rho_{a_{ij}}B_{jkl}-B_{ikl}+B_{ijl}-B_{ijk},\\qquad (a^*\\beta)_{ijkl}=\\beta(a_{ij},a_{jk},a_{kl}).\n$$\n\nA finite $2$-group background on $K$ is a flat edge cochain $a$ and an $A$-valued $2$-cochain $B$ satisfying $\\delta_aB=a^*\\beta$ on every ordered tetrahedron. This fixes the cochain representative, local coefficient action, and sign convention.\n\nA $1$-gauge transformation consists of vertex labels $g_i\\in G$ and edge labels $\\lambda_{ij}\\in A$ at vertex $i$. Define\n\n$$\na^g_{ij}=g_i a_{ij}g_j^{-1},\n$$\n\n$$\n\\zeta^g_{ijk}=-\\beta(g_i,a_{ij},a_{jk})+\\beta(a^g_{ij},g_j,a_{jk})-\\beta(a^g_{ij},a^g_{jk},g_k),\n$$\n\n$$\nB^{g,\\lambda}_{ijk}=\\rho_{g_i}B_{ijk}+\\zeta^g_{ijk}+(\\delta_{a^g}\\lambda)_{ijk},\\qquad (\\delta_{a^g}\\lambda)_{ijk}=\\rho_{a^g_{ij}}\\lambda_{jk}-\\lambda_{ik}+\\lambda_{ij}.\n$$\n\nChanging the chosen representative to $\\beta'=\\beta+\\delta_\\rho c$ for a normalized $2$-cochain $c$ and simultaneously replacing $B$ by $B+a^*c$ changes the cochain presentation but preserves the background equation: on every tetrahedron, flatness gives\n\n$$\n(\\delta_a(a^*c))_{ijkl}=\\rho_{a_{ij}}c(a_{jk},a_{kl})-c(a_{ik},a_{kl})+c(a_{ij},a_{jl})-c(a_{ij},a_{jk})=(\\delta_\\rho c)(a_{ij},a_{jk},a_{kl}).\n$$\n\nThis finite mathematical model makes no assertion that a physical theory realizes the symmetry or has an anomaly.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-14.proof-contracts.json:28:          "source": "thm-aqft-two-group-background-consistency",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-14.proof-contracts.json:30:          "quote": "Let $K$ be a finite ordered simplicial complex; let $G$ be a finite group, $A$ a finite abelian group written additively, $\\rho:G\\to\\operatorname{Aut}(A)$ a left action, and $\\beta:G^3\\to A$ a normalized group $3$-cocycle with the convention that its associator is directed from $(gh)k$ to $g(hk)$. Let $a_{ij}\\in G$ be flat on every ordered triangle, let $B_{ijk}\\in A$ be an $A$-valued $2$-cochain at the first vertex, and assume on every ordered tetrahedron that\n\n$$\n\\rho_{a_{ij}}B_{jkl}-B_{ikl}+B_{ijl}-B_{ijk}=\\beta(a_{ij},a_{jk},a_{kl}).\n$$\n\nFor arbitrary vertex labels $g_i\\in G$ and edge labels $\\lambda_{ij}\\in A$, put $a'_{ij}=g_i a_{ij}g_j^{-1}$ and\n\n$$\n\\zeta_{ijk}=-\\beta(g_i,a_{ij},a_{jk})+\\beta(a'_{ij},g_j,a_{jk})-\\beta(a'_{ij},a'_{jk},g_k).\n$$\n\n$$\nB'_{ijk}=\\rho_{g_i}B_{ijk}+\\zeta_{ijk}+\\rho_{a'_{ij}}\\lambda_{jk}-\\lambda_{ik}+\\lambda_{ij}.\n$$\n\nThen $a'$ is flat and $(a',B')$ satisfies the same twisted tetrahedron equation. If $(g,\\lambda)$ is followed by $(h,\\mu)$, with $a''_{ij}=h_i a'_{ij}h_j^{-1}$, their composite is $(hg,\\nu)$ where\n\n$$\n\\nu_{ij}=\\beta(h_i,g_i,a_{ij})+\\rho_{h_i}\\lambda_{ij}-\\beta(h_i,a'_{ij},g_j)+\\mu_{ij}+\\beta(a''_{ij},h_j,g_j).\n$$\n\nEach transformation is invertible: for a fixed source background, put $h_i=g_i^{-1}$ and\n\n$$\n\\mu_{ij}=-\\beta(h_i,g_i,a_{ij})-\\rho_{h_i}\\lambda_{ij}+\\beta(h_i,a'_{ij},g_j)-\\beta(a_{ij},h_j,g_j);\n$$\n\nthen its composite has $\\nu_{ij}=0$. Treating a gauge arrow as its induced map on background cochains, sequential maps compose associatively, and the displayed formula gives a representative for each composite. This is a mathematical consistency result and does not assert a physical anomaly theorem.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-14.proof-contracts.json:48:          "id": "conditional-two-groupoid-closure",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-14.proof-contracts.json:50:          "claim": "Under the explicitly conditional page-13 consistency assertion, candidate gauge maps preserve backgrounds and their induced maps have identities, inverses, and associative composition.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-14.proof-contracts.json:136:      "finite_checks_disposition": "This is a data definition and conditional finite-groupoid construction; its concrete point and C2 cases are checked in the assigned B examples. The two-group specialization remains conditional."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-14.proof-contracts.json:142:          "source": "def-aqft-background-gauging-data",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-14.proof-contracts.json:144:          "quote": "Let $K$ be a finite ordered simplicial complex. A **finite background field groupoid** $\\mathcal F(K)$ is a category whose objects are declared background fields and whose morphisms are the declared gauge equivalences, such that every morphism is invertible, there are finitely many isomorphism classes, and every automorphism group is finite. Distinct automorphisms are retained as distinct morphisms whenever the selected field model declares them distinct; their multiplicities determine the gauge-volume factor. Write $\\pi_0(\\mathcal F(K))$ for the isomorphism classes.\n\nA **cocycle/action weight** on $\\mathcal F(K)$ is a function $W_K:\\operatorname{Ob}(\\mathcal F(K))\\to U(1)$ such that $W_K(y)=W_K(x)$ for every morphism $x\\to y$; this invariance is the degree-zero cocycle condition used here. Its normalized **gauging sum** is\n\n$$ Z_K(W_K):=\\sum_{[x]\\in\\pi_0(\\mathcal F(K))} \\frac{W_K(x)}{|\\operatorname{Aut}_{\\mathcal F(K)}(x)|}. $$\n\nThe quotient notation in this sum means isomorphism classes, not a set quotient with automorphisms discarded. The sum is the integral of $W_K$ against finite groupoid cardinality.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-14.proof-contracts.json:309:          "source": "def-aqft-background-gauging-data",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-14.proof-contracts.json:311:          "quote": "Let $K$ be a finite ordered simplicial complex. A **finite background field groupoid** $\\mathcal F(K)$ is a category whose objects are declared background fields and whose morphisms are the declared gauge equivalences, such that every morphism is invertible, there are finitely many isomorphism classes, and every automorphism group is finite. Distinct automorphisms are retained as distinct morphisms whenever the selected field model declares them distinct; their multiplicities determine the gauge-volume factor. Write $\\pi_0(\\mathcal F(K))$ for the isomorphism classes.\n\nA **cocycle/action weight** on $\\mathcal F(K)$ is a function $W_K:\\operatorname{Ob}(\\mathcal F(K))\\to U(1)$ such that $W_K(y)=W_K(x)$ for every morphism $x\\to y$; this invariance is the degree-zero cocycle condition used here. Its normalized **gauging sum** is\n\n$$ Z_K(W_K):=\\sum_{[x]\\in\\pi_0(\\mathcal F(K))} \\frac{W_K(x)}{|\\operatorname{Aut}_{\\mathcal F(K)}(x)|}. $$\n\nThe quotient notation in this sum means isomorphism classes, not a set quotient with automorphisms discarded. The sum is the integral of $W_K$ against finite groupoid cardinality.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-14.proof-contracts.json:397:          "source": "def-aqft-background-gauging-data",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-14.proof-contracts.json:399:          "quote": "Let $K$ be a finite ordered simplicial complex. A **finite background field groupoid** $\\mathcal F(K)$ is a category whose objects are declared background fields and whose morphisms are the declared gauge equivalences, such that every morphism is invertible, there are finitely many isomorphism classes, and every automorphism group is finite. Distinct automorphisms are retained as distinct morphisms whenever the selected field model declares them distinct; their multiplicities determine the gauge-volume factor. Write $\\pi_0(\\mathcal F(K))$ for the isomorphism classes.\n\nA **cocycle/action weight** on $\\mathcal F(K)$ is a function $W_K:\\operatorname{Ob}(\\mathcal F(K))\\to U(1)$ such that $W_K(y)=W_K(x)$ for every morphism $x\\to y$; this invariance is the degree-zero cocycle condition used here. Its normalized **gauging sum** is\n\n$$ Z_K(W_K):=\\sum_{[x]\\in\\pi_0(\\mathcal F(K))} \\frac{W_K(x)}{|\\operatorname{Aut}_{\\mathcal F(K)}(x)|}. $$\n\nThe quotient notation in this sum means isomorphism classes, not a set quotient with automorphisms discarded. The sum is the integral of $W_K$ against finite groupoid cardinality.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-14.proof-contracts.json:497:          "source": "def-aqft-background-gauging-data",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-14.proof-contracts.json:499:          "quote": "Let $K$ be a finite ordered simplicial complex. A **finite background field groupoid** $\\mathcal F(K)$ is a category whose objects are declared background fields and whose morphisms are the declared gauge equivalences, such that every morphism is invertible, there are finitely many isomorphism classes, and every automorphism group is finite. Distinct automorphisms are retained as distinct morphisms whenever the selected field model declares them distinct; their multiplicities determine the gauge-volume factor. Write $\\pi_0(\\mathcal F(K))$ for the isomorphism classes.\n\nA **cocycle/action weight** on $\\mathcal F(K)$ is a function $W_K:\\operatorname{Ob}(\\mathcal F(K))\\to U(1)$ such that $W_K(y)=W_K(x)$ for every morphism $x\\to y$; this invariance is the degree-zero cocycle condition used here. Its normalized **gauging sum** is\n\n$$ Z_K(W_K):=\\sum_{[x]\\in\\pi_0(\\mathcal F(K))} \\frac{W_K(x)}{|\\operatorname{Aut}_{\\mathcal F(K)}(x)|}. $$\n\nThe quotient notation in this sum means isomorphism classes, not a set quotient with automorphisms discarded. The sum is the integral of $W_K$ against finite groupoid cardinality.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-14.proof-contracts.json:509:          "quote": "Let $K=K_1\\cup_\\Sigma K_2$ be a finite ordered simplicial complex written as a union of two finite subcomplexes with common subcomplex $\\Sigma$. Let $\\mathcal F$, $\\mathcal F_1$, $\\mathcal F_2$, and $\\mathcal S$ be finite background field groupoids for $K$, $K_1$, $K_2$, and $\\Sigma$, with restriction functors $r_i:\\mathcal F_i\\to\\mathcal S$. The homotopy pullback $\\mathcal H=\\mathcal F_2\\times^h_{\\mathcal S}\\mathcal F_1$ has objects $(x_2,x_1,\\alpha)$ with $\\alpha:r_2(x_2)\\overset{\\cong}{\\longrightarrow}r_1(x_1)$ and morphisms $(u_2,u_1)$ satisfying $r_1(u_1)\\circ\\alpha=\\alpha'\\circ r_2(u_2)$.\n\nAssume that restriction induces an equivalence $J:\\mathcal F\\to\\mathcal H$. Let $W,W_1,W_2$ be $U(1)$-valued cocycle/action weights on these groupoids, and assume that under $J$ the weight factors as $W(x_2,x_1,\\alpha)=W_2(x_2)W_1(x_1)$. For each object $s$ of $\\mathcal S$, define the homotopy fiber $(\\mathcal F_i)_s$ to have objects $(x_i,\\eta_i:r_i(x_i)\\overset{\\cong}{\\longrightarrow}s)$ and morphisms $u_i$ satisfying $\\eta'_i\\circ r_i(u_i)=\\eta_i$, and put\n\n$$\nZ_i(s):=\\sum_{[(x_i,\\eta_i)]\\in\\pi_0((\\mathcal F_i)_s)}\n\\frac{W_i(x_i)}{|\\operatorname{Aut}_{(\\mathcal F_i)_s}(x_i,\\eta_i)|}.\n$$\n\nThen the normalized gauging sums are finite, are invariant under replacement of a field by a gauge-isomorphic field, and satisfy\n\n$$\nZ_K(W)=\n\\sum_{[s]\\in\\pi_0(\\mathcal S)}\n\\frac{Z_2(s)Z_1(s)}{|\\operatorname{Aut}_{\\mathcal S}(s)|}.\n$$\n\nThis is a finite groupoid theorem. A finite cocycle action is covered only when its evaluated weights obey the stated arrow-invariance and gluing-factorization hypotheses.",
