research/plan-spec.json:21453:          "statement": "Adopt as physical assumptions of the framework the following statements about frames, in the spacetime structure of [[post-cm01-spacetime]].\n\n**(i) Rotations.** A **rotation** is a matrix $R\\in SO(3)$, where\n\n$$SO(3)=\\left\\{R\\in\\mathbb R^{3\\times3}:R^{\\mathsf T}R=I_{3},\\ \\det R=1\\right\\}$$\n\nin the sense of [[def-cm-special-orthogonal-group]]. A rotation acts on spatial\ncolumn vectors by matrix multiplication; its entries are dimensionless under\nthe radian convention of [[def-cm01-units]]. Rotations are part of the\nformulation of spatial changes of basis; the definition of $SO(3)$ itself\nasserts nothing about physical space.\n\n**(ii) Inertial frames.** An **inertial frame** is the global adapted affine\nchart of the spacetime data determined by\n\n$$o\\in A,\\qquad u\\in V\\ \\text{with}\\ \\tau(u)=1,\\qquad (e_{1},e_{2},e_{3})\\ \\text{an ordered,}\\ g\\text{-orthonormal, positively oriented basis of }K,$$\n\nwith adapted coordinates $(t,x)$ as in [[def-cm-affine-time-space]]: the time\ncoordinate is $t(p)=\\tau(p-o)$ and the spatial coordinates are the coefficients\nof $p-o-tu\\in K$ in the basis $(e_{1},e_{2},e_{3})$. Existence and uniqueness\nof the coordinates, and the affine structure of the slices, are the mathematical\ncontent of [[lem-cm-adapted-coordinates-exist-and-are-unique]] and\n[[def-cm-affine-time-space]]; no physical claim beyond the reading of clause\n(iii) is attached to them here.\n\nAn inertial frame is a **coordinate chart**: the triple $(o,u,(e_{i}))$. It is\ndefinitively not an observer congruence and not a time-varying basis field. In\nthe model the basis $(e_{i})$ is fixed once and for all; a family of bases\ndepending on the time coordinate is a different structure, whose kinematics is\ndefined separately where it is used, and is not an inertial frame. The physical\nreading of the frame is that $t$ is the model's time coordinate in seconds and\n$x$ the spatial position in metres, by the conventions of\n[[def-cm01-units]].\n\n**(iii) Stationary observers.** For $x_{0}\\in\\mathbb R^{3}$ the curve\n$\\alpha_{x_{0}}:\\mathbb R\\to A$, $\\alpha_{x_{0}}(t):=o+tu+\\sum_{j=1}^{3}(x_{0})_{j}e_{j}$,\nis the **observer at rest in the frame at position $x_{0}$**; the family\n$\\{\\alpha_{x_{0}}\\}_{x_{0}\\in\\mathbb R^{3}}$ is the **stationary observer\ncongruence** of the frame. Each curve has $t(\\alpha_{x_{0}}(s))=s$, is\n$C^{\\infty}$ in adapted coordinates, and in the frame's own spatial\ncoordinates has constant coordinate triple $x_{0}$. Here an **observer\nworldline** means precisely a $C^2$ curve $\\alpha:I\\to A$ on a nondegenerate\nopen real interval with $t(\\alpha(s))=s$, used to describe an observer; the\nstationary curves just defined have $I=\\mathbb R$. This kinematic definition\nassigns neither a particle label nor a mass or a dynamical law to an observer.\nA particle worldline will separately be assigned to a particle label in the\nparticle postulate. The congruence is a derived object, determined by the frame and the spacetime data, not an additional primitive. Distinct frames can have the same family of parametrized curves: if $o' = o+\\sum_jc_je_j$ while $u'=u$ and $e_j'=e_j$, then $\\alpha'_{x_0}(t)=\\alpha_{x_0+c}(t)$ for all $t$. Thus shifting only the spatial origin relabels this family. Changing $u$ changes the curve family, since every curve in it has the affine derivative $u$.\n\n**(iv) Allowed changes between frames.** The model adopts the class of\ntransformations between inertial frames\n\n$$t'=t+b,\\qquad x'=Rx+vt+a,$$\n\nwhere $b\\in\\mathbb R$ is a **time shift** ($[b]=\\tau$), $a\\in\\mathbb R^{3}$ a\n**spatial translation** ($[a_{i}]=\\ell$), $v\\in\\mathbb R^{3}$ a **boost\nvelocity** ($[v_{i}]=\\ell\\tau^{-1}$) and $R\\in SO(3)$ a **rotation**\n($[R_{ij}]=1$). Every pair of inertial frames is related by exactly one such\ntransformation: that two adapted charts of the same data differ by a\ntransformation of this form, with $R\\in SO(3)$ because both bases are\npositively oriented, is the mathematical transformation law proved in\n[[lem-cm-adapted-charts-and-galilean-transformations]]. Uniqueness follows by comparing the coordinate maps on all $(t,x)\\in\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^3$: evaluating the time map fixes $b$; evaluating the spatial map at $(0,0)$ fixes $a$, at $(1,0)$ fixes $v$, and at $(0,e_j)$ for the three standard coordinate vectors fixes each column of $R$. The kinematic\nconsequences of the law — the transformation of coordinate velocity and\nacceleration, and the frame-independence of zero coordinate acceleration — are\nproved as a statement about the model in\n[[pthm-cm01-galilean-kinematics]].\n\n**(v) Free motion is not asserted here.** The framework's dynamics enters\nthrough the force and equation-of-motion postulates of the pages that follow;\nthose pages adopt, as the content of Newton's first law, that there exist\ninertial frames in the sense of clause (ii) in which a particle with zero\napplied interaction has zero coordinate acceleration. This postulate\nintroduces the frames and their changes but asserts no dynamics: it does not\nstate that any particular motion has zero acceleration, and it does not\nprivilege any one inertial frame — each of them is a legitimate arena for the\nformulation of the model's equations.\n\n**(vi) Kinematic symmetry is not conservation.** The class (iv) is a class of\ncoordinate changes. A quantity is **conserved along a motion** when its value as a function of the frame time is constant on the interval of that motion. No such constancy is asserted here, nor is it asserted that every\nmechanical system's description is invariant in form; a force law must also\ntransform appropriately under (iv) to be a law of the model in every frame, and\nan external apparatus or background field can single out frames for the\ndescription of a concrete experiment. Conservation statements, when they hold,\nare theorems of later pages with their own hypotheses, not consequences of\nthis postulate.",
research/plan-spec.json:22423:          "statement": "**(i) Setting.** Work in the force branch of\n[[post-cm02-force-superposition]] with one labeled particle of fixed mass\n$m>0$ ([[post-cm01-worldlines]] clause (v)) in a fixed inertial frame, with\ntime window $\\mathbb R$ and configuration domain $Q=\\mathbb R^{3}$, and with\nthe particle's coordinate position $x$ and coordinate velocity\n$v=\\dot x$ ([[def-cm01-velocity-acceleration]]). The particle moves through a\n**medium** that fills the region of the motion and is at rest in the chosen\nframe. The medium is not one of the model's particle labels, so its effect on\nthe label is an **external force** of the model in the sense of clause (i) of\n[[post-cm02-interactions]]: the decomposition into the label's own dynamics\nand the medium's effect is part of the model's declaration.\n\n**(ii) The model.** Adopt as a **constitutive model** of the medium's net\nforce the **linear drag model** with a declared coefficient\n$c\\ge0$ of dimension $\\mu\\tau^{-1}$, read in kilograms per second\n([[def-cm01-units]]):\n\n$$F^{\\mathrm{drag}}(v):=-c\\,v,$$\n\noptionally combined, through the additive composition of clause (v) of\n[[post-cm02-force-superposition]], with a declared constant applied force\n$m\\mathbf g$, where $\\mathbf g\\in\\mathbb R^{3}$ is a constant parameter of\ndimension $\\ell\\tau^{-2}$ ([[def-cm01-units]]). The resulting force map of the\nmodel is\n\n$$F(t,x,v)=m\\mathbf g-c\\,v,\\qquad\\text{that is,}\\qquad m\\,\\ddot x=m\\mathbf g-c\\,\\dot x,$$\n\nthe equation of a label whose only interactions are the constant applied field\nand the linear resistance of the medium. The model is defined for all\n$(t,x,v)\\in\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^{3}\\times\\mathbb R^{3}$.\n\n**(iii) Regularity.** The force map is affine in the state: it is continuous;\nand for two states $z=(x,v)$, $z'=(x,v')$ sharing a position,\n$\\lVert F(t,z)-F(t,z')\\rVert_{2}=c\\,\\lVert v-v'\\rVert_{2}\\le c\\,\\lVert z-z'\\rVert_{2}$,\nso it is locally Lipschitz in the state variable with the global constant $c$,\nuniformly in time ([[def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable]]). It is\ntherefore a force map of the branch [[post-cm02-force-superposition]] clause\n(iii), and the existence, uniqueness and continuation theorems of this page\napply to it; the regularity check is recorded with the model checks on the\ncompanion page.\n\n**(iv) Physical status and regime.** This is a **phenomenological**\nconstitutive model, adopted rather than derived: it represents the net effect\nof a medium whose microscopic state is not modelled, and the coefficient $c$\nis a model parameter that depends on the body and the medium. The linear form\nis a restricted regime — slow motion through a viscous medium, of which the\nStokes force on a sphere, $c=6\\pi\\eta a$ for dynamic viscosity $\\eta$ and\nradius $a$, is the recorded example — and other regimes are **different\nmodels**: a force growing quadratically in the speed at higher speeds, or\nCoulomb sliding friction, whose expression contains the factor\n$v/\\lvert v\\rvert$ and is undefined at $v=0$, and which is outside clause\n(iii) of [[post-cm02-force-superposition]] altogether. The model also neglects\nthe random component of the environment's force. The cited fluctuation\nqualification is specifically Likharev's damped harmonic oscillator model\n$m\\ddot q+\\gamma\\dot q+\\kappa q=0$, with $m,\\kappa>0$ and damping coefficient\n$\\gamma>0$ (denoted $\\eta$ in the source), oscillator energy\n$E:=\\tfrac12m\\dot q^{2}+\\tfrac12\\kappa q^{2}$, and natural angular frequency\n$\\omega_{0}:=\\sqrt{\\kappa/m}$: footnote 4 says\nthat neglecting its random-force component is admissible only when, at thermal\nequilibrium, $E$ is much greater than $\\max(k_{\\mathrm B}T,\\hbar\\omega_{0}/2)$,\nwhere $T$ is temperature, $k_{\\mathrm B}$ is Boltzmann's constant and $\\hbar$\nis the reduced Planck constant. The free-particle model stated here has no\nrestoring term and defines no corresponding $E$, $T$ or $\\omega_{0}$, so the\noscillator condition is not an additional admissibility condition on this\nmodel. Applying the idealization to a concrete medium requires a separate\nmodel of its fluctuations.\nNo value of $c$, of $m$, or of $\\mathbf g$ is asserted by this item, and using\nmeasured values for them is an empirical premise with its own conditions and\nuncertainty.\n\n**(v) Status.** Adopted assumption of the framework when a model invokes it;\nit selects no law of nature on its own and proves nothing about the actual\nmotion of any body in any medium. Its consequences within the model are the\nsubject of the companion example on terminal-velocity approach.",
research/plan-spec.json:23279:          "statement": "Adopt the force model and the notation of\n[[def-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-momenta]], and let\n$$F=\\bigl(o,u,(e_{1},e_{2},e_{3})\\bigr),\\qquad F'=\\bigl(o',u',(e_{1}',e_{2}',e_{3}')\\bigr)$$\nbe two inertial frames with coordinates $(t,x)$ and $(t',x')$ related by\n$$t'=t+b,\\qquad x'=Rx+v\\,t+a_{0},\\qquad b\\in\\mathbb R,\\ a_{0},v\\in\\mathbb R^{3},\\ R\\in SO(3)$$\n([[pthm-cm01-galilean-kinematics]] clause 1,\n[[def-cm-special-orthogonal-group]]). Let $x:J\\to Q$ be a curve family of the\nmodel in the first frame and let\n$$\\eta_{i}(t'):=R\\,x_{i}(t'-b)+v\\,(t'-b)+a_{0}\\qquad(t'\\in J+b)$$\nbe the description of the same worldlines in the second frame. Write\n$\\sigma_{i}(t):=F_{i}(t,x(t),w(t))$ and\n$\\Sigma(t):=\\sum_{i}\\sigma_{i}(t)$ for the evaluated total forces in the first\nframe, and read the second frame's quantities from the description $\\eta$\nwith the transferred force map $F_{*}$ of clause (vi) of\n[[post-cm02-force-superposition]]: $\\tilde\\sigma_{i}(t'):=F_{*i}(t',\\eta(t'),\\eta'(t'))$,\n$\\tilde P:=\\sum_{i}m_{i}\\eta_{i}'$,\n$\\tilde X:=M^{-1}\\sum_{i}m_{i}\\eta_{i}$,\n$\\tilde K:=\\frac12\\sum_{i}m_{i}\\lVert\\eta_{i}'\\rVert_{2}^{2}$,\n$\\tilde J_{\\tilde o}:=\\sum_{i}(\\eta_{i}-\\tilde o)\\times m_{i}\\eta_{i}'$ and\n$\\tilde T_{\\tilde o}:=\\sum_{i}(\\eta_{i}-\\tilde o)\\times\\tilde\\sigma_{i}$\nfor any $C^{1}$ reference curve $\\tilde o:J+b\\to\\mathbb R^{3}$ (clause 3\nuses the curve corresponding to $o$),\n$\\tilde{\\mathcal P}:=\\sum_{i}\\langle\\tilde\\sigma_{i},\\eta_{i}'\\rangle$ and\n$\\tilde W([t_{0}',t_{1}']):=\\int_{t_{0}'}^{t_{1}'}\\tilde{\\mathcal P}(t')\\,dt'$\nfor $t_{0}'\\le t_{1}'$ in $J+b$. Then, with all first-frame quantities read\nat the corresponding time $t'-b$:\n\n1. **Centre of mass and momentum.**\n   $$\\tilde X(t')=R\\,X(t'-b)+v\\,(t'-b)+a_{0},\\qquad \\tilde X'(t')=R\\,V(t'-b)+v,\\qquad \\tilde P(t')=R\\,P(t'-b)+M\\,v\\,;$$\n2. **Kinetic energy.**\n   $$\\tilde K(t')=K(t'-b)+\\bigl\\langle v,\\,R\\,P(t'-b)\\bigr\\rangle+\\frac12M\\lVert v\\rVert_{2}^{2};$$\n3. **Angular momentum and torque.** For every $C^{1}$ reference curve\n   $o:J\\to\\mathbb R^{3}$ of the definition, let\n   $\\tilde o(t'):=R\\,o(t'-b)+v(t'-b)+a_{0}$ and\n   $X_{o}(t):=M^{-1}\\sum_{i}m_{i}(x_{i}(t)-o(t))=X(t)-o(t)$. Then\n   $$\\tilde J_{\\tilde o}(t')=R\\,J_{o}(t'-b)+\\bigl(R\\,X_{o}(t'-b)\\bigr)\\times(M\\,v),\\qquad \\tilde T_{\\tilde o}(t')=R\\,T_{o}(t'-b),$$\n   where $T_{o}(t):=\\sum_{i}(x_{i}(t)-o(t))\\times\\sigma_{i}(t)$ is the\n   evaluated total torque of the first frame about $o(t)$;\n4. **Power and work.**\n   $$\\tilde{\\mathcal P}(t')=\\mathcal P(t'-b)+\\bigl\\langle R\\,\\Sigma(t'-b),\\,v\\bigr\\rangle,$$\n   and for every curve family and $t_{0}<t_{1}$ in $J$\n   $$\\tilde W\\bigl([t_{0}+b,\\,t_{1}+b]\\bigr)=W([t_{0},t_{1}])+\\int_{t_{0}}^{t_{1}}\\bigl\\langle R\\,\\Sigma(t),v\\bigr\\rangle\\,dt.$$\n   If in addition $x$ is a motion and the model invokes the pair presentation\n   of [[post-cm02-interactions]] with the antisymmetry clause, then\n   $\\Sigma=P'$ along the motion by [[pthm-cm03-momentum-balance]], and\n   $$\\tilde W\\bigl([t_{0}+b,\\,t_{1}+b]\\bigr)=W([t_{0},t_{1}])+\\bigl\\langle R\\bigl(P(t_{1})-P(t_{0})\\bigr),\\,v\\bigr\\rangle;$$\n5. **Potential and mechanical energy.** If the model is a global potential\n   model of [[def-cm03-potential-and-mechanical-energy]] clause (a) with\n   potential $U:I_{0}\\times Q\\to\\mathbb R$ of class $C^{2}$ and the second\n   frame's model declares the **scalar transfer**\n   $$U_{*}(t',y):=U\\bigl(t'-b,\\ R^{\\mathsf T}(y_{1}-v(t'-b)-a_{0}),\\dots,R^{\\mathsf T}(y_{N}-v(t'-b)-a_{0})\\bigr),$$\n   then $U_{*}$ is $C^{2}$ on its open transferred domain,\n   $F_{*i}(t',y,u)=-\\nabla_{y_{i}}U_{*}(t',y)$ at corresponding data, and the\n   mechanical energies of clause (b) of that definition satisfy\n   $$\\tilde E(t'):=\\tilde K(t')+U_{*}\\bigl(t',\\eta(t')\\bigr)=E(t'-b)+\\bigl\\langle v,\\,R\\,P(t'-b)\\bigr\\rangle+\\frac12M\\lVert v\\rVert_{2}^{2}.$$\n\nWhether the second frame's model uses the scalar transfer of clause 5 is a\nmodelling declaration; a model whose potential is not transferred by this rule\n(for instance a background potential that selects a frame) is a different\nmodel in the second frame, and no transformation law of $E$ is asserted for\nit. No empirical content is claimed.",
research/plan-spec.json:24293:          "statement": "**(i) Setting.** Work in the one-dimensional model class of\n[[def-cm04-one-dimensional-models-and-equilibria]] with time window\n$I_{0}=\\mathbb R$, configuration domain $Q=\\mathbb R$, mass $m>0$, spring\nconstant $k>0$ ([[post-cm04-harmonic-model]]) and drag coefficient $c\\ge0$\n([[post-cm04-linear-damping-model]]). Let a real **drive amplitude**\n$f_{0}\\in\\mathbb R$ of dimension $\\mu\\ell\\tau^{-2}$ (newtons) and an\n**angular drive frequency** $\\Omega>0$ of dimension $\\tau^{-1}$ (radians per\nsecond; the radian is dimensionless in the framework's angle convention,\n[[def-cm01-units]]) be declared. Adopt, through the additive composition of\nforce contributions of clause (v) of [[post-cm02-force-superposition]] and\nthe external-contribution reading of [[post-cm02-interactions]], the force\nlaw\n$$F(t,x,v):=f_{0}\\cos(\\Omega t)-k\\,x-c\\,v .$$\nThe **sinusoidally driven oscillator model** is the one-dimensional model\nwith this force law; its equation of motion is\n$$m\\,x''+c\\,x'+k\\,x=f_{0}\\cos(\\Omega t),$$\nand its data $(m,c,k,f)$ with $f(t)=f_{0}\\cos(\\Omega t)$ are oscillator-type\ndata in the sense of clause (f) of\n[[def-cm04-one-dimensional-models-and-equilibria]]. The term\n$f_{0}\\cos(\\Omega t)$ is the **drive** or **driving force**; the model is\nundamped when $c=0$ and strictly damped when $c>0$.\n\n**(ii) The drive is prescribed external data.** The driving force is a\nprescribed function of time alone: it does not depend on $x$ or on $x'$, and\nthe object producing it is not one of the model's labels and is not modelled\ndynamically. This is the same modelling status as the medium of\n[[post-cm02-drag]]: the drive is an external contribution declared as part\nof the model. A real apparatus, background or force generator that reacts to\nthe motion is **not** represented by this model; representing such a\nback-reaction requires a larger model with additional states.\n\n**(iii) Potential presentation with residual force.** The model presents its\nforce law through the harmonic potential $U(x)=\\tfrac12kx^{2}$ with residual\nforce $d(t,x,v)=f_{0}\\cos(\\Omega t)-c\\,v$, in the sense of clause (d) of\n[[def-cm04-one-dimensional-models-and-equilibria]]:\n$F(t,x,v)=-U'(x)+d(t,x,v)$. The mechanical energy of the model is\n$E(x,v)=\\tfrac12mv^{2}+\\tfrac12kx^{2}$ of that clause, defined along every\nmotion; the model is driven and not a global potential model whenever\n$f_{0}\\neq0$.\n\n**(iv) Regularity.** Let $X:=x_{\\rm phys}/(1\\,\\mathrm m)$,\n$V:=v_{\\rm phys}/(1\\,\\mathrm{m/s})$, and $T:=t_{\\rm phys}/(1\\,\\mathrm s)$\nbe the dimensionless numerical SI coordinates (the real inputs of the force\nmap). Write\n$K:=k_{\\rm phys}/(1\\,\\mathrm{N/m})$,\n$C:=c_{\\rm phys}/(1\\,\\mathrm{kg/s})$,\n$\\widehat f_{0}:=f_{0}/(1\\,\\mathrm N)$, and\n$\\widehat\\Omega:=\\Omega(1\\,\\mathrm s)$. The numerical force map is\n$$\\widehat F(T,X,V)=\\widehat f_{0}\\cos(\\widehat\\Omega T)-KX-CV,$$\nso it is continuous and globally Lipschitz in the state variable with\nnumerical-coordinate constant $K+C$, uniformly in time. This is a bound on\ndimensionless SI coordinates and force values, not a sum of the dimensionful\n$k$ and $c$ ([[def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable]]). It is an\nadmissible force law of clause (a) of [[def-cm04-one-dimensional-models-and-equilibria]],\nand its equation is the forced model $m\\,x''+cx'+kx=f$ of\n[[def-cm-oscillator-model-and-phase-system]] with the continuous function\n$f(t)=f_{0}\\cos(\\Omega t)$.\n\n**(v) Physical status and regime.** The model is a **restricted idealization**\nadopted rather than derived: exactly sinusoidal, with constant amplitude and\nfrequency and no dependence on the motion. The drive amplitude and frequency\nare model parameters; when their values come from an apparatus calibration,\nthat use is an empirical premise carrying the apparatus conditions and the\nmeasurement uncertainty, and this item asserts no such value. The sinusoidal\nform is the elementary component used by the recorded treatments; the\nresponse to a non-sinusoidal periodic drive is a superposition statement\nbelonging to the mathematical theory, not an additional claim here. The\nmodel's undamped case $c=0$ is included as a parameter value, and it is where\nthe resonant unboundedness of the following theorem occurs.\n\n**(vi) Status.** Adopted assumption of the framework when a model invokes\nit; it selects no law of nature on its own, and no measured response, no\nresonance curve of any apparatus and no empirical resonance phenomenon is\nasserted by the postulate.",
research/plan-spec.json:24321:          "physical_scope": "Adoption of the driven oscillator as a restricted one-dimensional model: the damped oscillator with parameters m>0, k>0, c>=0 together with an external driving force f(t)=f_0 cos(Omega t) of declared amplitude f_0 and angular frequency Omega>0, prescribed as a function of time and independent of the motion. The drive is an idealization of an apparatus or background that maintains a fixed sinusoidal force; it is adopted for a stated regime, its amplitude and frequency are model parameters, and no measured value, no back-reaction model, no non-sinusoidal waveform and no claim that a given apparatus realizes the model is asserted.",
research/plan-spec.json:25738:          "statement": "Adopt the force branch of [[post-cm02-force-superposition]]: $N\\ge1$ particle\nlabels with fixed masses $m_{1},\\dots,m_{N}>0$, a nondegenerate open time window\n$I_{0}\\subseteq\\mathbb R$, an open configuration domain $Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3N}$\n([[def-cm01-configuration]] clause (a)), and a force map\n$$F:I_{0}\\times Q\\times(\\mathbb R^{3})^{N}\\longrightarrow(\\mathbb R^{3})^{N}$$\nwith the standing continuity and state-local-Lipschitz hypotheses of clause\n(iii) of that postulate. Fix one inertial frame of the model with adapted\ncoordinates $(t,x)$ ([[post-cm01-frames]]), identify\n$x=(x_{1},\\dots,x_{N})\\in(\\mathbb R^{3})^{N}=\\mathbb R^{3N}$ with the position\ntuple of the labels ([[def-cm01-configuration]]), and let $\\mathsf M$ be the\nmass operator of clause (d) of that definition, $\\mathsf M(x_{1},\\dots,x_{N})\n=(m_{1}x_{1},\\dots,m_{N}x_{N})$, a symmetric positive definite linear map with\nblock-diagonal matrix and blocks $m_{i}I_{3}$. All components below are read in\nthis frame, and dimensions are read by [[def-cm01-units]].\n\n**(a) Constraint datum.** A **holonomic-constraint datum** on this data is a\ntriple $(O,k,G)$ with:\n\n1. an **ambient set** $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^{3N}$, open and\n   nonempty;\n2. an integer $k$ with $1\\le k\\le 3N-1$;\n3. a map $G=(G_{1},\\dots,G_{k}):O\\to\\mathbb R^{k}$ of class $C^{2}$\n   ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]), each component carrying a\n   declared dimension $[G_{\\alpha}]=\\gamma_{\\alpha}$ fixed by the model (the\n   model may rescale a component by a nonzero constant, which changes\n   $\\gamma_{\\alpha}$ and the component but not its zero set);\n\nsubject to the two hypotheses\n\n4. **nonempty admissible locus:** the **constraint locus**\n   $$\\Sigma:=\\bigl\\{(t,x)\\in O:\\ G(t,x)=0\\bigr\\}$$\n   is nonempty and contained in $I_{0}\\times Q$: every constrained\n   configuration is an admissible configuration of the force-branch model and\n   every constrained time lies in its time window;\n5. **regularity:** for every $(t,x)\\in\\Sigma$ the Jacobian $D_{x}G(t,x)$\n   ([[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]]) is surjective, equivalently has rank\n   $k$.\n\nFor $t\\in\\mathbb R$ write $\\Sigma_{t}:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^{3N}:(t,x)\\in\\Sigma\\}$\nfor the **instantaneous constraint locus**. Here a **$C^{2}$ embedded\nsubmanifold of dimension $d$** in $\\mathbb R^{n}$ means a subset $S$, with\nits inherited topology, such that each $p\\in S$ has an open neighbourhood\n$U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{n}$ and a $C^{2}$ diffeomorphism\n$h:U\\to W$ onto an open $W\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{n}$ satisfying\n$h(S\\cap U)=W\\cap(\\mathbb R^{d}\\times\\{0\\})$; both $h$ and its inverse\nare $C^{2}$ in the sense of [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]].\nThis is the finite-regularity slice convention used for these loci;\n[[def-embedded-submanifold-and-slice-chart]] instead requires smooth charts.\n\nEach nonempty $\\Sigma_t$ is a $C^{2}$ embedded submanifold of dimension\n$3N-k$. Indeed, at any $p\\in\\Sigma_t$ choose $k$ spatial columns of\n$D_xG(t,p)$ giving an invertible minor, and permute coordinates as\n$x=(u,v)\\in\\mathbb R^{3N-k}\\times\\mathbb R^k$. The fixed-time map\n$H(u,v):=G(t,(u,v))$ is $C^2$ on the open slice of $O$, vanishes at $p$,\nand has invertible $D_vH(p)$. Apply\n[[thm-parametrized-implicit-function-theorem-with-higher-regularity]] with\nregularity order $2$ and no parameter block: on open neighbourhoods\n$P\\times Q$ of $p$, its zero set is exactly $v=\\varphi(u)$ for a\n$C^2$ map $\\varphi:P\\to Q$. The map\n$h(u,v)=(u,v-\\varphi(u))$ is a $C^2$ diffeomorphism onto the open set\n$W=\\{(u,w):u\\in P,\\ w+\\varphi(u)\\in Q\\}$, with inverse\n$(u,w)\\mapsto(u,w+\\varphi(u))$, and sends that graph to\n$W\\cap(\\mathbb R^{3N-k}\\times\\{0\\})$. This proves the claimed local\nslice property. If $G$ is smooth, the smooth regular-value terminology of\n[[def-regular-and-critical-points-and-values]] and the smooth conclusion of\n[[thm-a-regular-level-set-is-an-embedded-submanifold]] also apply.\nWhen $k=3N-1$ the instantaneous locus is locally a $C^2$ curve, and the number\n$$\\dim\\Sigma_{t}=3N-k$$\nis the **instantaneous degrees of freedom** of the constrained branch. The\nset $V(t,x):=\\ker D_{x}G(t,x)\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3N}$ has dimension $3N-k$ by\nthe rank-nullity theorem for matrices ([[cor-matrix-rank-nullity]]) and is called the space of **virtual displacements** of the datum\nat $(t,x)$; its elements are the first-order differences of constrained\nconfigurations at fixed time, in the sense of the mathematical definition\n[[def-cm-holonomic-constraint-and-ideal-reactions]]. A vector\n$v\\in\\mathbb R^{3N}$ is a **permitted velocity** at $(t,x)\\in O$ when\n$$G_{t}(t,x)+D_{x}G(t,x)\\,v=0 .$$\n\n**(b) Constrained curve families.** Let $J\\subseteq I_{0}$ be an interval with\nnonempty interior ([[def-interval]]). A **constrained curve family** of the\ndatum is a map $x:J\\to\\mathbb R^{3N}$ of class $C^{2}$ with\n$(t,x(t))\\in O$ and $G(t,x(t))=0$ for every $t\\in J$; writing\n$x=(x_{1},\\dots,x_{N})$ with coordinate curves $x_{i}:J\\to\\mathbb R^{3}$ and\n$w_{i}:=x_{i}'$, the componentwise derivatives of\n[[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]] ([[def-cm01-velocity-acceleration]];\n$w_{i}$ is the coordinate velocity of label $i$), the chain rule\n([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]]) applied to $t\\mapsto G(t,x(t))$\ngives\n$$G_{t}\\bigl(t,x(t)\\bigr)+D_{x}G\\bigl(t,x(t)\\bigr)\\,w(t)=0\\qquad(t\\in J),$$\nso the velocity of a $C^{1}$ constrained curve family is permitted at every\ntime. A tuple $(t_{0},x_{0},v_{0})\\in O\\times\\mathbb R^{3N}$ is **consistent\ndata** of the datum when $G(t_{0},x_{0})=0$ and\n$G_{t}(t_{0},x_{0})+D_{x}G(t_{0},x_{0})v_{0}=0$; these are the initial data at\nwhich constrained motions are posed below.\n\n**(c) Reaction field and the constrained equation.** A **reaction field along\na curve family** $x:J\\to\\mathbb R^{3N}$ is a continuous map\n$R:J\\to\\mathbb R^{3N}$, of force dimension $\\mu\\ell\\tau^{-2}$ in each\ncomponent. The **equation of the constrained datum along $x$** is\n$$\\mathsf M\\,x''(t)=F\\bigl(t,x(t),w(t)\\bigr)+R(t)\\qquad(t\\in J),$$\ncomponentwise $m_{i}x_{i}''(t)=F_{i}(t,x(t),w(t))+R_{i}(t)$. A\n**holonomic-constraint model** consists of the force-branch data, a\nholonomic-constraint datum $(O,k,G)$, and the declaration that the position\ntuple of the labels satisfies $G(t,x(t))=0$ for all times while a reaction\nfield acts: a **constrained motion** of the model (with reaction $R$) is a\nconstrained curve family $x:J\\to\\mathbb R^{3N}$ together with a reaction field\n$R$ satisfying the displayed equation. The force map $F$ is the model's\ndeclared interaction force of the force branch, and $R$ is the additional\nforce contribution exerted by the mechanism that realizes the constraint; the\ntotal force along a constrained motion is the pointwise sum $F+R$ in the sense\nof clause (v) of [[post-cm02-force-superposition]], read along the curve. The\nreaction is **not** part of the force map: the constrained branch is the branch\nexcluded by clause (i) of that postulate, and the equation above is the\nadopted equation of the constrained model. For any specified constrained curve\nfamily $x$, this equation fixes the reaction uniquely as the residual field\n$$R(t)=\\mathsf Mx''(t)-F\\bigl(t,x(t),x'(t)\\bigr).$$\nWithout an additional reaction law, the constraint and this equation do not\nselect which constrained curve families are dynamically admissible: every\n$C^{2}$ constrained curve can be paired with its continuous residual field to\nsatisfy the equation. The ideal-reaction law of the postulate of this page is\nthe additional model assumption that selects among these candidates by\nrequiring the reaction to annihilate the virtual displacements.\n\n**(d) Scleronomic and rheonomic constraints.** The datum is **scleronomic**\nwhen the constraint locus is time-independent, in the precise sense that there\nis a set $N\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3N}$ with\n$$\\Sigma=\\bigl\\{(t,x)\\in O:\\ x\\in N\\bigr\\};$$\nequivalently, membership in the locus is a condition on $x$ alone within $O$:\nif $(t,x)\\in\\Sigma$ and $(t',x)\\in O$, then $(t',x)\\in\\Sigma$ (the two\nformulations are exchanged by passing between $N$ and the projection of\n$\\Sigma$ to $\\mathbb R^{3N}$). For the standard product ambient set\n$O=I_{0}\\times Q$ the condition reads $\\Sigma=I_{0}\\times(N\\cap Q)$. The datum\nis **rheonomic** otherwise.\n\nIf the datum is scleronomic, then the declared presentation $G$, and every\n$C^{2}$ presentation of the same locus with the rank hypothesis, satisfy\n$$G_{t}(t,x)=0\\qquad\\text{for every }(t,x)\\in\\Sigma,$$\nbecause for $x\\in N$ every $(t,x)\\in O$ lies in $\\Sigma$, so $t\\mapsto G(t,x)$\nvanishes identically on the open set of times $\\{t:(t,x)\\in O\\}$; this is the\nform in which the classification is used by the balance theorems of this page.\n\nThe identity is a consequence of time-independence and **not** a criterion for\nit. Write $s(x):=(x_{1})_{1}\\in\\mathbb R$ for the first Cartesian\ncomponent of particle $1$, of length dimension $\\ell$, and fix a nonzero\nspeed $u$ of dimension $\\ell\\tau^{-1}$. The scalar presentation\n$G(t,x):=(s(x)-ut)^{2}$, of dimension $\\ell^{2}$, on\n$O=\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^{3N}$ has the moving zero set $\\{s(x)=ut\\}$ while\n$G_{t}(t,x)=-2u(s(x)-ut)$ vanishes at every point of that set; this presentation\nviolates the rank hypothesis, but the identity can also hold for a\nrank-regular presentation: on\n$O=\\bigl((0,T_{*})\\cup(2T_{*},3T_{*})\\bigr)\\times\\mathbb R^{3N}$,\nwith $T_{*}>0$ of time dimension, the scalar $C^{2}$ map equal to\n$s(x)$ on $(0,T_{*})\\times\\mathbb R^{3N}$ and to a fixed\n$L_{*}>0$ of length dimension on $(2T_{*},3T_{*})\\times\\mathbb R^{3N}$\nhas dimension $\\ell$ and the rank-$1$ zero set\n$\\Sigma=(0,T_{*})\\times\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^{3N}:s(x)=0\\}$ with $G_{t}=0$ on $\\Sigma$,\nyet $\\Sigma$ is not of the form $\\{(t,x)\\in O:x\\in N\\}$ for any $N$: the point\n$(T_{*}/2,0)\\in\\Sigma$ would force $0\\in N$, and then $(5T_{*}/2,0)\\in O$ would lie in\n$\\Sigma$. Hence the classification is a property of the locus, and the\nidentity is used only in the direction proved above.\n\nUnder a nowhere-vanishing $C^{2}$ rescaling $G'=\\rho G$ one has\n$D_{x}G'=\\rho\\,D_{x}G$ and $G'_{t}=\\rho_{t}G+\\rho G_{t}=\\rho G_{t}$ at the\npoints of $\\Sigma$, so the virtual displacements and permitted velocities are\nunchanged, the multiplier of a motion transforms as\n$\\lambda'=\\rho^{-1}\\lambda$, and the reaction $D_{x}G'^{\\mathsf T}\\lambda'$\nand the product $-\\lambda'\\cdot G'_{t}=-\\lambda\\cdot G_{t}$ of the rheonomic\npower theorem are unchanged at the locus.\n\n**(e) Status.** This item defines a model class: it fixes the constraint\ndatum, the constrained curves, the virtual displacements, the reaction field\nand the equation of the constrained branch. It adopts no ideal-reaction law\n(that is the content of the ideal-reaction postulate of this page), no particular force\nlaw, and no measured value. Whether a concrete apparatus — a frictionless\njoint, a smooth track, a taut rod or string, a no-slip contact — is described\nby a holonomic-constraint model is an application-level judgement recorded with\nthe apparatus, together with the regime in which the rigidity, frictionlessness\nor contact assumptions are adequate; the framework itself attaches no empirical\nclaim to the class.",
research/plan-spec.json:25756:            "thm-parametrized-implicit-function-theorem-with-higher-regularity"
research/plan-spec.json:25774:            "thm-parametrized-implicit-function-theorem-with-higher-regularity": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json:26975:          "statement": "Adopt the force model with potential presentation $(U,d)$ and mechanical\nLagrangian $L=K-U$ of [[def-cm07-action-and-mechanical-lagrangian]], with\nmasses $m_{1},\\dots,m_{N}>0$, open configuration domain $Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3N}$,\ntime window $I_{0}$, mass operator $\\mathsf M$ and $C^{2}$ potential\n$U:I_{0}\\times Q\\to\\mathbb R$ ([[def-cm03-potential-and-mechanical-energy]]).\nDimensions are read by [[def-cm01-units]]; gradients and Jacobians are those of\n[[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]], transposes those of\n[[def-transpose-of-a-matrix]], and $C^{r}$ classes those of\n[[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]].\n\n**(a) Generalized-coordinate map.** Let $r\\ge1$ and let\n$\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{r}$ be open and nonempty. A **generalized-coordinate\nmap of the model** is a map $X:\\Omega\\to Q$ of class $C^{3}$ whose derivative\n$DX(q)$ is injective for every $q\\in\\Omega$\n([[def-euclidean-submersions-and-immersions]]: an immersion); the coordinates\n$q=(q_{1},\\dots,q_{r})$ each carry a declared dimension $\\gamma_{i}$ and unit\nfixed as part of the model declaration. When $r=3N$, injectivity makes\n$DX(q):\\mathbb R^{3N}\\to\\mathbb R^{3N}$ a bijection: its kernel is $\\{0\\}$,\nso rank-nullity gives image dimension $3N$, equal to the target dimension.\nThe inverse is linear: if $y_{1}=DX(q)u_{1}$ and $y_{2}=DX(q)u_{2}$, then\nlinearity and uniqueness of preimages give\n$DX(q)^{-1}(a y_{1}+b y_{2})=a u_{1}+b u_{2}$. Thus $DX(q)$ is invertible in\nthe sense of [[def-invertible-euclidean-linear-map]]. The inverse-function\ntheorem and its higher-regularity result\n([[thm-euclidean-inverse-function-theorem]],\n[[thm-higher-regularity-of-local-inverses]]) make $X$ a local $C^{3}$\ndiffeomorphism at each point. This local property does not imply that $X$ is\nglobally one-to-one: call $X$ a global coordinate chart onto $X(\\Omega)$ only\nwhen it is also injective. The other intended case is $r=3N-k$ for an integer\n$k$ with $1\\le k\\le3N-1$, where $X$ may be a surjective immersion of a regular\nholonomic-constraint locus as in clause (d); injectivity is not required there.\nThe **admissible coordinate curves** are the $C^{2}$ maps $q:J\\to\\Omega$ on an\ninterval $J\\subseteq I_{0}$ with nonempty interior ([[def-interval]]); each such\ncurve induces the configuration curve $x:=X\\circ q:J\\to Q$.\n\n**(b) Substituted Lagrangian and kinetic metric.** For a generalized-coordinate\nmap $X$ define\n\n$$\\widetilde L:I_{0}\\times\\Omega\\times\\mathbb R^{r}\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R,\\qquad \\widetilde L(t,q,v):=L\\bigl(t,X(q),DX(q)v\\bigr)=\\tfrac12\\,v^{\\mathsf T}G(q)v-\\widetilde U(t,q),$$\n\nwith the **kinetic metric** and the substituted potential\n\n$$G(q):=DX(q)^{\\mathsf T}\\mathsf M\\,DX(q),\\qquad \\widetilde U(t,q):=U\\bigl(t,X(q)\\bigr).$$\n\n$G(q)$ is symmetric and positive definite, because $DX(q)$ is injective and\n$\\mathsf M$ is symmetric positive definite\n([[def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix]], [[def-linear-map]],\n[[def-euclidean-inner-product]]); its entry\n$G_{ij}$ has dimension $\\mu\\ell^{2}\\gamma_{i}^{-1}\\gamma_{j}^{-1}$ and the\nquadratic quantity $\\frac12v^{\\mathsf T}G(q)v$ has dimension\n$\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-2}$ for every choice of coordinate dimensions.\n$\\widetilde L$ is of class $C^{2}$; the **substituted action** of an admissible\ncoordinate curve $q:[a,b]\\to\\Omega$ with $[a,b]\\subseteq I_{0}$ is\n$\\widetilde S(q):=\\int_{a}^{b}\\widetilde L(t,q(t),q'(t))dt$ whenever the\nchart conventions of [[def-cm07-action-and-mechanical-lagrangian]] apply to\n$\\widetilde L$, and the **Lagrange operator** of $\\widetilde L$ is\n\n$$\\Lambda(\\widetilde L)(q)(t):=\\frac{d}{dt}\\Bigl(\\frac{\\partial\\widetilde L}{\\partial v}\\bigl(t,q(t),q'(t)\\bigr)\\Bigr)-\\frac{\\partial\\widetilde L}{\\partial q}\\bigl(t,q(t),q'(t)\\bigr)\\in\\mathbb R^{r}.$$\n\n**(c) Generalized forces.** Let $f:I_{0}\\times Q\\times\\mathbb R^{3N}\\to\\mathbb R^{3N}$\nbe a force field of the model's standing regularity\n([[post-cm02-force-superposition]] clause (iii)) — for instance the residual\nforce $d$ of the presentation, or the model's full force map $F$. Its\n**generalized force** in the coordinates $X$ is\n\n$$Q_{f}(t,q,v):=DX(q)^{\\mathsf T}f\\bigl(t,X(q),DX(q)v\\bigr)\\in\\mathbb R^{r};$$\n\nits $i$-th component has the dimension of energy per unit of the coordinate\n$q_{i}$, namely $\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-2}\\gamma_{i}^{-1}$: the Jacobian factor\n$\\partial X_{a}/\\partial q_{i}$ carries the dimension $\\ell\\gamma_{i}^{-1}$ and\nthe force $f_{a}$ the dimension $\\mu\\ell\\tau^{-2}$. Thus $Q_{f}\\cdot\\delta q$\nhas energy dimension; for a length-valued coordinate $Q_{f,i}$ has force\ndimension, and for a dimensionless angle it has torque dimension\n$\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-2}$. For the presentation\n$(U,d)$ write $Q^{\\mathrm{res}}:=Q_{d}$ for the generalized force of the\nresidual part; when $d\\equiv0$ one has $Q^{\\mathrm{res}}\\equiv0$.\n\n**(d) Constraint parametrizations.** Let a regular holonomic-constraint datum\n$(O,k,g_{c})$ of [[def-cm06-holonomic-constraint-model]] be given, where\n$g_{c}:=G$ is the constraint map called $G$ in that definition; this separate\nnotation distinguishes it from the kinetic matrix $G(q)$ in clause (b). Let\n$\\mathcal C\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3N}$ be its time-independent configuration\nlocus, so $\\Sigma=\\{(t,x)\\in O:x\\in\\mathcal C\\}$ (the scleronomic case).\nA **parametrization of the constrained configurations** is a\ngeneralized-coordinate map $X:\\Omega\\to Q$ with $r=3N-k$,\n$X(\\Omega)=\\mathcal C\\cap Q$, and $(t,X(q))\\in O$ for all\n$(t,q)\\in I_{0}\\times\\Omega$. Thus $X$ covers every constrained configuration,\nbut a configuration may have more than one coordinate representative; a\ncovering or other surjective immersion is allowed. Injectivity is an optional\nadditional condition for applications that require unique coordinates. Each\nadmissible coordinate curve induces a constrained curve family with permitted\nvelocity; the reduction theorem of this page also identifies the virtual\ndisplacements with the image of $DX$.\n\n**(e) Declared Lagrange equations.** With the data of clauses (a)-(c), the\nmodel with presentation $(U,d)$ **declares as its Lagrange equations in the\ncoordinates $X$**\n\n$$\\Lambda(\\widetilde L)(q)(t)=Q^{\\mathrm{res}}\\bigl(t,q(t),q'(t)\\bigr)\\qquad(t\\in J),$$\n\nposed for $C^{2}$ admissible coordinate curves $q:J\\to\\Omega$. These equations\nare part of the coordinate description: for $d\\equiv0$ they reduce to the\nEuler-Lagrange equation $\\Lambda(\\widetilde L)(q)=0$ of the substituted\nLagrangian. For a chart ($r=3N$), the transport identity of\n[[prop-cm-coordinate-covariance-of-the-euler-lagrange-expression]] clause 4,\napplied to the time-independent map $X$, gives\n$\\Lambda(\\widetilde L)=DX^{\\mathsf T}(\\mathsf Mx''-F+d)$ along\n$x=X\\circ q$: here $L_v=\\mathsf Mx'$ and $L_x=-\\nabla_xU$, while\n$F=-\\nabla_xU+d$. Hence $\\Lambda(\\widetilde L)=DX^{\\mathsf T}d$\nis equivalent to $\\mathsf Mx''=F$, since $DX^{\\mathsf T}$ is invertible.\nOn any interval $J$, the transport calculation applies on compact\nsubintervals around each interior time and extends to included endpoints\nby continuity of these expressions. Whether\nthe same equations describe an ideal constrained motion for a\nparametrization of clause (d) is a separate statement, proved as the reduction\ntheorem of this page; it is not part of this definition.\n\n**(f) Status.** This item defines the dictionary between the Cartesian and the\ngeneralized-coordinate descriptions: the coordinate map, the substituted\nLagrangian, the kinetic metric, generalized forces and the declared Lagrange\nequations. It adopts no force law beyond the model's presentation, asserts no\nsolution existence and makes no empirical claim; the equivalence of the\ndeclared equations with the model's Cartesian equation (for charts) and with\nthe ideal constrained equation (for parametrizations) is proved on this page.",
