research/plan-spec.json:49502:          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $R>0$, let\n$V\\in L^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R)$ with $\\operatorname{supp}V\\subseteq B_R$,\nlet $k>0$ and $d\\in S^2$. On $\\mathcal H=L^2(B_R;\\mathbb C)$ let\n$K_kf(x)=\\int_{B_R}G_k(x-y)V(y)f(y)dy$, where\n$G_k(z)=e^{ik|z|}/(4\\pi|z|)$. The supplier\n[[thm-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-outgoing-fredholm-invertibility]] supplies\nbounded invertibility of $I+K_k$. Define\n\n$$u=(I+K_k)^{-1}(e^{ikd\\cdot x}|_{B_R}),\\qquad \\Psi(x)=e^{ikd\\cdot x}-\\int_{B_R}G_k(x-y)V(y)u(y)dy,\\qquad \\Psi_{\\mathrm{sc}}=\\Psi-e^{ikd\\cdot x}.$$\n\nThen:\n\n(a) $\\Psi\\in H^2_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\mathbb R^3)$,\n$(-\\Delta+V-k^2)\\Psi=0$ distributionally, and $\\Psi|_{B_R}=u$.\n\n(b) With $r=|x|>2R$, $\\Omega=x/r$, the smooth amplitude\n\n$$f_k(\\Omega)=-\\frac1{4\\pi}\\int_{B_R}e^{-ik\\Omega\\cdot y}V(y)u(y)dy$$\n\nsatisfies $\\Psi=e^{ikd\\cdot x}+e^{ikr}f_k(\\Omega)/r+\\mathcal E(r,\\Omega)$.\nThe error and its first radial derivative are bounded uniformly in $\\Omega$\nby $C(k,R,\\|Vu\\|_1)r^{-2}$. For every fixed finite product $D_\\Omega$ of\nsmooth tangential differential operators on $S^2$, also\n$|D_\\Omega\\mathcal E|+|D_\\Omega\\partial_r\\mathcal E|\\le C_Dr^{-2}$.\n\n(c) Fix normalization constants $\\hbar,m>0$. The scattered radial current is\n\n$$j_r^{\\mathrm{sc}}(r,\\Omega)=\\frac\\hbar m\\operatorname{Im}(\\overline{\\Psi_{\\mathrm{sc}}}\\,\\partial_r\\Psi_{\\mathrm{sc}})=\\frac{\\hbar k}{m}\\frac{|f_k(\\Omega)|^2}{r^2}+O(r^{-3}),$$\n\nuniformly in $\\Omega$. With incident plane-wave flux density\n$J_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\hbar k/m$, the stationary elastic model's differential\ncross section is\n\n$$\\frac{d\\sigma}{d\\Omega}=\\lim_{r\\to\\infty}\\frac{r^2j_r^{\\mathrm{sc}}(r,\\Omega)}{J_{\\mathrm{in}}}=|f_k(\\Omega)|^2,\\qquad \\sigma=\\int_{S^2}|f_k|^2d\\sigma<\\infty.$$\n\nThese are conditional model statements. Measured count rates additionally\nrequire incident fluence, target and detector acceptance, efficiency and\nbackground modelling; none is asserted. Part (a) is a local regularity\nstatement for a stationary scattering wave, with no assertion of membership\nin the global $L^2$ operator domain.",
research/plan-spec.json:53104:          "statement": "**(a) Dimensions.** Write $\\ell$, $\\tau$, $\\mu$, $\\vartheta$ for the four independent dimension symbols of length, duration, mass and electric charge. A **dimension** is an element of $\\mathbb Q^{4}$, written $\\ell^{a}\\tau^{b}\\mu^{c}\\vartheta^{d}$ with $a,b,c,d\\in\\mathbb Q$; products and inverses of dimensions are componentwise addition and negation, and the dimensionless dimension $\\ell^{0}\\tau^{0}\\mu^{0}\\vartheta^{0}$ is abbreviated $1$. The conventions assign to each quantity of the framework a dimension $[X]$, subject to $[XY]=[X][Y]$, $[X/Y]=[X][Y]^{-1}$ and $[dX/du]=[X][u]^{-1}$, with sums meaningful only between quantities of equal dimension, in which case the sum carries that dimension. For a rational power $q$, dimension powers mean $[X]^q=\\ell^{qa}\\tau^{qb}\\mu^{qc}\\vartheta^{qd}$ when $[X]=\\ell^{a}\\tau^{b}\\mu^{c}\\vartheta^{d}$. Rational exponents include the half-integer wave-function dimensions in clause (d); the integer-exponent dimensions form the subgroup $\\mathbb Z^4$. These are consistency requirements on the conventions, not laws of nature.\n\n**(b) Reference units.** The **metre** (m), **second** (s), **kilogram** (kg) and **coulomb** (C) are the reference units of length, duration, mass and charge; a quantity of dimension $\\ell^{a}\\tau^{b}\\mu^{c}\\vartheta^{d}$ is expressed in the unit $\\mathrm m^{a}\\mathrm s^{b}\\mathrm kg^{c}\\mathrm C^{d}$. Coordinates of an inertial frame are read with the time coordinate $t$ in seconds, $[t]=\\tau$, and the spatial coordinates $x_{i}$ in metres, $[x_{i}]=\\ell$; slice distances of the arena are read in metres.\n\n**(c) Defining constants of the current SI.** The framework keeps $\\hbar$ explicit. In the current SI the constants $h$, $c$ and $e$ are exact defining constants, with the values $h=6{,}626\\,070\\,15\\times10^{-34}$ J s, $c=2{,}99792458\\times10^{8}$ m s$^{-1}$ and $e=1{,}602\\,176\\,634\\times10^{-19}$ C, and $\\hbar:=h/(2\\pi)$. These are real scalar constants with dimensions and units $[h]=[\\hbar]=\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-1}$ (J s), $[c]=\\ell\\tau^{-1}$ (m s$^{-1}$), and $[e]=\\vartheta$ (C). The vacuum permeability $\\mu_{0}$ is a positive real scalar that is experimentally determined in the current SI, with $[\\mu_{0}]=\\mu\\ell\\vartheta^{-2}$ (N A$^{-2}$ = H m$^{-1}$); $\\varepsilon_{0}:=(\\mu_{0}c^{2})^{-1}$ is a positive real scalar with $[\\varepsilon_{0}]=\\mu^{-1}\\ell^{-3}\\tau^{2}\\vartheta^{2}$ (F m$^{-1}$). These are unit-convention statements of the SI, not measurements reported by this framework; $c$ enters the framework only through the separate radiation assumptions adopted on their own pages, not in the nonrelativistic spacetime of [[def-nrqm-framework-newtonian-spacetime]].\n\n**(d) Dimensions and types of the framework's quantities.** The dimension map is fixed on the following quantities.\n\n1. State quantities: a position wave function of a system with $d$ spatial dimensions and $r$ internal components is an almost-everywhere equivalence class in $L^{2}(\\mathbb R^{d};\\mathbb C^{r})$ with dimension $\\ell^{-d/2}$, its norm $\\|\\psi\\|$ is dimensionless, and an abstract normalized state and the density operators of the state postulate are dimensionless.\n2. Probability: the outcome probability $\\mu(B)$ of the observable postulate is a dimensionless number; a position density $|\\psi|^{2}$ has dimension $\\ell^{-d}$; a momentum wave function normalized against $\\mathrm d^{d}p$ has dimension $(\\mu\\ell\\tau^{-1})^{-d/2}$; an amplitude for a finite outcome is a dimensionless complex number, while an amplitude read against a detector coordinate carries the square-root of the inverse of the detector measure.\n3. Frame parameters ([[def-nrqm-framework-galilei-transformations]]): $[b]=\\tau$, $[a_{i}]=\\ell$, $[v_{i}]=\\ell\\tau^{-1}$ and $[R_{ij}]=1$.\n4. Mechanical quantities: action $[\\hbar]=\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-1}$; energy and the Hamiltonian eigenvalues $\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-2}$; momentum $\\mu\\ell\\tau^{-1}$; position components $\\ell$; angular momentum and spin $\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-1}$; mass $\\mu$; velocity of a free spinless model $\\ell\\tau^{-1}$; force $\\mu\\ell\\tau^{-2}$; a real potential energy $\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-2}$.\n5. Probability current: $j$ has dimension $\\ell^{1-d}\\tau^{-1}$; it is a probability flux, not a charge flux.\n6. Electric quantities of the model parameters: charge has dimension $\\vartheta$; a magnetic moment has dimension $\\ell^{2}\\vartheta\\tau^{-1}$, equivalently $\\mathrm A\\,\\mathrm m^{2}=\\mathrm J\\,\\mathrm T^{-1}$, and a gyromagnetic parameter $\\gamma$ in the relation $\\boldsymbol\\mu=\\gamma\\mathbf S$ has dimension $\\mu^{-1}\\vartheta$, equivalently $\\mathrm s^{-1}\\mathrm T^{-1}$; these quantities appear only in the model assumptions that introduce them.\n7. Statistical quantities: a mean has the dimension of its outcome coordinate, a variance the square of it and a standard deviation it; a mean, variance or higher moment exists only when the corresponding moment of the outcome probability is finite, so a symbolic average is not a number unless that integrability is established. Angles and phases are dimensionless under the radian convention.\n8. Quantities of later extension assumptions - radiation frequencies and energies, electric dipole moments, cross sections, temperatures and entropies - are declared with their dimensions and units by the items that introduce those assumptions, under the rules fixed here; they are not part of the present list.\n\n**(e) Conventions only.** No numerical value of any model parameter is fixed by this item: no mass, charge, spin label, potential or measured constant is asserted, and natural-unit shorthand (for example setting $\\hbar=1$ or $c=1$) is read as an abbreviation for the corresponding SI-dimensioned statement with the constant restored. For temperature extensions, use the independent Theta dimension in Q^4 x Q and the kelvin reference unit; all core dimensions embed at exponent zero.",
research/plan-spec.json:53335:          "statement": "Adopt as physical assumptions of the framework the following statements about sharp and unsharp real-valued quantities. Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice and the Axiom of Choice ([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]]). The latter is the explicit hypothesis used to obtain the spectral PVM of a general self-adjoint operator from [[thm-spectral-theorem-for-unbounded-self-adjoint-operators]] in clause (ii); consumers invoking this construction inherit it.\n\n**(i) Sharp observables.** An **ideal sharp real-valued quantity** (observable) of a system with state space $H$ is a densely defined self-adjoint operator $A:D(A)\\subset H\\to H$ ([[def-adjoint-of-a-densely-defined-unbounded-operator]], [[def-symmetric-self-adjoint-and-essentially-self-adjoint]]), meaning $D(A)=D(A^*)$ and $A\\psi=A^*\\psi$ on this common domain, together with a declared unit in which its real spectral coordinate is read; the unit is part of the assignment, and no numerical value of the coordinate is asserted by the assignment. Symmetry of $A$ on a small domain is not sufficient for self-adjointness, and the domain is a declared part of the observable.\n\n**(ii) Spectral probability.** Let $E_{A}$ be the spectral projection-valued measure of the self-adjoint operator $A$ on the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra of $\\mathbb R$ ([[thm-spectral-theorem-for-unbounded-self-adjoint-operators]], [[def-projection-valued-measure]], [[def-borel-sigma-algebra]]). For a preparation $\\rho$ and a Borel set $B\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ the **probability of the outcome in $B$** is\n$$\\mu^{A}_{\\rho}(B):=\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl(\\rho\\,E_{A}(B)\\bigr),\\qquad\\text{and for a pure preparation }[\\psi]\\text{ it is }\\mu^{A}_{\\psi}(B)=\\|E_{A}(B)\\psi\\|^{2}.$$\nBy clause (b) of [[lem-nrqm-composites-density-and-scalar-spectral-probability]] the assignment $B\\mapsto\\mu^{A}_{\\rho}(B)$ is a probability measure on the Borel sets ([[def-probability-measure]], [[lem-scalar-and-complex-measures-from-a-pvm]]). It is the general form of the Born rule adopted here.\n\n**(iii) Nature of the outcome law.** The probability law is a measure, not necessarily a density with respect to Lebesgue measure and not necessarily a finite or countable sum of point masses: a self-adjoint operator with continuous spectrum ordinarily has no normalizable eigenvector at a point of its continuous spectrum, so an \"exact value\" of a continuum quantity is not represented by a normalized state. An ideal point value is an idealization; a real detector reports a finite-resolution outcome, that is, the probability of a declared Borel set $B$ such as a bin, and the recorded-event probability is that of (ii) for the bin, not the value of a density at a point.\n\n**(iv) Unsharp finite outcomes.** Outcomes of a finite-resolution apparatus with finitely many channels are described, when the idealization uses bounded effects, by a finite family $(F_{a})_{a=1}^{p}$ of effects on $H$ as defined above, with $\\sum_aF_a=I$. The **outcome probabilities** are $p_{a}=\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho F_{a})$. They are normalized for every state space assigned by P1, as shown by the preceding general-$H$ calculation. This unsharp extension is an operational description tied to an apparatus model; it is not obtained from the sharp observable by mathematical manipulation alone, and the effects alone do not determine what happens to the state after the outcome (P3).\n\n**(v) Moments are a separate matter.** Statements about the mean, variance or higher moments of the outcome law require the corresponding integrability of $t\\mapsto t^{n}$ against $\\mu^{A}_{\\rho}$ and are not part of this postulate; where they are used they are stated with their hypotheses. In particular the existence of $\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho A)$ for an unbounded $A$ is not asserted.\n\n**(vi) Status.** Clauses (i)-(v) are adopted assumptions of the model. They assert nothing about which quantities of a concrete apparatus are sharp, about the physical mechanism producing an outcome or about the completeness of the description; a laboratory record is a classical event whose relation to the model is a tested identification with uncertainty, in the sense of P7.",
research/plan-spec.json:53635:          "statement": "Adopt as physical assumptions of the framework the following discipline for comparing the model with recorded data.\n\n**(i) Declared model of each comparison.** A comparison of a model prediction with an experiment of the framework requires, in addition to the state and dynamics assumptions P1-P5, the following declared items: a **preparation model**, that is, a specification of the state or preparation assigned to the system together with the physical procedure that realizes it; an **isolation model**, that is, the energy operator used and a statement of the interactions neglected in the approximation; a **detection model**, that is, the response of the apparatus, including efficiency, resolution, background and any binning; and **sampling assumptions**, that is, the statistical model under which repetitions of the preparation and detection are combined. None of these items is supplied by the mathematical framework alone.\n\n**(ii) Repetitions.** The treatment of repeated trials as independent and identically distributed draws from the model's outcome law is an additional statistical model of the apparatus and the procedure. It is not a consequence of the state postulate and is not guaranteed by the mathematics; where it is assumed it is stated, and where it fails (correlated trials, drifting preparation, dead time, selection) the statistical analysis must model the departure or record the limitation.\n\n**(iii) Identification with uncertainty.** The claim \"this apparatus realizes the preparation $\\rho$ and the dynamics $H_{\\mathrm{op}}$\" and the claim \"this detector realizes the effect $F$\" are physical identifications: they are tested against recorded behaviour of the apparatus, carry uncertainty and can fail. They are not derived from the framework, and a model prediction obtained under them inherits their qualifications. Calibration procedures, control experiments and their limitations are recorded with the comparison.\n\n**(iv) Empirical premises.** Where a measured value enters a deduction - a mass, a charge, a field strength, a detector efficiency - it enters as a qualified empirical premise with its uncertainty, conditions of measurement and source, and the conclusion of the deduction carries those qualifications. A deduction that uses a measured input is not an exact statement about nature.\n\n**(v) Status.** Clauses (i)-(iv) are adopted as the framework's conditions for comparing model with data. They make no claim that the framework or the apparatus description is exact; agreement of a prediction with a dataset is evidence about the combined model and apparatus assumptions, not a proof of the framework and not a statement about nature deduced from the mathematics.",
research/plan-spec.json:53727:          "statement": "This remark records the status of the framework's quantities under the conventions of [[def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units]] and the postulates of this page. The classification is internal to the framework: a quantity called primitive here may be derived in another framework, and the same name in another framework need not denote the same object.\n\n**Primitive and adopted model inputs.** The following enter the model as inputs rather than being derived within it: the arena objects and their readings (events, absolute time, the spatial metric and the frame parameters of the adopted frames); the state space assigned to each system, with its linear structure [[post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition]]; the energy operator of an autonomous system [[post-nrqm-framework-autonomous-dynamics]]; the action constant $\\hbar$, the particle mass $m>0$, the spin label and the potential of the one-particle model [[post-nrqm-framework-nonrelativistic-kinematics-and-massive-particle]]; and the species parameters of [[post-nrqm-framework-identical-particles-and-spin]] (mass, charge, spin, statistics, magnetic couplings), which are empirical model inputs.\n\n**Adopted structures.** Separable complex Hilbert spaces with their inner products, the completed tensor product and the space of trace-class operators are mathematical structures; the framework adopts them as the description of states, and their mathematical properties are not physical findings. Similarly the projective measurement family of the conditional-measurement postulate is an adopted apparatus model, and the permutation sector of a species is an adopted assignment.\n\n**Derived quantities and statements.** Within the stated assumptions the following are derived, and each is accompanied by the hypotheses of its derivation: the outcome probability measures $\\mu^{A}_{\\rho}$ and their finite-resolution restrictions (from P2 and the spectral calculus); the updated preparation of the conditional-measurement rule and the discarded-outcome ensemble (from P3); the evolved vector state and density operator, with the Schroedinger equation on the operator domain (from P4); the position and momentum operators with their domains, the canonical commutator on the Schwartz core and the self-adjointness of the bounded-potential Hamiltonian (mathematical consequences of the operators assigned in P5); the probability current $j=(\\hbar/m)\\operatorname{Im}(\\overline\\psi\\nabla\\psi)$ and pointwise continuity equation only under the hypotheses of [[thm-nrqm-magnetic-and-detection-smooth-local-continuity-calculation]] clause (a): $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ and $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ are nonempty open sets, $m,\\hbar>0$, $V:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous, and $\\psi$, $\\partial_t\\psi$, $\\partial_j\\psi$, and $\\partial_k\\partial_j\\psi$ are continuous and solve the displayed Schroedinger equation pointwise. The integrated balance additionally requires a bounded open $C^1$ region $D$ with $\\overline D\\subseteq\\Omega$; global norm conservation requires the domination and vanishing-flux hypotheses of clause (c). Means, variances and higher moments exist only where their corresponding integrals are finite; energies are spectral quantities of the specified model Hamiltonian, while angular momenta and other spectral quantities belong to their corresponding specified self-adjoint observables, with the units declared under P2. No derived statement is asserted outside its hypotheses.\n\n**Empirical status of measured inputs.** The numerical values of $m$, of charges and of magnetic parameters are not derived by the framework; they are qualified empirical premises in the sense of P7, with uncertainty and measurement conditions. The exact defining constants of the current SI recorded in the unit conventions are conventions of the system of units, not measured findings of this framework; the measured status of $\\mu_{0}$ in the same conventions is that of a qualified empirical constant wherever it is used.\n\n**No interpretation claimed.** The classification above records where each object enters the model. It asserts nothing about the ontological status of states, about the completeness of the description or about the empirical truth of the framework; those questions are outside the framework's deductive content.",
research/plan-spec.json:55381:          "statement": "Adopt the state and composition postulate [[post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition]] (P1) and the observable and probability postulate [[post-nrqm-framework-observable-and-probability]] (P2), and assume the Axiom of Countable Choice and the Axiom of Choice ([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]]), the choice hypotheses of the spectral suppliers. Let $H$ be the state space assigned to a declared system, let $(A,u_A)$ be an assigned sharp observable of the framework with spectral projection-valued measure $E_A$ and spectrum $\\sigma(A)$, and let $\\mu^{A}_{\\rho}$ be the outcome law of [[def-nrqm-observables-framework-observable-and-outcome-law]]. Then for every preparation $\\rho$ ([[def-nrqm-state-geometry-framework-state-space]]) and every Borel set $B\\subseteq\\mathbb R$:\n\n(a) **Probability measure.** $0\\le\\mu^{A}_{\\rho}(B)\\le1$ and $\\mu^{A}_{\\rho}(\\mathbb R)=1$; for pairwise disjoint Borel sets $B_n$ with union $B$ one has $\\mu^{A}_{\\rho}(B)=\\sum_n\\mu^{A}_{\\rho}(B_n)$; and for the pure preparation of a unit vector $\\psi$ the law is $\\mu^{A}_{\\psi}(B)=\\|E_A(B)\\psi\\|^{2}$. The declared outcome law of the model is therefore a probability measure on the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra of $\\mathbb R$, not a formal weight attached to eigenvectors.\n\n(b) **Mixtures.** If $\\rho=\\sum_nw_n\\rho_n$ is a statistical mixture of preparations with weights $w_n\\ge0$, $\\sum_nw_n=1$, then $\\mu^{A}_{\\rho}(B)=\\sum_nw_n\\mu^{A}_{\\rho_n}(B)$ for every Borel $B$, the series converging absolutely: the outcome law of a mixture is the weighted average of the outcome laws, with classical weights.\n\n(c) **Exact values.** For $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R$ the probability of the exact value $\\lambda$ is $\\mu^{A}_{\\rho}(\\{\\lambda\\})=\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho E_A(\\{\\lambda\\}))$; it is zero for every preparation whenever $\\lambda$ is not an eigenvalue of $A$, and when $\\lambda$ is an eigenvalue it equals the trace weight of the eigenspace $\\ker(A-\\lambda)$, with $\\mu^{A}_{T_{u,u}}(\\{\\lambda\\})=1$ for every unit eigenvector $u$.\n\n(d) **Spectral support.** $\\mu^{A}_{\\rho}(B)=\\mu^{A}_{\\rho}(B\\cap\\sigma(A))$ and $\\mu^{A}_{\\rho}(\\sigma(A))=1$: outcomes outside the spectrum of the observable have probability zero.\n\n(e) **No density and no discreteness are asserted.** The postulate asserts that $\\mu^{A}_{\\rho}$ is a probability measure on the Borel sets and does not assert that it is a density with respect to Lebesgue measure or a finite or countable sum of point masses; the operational object named there is the probability of a declared Borel set, such as a finite-resolution bin, not the value of a density at a point ([[post-nrqm-framework-observable-and-probability]] clause (iii)). Which structure a particular pair $(A,\\rho)$ exhibits is a property of that pair and is not fixed by the adopted law.\n\n(f) **What is not claimed.** No mean, variance or higher moment is asserted by this item; their existence is conditional and is treated separately. No claim is made about the joint statistics of two or more observables, about the state after the outcome, about the physical mechanism producing an outcome, or about any concrete apparatus. The statement is conditional on P1 and P2 and on the cited mathematics.",
research/plan-spec.json:56560:          "statement": "Adopt P1, P2 and P3 for the declared system and assume Countable Choice, as in [[def-nrqm-measurement-framework-declared-measurements-and-instruments]]. Declare a two-level system whose assigned Hilbert space is $H=\\mathbb C^{2}$ with the standard inner product and orthonormal basis $(e_{+},e_{-})$ ([[def-nrqm-state-geometry-framework-state-space]], [[def-inner-product-space]], [[def-hilbert-space]]), interpreted as the two eigenstates of the $z$-component of a spin-half degree of freedom. Use SI units: $\\hbar>0$ is the adopted reduced Planck constant, with action dimension $M L^2 T^{-1}$ and unit joule second ($\\mathrm{J}\\,\\mathrm{s}$); the matrices $J_i$ and the angles are dimensionless. Thus $S_i$, $S_n$ and their outcome values have angular-momentum dimension $M L^2 T^{-1}$ and are read in $\\mathrm{J}\\,\\mathrm{s}$. No numerical value of $\\hbar$ is used. By the spin assignment of P6 with $s=\\tfrac12$, the spin operators are $S_{i}=\\hbar J_{i}$ with $J_{i}=\\tfrac12\\sigma_{i}$ for the Pauli matrices in this basis ([[post-nrqm-framework-identical-particles-and-spin]] clause (ii), [[prop-nrqm-orbital-and-spin-finite-spin-matrices]] clause (e)).\n\nFor a unit vector $n=(\\sin\\theta,0,\\cos\\theta)$, $0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi$, put\n$$u(\\theta):=\\cos\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\,e_{+}+\\sin\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\,e_{-},\\qquad v(\\theta):=-\\sin\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\,e_{+}+\\cos\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\,e_{-}.$$\nThen $u(\\theta)$ and $v(\\theta)$ are orthonormal and\n$$S_{n}:=n\\cdot \\mathbf S=\\frac{\\hbar}{2}(\\sin\\theta\\,\\sigma_{x}+\\cos\\theta\\,\\sigma_{z}),\\qquad S_{n}u(\\theta)=\\frac{\\hbar}{2}u(\\theta),\\qquad S_{n}v(\\theta)=-\\frac{\\hbar}{2}v(\\theta).$$\nDeclare the **ideal filter** $F(n)$ to be the ideal sharp measurement with the two outcomes $+$ and $-$, whose outcome projections are $T_{u(\\theta),u(\\theta)}$ and $T_{v(\\theta),v(\\theta)}$, the spectral projections of $S_{n}$ at $\\pm\\hbar/2$ ([[def-nrqm-measurement-framework-declared-measurements-and-instruments]] clause (c), [[lem-nrqm-composites-rank-one-operator-calculus]]).\n\nThe example considers three ideal filters in series, $F_{1}=F(e_{z})$ (so $\\theta=0$), $F_{2}=F(n)$ with axis $n$ at angle $\\theta$, and $F_{3}=F(m)$ with axis $m=(\\sin\\varphi,0,\\cos\\varphi)$, and the initial preparation $\\rho_{0}=T_{e_{+},e_{+}}$, the state of a spin-half system with $S_{z}=+\\hbar/2$. Then:\n\n**(i)** Through $F_{1}$ the outcome $+$ has probability $1$ and the conditional state is again $\\rho_{0}$.\n\n**(ii)** Through $F_{2}$, conditioned on having passed $F_{1}$,\n$$\\mathbb P(+)=\\cos^{2}\\frac{\\theta}{2},\\qquad \\mathbb P(-)=\\sin^{2}\\frac{\\theta}{2},$$\nand, if $\\cos^2(\\theta/2)>0$, after the outcome $+$ the conditional state is the pure state of $u(\\theta)$.\n\n**(iii)** If $\\cos^2(\\theta/2)>0$, through $F_{3}$, conditioned on having passed $F_{2}$ with the outcome $+$, the outcome $+$ has probability\n$$\\mathbb P(+)=\\Bigl|\\langle u(\\varphi),u(\\theta)\\rangle\\Bigr|^{2}=\\cos^{2}\\frac{\\varphi-\\theta}{2}.$$\nFor all $0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi$, including the zero-probability first branch, the joint probability of the outcomes $+,+,+$ is $\\cos^{2}(\\theta/2)\\cos^{2}((\\varphi-\\theta)/2)$.\n\n**(iv)** In particular, for $\\theta=\\pi/2$ and $\\varphi=\\pi$, the model probability of outcome $+$ at $F_{2}$ is $\\tfrac12$, and conditioned on that outcome the model probability of outcome $+$ at $F_{3}$ is also $\\tfrac12$, giving joint probability $\\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac12=\\tfrac14$ for passing all three declared filters. If $F_{2}$ is omitted, $F_{3}$ has model probability $\\cos^{2}(\\pi/2)=0$ of outcome $+$ on the state emerging from $F_{1}$. Thus the intervening declared measurement changes the later model probabilities; these are not measured transmission fractions.\n\nThe computation is a model computation for declared ideal filters and the declared spin assignment: it does not derive the spatial Stern-Gerlach deflection or the inhomogeneous-field geometry, records no detector counts, resolution, background or uncertainty, and asserts nothing about the historical silver-atom experiments or about any particular apparatus.",
research/plan-spec.json:56620:          "statement": "Adopt the following finite polarization assignment and measurement rules as additional model assumptions for this example, using the declared-instrument vocabulary of [[def-nrqm-measurement-framework-declared-measurements-and-instruments]]. These are stipulated for the finite polarization space, beyond the massive-matter sector of P1; they are not consequences of its massive-particle assignment. Assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]), as required by the mathematical suppliers. Fix a propagation label $k_0\\in\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$ in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$ relative to fixed Euclidean spatial coordinates, and a real orthonormal transverse pair $v_x,v_y\\in\\mathbb R^3$ with $k_0\\cdot v_x=k_0\\cdot v_y=0$. Identify $H=\\mathbb C^2$, with its standard inner product and orthonormal basis $(e_x,e_y)$, unitarily with $\\mathbb Cv_x\\oplus\\mathbb Cv_y$ by $e_x\\mapsto v_x$, $e_y\\mapsto v_y$. Its state space is $D(H)$ and a unit ray $[\\psi]$ represents $T_{\\psi,\\psi}$ for $\\|\\psi\\|=1$ ([[def-nrqm-state-geometry-pure-mixed-states-and-purity]], [[def-inner-product-space]], [[def-hilbert-space]]). The identification of these unit rays as polarization preparations is the adopted finite assignment, motivated by the source account in Binney and Skinner, Section 1.3.6, printed pages 14-15. It supplies no photon number or radiation dynamics. Angles are dimensionless, read in radians. For a real angle $\\alpha$ put\n$$e_{\\alpha}:=\\cos\\alpha\\,e_{x}+\\sin\\alpha\\,e_{y},\\qquad e_{\\alpha}^{\\perp}:=-\\sin\\alpha\\,e_{x}+\\cos\\alpha\\,e_{y}.$$\nThe **ideal analyser** $A(\\alpha)$ is the declared ideal sharp measurement with the two outcomes transmit and block, whose projections are $T_{e_{\\alpha},e_{\\alpha}}$ and $T_{e_{\\alpha}^{\\perp},e_{\\alpha}^{\\perp}}$ respectively, with outcome and update rules stipulated in [F2] below ([[def-nrqm-measurement-framework-declared-measurements-and-instruments]] clause (c), [[lem-nrqm-composites-rank-one-operator-calculus]]).\n\nThe example takes the preparation $[e_{x}]$, the chain of analysers $A(0)$ with $\\alpha=0^\\circ$, $A(\\pi/4)$ with $\\alpha=45^\\circ$ and $A(\\pi/2)$ with $\\alpha=90^\\circ$, and computes:\n\n**(i)** The analyser $A(\\alpha)$ transmits the preparation $[e_{\\beta}]$ with probability\n$$\\mathbb P(\\text{transmit})=\\bigl|\\langle e_{\\alpha},e_{\\beta}\\rangle\\bigr|^{2}=\\cos^{2}(\\alpha-\\beta),$$\nand, when $\\cos^2(\\alpha-\\beta)>0$, after transmission the conditional state is the pure state of $e_{\\alpha}$.\n\n**(ii)** Consequently the chain $0^\\circ,45^\\circ,90^\\circ$ transmits with joint probability\n$$\\cos^{2}(0-0)\\cdot\\cos^{2}(\\tfrac{\\pi}{4}-0)\\cdot\\cos^{2}(\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}-\\tfrac{\\pi}{4})=1\\cdot\\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac12=\\tfrac14 ,$$\nwhereas the chain $0^\\circ,90^\\circ$ alone transmits with probability $\\cos^{2}(\\pi/2)=0$.\n\n**(iii)** Removing the middle analyser therefore changes the transmission of the chain from $\\tfrac14$ to $0$ within the declared model: the intervening declared measurement changes the state, and the later analyser sees the state selected by the earlier one, not the original preparation.\n\n**(iv)** *Model comparison only.* A declared classical amplitude model for a transverse field in the same plane, in which the analyser transmits the component along $e_{\\alpha}$ and the transmitted intensity is stipulated to be proportional to the squared modulus of that component, gives the same ratio $\\cos^{2}(\\alpha-\\beta)$ for a preparation along $e_{\\beta}$. The agreement of these two model computations does not establish that either describes nature, and it does not by itself establish field quantization ([[rem-nrqm-measurement-examples-scope-limits]]); no classical Maxwell theorem is invoked, and the field model is a stipulated finite-dimensional comparison. No transmission counts, detector efficiencies, backgrounds or sampling statistics are reported.",
research/plan-spec.json:56782:          "statement": "**Serial filters.** [[ex-nrqm-measurement-serial-stern-gerlach-filters]] computes, in a declared two-level spin-half model with the spin assignment of P6, the outcome probabilities $\\cos^{2}(\\theta/2)$ and $\\cos^{2}((\\varphi-\\theta)/2)$ of successive ideal filters and the quarter-probability case. The computation verifies the model's serial Lueders statistics for declared ideal filters. It does not derive the inhomogeneous-field force, the spatial splitting or the detector response of a Stern-Gerlach apparatus, and it reports no measured fraction and no property of silver atoms; the reported Stern-Gerlach experiments of 1922 remain a candidate experiment item that is not asserted by this page.\n\n**Analyser chain.** [[ex-nrqm-measurement-three-polarizer-chain]] computes the transmission probabilities of a chain of declared ideal analysers in a declared two-level model and exhibits the standard $\\tfrac14$ case, together with an explicit caveat that a stipulated classical amplitude model with the same ideal analyser convention gives the same ratio. The agreement of the two model computations does not establish field quantization or the applicability of either model to nature, and no transmission counts, efficiencies or backgrounds are reported.\n\n**Absorbing instrument.** [[ex-nrqm-measurement-absorption-instrument]] exhibits a declared two-outcome instrument whose detected branch leaves an absorbed marker with no outgoing polarization-plane state and whose non-detection branch suppresses one amplitude by $\\sqrt{1-p}$ rather than projecting it away; neither outcome is repeatable. The computation verifies that a declared instrument can describe a destructive apparatus within the framework's instrument vocabulary and that its update is part of its declaration. The three-level space is an idealisation; no real detector, photon number, efficiency or rate is described.\n\n**Degenerate readout versus fine readout.** [[ex-nrqm-measurement-degenerate-coarse-versus-fine-update]] contrasts a coarse readout of a degenerate subspace with a fine readout resolving it and shows that the coarse Lueders update retains the coherent superposition while the eigenvector-selection mixture changes the law of a suitable bounded observable. The computation verifies the degeneracy clause of the projective update inside an explicit three-level model; it asserts no preferred basis and no apparatus.\n\n**Empirical neutrality.** None of the four items is an experiment. Each is a finite computation for a declared preparation and a declared measurement model; none records data, calibration, sampling, uncertainty or a comparison with observation, and none claims that a laboratory device realizes the declared instrument. Agreement or disagreement with data would test the combined model and apparatus assumptions under the preparation and apparatus postulate P7 ([[post-nrqm-framework-preparation-and-apparatus]]) and is not established or claimed here.",
research/plan-spec.json:57595:          "statement": "**Two-level precession.** [[ex-nrqm-autonomous-dynamics-two-level-precession]] computes, on the explicit two-dimensional state space, the spectral measure and flow of the assigned energy observable $E_{1}T_{u_{1},u_{1}}+E_{2}T_{u_{2},u_{2}}$ with $E_{1}\\ne E_{2}$, the exact phases of an evolved preparation, the outcome probabilities and expectations of three bounded observables, and the rotation of the expectation-value triple about the third axis with angular frequency $\\omega=(E_{2}-E_{1})/\\hbar$. The computation verifies the flow and law of [[def-nrqm-autonomous-dynamics-framework-evolution]] and [[def-nrqm-observables-framework-observable-and-outcome-law]] on finite data, and it confirms the energy conservation of [[pthm-nrqm-autonomous-dynamics-stationary-states-and-energy-conservation]] clause (a) in the model. No apparatus realization, detector resolution or measured frequency is asserted.\n\n**Finite superpositions.** [[ex-nrqm-autonomous-dynamics-finite-stationary-superposition]] computes the evolution of a nonzero finite superposition of orthogonal energy eigenrays, the trigonometric-polynomial form of the outcome laws of a bounded assigned sharp observable, the exact return-time criterion $(\\lambda_{m}-\\lambda_{n})T/\\hbar\\in2\\pi\\mathbb Z$ for $m,n$ in the support of the coefficients, the existence criterion for a positive return time via a common frequency, and the two-energy specialization with least return time $2\\pi\\hbar/|\\lambda_{1}-\\lambda_{2}|$. These are exact model statements under the flow of [[pthm-nrqm-autonomous-dynamics-schrodinger-picture-flow]].\n\n**A failed universal claim.** [[cex-nrqm-autonomous-dynamics-superposition-with-distinct-energies-is-not-stationary]] refutes the informal universal statement that every superposition of distinct energy eigenrays is stationary: on the two-level model with $\\psi_{0}=(u_{1}+u_{2})/\\sqrt2$ the outcome law of the flip observable takes the values $1$ at $t=0$ and $0$ at $t=\\pi/|\\omega|$. The refutation isolates the relative phase as the mechanism and is consistent with the exact stationarity criterion of [[pthm-nrqm-autonomous-dynamics-stationary-states-and-energy-conservation]] clause (c): the individual eigenrays are stationary, the equal-weight superposition of distinct eigenrays is not.\n\n## What the page does not establish\n\nNone of the three items is an experiment: no counts, calibration, resolution, efficiency, background, uncertainty or data comparison appears anywhere on the page, and no energy value is measured. The two-level system is an abstract model and is not identified with a spin, a magnetic moment, a photon polarization or any other species; the coupling of spin to a magnetic field belongs to the corresponding model pages, and driven or resonant dynamics is deferred to the driven-dynamics development, which adopts its own time-dependent assumptions. The return-time and precession results are statements about the model's flow and outcome law, not about the stability, decoherence or control of any laboratory preparation.",
research/plan-spec.json:58563:          "statement": "## Three worked items\n\n**The Gaussian packet.** [[ex-nrqm-kinematics-gaussian-position-momentum-packet]]\ncomputes, in the spin-zero massive model, the normalized Gaussian\n$\\psi_{\\sigma}(x)=(\\pi\\sigma^{2})^{-3/4}e^{-|x|^{2}/(2\\sigma^{2})}$, shows\nthat its momentum representative is again a normalized Gaussian with width\n$\\sigma_{p}=\\hbar/\\sigma$, and evaluates the position and momentum outcome\ndensities of the model together with their variances $\\sigma^{2}/2$ and\n$\\hbar^{2}/(2\\sigma^{2})$ and the product $\\hbar/2$. This verifies the model's\nsharp laws of [[pthm-nrqm-kinematics-spin-s-outcome-laws]] on a family of\nSchwartz preparations; it computes no dynamics, no measurement and no general\nuncertainty statement.\n\n**The plane wave as a generalized functional.** [[ex-nrqm-kinematics-plane-wave-generalized-eigenfunction]]\ndefines the conjugate-linear functional $u_{k}(\\varphi)=\\int e^{ik\\cdot\nx}\\overline{\\varphi}$ on Schwartz classes, proves the eigenrelation\n$u_{k}(P_{j}\\varphi)=\\hbar k_{j}u_{k}(\\varphi)$, and proves that no $L^{2}$\nclass represents $u_{k}$. This is the precise sense in which the plane wave is\na generalized momentum eigenfunction: it diagonalizes the momentum action on\nthe test space while carrying no state or probability density. The framework's\nstate assignment excludes it as a preparation, in agreement with the model's\natomless sharp laws.\n\n**The boundary warning.** [[texp-nrqm-kinematics-dirichlet-momentum-warning]]\nshows, in a hypothetical confined-model extension, that the formal interval\nmomentum $-i\\hbar D$ on the Dirichlet domain is symmetric but not self-adjoint,\nthat no self-adjoint extension keeps the Dirichlet domain, and that the\nDirichlet functions are not momentum eigenfunctions of any self-adjoint\nextension. The companion counterexample\n[[cex-nrqm-kinematics-ccr-cannot-hold-for-bounded-operators]] shows that the\ncanonical commutation relation cannot hold as an identity of bounded\noperators at all, so the core relation of\n[[lem-nrqm-kinematics-ccr-and-internal-factor-commutation]] is genuinely a\nrelation on a dense core of unbounded operators rather than a bounded\noperator identity.\n\n## What the page does not establish\n\nNone of the items is an experiment: no counts, calibration, resolution,\nefficiency, background, uncertainty or data comparison appears anywhere on the\npage, and no value of $m$, $\\hbar$ or any model parameter is measured. The\nGaussian computation is a single family of preparations; the plane-wave item\nis a distributional statement without a state; the boundary warning and the\nbounded-commutator counterexample are conditional mathematical statements\nabout hypothetical or bounded models, not claims about apparatuses. In\nparticular, no item asserts that confined models, twisted-periodic momenta or\nany self-adjoint realization describe a laboratory device, and no item draws\nconclusions about decoherence, measurement disturbance or relativistic\neffects, which belong to other model pages.",
research/plan-spec.json:58772:          "statement": "Adopt the declared two-path preparation model of\n[[def-nrqm-interference-two-path-preparation-and-detection-model]], the\nunsharp finite-outcome clause (iv) of the observable and probability postulate\nP2, and the preparation and apparatus discipline P7\n([[post-nrqm-framework-observable-and-probability]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-preparation-and-apparatus]]). Assume the Axiom of\nCountable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). The following vocabulary is used\nby this page.\n\n**(a) Declared recorded detector.** A **declared recorded detector** of the\narrangement is a finite set $A$ of **recorded outcome labels** with\n$0\\notin A$, together with measurable **recorded weights**\n$w_{a}:\\mathbb R^{3}\\to[0,1]$ for $a\\in A$ such that\n$\\sum_{a\\in A}w_{a}(x)\\le1$ for almost every $x\\in\\mathbb R^{3}$. The\n**effect** of the label $a$ is the multiplication operator\n$$F_{a}:=M_{w_{a}},\\qquad (M_{w}\\psi)(x):=w(x)\\psi(x)\\quad\\text{a.e.},$$\non $H_{s}=L^{2}(\\mathbb R^{3};\\mathbb C^{r})$, and the **no-record effect** is\n$$F_{0}:=I-\\sum_{a\\in A}F_{a}.$$\nThe multiplier is a well-defined linear map on almost-everywhere classes,\nsince changing a representative on a null set changes its product only on\nthat null set.\nEach $F_a$ is bounded: for $\\psi\\in H_s$,\n$$\\|M_{w_a}\\psi\\|^2=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}w_a(x)^2\\|\\psi(x)\\|_{\\mathbb C^r}^2dx\\le\\|\\psi\\|^2,$$\nso the boundedness inequality holds with bound $C=1$ by monotonicity of the\nnonnegative integral ([[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]).\nThus $F_a$ is a bounded linear operator ([[def-bounded-linear-operator]]). Because $w_a$\nis real, the componentwise pairing gives\n$\\langle M_{w_a}\\psi,\\phi\\rangle=\\langle\\psi,M_{w_a}\\phi\\rangle$ for all\n$\\psi,\\phi\\in H_s$; hence $F_a$ is self-adjoint by\n[[def-hilbert-space-adjoint]]. At almost every $x$, the finite-dimensional\noperator $w_a(x)I_{\\mathbb C^r}$ is nonnegative in the sense of\n[[def-non-negative-and-positive-operator]], and therefore\n$$0\\le\\langle F_a\\psi,\\psi\\rangle=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}w_a(x)\\|\\psi(x)\\|_{\\mathbb C^r}^2dx\\le\\|\\psi\\|^2.$$\nHere $0\\le F_a\\le I$ means these quadratic-form inequalities for every\n$\\psi\\in H_s$.\n\nDefine $w_0(x):=\\max\\{0,1-\\sum_{a\\in A}w_a(x)\\}$. This is measurable,\n$0\\le w_0\\le1$, and it equals $1-\\sum_aw_a$ almost everywhere by the\nadmissibility condition. Thus $F_0=M_{w_0}=I-\\sum_aF_a$ on $H_s$, and the\nsame argument gives $0\\le F_0\\le I$ and self-adjointness. Consequently\n$\\sum_{a\\in A}F_a+F_0=I$, so the completed family is a finite family of\nbounded effects of clause (iv) of P2\n([[post-nrqm-framework-observable-and-probability]]).\n\n**(b) Recorded probabilities.** For a preparation $\\rho\\in D(H_{s})$ the\n**recorded probability** of a label $a\\in A$ and the **no-record probability**\nare\n$$\\nu(a):=\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl(\\rho F_{a}\\bigr),\\qquad \\nu(0):=\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl(\\rho F_{0}\\bigr)=1-\\sum_{a\\in A}\\nu(a),$$\nthe outcome reading of clause (iv) of P2 for the declared effects\n([[def-nrqm-observables-framework-observable-and-outcome-law]] clause (f)\nrecords the reading; the numbers are nonnegative and sum to one). The\n**recorded-event mass** is $\\nu(A):=\\sum_{a\\in A}\\nu(a)=1-\\nu(0)\\in[0,1]$. A\ndetector is **full-efficiency** when $\\sum_{a}w_{a}=1$ almost everywhere, so\nthat $F_{0}=0$ and $\\nu(0)=0$ for every preparation. For a fixed preparation\n$\\rho$, the model **loses** events when $\\nu(0)>0$, and the no-record channel\ncarries this lost mass. A detector that is not full-efficiency can nevertheless\nhave $\\nu(0)=0$ for a particular preparation. A **declared recorded bin** $B$ is\nthe special case with one label $a$ and $w_{a}=\\mathbf 1_{B}$; then\n$F_{a}=G(B)$ is the position projection of clause (e) of\n[[def-nrqm-interference-two-path-preparation-and-detection-model]], with the\nposition set $B$ read in the metre unit of\n[[def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units]], and the recorded probability is\nthe ideal bin probability.\n\n**(c) Loss conditioning.** When $\\nu(A)>0$, the **conditioned recorded law** is\n$$\\bar\\nu(a):=\\frac{\\nu(a)}{\\nu(A)}\\qquad(a\\in A).$$\nIt is a probability measure on the finite set $A$\n([[def-probability-measure]]); it is the law of the recorded label under the\ndeclared model conditioned on the occurrence of a record. Conditioning is a\nstatistical operation on the recorded data; it is not a state update, and no\nclaim of P3 is made here.\n\n**(d) Sampling model.** A **declared $N$-trial sampling model** for a fixed\npreparation $\\rho$ is a probability space $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)$\ncarrying measurable maps\n$O_{1},\\dots,O_{N}:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F)\\to\n(A\\cup\\{0\\},\\mathcal P(A\\cup\\{0\\}))$ ($N\\ge1$), where $0$ is the no-record\nlabel of clause (b) and is distinct from every recorded label because\n$0\\notin A$. The maps are **mutually independent** in the sense of\n[[def-independence-of-finite-random-variables]] and **identically distributed**\nwith $\\mathbb P(O_{k}=a)=\\nu(a)$ for every $a\\in A$, $\\mathbb P(O_{k}=0)=\\nu(0)$\nand every $k$, where $\\nu$ is the recorded law of clause (b); the numbers\n$\\nu(a)$ and $\\nu(0)$ sum to one, so this is a probability law on the finite\nset $A\\cup\\{0\\}$. The **empirical frequency** of the label $a$ in the sample is\n$$F_{N}(a):=\\frac{1}{N}\\#\\{k:O_{k}=a\\}.$$\nA **declared fringe criterion** for sample size $N$ is a subset\n$C\\subseteq(A\\cup\\{0\\})^{N}$; the model **accepts** the sample when\n$(O_{1},\\dots,O_{N})\\in C$ and **fails the criterion** otherwise. The\nidentification of repetitions of a concrete procedure with the independent\nidentically distributed model is an additional statistical assumption in the\nsense of clause (ii) of P7; it is not a consequence of P1-P6, and correlated\ntrials, drifting preparation, dead time and selection must be modeled or\nrecorded as limitations if present ([[post-nrqm-framework-preparation-and-apparatus]]).\n\n**(e) Status.** Clauses (a)-(d) fix the recorded-detector and sampling\nvocabulary of the pair and read clause (iv) of P2 and clauses (i)-(iii) of P7.\nThe recorded weights, the label set, the conditioning convention and the\nsampling model are declared apparatus and procedure inputs. The claim \"this\napparatus realizes the effect family $F_{a}$\" and the claim \"its repetitions\nare independent and identically distributed draws of $\\nu$\" are physical\nidentifications with uncertainty under P7(iii) and P7(ii), can fail, and are\nnot derived here. No calibration, efficiency, background or resolution value\nis asserted, no counts are recorded, and no statistical-inference conclusion\nis drawn by this definition.",
research/plan-spec.json:58889:          "statement": "Adopt the observable and probability postulate P2 in its unsharp finite-outcome\nreading, the declared two-path preparation model\n[[def-nrqm-interference-two-path-preparation-and-detection-model]] with path\ncomponents $\\psi_{1},\\psi_{2}\\ne0$, relative phase $\\varphi$ and weight $w$, and\nthe declared recorded detector\n[[def-nrqm-interference-recorded-detector-and-sampling-model]] with finite\nlabel set $A$ satisfying $0\\notin A$, recorded weights $w_{a}$ and effects\n$F_{a}=M_{w_{a}}$, no-record\neffect $F_{0}=I-\\sum_{a}F_{a}$, recorded probabilities $\\nu(a)$ and recorded-event\nmass $\\nu(A)=\\sum_{a\\in A}\\nu(a)$\n([[post-nrqm-framework-observable-and-probability]]). Assume the Axiom of\nCountable Choice and the Axiom of Choice ([[def-countable-choice]],\n[[def-axiom-of-choice]]), the choice hypotheses of the cited trace-class,\nrank-one and square-root suppliers. Let\n$\\rho_{\\mathrm{coh}}(\\varphi)$ and $\\rho_{\\mathrm{mix}}(w)$ be the declared\ncoherent and which-path preparations and put $N:=\\|\\psi_{1}\\|^{2}+\\|\\psi_{2}\\|^{2}$,\n$c:=\\operatorname{Re}\\langle\\psi_{1},\\psi_{2}\\rangle$ and\n$w_{*}:=\\|\\psi_{1}\\|^{2}/N$. Then:\n\n(a) *Recorded coherent law.* If the model is admissible at phase $\\varphi$,\nthen for every label $a\\in A$\n$$\\nu_{\\mathrm{coh}(\\varphi)}(a)=\\frac{\\displaystyle\\int_{\\mathbb R^{3}}w_{a}(x)\\,\\|\\psi_{1}(x)+e^{i\\varphi}\\psi_{2}(x)\\|_{\\mathbb C^{r}}^{2}\\,dx}{\\|\\psi_{1}+e^{i\\varphi}\\psi_{2}\\|^{2}} ,$$\nand the numerator equals\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^{3}}w_{a}\\bigl(\\|\\psi_{1}\\|^{2}+\\|\\psi_{2}\\|^{2}\\bigr)dx+2\\operatorname{Re}\\Bigl(e^{-i\\varphi}\\int_{\\mathbb R^{3}}w_{a}\\langle\\psi_{1},\\psi_{2}\\rangle_{\\mathbb C^{r}}dx\\Bigr).$$\n\n(b) *Recorded which-path law.* For the declared weight $w\\in[0,1]$ and every\n$a\\in A$,\n$$\\nu_{\\mathrm{mix}(w)}(a)=w\\,\\frac{\\displaystyle\\int_{\\mathbb R^{3}}w_{a}\\|\\psi_{1}\\|^{2}dx}{\\|\\psi_{1}\\|^{2}}+(1-w)\\,\\frac{\\displaystyle\\int_{\\mathbb R^{3}}w_{a}\\|\\psi_{2}\\|^{2}dx}{\\|\\psi_{2}\\|^{2}} .$$\n\n(c) *Loss.* The no-record probability is\n$\\nu(0)=1-\\nu(A)=\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho F_{0})\\ge0$, so $\\nu(A)\\le1$. If\n$\\sum_{a}w_{a}=1$ almost everywhere, then $F_{0}=0$ and $\\nu(0)=0$ for every\npreparation; if $F_{0}\\ne0$, then there is a declared pure preparation with\n$\\nu(0)>0$. When $\\nu(A)>0$, the conditioned recorded law\n$\\bar\\nu(a)=\\nu(a)/\\nu(A)$ is a probability measure on $A$.\n\n(d) *Ideal bins and coincidence.* If $w_{a}=\\mathbf 1_{B_{a}}$ for a declared\nideal detector $(B_{a})_{a=1}^{m}$, then\n$\\nu(a)=\\mu^{G}_{\\rho}(B_{a})$ is the ideal bin probability of\n[[pthm-nrqm-interference-coherent-and-mixture-detection-laws]]. Moreover, if\nthe declared model is admissible at phase $0$ and if\nthe almost-everywhere relation\n$$N\\operatorname{Re}\\langle\\psi_{1}(x),\\psi_{2}(x)\\rangle_{\\mathbb C^{r}}=c\\,\\bigl(\\|\\psi_{1}(x)\\|_{\\mathbb C^{r}}^{2}+\\|\\psi_{2}(x)\\|_{\\mathbb C^{r}}^{2}\\bigr)$$\nof clause (c) of that theorem holds, then the recorded coherent law of phase\n$0$ and the recorded which-path law of weight $w_{*}$ agree, $\\nu_{\\mathrm{coh}(0)}(a)=\\nu_{\\mathrm{mix}(w_{*})}(a)$,\nfor every label $a$ of every declared recorded detector.\n\n(e) *Scope.* Clauses (a)-(d) are conditional on P2, the declared preparation\nand detector models and the cited mathematics; they assert no calibration, no\nresolution, background or efficiency value, no state update, no counts and no\nempirical result, and the claim that a concrete apparatus realizes the declared\neffects is a tested identification with uncertainty.",
research/plan-spec.json:59079:          "statement": "## Scope of the page\n\n**What is established.** The page declares the two-path preparation model\n[[def-nrqm-interference-two-path-preparation-and-detection-model]] with its\npath components, coherent and which-path preparations and ideal detection bins,\nand the recorded detector and sampling model\n[[def-nrqm-interference-recorded-detector-and-sampling-model]] with its bounded\neffects, no-record channel, loss conditioning and declared independent trials.\n[[pthm-nrqm-interference-coherent-and-mixture-detection-laws]] derives the\nnormalized coherent bin law with its amplitude cross term, the which-path bin\nlaw, and the criterion under which the two declared laws coincide on every bin;\nthe criterion comparing phase $0$ is conditional on the model being admissible\nat phase $0$.\n[[pthm-nrqm-interference-recorded-bin-laws-loss-and-conditioning]] derives the\nrecorded-bin laws, the no-record mass and conditioned law, the reduction of\nindicator weights to ideal bins, and a sufficient condition for the recorded\nlaws to coincide when the model is admissible at phase $0$.\n[[pthm-nrqm-interference-continuity-and-local-probability-balance]]\nderives the continuity equation and local squared-amplitude balance for\ncomponentwise $C^1$-in-time, $C^2$-in-space solutions of the P4/P5 equation\nalmost everywhere with the bounded measurable P5 potential. This density is a\nposition probability density when the full-space state is normalized; under\nthe theorem's domination and surface-flux hypotheses its finite total\nsquared-amplitude integral is conserved on an interval. P4 separately gives\nnorm conservation of the model's flow and total probability one for unit\npreparations.\n[[texp-nrqm-interference-finite-sample-fringe-failure]] shows that, within the\ndeclared independent-trials model, a declared fringe criterion that rejects a\npositive-probability outcome sequence fails with positive probability, and\nthat no criterion distinguishes two recorded laws that coincide.\n\n**Adopted rather than derived.** The path components, the relative phase, the\npreparation weight, the recorded weights and the independence of trials are\ndeclarations of the preparation and apparatus models in the sense of P7; the\nidentification of a concrete arrangement with these declarations is a tested\nidentification with uncertainty and can fail. The appearance of an\ninterference pattern is not derived for any laboratory apparatus, and no\ncount, efficiency, resolution, background or calibration value appears on this\npage.\n\n**Not claimed.** The page does not assert which-path ontology, does not assign\na trajectory to the particle between preparation and detection, and does not\nclaim that the coherent preparation is the only admissible model of any\napparatus. It makes no claim that a lower-dimensional screen is a spatial\nregion of the model: a marginal, coarse-graining or flux reduction of a state\nto a screen coordinate or surface is a separate detection model. It also makes\nnone of the following claims: that the flow of an arbitrary $L^{2}$ initial\nvector has a $C^{2}$ representative; that a recorded screen rate equals the\nsurface flux; or that agreement of a finite dataset with a declared law\nestablishes the framework.\n\n**Deferred content and destinations.** Visibility loss by path marking and\nentanglement is not established here: the reusable marker-overlap physical\nresult is homed on the entanglement page\n`qm-composites-entanglement-and-reduced-states`, and this page neither states\nnor consumes it. Photon polarization and optical interference are out of scope\nand belong to the photon and radiation page; the electron double-slit report is\nreported on the companion examples page, not here. Comparative classical field\nmodels and quantum-versus-classical model selection are likewise separate\nmodels.\n\n**Evidence status.** The page contains no experiment and no measured value;\nthe bounded, conditional statements above are consequences of the cited\npostulates, declarations and mathematics. The companion examples page reports\none source-backed electron double-slit experiment with its disclosed\nlimitations, and that report tests the combined model and apparatus\nassumptions rather than proving them.",
research/plan-spec.json:59227:                "title": "D. Tong, Quantum Mechanics (author-hosted lecture set), Section 1.3, printed pages 14-18 (PDF pages 17-21): two-slit cross terms and loss of interference under which-path monitoring; background, not a derivation of this finite phase ensemble",
research/plan-spec.json:59231:                "title": "J. Binney and D. Skinner, The Physics of Quantum Mechanics, Sections 1.2-1.2.1, printed pages 5-7 (PDF pages 13-15): alternative-route cross terms and coarse spatial averaging; background, not averaging of the preparation phase",
research/plan-spec.json:59267:            "uncertainty": "The report supplies no numerical uncertainty budget: no calibrated detector efficiency or background map, no numerical localization standard deviation (the blob size is said to represent the localization error, without a value), no arrival-process law or test of mutual independence of detections, no event-selection error rate, no statistical significance, confidence interval or fitted competing-model comparison, and no uncertainty on beam energy, slit dimensions or mask position. A source inconsistency in the mask height (5 micrometre by 10 micrometre in the body versus 5 micrometre by 20 micrometre in the figure 1 caption) is unresolved. The statements that at most one electron is present and that electron-electron interactions are eliminated rest on an average-separation estimate (2.3e6 m) rather than on a bound on two-electron occupancy. Approximate rate and duration values are not converted into an exact trial count, efficiency or calibrated rate.",
research/plan-spec.json:59277:          "statement": "**The two computations.** [[ex-nrqm-interference-ideal-flat-two-path-screen-distribution]]\ncomputes, in the declared two-path model with flat product-form amplitudes, the\nexact coherent and which-path probabilities of the overlap and outer regions of\nthe declared detection coordinate: at relative phase $\\pi$ the coherent law\nassigns probability zero to the whole overlap region while the balanced\nwhich-path law assigns it positive probability, and a slab bin\n$B_1\\times\\mathbb R^2$ has positive coherent probability at that phase\nexactly when $|B_1\\cap(I_1\\triangle I_2)|>0$, where $I_1,I_2$ are the\ndeclared intervals and $I_1\\triangle I_2$ is their symmetric difference\n(the two outer regions). Mere nonempty intersection does not suffice.\n[[ex-nrqm-interference-uniform-phase-averaging]]\nshows that for orthogonal path components a declared ensemble whose relative\nphase is uniformly distributed over the finitely many $n$-th roots of unity has\nthe which-path law with the norm weights as its averaged law. Both are\nconditional computations in the declared model under all the stated supplier\nhypotheses, including Countable Choice and Choice, and the pair's detection laws\n([[pthm-nrqm-interference-coherent-and-mixture-detection-laws]]); neither is a\ndynamics, an environmental decoherence model, a detector response or a\nstatement about an apparatus.\n\n**The reported experiment.** [[exp-nrqm-interference-bach-electron-double-slit]]\nreports the primary preprint of Bach, Pope, Liou and Batelaan: a 600 eV\nelectron beam through a nanofabricated double-slit with slit-selective\ndistributions and a single-electron event build-up over 2, 7, 209, 1004 and\n6235 accumulated localized events. The report supplies no calibrated\nefficiency or background, no numerical localization error, no uncertainty for\nits apparatus parameters, no occupancy bound and no statistical comparison\nwith a specified competing model; the mask-height inconsistency between its\nbody and its figure caption is recorded as unresolved. The item therefore\nsupports a qualitative operational account only, and its agreement with any\nmodel is evidence about the combined model and apparatus assumptions, not a\nproof of the framework, not a demonstration that every event is independent,\nand not a demonstration that only one electron is in the apparatus at a time.\n\n**What the page does not address.** No photon experiment is reported here; a\nsingle-photon polarization or counting experiment belongs to the photon and\nradiation page, and the classical wave comparison for light is a separate\ndeclared model. No experiment testing the which-path or marker variants of the\narrangement is reported. No claim is made that the finite sample of the\nreported experiment would distinguish the coherent from the which-path\nrecorded law; [[texp-nrqm-interference-finite-sample-fringe-failure]] shows\nthat both can fail or pass a finite-sample criterion and that coincident\nrecorded laws are indistinguishable by any criterion.\n\n**Evidence status.** The page combines exact conditional computations with one\nfaithfully reported experiment. It contains no simulation presented as data,\nno invented count, precision or significance, and no conclusion about nature;\nthe empirical qualifications of the report are carried in its Observations and\nUncertainty sections and are not strengthened here.",
research/plan-spec.json:60327:          "statement": "## Three distinct objects\n\n**Preparation spread.** For an assigned sharp observable $(A,u_{A})$ and a\npreparation $\\rho$, the spread $\\Delta_{\\rho}A$ of\n[[def-nrqm-uncertainty-preparation-spread]] is the standard deviation of the\noutcome law that the observable and probability postulate assigns to the pair\n$(\\rho,A)$: it is a property of the preparation and the assigned observable,\ncomputed from the Born law alone, and it exists only under the stated moment\nhypotheses. The inequalities of\n[[pthm-nrqm-uncertainty-robertson-relation]] concern these spreads and nothing\nelse.\n\n**Apparatus resolution or error.** A finite-resolution apparatus is modeled in\nthis category by a declared instrument with effects and an update rule\n([[def-nrqm-measurement-framework-declared-measurements-and-instruments]];\nclause (iv) of P2 and the ideal conditional measurement postulate P3 in the\nreadings of [[post-nrqm-framework-ideal-conditional-measurement]]). Whether a\ndeclared effect family approximates a target observable, and by how much, is a\nproperty of the chosen instrument: the postulates do not attach a universal\nerror parameter to a sharp observable, and the effects alone do not determine\nthe update ([[pthm-nrqm-measurement-instrument-updates-not-determined-by-effects]]).\n\n**Disturbance.** The state change produced by a measurement is likewise part\nof the declared instrument: for the framework's declared ideal sharp\nmeasurement the conditional and discarded-outcome updates are specified in\n[[pthm-nrqm-measurement-projective-outcomes-and-discarded-ensembles]], and\nthey are idealizations, not claims about every apparatus. The framework's\npostulates assign no universal disturbance to \"a measurement of position\" as\nsuch.\n\n## What is and is not asserted\n\n**No conflation.** The Robertson relation bounds the product of two\npreparation spreads; it does not bound an apparatus error, it does not bound a\ndisturbance, and it is not a statement that measuring one quantity \"must\"\nperturb another by a fixed amount. The heuristic accounts of measurement\nintrusion in the literature are accordingly used on this page at most as\nmotivation: the exact statements are the preparation-spread results, and the\nidentification of a concrete apparatus with a declared instrument is a tested\nidentification with uncertainty under the preparation and apparatus postulate\nP7 ([[post-nrqm-framework-preparation-and-apparatus]]).\n\n**No universal error-disturbance relation is recorded.** This page and the\nmeasurement page contain no theorem of the form \"error times disturbance is at\nleast $\\hbar/2$\": such a statement would require declared error and\ndisturbance quantifiers for an instrument and its own proof. What is recorded\nhere is only the separation of the three objects and the conditional\nresults actually proved on the two pages.\n\n## Remarks\n\n- **Why the distinction is load-bearing.** An experiment that reports a finite\n  resolution, a detection efficiency or a background model is reporting\n  apparatus properties; a preparation spread is a model quantity of the state\n  and the assigned observable. Agreement of a measured histogram with a\n  predicted distribution tests the combined preparation, apparatus and\n  observable identification, not the inequality alone.\n- **Disturbance is model-dependent.** Two declared instruments with the same\n  outcome effects can have different updates and hence different disturbances,\n  as [[pthm-nrqm-measurement-instrument-updates-not-determined-by-effects]]\n  records; a disturbance claim must therefore name its instrument.",
research/plan-spec.json:61054:          "statement": "\n## Scope of the page\n\n**What is established.** The page fixes a declared effective one-dimensional\nmodel of a spinless massive particle\n([[def-nrqm-potentials-one-dimensional-line-model]]) and proves, as\nconditional consequences of P1, P2, P4, P5 and the cited mathematics, the\nfollowing statements. The mathematical lemmas\n[[lem-nrqm-potentials-free-operator-differential-identification]] and\n[[lem-nrqm-potentials-positive-scaling-of-self-adjoint-operators]] identify the\nfree operator with the weak second-derivative operator and transfer\nself-adjointness, resolvents, spectra and eigenfamilies along positive\nscaling; [[lem-nrqm-potentials-one-dimensional-flux-identity]] proves the\none-dimensional continuity identity and the constancy of the current of a\nstationary solution.\n[[pthm-nrqm-potentials-line-model-energy-and-stationary-states]] establishes\nthat the declared energy operator of the line model with a real bounded\nmeasurable potential is an assigned sharp observable, generates the unitary\nflow of the model, and that its stationary preparations are exactly the\neigenrays with point-mass energy law.\n[[pthm-nrqm-potentials-interval-spectra-completeness-and-nodes]] establishes,\nfor the declared Dirichlet and separated Robin interval models with continuous\nreal potentials, self-adjointness with compact resolvent, complete\northonormal eigenbases, discreteness and simplicity of the spectra, the\nmin-max comparison of the Dirichlet energies with the free box energies and\nthe nodal counts, together with the Robin eigenvalue condition.\n[[pthm-nrqm-potentials-finite-well-bound-states]] gives the exact root\nconditions, simplicity, parity and count of the bound states of the finite\nsquare well, and\n[[pthm-nrqm-potentials-delta-point-interaction-bound-state]] gives the\nself-adjointness of the declared zero-range interface operator and its unique\nbound state for attractive coupling with the exact energy\n$-mg^{2}/(2\\hbar^{2})$. [[pthm-nrqm-potentials-barrier-flux-and-transmission]]\ngives the unique scattering solution of the rectangular barrier, its conserved\ncurrent, the exact coefficients $T$ and $R$, the flux balance $T+R=1$, the\nreading of $T$ and $R$ as fractions of the reference current, and the\nthick-barrier error bound.\n\n**What is assumed, not derived.** The page's model data are declared, not\nderived: the reduction of the three-dimensional massive-particle model to one\nspatial dimension, the position and momentum assignments of the line model,\nthe Dirichlet and Robin boundary conditions with their boundary parameters,\nthe zero-range interface condition with its coupling, and the scattering\nreading of the current ratios. Each is an assumption of the pair in the sense\nof the preparation and apparatus discipline P7; an identification of a\nconcrete material system with any of them is a tested identification with\nuncertainty and is not produced by these items. The operators rest on the\nmathematical supplier [[thm-nrqm-composites-real-multipliers-and-free-bounded-potential-operators]]\nand on the interval and contact mathematics of\n[[def-nrqm-intervals-hilbert-sobolev-spaces]] and the items of the intervals\npair, whose Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice hypotheses are inherited by\nevery consumer above.\n\n**What is not addressed.** No wave-packet or time-dependent scattering limit\nis computed; no arrival-time, detector-response, efficiency or background\nmodel is declared; no count, rate, cross-section or measured spectrum appears.\nThe scattering solution of the barrier item is not an element of the model\nstate space, so no Born probability is read from it. Potentials with\nunbounded or singular growth, the harmonic oscillator, the Coulomb problem,\nhigher-dimensional scattering, resonant and above-barrier behaviour, and the\nAiry/WKB connection problem for smooth barriers belong to other pages of the\ncategory and are not consumed or anticipated here. The example page of the\npair carries exact finite checks of the barrier and well formulas; those are\nmodel computations, not measurements.\n\n**Evidence status.** The page is a conditional model development. It contains\nno experiment, no reported observation, no uncertainty statement and no\nstatistical inference; agreement of any of its formulas with data would be\nevidence about the combined model, apparatus and analysis assumptions and\nwould not prove the model.\n",
research/plan-spec.json:62903:          "statement": "## What the page establishes\n\nThe mathematical lemmas of this page follow from their stated mathematical\nhypotheses; its physics theorems follow conditionally from their stated model\npostulates and mathematical hypotheses.\n[[def-nrqm-symmetries-framework-symmetry-groups-and-conservation]] fixes the\nvocabulary: a symmetry of the declared dynamics is a unitary preserving the\nenergy domain and commuting with the energy operator, a unitary symmetry group\nis a group of such symmetries, a conserved sharp observable is one whose\noutcome law is time-independent in every preparation, and the translations,\nspatial rotations, parity and gauge multipliers are recorded as the model's\ncandidate actions, not as symmetries.\n[[lem-nrqm-symmetries-unitary-conjugation-and-commutation-calculus]] proves the\nequivalence between flow commutation, strong spectral commutation and domain\ninvariance of a self-adjoint observable;\n[[lem-nrqm-symmetries-spatial-rotation-action]],\n[[lem-nrqm-symmetries-parity-action]],\n[[lem-nrqm-symmetries-rotation-generators-on-schwartz-classes]] and\n[[lem-nrqm-symmetries-free-flow-multiplier-form]] provide the concrete actions\nand their operator identities on the component-valued model.\n[[pthm-nrqm-symmetries-generator-conservation]] proves the equivalence of its\nthree commutation conditions, deduces conservation of the outcome law and of\ndomain-qualified moments, and covers self-adjoint unitary symmetries;\n[[pthm-nrqm-symmetries-translation-invariance-and-momentum-conservation]]\ndecides exactly which bounded potentials have the translations as symmetries\nand derives momentum conservation for the translation-invariant case;\n[[pthm-nrqm-symmetries-rotational-and-parity-invariance]] does the same for\nrotations (with conservation of the coordinate-rotation generators) and for\nparity; and [[pthm-nrqm-symmetries-gauge-covariance]] transports the\nprescribed-coupling operator under gauge transformations of\n[[post-nrqm-symmetries-prescribed-external-coupling]], exhibits the exact\ncriterion under which a gauge multiplier is a symmetry of the fixed dynamics\nand identifies the global-phase case. The mathematical lemmas are conditional\non their displayed mathematical hypotheses, including the stated choice\nassumptions; they do not depend on physical postulates. The physics theorems\nand examples are conditional on the postulates and model hypotheses stated in\neach item: the general conservation result uses P1, P2 and P4; the bounded-\npotential translation, rotation and parity results use P1, P2, P4, P5 and P6;\nand gauge covariance uses P1, P2, P4 and the prescribed-coupling assumption\nP5a. P5a is adopted and is not derived by the gauge theorem. None of these\nconditional results establishes that its assumptions describe a laboratory\nsystem.\n\n## Empirical status of the symmetry and conservation statements\n\nThe conservation laws proved here are model consequences: they hold for the\ndeclared massive model with the displayed potential class and follow from the\ninvariance hypotheses as identities about that model. They are not reports of\nmeasurements and do not establish that any laboratory preparation is exactly\ndescribed by the model. In particular, the mass parameter $m>0$, the charge\nparameter $q$, the spin label $s$ and the potentials $(\\mathbf A_{\\rm ext},\\Phi_{\\rm ext})$\nare declared model data in the units of\n[[def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units]]; the statement that a concrete\nspecies, field configuration or apparatus realizes them is a tested\nidentification with uncertainty, external to these items. A selection rule or\na conservation law deduced here becomes an empirical prediction only after a\npreparation map, a detection model, a sampling model and an uncertainty\nbudget are supplied, none of which this page provides. The agreement of such\nan identified model with data is evidence about the combined model and\napparatus assumptions; it is not a proof of the postulates.\n\n## What is not claimed\n\nThe page asserts no symmetry of any concrete potential outside the displayed\nalmost-everywhere invariance hypotheses, no classification of centrally\nsymmetric or parity-even systems beyond those hypotheses, no definite value\nof a conserved observable, and no converse from the constancy of outcome laws\nto the commutation conditions for unbounded observables. The rotation and\nparity actions act on the spatial factor only: no SU(2) lift, no rotation of\nthe internal factor and no spin angular momentum appear here; antiunitary and\ntime-reversal symmetries, superselection sectors, gauge freedom beyond the\nprescribed bounded-coefficient class, singular or unbounded potentials,\nconfined and boundary realizations, periodic potentials, time-dependent\nexternal fields, relativistic matter and full field theory are all outside\nthe scope of these items and require their own model assumptions.\n\n## Recorded supplier note\n\nThe gauge items of this page consume the magnetic-operator development of an\nearlier batch of this run. The batch-53 notes preserve historical reports of\nrole-metadata and source-locator defects in earlier versions of those\nsuppliers; those notes are not a description of their current text. The\ncurrent files inspected for this review declare the mathematics dependencies\nwith role `mathematical-premise` and have these source locators:\n\n- `def-nrqm-magnetic-and-detection-magnetic-schrodinger-operator` cites\n  Teschl, Sections 6.1 and 7.2, and Binney and Skinner, Sections 3.3 and\n  3.3.1.\n- `thm-nrqm-magnetic-and-detection-gauge-h2-domain-preservation` cites\n  Teschl, Section 7.2, equations (7.22)-(7.24) and Theorem 7.8, and Binney\n  and Skinner, Section 3.3.1, equations (3.40)-(3.45).\n- `thm-nrqm-magnetic-and-detection-gauge-transported-propagator` cites\n  Teschl, Section 5.1, Theorem 5.1, and Binney and Skinner, Section 3.3.1,\n  equations (3.40)-(3.45).\n\nThe current Teschl text has the cited free-operator material in Section 7.2\n(printed pages 167-168), the Kato-Rellich theorem in Section 6.1 (printed\npage 135), and Stone's theorem in Section 5.1 (printed pages 123-124). The\ncurrent Binney text has the static gauge phase and covariant-momentum\nintertwining in Section 3.3.1 (printed pages 46-47). I checked these current\nlocators against the hosted full texts. They provide background for the\nmagnetic results; the bounded-coefficient operator and time-dependent\npropagator arguments are supplied locally in the magnetic items. The earlier\nfalse locators recorded in the batch notes are absent from the current files.",
research/plan-spec.json:63287:          "statement": "## The worked items and what they establish\n\n[[ex-nrqm-symmetries-free-translation-and-momentum]] is the exact free-model\ncomputation: with $V\\equiv0$ every translation is a symmetry, the free flow in\nthe momentum representation is the unimodular multiplier\n$M_{f_{t}}$, $f_{t}(p)=e^{-it|p|^{2}/(2m\\hbar)}$, the translations are the\nmultipliers $M_{e^{-ia\\cdot p/\\hbar}}$, and the momentum components are\nconserved sharp observables with the law, moment and generator statements of\n[[pthm-nrqm-symmetries-translation-invariance-and-momentum-conservation]];\nthe modulus of the momentum representative is invariant in time almost\neverywhere.\n[[cex-nrqm-symmetries-nonconstant-potential-breaks-translation]] shows that\ntranslations need not remain symmetries for nonconstant potentials:\nfor the bounded measurable potential energy $E_{*}1_{B(0,R)}$, with\n$E_{*}>0$ in J and $R=1\\,\\mathrm m$, no nonzero translation is a symmetry,\nbecause the shifted potential differs from the original on a set of positive\nmeasure, so the symmetry criterion of the translation theorem is not met.\nFor a specified translation $a$, the criterion is exactly\n$V(\\cdot-a)=V$ almost everywhere; nonconstancy in a direction alone does\nnot exclude discrete translations along that direction.\n[[ex-nrqm-symmetries-parity-selection-rule]] derives, for a potential even\nalmost everywhere, that every energy eigenspace is invariant under the parity\naction, that a nondegenerate eigenstate has definite parity, and that the\nposition matrix element of two states of equal parity vanishes.\n[[ex-nrqm-symmetries-central-potential-rotation-invariance]] verifies the\nalmost-everywhere rotational and parity invariance of a bounded radial\npotential and instantiates\n[[pthm-nrqm-symmetries-rotational-and-parity-invariance]]: all rotations are\nsymmetries, the rotation action is a strongly continuous unitary symmetry\ngroup, and the coordinate-rotation generators $L_{i}$ are conserved sharp\nobservables, with the underlying conservation mechanism of\n[[pthm-nrqm-symmetries-generator-conservation]].\n\n## What none of the items establishes\n\nNone of the four items is an experiment: no apparatus, preparation map,\ncalibration, detection model, efficiency, resolution, background model,\nsampling model, uncertainty budget, count or dataset appears, and no measured\nvalue of any model parameter is asserted. The objects $m$, $q$ when present,\n$s$, the potentials and the conserved laws are model data and model statements\nin the units of [[def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units]]; deciding that a\nlaboratory system realizes the model, that its potential is approximately\nradial or even, or that a transition amplitude is the matrix element computed\nhere, is a tested identification with uncertainty under the preparation and\napparatus assumptions, and it is external to this page. In particular the\nparity selection rule is an identity about the model's assigned position\nobservables: turning it into a statement about observed intensities requires a\ntime-dependent coupling model, the corresponding transition-rate analysis, a\ndetection model and a sampling model, none of which is supplied here.\n\n## Limits of the statements\n\nThe free computation is conditional on $V\\equiv0$; the counterexample concerns\none potential inside the bounded measurable class and refutes only the\nuniversal claim that every bounded nonconstant potential has all nonzero\ntranslations as symmetries (potentials constant along a direction keep the\ncorresponding translations). The central-potential example assumes the radial\nidentity almost everywhere and a bounded radial function; it asserts no\neigenvalue, bound state, degeneracy, radial solution or spectral line, and its\nboundedness hypothesis excludes the Coulomb and other unbounded radial\nmodels. The parity example requires the evenness hypothesis and, for definite\nparity, the nondegeneracy of the eigenvalue; at a degenerate eigenvalue only\nthe eigenspace invariance is asserted. No item of this page makes a claim about\nantiunitary or time-reversal symmetries, about superselection, about spinorial\nrotations or the internal factor, or about relativistic and field-theoretic\nstructures.",
research/plan-spec.json:64584:          "statement": "- **Recorded content.** The companion page contains exact model computations:\n  [[ex-nrqm-angular-momentum-spin-half-directional-probabilities]] evaluates\n  the Born probabilities of the directional spin components for an arbitrary\n  unit spinor and works the coordinate cases;\n  [[ex-nrqm-angular-momentum-l-eigenfunctions]] computes the low spherical\n  harmonics, their orthonormality and their orbital eigenvalues;\n  [[ex-nrqm-angular-momentum-zeeman-toy-model]] records the sector expressions\nof the uniform-field Zeeman operator on product vectors in $D(H_{0})$, its\nfactorized flow on all product vectors, an explicitly labeled two-level\ntruncation with its level splitting, and the explicit precession matrices;\n  and [[texp-nrqm-angular-momentum-stern-gerlach-splitting]] derives, under an\n  explicitly hypothetical containment premise, the change in the difference\n  of the two sector mean transverse momenta.\n\n- **Status.** All four items are conditional statements inside the model of\n  [[def-nrqm-kinematics-framework-massive-spin-s-model]] with the readings of\n  [[def-nrqm-angular-momentum-framework-angular-momentum-observables]] and, in\n  the last two, with the adopted magnetic-moment coupling. None of them\n  reports a measurement; the thought experiment's containment premise is a\n  hypothetical assumption, and the toy truncation is a toy model assumption;\n  the numerical values appearing in them ($\\hbar$-multiples, probabilities\n  and frequencies) are model expressions, not measured numbers.\n\n- **Empirical qualifications.** The identification of any of these\n  preparations and observables with a laboratory setup is a tested\n  identification with uncertainty under\n  [[post-nrqm-framework-preparation-and-apparatus]]; detector resolution,\n  background, calibration, sampling and selection are not modeled here. The\n  directional-probability example specializes\n  [[pthm-nrqm-angular-momentum-spin-spectra-and-outcome-laws]], while the\n  Zeeman toy example specializes the uniform static-field result\n  [[pthm-nrqm-angular-momentum-zeeman-precession]], retaining its isolated\n  evolution and its separate truncation assumption. The gradient-field\n  thought experiment instead retains its own hypotheses from\n  [[texp-nrqm-angular-momentum-stern-gerlach-splitting]]: real bounded\n  $B_z\\in C^1(\\mathbb R)$ with bounded derivative, an initial unit vector\n  in $D(H_Z^2)$, a constant gradient on the declared interval and the\n  hypothetical containment premise throughout $[0,\\tau]$; normalized\n  sector means require positive sector weights. It does not specialize the\n  uniform-field precession theorem. The orbital example retains its\n  harmonic-polynomial and normalization hypotheses. Each computation\n  inherits the qualifications of the suppliers it actually uses.\n\n- **What is deliberately absent.** No experiment item, no measured\n  frequency, no measured gyromagnetic parameter, no source-backed\n  Stern-Gerlach dataset, no spin-orbit or fine-structure computation and no\n  radiation or relaxation model appears on the examples companion; those\n  belong to other pages of the category, with their own sources and\n  qualifications.",
research/plan-spec.json:65285:          "statement": "- **Adopted readings.** The page adopts the composition clause of P1\n  ([[post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition]]) for a declared pair of\n  distinguishable subsystems, together with the observable and probability\n  postulate P2 and the ideal conditional-measurement postulate P3 in the\n  readings of [[def-nrqm-composites-framework-composite-systems-and-local-readouts]];\n  the reduced preparations of [[def-nrqm-composites-framework-reduced-preparations]];\n  and, for the marked interference model,\n  [[def-nrqm-composites-marker-model-and-fringe-visibility]], whose path\n  components, marker states and detector bin are declared apparatus data under\n  P7 ([[post-nrqm-framework-preparation-and-apparatus]]). The composition\n  assignment, the local readout convention and the marked model are adopted,\n  not derived from the rest of the framework or from observation.\n- **Derived results.** The conditional results of the page are the Schmidt\n  reduction, purity criterion and exact pure separability/product equivalence for pure preparations\n  ([[pthm-nrqm-composites-schmidt-reduction-and-pure-entanglement]]), the\n  identification of local readout laws with the laws of the reduced\n  preparation and its converse\n  ([[pthm-nrqm-composites-local-readout-laws-and-reduced-preparations]]), the\n  invariance of the untouched factor's reduced preparation under discarded\n  local instruments, with the conditional reductions averaging back to it\n  ([[pthm-nrqm-composites-local-instruments-and-no-signalling]]), and the\n  contraction of the marker cross term with the visibility bound\n  ([[pthm-nrqm-composites-marker-overlap-and-visibility-bound]]). Each is\n  conditional on its stated hypotheses, including the choice hypotheses of the\n  cited mathematics.\n- **Empirical status.** No item of the page reports an experiment, a count, a\n  calibration or a measured correlation, and none is a proof about nature. The\n  detection weights and the visibility of the marked model are probabilities\n  and contrasts of a declared model; whether a concrete interferometer\n  realizes a marked model with particular components, marker states and bin is\n  a tested identification with uncertainty in the sense of clauses (i)-(iii)\n  of P7, and a comparison of the model with recorded data would require the\n  sampling, efficiency, resolution and background qualifications of the\n  apparatus, which this page does not supply.\n- **Localized detections and accumulated distributions.** The detection\n  weights of the page are probabilities for one trial of the declared model.\n  An interference pattern is a property of the accumulated distribution of\n  many detections, not of a single event, and a finite sample can fail a\n  declared fringe criterion with positive probability under ordinary\n  independent sampling even when the model is correct; nothing in the page\n  draws a statistical conclusion from the displayed probabilities.\n- **No-signalling is a model statement.** The invariance proved in\n  [[pthm-nrqm-composites-local-instruments-and-no-signalling]] is a statement\n  about declared local instruments of the adopted framework and its\n  mathematics. It is not a relativistic causality principle, does not assert\n  anything about spacelike separation, and does not by itself exclude\n  alternative models; the conditional changes of the measured factor's partner\n  are the correlations of the model, and every interpretation of the\n  alternatives, of the marker and of \"which path\" is outside the page.\n- **Deferred content.** The following are outside this pair and are not\n  supplied or assumed here: setting-independent local-response models and the\n  classical-versus-quantum Bell comparison, with specified angles and\n  finite-data caveats, which belong to the later Bell-models pair; a general\n  theory of mixed-state separability, entanglement measures and separability\n  criteria beyond the definition given in\n  [[def-nrqm-composites-separable-and-entangled-states]]; the exchange\n  sectors and exclusion statements for identical particles; quantum circuits,\n  teleportation and computation; and any dynamics, interaction or open-system\n  model of decoherence, the marked model of this page being a kinematic\n  preparation model.",
research/plan-spec.json:66064:          "statement": "## Remarks\n\n- **What the comparison establishes.** For the declared spin correlation\n  arrangement of [[def-nrqm-bell-declared-spin-arrangement]], the four sign\n  tables are the laws $\\nu^{\\rho}_{ij}(\\varepsilon,\\delta)$ of clause (d) of\n  that definition. If the arrangement's CHSH functional satisfies\n  $|S(\\nu^{\\rho})|>2$, then the four tables admit no local-response model:\n  every local-response representation would give $|S|\\le2$ by\n  [[thm-nrqm-bell-local-representations-satisfy-chsh]]. The singlet at the\n  directions of [[texp-nrqm-bell-singlet-arrangement-contrast]] is the\n  displayed instance, with $|S|=2\\sqrt2$. The statement is a conditional\n  no-go for one precisely defined class of models of one precisely defined set\n  of four tables.\n\n- **What is not excluded.** The theorem concerns models of the four tables\n  that consist of one specification space whose probability law is the same\n  for all setting pairs, together with per-station response laws that do not\n  depend on the distant setting ([[def-nrqm-bell-local-response-representation]]\n  clauses (a), (c)). Models outside that class are not addressed: a model\n  whose specification or response is allowed to depend on the distant setting,\n  a model that does not reproduce all four tables simultaneously, or a model\n  of a different arrangement or of a different measurement structure is not\n  excluded. In particular the comparison does not rule out \"hidden variables\"\n  in general, and it does not claim that no completion of quantum mechanics\n  exists; it excludes the declared local-response class for these tables.\n\n- **Assumptions used.** The deduction rests on the framework readings P1, P2,\n  P3, P5, P6 and P7 fixed by\n  [[def-nrqm-bell-declared-spin-arrangement]], on the declared composite,\n  preparation and spin-half assignments, on the ideal sharp character of the\n  declared measurements ([[def-nrqm-measurement-framework-declared-measurements-and-instruments]]),\n  on the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]) and the Axiom\n  of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]) stated in the arrangement and\n  singlet-attainment hypotheses, and on the cited mathematics (the\n  finite-dimensional tensor calculus, the CHSH bound for representable models\n  and the operator bound of\n  [[pthm-nrqm-bell-tsirelson-bound-for-arrangements]]). Changing one of these\n  readings voids the conditional conclusion for the changed model; it does not\n  weaken it, and a replacement conclusion needs its own proof.\n\n- **Measurement-model boundary.** The declared sign measurements have exactly\n  two outcomes with sign labels $\\pm1$; detection efficiency, finite\n  resolution, dark counts, background, drift and loss are outside the\n  arrangement and require their own apparatus models. In particular an\n  observed violation on a recorded sub-ensemble does not by itself contradict\n  the declared model class: the recorded tables are conditional tables, and\n  the CHSH bound transfers to them when the selection is determined by a\n  positive-probability event of the specification space that is the same for\n  every setting pair, as in\n  [[lem-nrqm-bell-setting-independent-selection-closure]]; that lemma is a\n  sufficient condition, not a necessary one, and is not a claim about any\n  real detector. A different selection model may also yield representable\n  conditional tables; its consequences require their own justification. Finite\n  samples add a sampling model and its fluctuations, treated on the examples\n  page. Whether a finite record deductively excludes one of two specified\n  models depends on their sampling supports: a record with positive\n  probability under both is compatible with both, while an observed\n  zero-probability record can exclude one specified ideal model conditional on\n  its assumptions. No universal claim is made about every model pair or every\n  finite sample.\n\n- **No-signalling is not the separator.** Every local-response model is\n  no-signalling ([[prop-nrqm-bell-local-representations-are-no-signalling]]),\n  and every declared arrangement's marginals are also setting-independent:\n  summing its joint law over $\\delta$ gives\n  $\\nu^{\\rho,A}_{ij}(\\varepsilon)=\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho(P_{a_i,\\varepsilon}\\otimes I))$\n  because $\\sum_\\delta P_{b_j,\\delta}=I$, and the symmetric sum is\n  $\\nu^{\\rho,B}_{ij}(\\delta)=\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho(I\\otimes P_{b_j,\\delta}))$\n  ([[def-nrqm-bell-declared-spin-arrangement]] clauses (a), (d)). For the\n  singlet both marginals are $1/2$\n  ([[texp-nrqm-bell-singlet-arrangement-contrast]] clause (d)); a discarded\n  declared local instrument on one factor leaves the other factor unchanged\n  ([[pthm-nrqm-composites-local-instruments-and-no-signalling]] clause (b)).\n  The contrast between the two classes therefore lies in the joint tables,\n  not in any dependence of one station's marginal law on the distant setting.\n\n- **Attainment and sharpness.** The value $2\\sqrt2$ is attained for the\n  declared arrangement\n  ([[pthm-nrqm-bell-tsirelson-bound-for-arrangements]] clause (b)), so within\n  the declared class of binary sharp readouts of self-adjoint involutions no\n  smaller uniform bound for the CHSH functional is available. This says\n  nothing about other functionals, other numbers of settings, unsharp\n  measurements or other systems.\n\n- **Interpretation.** The result is a statement about four probability\n  tables. It identifies no mechanism, no influence between the stations and\n  no preferred frame; the arrangement carries no spatial or temporal\n  structure. Interpretations that attach additional physical content to the\n  violation are not part of the deduction and are not asserted here. Whether\n  a laboratory arrangement realizes the declared model and what a recorded\n  sample shows are separate matters under the preparation and apparatus\n  postulate P7 ([[post-nrqm-framework-preparation-and-apparatus]]).\n",
research/plan-spec.json:66120:          "statement": "## Remarks\n\n- **What the page contains.** Mathematical vocabulary for binary correlation\n  models and their local-response representations\n  ([[def-nrqm-bell-binary-correlation-models]],\n  [[def-nrqm-bell-local-response-representation]]), the CHSH bound for every\n  representable model ([[thm-nrqm-bell-local-representations-satisfy-chsh]]),\n  the preservation of representability and of the bound under a\n  setting-independent selection\n  ([[lem-nrqm-bell-setting-independent-selection-closure]]), the exact joint\n  laws of the two-factor singlet ([[lem-nrqm-bell-singlet-pairings]]), the\n  declared spin correlation arrangement of the framework\n  ([[def-nrqm-bell-declared-spin-arrangement]]), the singlet computation with\n  the failure of local-response representability at violating settings\n  ([[texp-nrqm-bell-singlet-arrangement-contrast]]) and the Tsirelson bound\n  with its attainment\n  ([[pthm-nrqm-bell-tsirelson-bound-for-arrangements]]). All are conditional\n  model statements; the spin-arrangement and spin-comparison claims carry the\n  Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]) and the Axiom of Choice\n  ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]) stated in their supplier hypotheses.\n\n- **What the page does not contain.** No experiment item, no reported count,\n  no calibration, no detector efficiency, no loss model, no background, no\n  trial-level uncertainty analysis, no p-value, no confidence level and no\n  measured correlation. None of the computations establishes that the\n  framework or any of its postulates describes nature, and none of them\n  asserts that any laboratory procedure realizes the declared arrangement.\n  The marginals, tables and correlations are model probabilities for the\n  declared preparation; the identification of a concrete apparatus with the\n  declared measurements is the\n  tested identification with uncertainty of clause (iii) of the preparation\n  and apparatus postulate P7\n  ([[post-nrqm-framework-preparation-and-apparatus]]). Treating repetitions\n  as independent and identically distributed draws is the additional\n  statistical model of clause (ii), to be declared and checked for the\n  apparatus and procedure; it is not implied by the state postulate.\n\n- **Deferred experiment destination.** The design of this pair reserves an\n  experiment item for a Bell test. Such an item is not built here: it would\n  require reading a primary or authoritative full report and recording its\n  source-specific preparation and apparatus, calibration, measured outputs,\n  trial count where reported, detection efficiency, loss and post-selection,\n  background, the reported statistical treatment and systematics, and the\n  source's omissions; none of the sources read for this page supplies that\n  level of detail. The sources' experimental remarks motivate the model\n  comparison only: Binney's section 6.1.4 refers to the 1972\n  Freedman-Clauser test and cites a 1998 photonic test, and the MIT chapter\n  closes with the statement that experiments have confirmed violations; no\n  numerical value from those secondary accounts is reproduced here as an\n  observation, and no interval, count or precision is asserted. The\n  statistics and primary-report gap named in the design is recorded as an\n  open destination.\n\n- **Source qualifications not imported.** The same sources contain general\n  conclusions that the comparison rules out hidden variables as such or that\n  quantum mechanics cannot be superseded by a theory with a definite spin\n  direction. Those conclusions are stronger than the conditional statement\n  proved here — which excludes only the declared local-response class of the\n  declared tables — and are not imported. Likewise the operator bound used on\n  this page is proved by the operator-norm route of the cited mathematics\n  (the identity for the square of the CHSH operator and the norm bound,\n  invoked in [[pthm-nrqm-bell-tsirelson-bound-for-arrangements]] fact F6 and\n  proof step 1.2). That route uses\n  $\\|[A_0,A_1]\\|\\le2$ and $\\|[B_0,B_1]\\|\\le2$ for the supplier's\n  self-adjoint involutions $A_i,B_j$, where $[A,B]:=AB-BA$ is the\n  commutator; no\n  reversed variance inequality of the form\n  $\\langle C^{2}\\rangle\\le\\langle C\\rangle^{2}$ is\n  used as a premise.\n\n- **What a comparison with data could show.** Under declared preparation,\n  detection, settings, selection and sampling models, agreement of recorded\n  frequencies with the arrangement's tables supports the combined model and\n  apparatus assumptions. The bound $|S(\\mu)|\\le2$ constrains the correlations\n  of a local-response model's probability tables, rather than every finite\n  sample estimate $\\widehat S$. A finite recorded estimate with\n  $|\\widehat S|>2$ can have positive probability under that model; finite-sample\n  fluctuations are treated in [[texp-nrqm-bell-finite-sample-chsh]]. Evidence\n  against a specified model class requires an explicit statistical test with\n  its sampling assumptions, uncertainty and error control, together with the\n  apparatus and selection qualifications. A threshold crossing alone does\n  not deductively exclude the class, and a p-value is not the probability\n  that the class is false. Agreement does not prove the framework, and a null\n  or reduced result does not by itself falsify it.\n\n- **Interpretation and scope of the model.** The arrangement assigns no\n  spatial positions, separation or temporal order to the two factors, so no\n  relativistic locality principle is modelled here; the exclusion is a\n  statement about the four tables. The boundaries of the comparison are\n  recorded in [[rem-nrqm-bell-boundaries-and-comparison-scope]], and no\n  interpretation of the violation beyond those boundaries is asserted.\n",
research/plan-spec.json:66317:          "statement": "## Remarks\n\n- **Recorded tables are conditional tables.** The arrangement's laws\n  $\\nu^{\\rho}_{ij}$ of\n  [[def-nrqm-bell-declared-spin-arrangement]] clause (d) describe a declared\n  pair of local sign measurements for every setting pair on the declared\n  preparation. A comparison with recorded data ordinarily uses only the runs\n  in which both stations register an event; the laws of the recorded runs are\n  therefore **conditional laws** of a larger model, and they are not the\n  arrangement's tables unless a detection and selection model connecting the\n  two is declared. This is part of the preparation and apparatus discipline of\n  P7 clause (i) ([[post-nrqm-framework-preparation-and-apparatus]]).\n\n- **A sufficient condition for the transfer.** If the full tables admit a\n  local-response representation and the recorded runs are exactly those\n  specifications lying in a measurable selection event $D$ of the\n  specification space with $\\mathbb P(D)>0$ — membership is determined by the\n  specification alone, using the same event for every setting pair — then the conditional\n  tables again admit a local-response representation, with the same response\n  laws over the conditional measure, and hence satisfy the CHSH bound\n  ([[lem-nrqm-bell-setting-independent-selection-closure]]). This is the\n  mathematical content of a **setting-independent detection** or\n  **fair-sampling** hypothesis for the declared model class. It does not\n  require statistical independence of $D$ from the outcomes: reweighting the\n  specifications can change the tables.\n\n- **The condition is a hypothesis, not a theorem about detectors.** The\n  selection-closure lemma is a conditional statement about the declared model.\n  It does not assert that any laboratory detector, coincidence circuit or\n  post-selection rule realizes a selection event of the required form;\n  identifying a concrete apparatus with such a model is a tested\n  identification with uncertainty under P7 and carries its calibration and\n  control-experiment qualifications. Efficiency, dead time, misalignment,\n  background and drift each require their own declared model.\n\n- **No transfer without a selection model.** If the recorded sample is\n  selected by a rule that depends on the settings or on the observed outcomes,\n  the selection-closure lemma does not apply, and no claim is made here that\n  the CHSH bound constrains the recorded tables. Correspondingly, a deviation\n  computed from recorded pairs is a statement about those recorded tables and\n  their own declared model; it becomes a statement about the full arrangement\n  only through an explicitly justified detection and selection model; the\n  condition above is sufficient, not necessary. Finite-sample fluctuations\n  are a further, separate matter. The result\n  [[texp-nrqm-bell-finite-sample-chsh]] assumes $n$ trials per setting pair,\n  all $4n$ outcome pairs mutually independent, with each trial law equal to\n  the arrangement's table $\\nu^{\\rho}_{ij}$. It applies to recorded pairs\n  only when their declared sampling model satisfies those hypotheses. A\n  postselected law different from $\\nu^{\\rho}_{ij}$, random recorded trial\n  counts or correlations induced by selection require a separate analysis;\n  the cited result does not supply one. The singlet contrast itself is the\n  conditional model computation of\n  [[texp-nrqm-bell-singlet-arrangement-contrast]].\n\n- **Settings choice.** In a specified experiment model, let $Q_{ij}$ be the\n  actual conditional probability law of the specification given that setting\n  pair $(i,j)$ is chosen whenever that conditional law is defined. The\n  common-law condition required by a local-response representation is that\n  these actual conditional laws are all one common law,\n  $Q_{00}=Q_{01}=Q_{10}=Q_{11}$ ([[def-nrqm-bell-local-response-representation]]\n  clause (c)); if a setting pair is never chosen, its law must still be\n  specified by the comparison model before the four-table representation is\n  applied. The possibility that a setting procedure could depend on the\n  specification does not establish that the actual conditional laws differ;\n  an analysis using a local-response representation must establish their\n  equality from its model and the relevant evidence. This page neither\n  assumes nor reports such a setting procedure or its conditional laws.\n",
research/plan-spec.json:66359:          "statement": "## Remarks\n\n- **What the page contains.** Three conditional computations in the declared\n  framework: the worked comparison at the specified directions between the\n  singlet arrangement and the EPR-type product mixture, with both CHSH values\n  and an explicit local-response representation of the mixture\n  ([[ex-nrqm-bell-worked-angles-and-product-mixture]]); the finite-sample\n  behaviour of the sample CHSH functional under a declared independent and\n  identically distributed sampling model, with its concentration inequality\n  and positive failure probabilities\n  ([[texp-nrqm-bell-finite-sample-chsh]]); and the selection and loss\n  qualification, with the precise hypothesis under which the CHSH bound\n  transfers to recorded pairs\n  ([[rem-nrqm-bell-selection-loss-and-fair-sampling]]).\n\n- **What the page does not contain.** No experiment item, no reported count,\n  no calibration, no detector efficiency, no loss fraction, no background\n  model, no trial-level uncertainty analysis, no p-value and no confidence\n  level. The worked tables, the sample functional and the failure\n  probabilities are probabilities of the declared models; no number on the\n  page is an observation. The identification of any apparatus with the\n  declared measurements and of any repetition procedure with the declared\n  sampling model is a tested identification with uncertainty under P7\n  ([[post-nrqm-framework-preparation-and-apparatus]]).\n\n- **Deferred Bell-test experiment destination.** The design of the pair\n  reserves an experiment item for a Bell test. It is not built here because\n  none of the sources read for the pair supplies the required trial-level\n  material: a primary or authoritative full report read directly, with the\n  source-specific preparation and apparatus, calibration, measured outputs,\n  trial count where reported, detection efficiency, losses and\n  post-selection, background, the reported statistical treatment and\n  systematics, and the source's omissions. The experimental remarks of the\n  secondary sources — Binney's reference to the 1972 Freedman-Clauser test\n  and its citation of a 1998 photonic test, and the MIT chapter's statement\n  that experiments have confirmed violations — motivate the comparison only\n  and are not used as measured records; no numerical value from them is\n  reproduced. The statistics and primary-report gap named in the design is\n  recorded as an open destination.\n\n- **Why a null or shifted sample is not a verdict.** The mixture's tables have\n  CHSH value $-\\sqrt2$ in absolute value, strictly below both the classical\n  bound $2$ and the singlet's $2\\sqrt2$\n  ([[ex-nrqm-bell-worked-angles-and-product-mixture]]), and the finite-sample\n  analysis bounds the probability that a model's sample functional crosses\n  either threshold by a strictly positive quantity\n  ([[texp-nrqm-bell-finite-sample-chsh]]). A finite sample therefore does not\n  by itself decide between the declared models; a comparison requires the\n  full battery of preparation, detection, selection and sampling assumptions,\n  and agreement would be evidence about the combined assumptions, not a proof\n  of the framework.\n\n- **Model contrast is not an interpretation.** The page compares the tables of\n  two declared arrangements and the class of local-response models of those\n  tables ([[thm-nrqm-bell-local-representations-satisfy-chsh]]); it makes no\n  claim about the completeness of quantum mechanics, about hidden variables in\n  general, about mechanisms or about any interpretation of the violation. The\n  boundaries of the comparison are recorded on the pair's first page.\n",
research/plan-spec.json:67208:          "statement": "**(a) Framework data.** Adopt the state and composition postulate P1, the\nobservable and probability postulate P2, the autonomous-dynamics postulate P4,\nthe kinematics and massive-particle model postulate P5, the identical-particle\nand spin postulate P6, and the preparation and apparatus discipline P7\n([[post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-observable-and-probability]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-autonomous-dynamics]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-nonrelativistic-kinematics-and-massive-particle]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-identical-particles-and-spin]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-preparation-and-apparatus]]). Fix a mass parameter\n$m>0$ and a spin label $s=0$, so that the framework's one-particle model\n[[def-nrqm-kinematics-framework-massive-spin-s-model]] reads its state space as\n$H_{0}=L^{2}(\\mathbb R^{3};\\mathbb C)$ with the pairing linear in the first\nargument, its componentwise position family $Q_{1},Q_{2},Q_{3}$, its\ncomponentwise momentum family $P_{1},P_{2},P_{3}$ and the action constant\n$\\hbar>0$; the spin-zero internal factor carries the trivial representation of\nP6. Units are those of [[def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units]]: position is\nread in metres, momentum in $\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$, energy and potential\nenergy in joules, and $\\hbar$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,s}$. Assume the Axiom of\nCountable Choice and the Axiom of Choice as the choice hypotheses of the cited\nmathematical suppliers ([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]]).\n\n**(b) The declared central-potential model.** As an additional model\nassumption of the pair, adopt the **central-potential model**. Its declared\nstate space is $H_{0}=L^{2}(\\mathbb R^{3};\\mathbb C)$ of clause (a), with the\nposition and momentum families $Q_{j}$, $P_{k}$ of that clause\n([[def-nrqm-kinematics-framework-massive-spin-s-model]]). A **declared central\npotential** of the model is a function $V:\\mathbb R^{3}\\to\\mathbb R$ of the\nform $V(x):=v(|x|)$ for $x\\ne0$, where $v:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is real,\nbounded and measurable and $\\|V\\|_{\\infty}:=\\|v\\|_{\\infty}=\\sup_{r>0}|v(r)|$ is\nfinite; the assignment $V(0):=0$ is a declared convention that does not affect\nthe $L^{2}$ theory. The potential energy is read in joules. The model declares\nthe **Born position law** of the framework for a unit class $\\psi$,\n$$\\mu^{G}_{\\psi}(B):=\\int_{B}|\\psi(x)|^{2}\\,dx\\qquad(B\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3}\\ \\text{Borel}),$$\nthe spin-zero reading of clause (b) of\n[[pthm-nrqm-kinematics-spin-s-outcome-laws]]. The pair asserts no derivation\nof a central force law from $H_{0}$ or from P5: the restriction to a declared\ncentral potential is an assumption of the model, and an assignment of a\nconcrete material system to the model is an identification in the sense of\nclause (iii) of P7 and carries the corresponding uncertainty.\n\n**(c) The assigned energy operator.** Let $\\mathcal F_{2}$ be the unitary\nPlancherel transform of\n[[thm-nrqm-composites-real-multipliers-and-free-bounded-potential-operators]]\nclause (b), $c:=2\\pi^{2}\\hbar^{2}/m$, and let\n$$H_{0}^{\\mathrm{free}}:=\\mathcal F_{2}^{*}M_{c|\\xi|^{2}}\\mathcal F_{2},\\qquad D(H_{0}^{\\mathrm{free}}):=\\bigl\\{f\\in H_{0}:|\\xi|^{2}\\mathcal F_{2}f\\in H_{0}\\bigr\\}$$\nbe the free operator of that clause with $d=3$ and $b=0$. With $M_{V}$ the\nbounded multiplication operator of clause (a) of the same theorem, the\n**assigned energy operator** of the model with central potential $V$ is\n$$H_{V}:=H_{0}^{\\mathrm{free}}+M_{V},\\qquad D(H_{V}):=D(H_{0}^{\\mathrm{free}}).$$\nThe declared energy unit is the joule. By clause (c) of the cited theorem\n$H_{V}$ is self-adjoint on $D(H_{V})$, bounded below by $-\\|V\\|_{\\infty}$; by\n[[lem-nrqm-central-free-operator-differential-identification]] the domain is\nthe Sobolev space $W^{2,2}(\\mathbb R^{3};\\mathbb C)$ and\n$$H_{V}f=-\\frac{\\hbar^{2}}{2m}\\Delta_{w}f+Vf\\qquad(f\\in D(H_{V})),$$\n$\\Delta_{w}=D_{1}^{2}+D_{2}^{2}+D_{3}^{2}$ the weak Laplacian of that lemma. If\n$\\psi\\in D(H_{V})\\setminus\\{0\\}$ and $E\\in\\mathbb R$ satisfy\n$H_{V}\\psi=E\\psi$, the time-independent equation\n$-\\frac{\\hbar^{2}}{2m}\\Delta_{w}\\psi+V\\psi=E\\psi$ is an equality in\n$L^{2}(\\mathbb R^{3};\\mathbb C)$ and hence in distributions; it is a partial\ndifferential equation in the three spatial variables. If $V\\in C^{2}(\\Omega)$\non an open set $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3}$, then the class $\\psi$ has a\nrepresentative in $C^{2,\\alpha}_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\Omega)$ for every\n$0<\\alpha<1/2$, and the equation holds pointwise there. To justify this,\nwrite $f:=(2m/\\hbar^{2})(E-V)\\psi$, so $-\\Delta\\psi=f$ in distributions.\nOn every relatively compact open subset of $\\Omega$, the weak product rule\n(obtained by local smooth mollification of the $C^{2}$ factor and passage to\nthe $L^{2}$ limit) gives, for every multi-index $\\beta$ with $|\\beta|\\le2$,\n$$D^{\\beta}f=\\frac{2m}{\\hbar^{2}}\\sum_{\\eta\\le\\beta}\\binom{\\beta}{\\eta}D^{\\eta}(E-V)D^{\\beta-\\eta}\\psi\\in L^{2};$$\nthe factors $D^{\\eta}(E-V)$ are bounded on compact subsets and\n$\\psi\\in W^{2,2}(\\mathbb R^{3})$, so $f\\in H^{2}_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\Omega)$.\nIntegration by parts against compactly supported smooth test functions makes\n$\\psi\\in H^{1}(\\Omega)$ a local weak solution in the sense of\n[[def-local-weak-solution-for-a-divergence-form-operator]] for\n$L=-D_i(\\delta_{ij}D_j)=-\\Delta$, whose divergence coefficients are\n$a^{ij}=\\delta_{ij}$ and $b^{i}=c=0$. They satisfy the uniform ellipticity\ncondition of [[def-uniformly-elliptic-divergence-form-operator]] with\n$\\theta=1$ and have the required local coefficient derivatives of every\norder. The interior regularity theorem\n[[thm-interior-h-k-plus-two-elliptic-regularity]] with $k=2$ therefore gives\n$\\psi\\in H^{4}_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\Omega)$. Every ball compactly contained in\n$\\Omega$ is a bounded smooth domain and hence a $W^{4,2}$ extension domain by\n[[thm-extension-theorem-for-bounded-smooth-domains]]. The higher-order Sobolev\nembedding [[thm-higher-order-sobolev-embedding]] with $n=3$, $k=4$, $p=2$\nthen gives a representative with continuous derivatives through order two and\nlocally Hölder-continuous second derivatives of every exponent\n$0<\\alpha<1/2$; this is the meaning of\n$C^{2,\\alpha}_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\Omega)$ here. These representatives agree on\noverlaps because they are continuous and represent the same almost-everywhere\nclass. Both sides of the equation are then continuous and agree almost\neverywhere, so they agree pointwise. The model adopts\n$(H_{V},\\mathrm J)$ as its declared energy observable; its self-adjointness is\nthe cited theorem's, and no further spectral property is presupposed here.\n\n**(d) The declared spherical reading.** Let $U$ be the unitary of\n[[thm-nrqm-coulomb-angular-radial-unitary-reduction]] from\n$H_{0}$ onto $K=\\bigoplus_{(l,\\bar m)}L^{2}((0,\\infty),dr;\\mathbb C)$, written\n$U\\psi=(u_{l\\bar m})_{l\\ge0,-l\\le\\bar m\\le l}$, and let $(Y_{l\\bar m})$ be the complete\northonormal family of that theorem; $l,\\bar m$ are integers, while $m>0$\ncontinues to denote the mass. For a fixed $(l,\\bar m)$, let\n$\\iota_{l\\bar m}:L^{2}((0,\\infty),dr;\\mathbb C)\\to K$ insert its argument\nin that coordinate and zero in every other coordinate, and define the\nisometric **spherical lift** $J_{l\\bar m}:=U^{-1}\\iota_{l\\bar m}$, so that\n$$J_{l\\bar m}u(x)=\\frac{u(r)}{r}Y_{l\\bar m}(\\Omega)\\qquad(x=r\\Omega,\\ r>0).$$\nThe **spherical sector** $(l,\\bar m)$ is the range of this lift. Its\n**admissible reduced domain** is fixed by the assigned energy domain:\n$$\\mathcal D_{l\\bar m}:=\\{u\\in L^{2}((0,\\infty),dr;\\mathbb C):J_{l\\bar m}u\\in W^{2,2}(\\mathbb R^{3};\\mathbb C)\\}.$$\nThus every weak Cartesian derivative through order two of the lift must\nbelong to $L^{2}$ on all of $\\mathbb R^{3}$, including neighbourhoods of the\norigin. This condition and the global square integrability specify\nadmissibility at both endpoints; no independent half-line boundary parameter\nis part of the model. In particular $C_c^\\infty((0,\\infty);\\mathbb C)\n\\subseteq\\mathcal D_{l\\bar m}$ by clause (d) of the cited theorem. The\n**effective potential** is\n$$v^{\\mathrm{eff}}_{l}(r):=v(r)+\\frac{\\hbar^{2}l(l+1)}{2mr^{2}}\\qquad(r>0),$$\nwhose second summand is called the **angular-momentum barrier**; both\nsummands, and $v^{\\mathrm{eff}}_{l}$, are read in joules. For $E\\in\\mathbb R$,\nthe model's **declared radial equation** in the sector $(l,\\bar m)$ is\n$$u\\in\\mathcal D_{l\\bar m},\\qquad H_VJ_{l\\bar m}u=EJ_{l\\bar m}u\\quad\\text{in }L^{2}(\\mathbb R^{3};\\mathbb C).$$\nA **radial eigenpair** additionally requires $u\\ne0$. The associated\none-dimensional differential expression is\n$$u\\longmapsto-\\frac{\\hbar^{2}}{2m}u''+\\Bigl(v(r)+\\frac{\\hbar^{2}l(l+1)}{2mr^{2}}\\Bigr)u,\\qquad u:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb C,$$\nwhere $u''$ means the distributional second derivative on $(0,\\infty)$\n([[def-weak-derivative-of-a-locally-integrable-function]]). The expression\nalone, without the specified lifted domain, does not define a radial\neigenvalue problem. On $C_c^\\infty((0,\\infty);\\mathbb C)$, its equation\nwith right-hand side $Eu$ is equivalent almost everywhere to the declared\nradial equation above by the spherical-reduction theorem of this page;\npointwise equivalence additionally requires continuity of $v$. No full-domain\ndifferential reduction or endpoint classification is presupposed here.\nThe angular-momentum quantum numbers $l$ and $\\bar m$\nappearing in the reading are labels of the orthonormal family $(Y_{l\\bar m})$; the\nidentification of $l$ with a total angular momentum of a material system is a\nseparate assignment of the framework's angular-momentum items and is not made\nhere.\n\n**(e) Stationary and bound-state vocabulary.** A **stationary vector** of the\nmodel with potential $V$ and **energy** $E\\in\\mathbb R$ is a nonzero class\n$\\psi\\in D(H_{V})$ with $H_{V}\\psi=E\\psi$; the pair $(\\psi,E)$ is an\n**eigenpair** of the model. A **bound state** of the model is a stationary\nvector with energy $E<0$ whose representative is normalised, $\\|\\psi\\|=1$; its\neigenvalue is its **binding energy**, read in joules, relative to the\nchosen **energy reference** $0$. Here $E<0$ is a vocabulary convention;\nno assertion that $0$ is a spectral continuum threshold follows for an\narbitrary bounded central potential. A continuum threshold requires a\nseparate spectral determination for the particular operator. In particular,\nadding a constant potential shifts the free operator and its spectrum by\nthat constant without changing its eigenvectors. The model reads the energy\noutcome law of the pure preparation of a unit eigenvector with eigenvalue $E$\nas the point mass $\\delta_{E}$, the reading of clause (c) of\n[[def-nrqm-observables-framework-observable-and-outcome-law]], and the\nposition density of that preparation as $|\\psi|^{2}$ by\n[[pthm-nrqm-kinematics-spin-s-outcome-laws]] clause (b), with $s=0$\nand the state, position and choice hypotheses of (a). These are\ndeclarations of the model under P2; they construct no sample, detector or\nmeasured value, and they assert no stability, decay or formation claim.\n\n**(f) Status and scope.** Clauses (a)-(e) fix the data of the pair: a declared\nstate space, position and momentum assignments, an assigned energy operator\nfor a real bounded measurable central potential, a declared spherical reading\nwith its effective potential and radial equation, and a stationary-state and\nbound-state vocabulary. They adopt P1, P2, P4, P5, P6, P7, the choice hypotheses of the cited\nmathematics, and the additional central-potential model assumption and\nspherical reading of clauses (b) and (d); in particular the central form of the\npotential, the model's use of the full three-dimensional space as its state\nspace and the identification of the spherical sectors with angular-momentum\nlabels are declarations of the pair. The model excludes from its scope, until\ntheir own items introduce them: the Coulomb potential $\\propto-1/r$ and other\nunbounded or singular potentials; spin and magnetic couplings; relativistic\ncorrections; finite nuclear size; the radiation extension, spontaneous\nemission and radiation reaction; external electromagnetic fields; and any\nmany-body or shielding modification of a central potential. No numerical value\nof $m$, $\\hbar$ or of any potential is fixed here, and no empirical result is\nasserted.",
research/plan-spec.json:67226:            "thm-higher-order-sobolev-embedding",
research/plan-spec.json:67252:            "thm-higher-order-sobolev-embedding": "mathematical-premise",
research/plan-spec.json:67282:                "title": "J. K. Hunter, Notes on Partial Differential Equations, Section 4.11, Theorem 4.28, printed page 114 (higher interior Sobolev regularity and the C^2 consequence when k>n/2)",
research/plan-spec.json:68296:                "title": "G. Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 1st edition 2009, Section 9.7 printed pages 214-220 (PDF pages 226-232): the spectra and relative bounds and spectra of one-dimensional Schrodinger operators (background only; no Airy connection or barrier-transmission theorem)",
research/plan-spec.json:68861:          "statement": "Adopt P1, P2, P4 and P6 for the massive spin-$s$ particle model of\n[[def-nrqm-kinematics-framework-massive-spin-s-model]]\n([[post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-observable-and-probability]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-autonomous-dynamics]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-identical-particles-and-spin]]), together with the\neffective magnetic-moment coupling\n[[post-nrqm-angular-momentum-magnetic-moment-coupling]] with real\ngyromagnetic parameter $\\gamma:=\\gamma_{1}$ and the spin-one internal factor\n$\\mathbb C^{3}$ with spin operators $S_i=\\hbar J_i$ of\n[[prop-nrqm-orbital-and-spin-finite-spin-matrices]] at $s=1$; read energies in\njoules as in [[def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units]]. Declare the\n**finite-level Zeeman model** by the perturbed-family model of\n[[def-nrqm-approximations-framework-perturbed-family-model]] with\n$$\\mathfrak H:=\\mathbb C^{3},\\qquad H_{0}:=\\varepsilon I_{3},\\qquad V:=-\\gamma B_{z}S_{3},$$\nwhere $\\varepsilon\\in\\mathbb R$ is a declared multiplet energy, $B_{z}>0$ is\nthe field magnitude in tesla of the uniform field $\\mathbf B=(0,0,B_{z})$,\nso that the family is $H(\\lambda)=H_{0}+\\lambda V$ with real coupling\n$\\lambda$ (dimensionless, equal to $1$ for the physical field) and\n$v=\\|V\\|=|\\gamma|B_{z}\\hbar$. For the physical value $\\lambda=1$ the family is\nthe model's Zeeman operator on the internal multiplet. Then:\n\n(a) the perturbation preserves the multiplet: $[H_{0},V]=0$, the\nfirst-order operator is $V_{E}=V$, and its eigenvalues counted with\nmultiplicity are $-\\hbar\\gamma B_{z},0,+\\hbar\\gamma B_{z}$ in joules. If\n$\\gamma\\ne0$, these are three distinct simple eigenvalues; if $\\gamma=0$,\nthe only eigenvalue is $0$ with multiplicity three. Thus the first-order\noperator describes a triply degenerate level when the adopted magnetic\ncoupling vanishes;\n\n(b) the exact energy eigenvalues of the family, counted with multiplicity,\nare\n$$\\varepsilon-\\lambda\\hbar\\gamma B_{z},\\qquad \\varepsilon,\\qquad \\varepsilon+\\lambda\\hbar\\gamma B_{z}.$$\nThey are three distinct simple levels exactly when $\\lambda\\gamma\\ne0$;\nwhen $\\lambda=0$ or $\\gamma=0$, the only energy is $\\varepsilon$ with\nmultiplicity three. Each eigenvalue is affine in $\\lambda$, so its\nsecond-order and higher Taylor coefficients vanish identically;\n\n(c) when $v>0$, the page's degenerate theorem applies for every $g>0$ (the\ngap condition is vacuous because the spectrum of $H_{0}$ is the single value\n$\\varepsilon$) and every real $\\lambda$ with $|\\lambda|v<g/4$; each\neigenvalue $z$ then satisfies\n$\\operatorname{dist}(z,\\varepsilon+\\lambda\\operatorname{spec}(V_{E}))\n\\le 4\\lambda^{2}v^{2}/g$, and the exact spectrum in (b) shows that this\ndistance is $0$. If $v=0$, equivalently $\\gamma=0$, then\n$H(\\lambda)=\\varepsilon I_{3}$ for every real $\\lambda$, so the same\ndistance is $0$ directly, without invoking the theorem's positive-norm\nhypothesis. In both cases the first-order eigenvalue set is exact, with\nmultiplicity retained as in (a)-(b);\n\n(d) for a unit preparation vector $u\\in\\mathbb C^3$, put\n$u(t)=e^{-itH(\\lambda)/\\hbar}u$ and $s_i(t)=\\langle S_i u(t),u(t)\\rangle$.\nWith $\\omega=\\lambda\\gamma B_z$ (in $\\mathrm{s^{-1}}$),\n$$s_3(t)=s_3(0),\\qquad s_1(t)=\\cos(\\omega t)s_1(0)+\\sin(\\omega t)s_2(0),\\qquad s_2(t)=\\cos(\\omega t)s_2(0)-\\sin(\\omega t)s_1(0).$$\nThus the transverse means rotate by $-\\omega t$, with the signed Larmor\nparameter $\\omega$ equal to $\\gamma B_z$ at $\\lambda=1$;\n\n(e) no branch selection: the energy eigenspaces of (b) are spectral data of\nthe family; a preparation in any eigenstate is stationary, and a superposition\nevolves with the relative phases of (b). The example asserts\nno instantaneous transition of any preparation to the lowest sublevel, no\nrelaxation and no measurement; which sublevel a preparation occupies is\nfixed by the preparation and by P4.",
research/plan-spec.json:70368:          "statement": "**(a) Framework data.** Adopt the state and composition postulate P1, the\nobservable and probability postulate P2, the autonomous-dynamics postulate P4\nand the preparation and apparatus discipline P7\n([[post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-observable-and-probability]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-autonomous-dynamics]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-preparation-and-apparatus]]), with the short-range\nscattering model of\n[[def-nrqm-scattering-framework-short-range-model]]: mass $m>0$, spin zero,\nstate space $H_{0}=L^{2}(\\mathbb R^{3};\\mathbb C)$, a declared short-range\npotential $V$, the free and interacting assigned energy observables\n$H^{\\mathrm{free}}$ and $H$, its reading of the flow by\n[[def-nrqm-autonomous-dynamics-framework-evolution]], and the units of\n[[def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units]]. Assume the Axiom of Countable\nChoice and the Axiom of Choice ([[def-countable-choice]],\n[[def-axiom-of-choice]]).\n\n**(b) Incident plane-wave reading and declared density scale.** As an\nadditional model assumption of the pair, adopt the **stationary elastic beam\nmodel**. Fix a **wave number** $k>0$ in $\\mathrm{m^{-1}}$, a **direction**\n$d\\in S^2$, and an **incident number-density scale** $n_{\\mathrm{inc}}>0$ in\n$\\mathrm{m^{-3}}$. This scale is an explicitly declared model normalization;\nno numerical sample density or measured beam value is supplied. Retain the\nunit-amplitude, dimensionless incident shape\n$$u_{\\mathrm{inc}}(x):=e^{ikd\\cdot x},$$\nwith energy reading $E:=\\hbar^2k^2/(2m)$ in joules. The **physical incident\nbeam field** is $\\Phi_{\\mathrm{inc}}:=\\sqrt{n_{\\mathrm{inc}}}\\,u_{\\mathrm{inc}}$,\nwith unit $\\mathrm{m^{-3/2}}$ and constant model number density\n$|\\Phi_{\\mathrm{inc}}|^2=n_{\\mathrm{inc}}$. For a dimensionless unit-amplitude\nshape $\\psi$, write $\\mathbf j^{\\mathrm{unit}}[\\psi]:=(\\hbar/m)\\operatorname{Im}(\\overline\\psi\\nabla\\psi)$ for the mathematical current of\n[[lem-nrqm-scattering-three-dimensional-current-and-flux]]. Its incident\nvalue is $(\\hbar k/m)d$, with velocity unit $\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$. The model's\n**physical number-current density** is the current of\n$\\Phi=\\sqrt{n_{\\mathrm{inc}}}\\psi$,\n$$\\mathbf j[\\Phi]:=\\frac{\\hbar}{m}\\operatorname{Im}(\\overline\\Phi\\nabla\\Phi)=n_{\\mathrm{inc}}\\mathbf j^{\\mathrm{unit}}[\\psi].$$\nThus the **incident current density** and **incident flux density** through a\nplane normal to $d$ are\n$$\\mathbf j_{\\mathrm{inc}}=n_{\\mathrm{inc}}\\frac{\\hbar k}{m}d,\\qquad J_{\\mathrm{inc}}:=n_{\\mathrm{inc}}\\frac{\\hbar k}{m}>0,$$\nboth in $\\mathrm{m^{-2}s^{-1}}$ as in\n[[def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units]] clause (d)5. The mathematical\nplane-wave current of the cited lemma remains the unit-shape computation;\nthe density factor supplies the declared physical beam normalization.\n\n**(c) Scattered-wave reading and generalized stationary solution.** For a\ndeclared short-range potential $V$ the model declares a **generalized\nstationary solution** at energy $E$ and direction $d$ to be a function\n$\\Psi:\\mathbb R^{3}\\to\\mathbb C$ in $H^2_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\mathbb R^3)$,\nmeaning that all weak derivatives of order at most two are locally square\nintegrable ([[def-sobolev-space-wkp-and-its-norm]]), with $V\\Psi\\in L^2_{\\mathrm{loc}}$,\nof class $C^{2}$ on the complement of the essential closed support of $V$,\nand satisfying the equation on all of $\\mathbb R^3$ distributionally:\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\Psi(x)\\left[-\\frac{\\hbar^2}{2m}\\Delta\\eta(x)+(V(x)-E)\\eta(x)\\right]dx=0\\qquad(\\eta\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C)).$$\nHere the essential closed support is the complement of the union of open\nsets on which $V=0$ almost everywhere; the test functions are smooth and\ncompactly supported; $\\Delta=\\sum_{i=0}^{2}\\partial_i^2$ uses the spatial\ncoordinate convention of the current lemma. Outside the essential closed\nsupport, $V=0$ almost everywhere and $\\Psi$ is $C^2$, so the continuous\nfree-equation residual has zero test pairing and hence vanishes pointwise:\n$-\\frac{\\hbar^2}{2m}\\Delta\\Psi=E\\Psi$. The equation containing the declared\nrepresentative $V(x)$ holds almost everywhere there; it holds pointwise there\nwhen $V$ is replaced by its zero representative on this exterior open set.\nNo pointwise equation with arbitrary null-set values of $V$ is asserted.\nThe solution has the **outgoing asymptotic reading**\n$$\\Psi(x)=e^{ikd\\cdot x}+\\frac{f_{k}(\\Omega)}{r}e^{ikr}+\\mathcal E(x),\\qquad x=r\\Omega,\\ r\\to\\infty,$$\nwith a **scattering amplitude** function $f_k:S^2\\to\\mathbb C$, read in\nmetres relative to the unit incident shape, and a physical beam field\n$\\Phi:=\\sqrt{n_{\\mathrm{inc}}}\\Psi$ with unit $\\mathrm{m^{-3/2}}$. The same\nconstant factor multiplies its incident, outgoing and remainder terms;\n$f_k$ remains the amplitude relative to the incident field, in metres.\nHere $\\Omega:=x/r$ is the **outgoing unit direction**. Require\n$f_k\\in C^1(S^2)$ in the radial-extension convention of\n[[lem-nrqm-scattering-three-dimensional-current-and-flux]] (c), and require\n$|\\mathcal E(r,\\Omega)|+|\\partial_r\\mathcal E(r,\\Omega)|\\le Cr^{-2}$\nuniformly in $\\Omega$ for all sufficiently large $r$, with some finite $C$.\nFor the bounded compact-support class the model selects the outgoing\nintegral solution of\n[[thm-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-stationary-outgoing-integral-and-flux]],\nwith its potential parameter replaced by $W_x=2mV/\\hbar^2$. Explicitly,\nchoose $R>0$ enclosing the essential support, put\n$G_k(z)=e^{ik|z|}/(4\\pi|z|)$ for $z\\ne0$, and on $L^2(B_R;\\mathbb C)$ put\n$K_ku(x)=\\int_{B_R}G_k(x-y)W_x(y)u(y)dy$. Select\n$u=(I+K_k)^{-1}(u_{\\mathrm{inc}}|_{B_R})$ and\n$\\Psi(x)=u_{\\mathrm{inc}}(x)-\\int_{B_R}G_k(x-y)W_x(y)u(y)dy$,\nusing the locally continuous representative supplied by $H^2_{\\mathrm{loc}}$.\nThe cited theorem proves invertibility and the global equation, with a smooth\namplitude and these uniform remainder bounds. The selection is independent\nof enlarging $R$: the restriction of a solution constructed in a larger ball\nsolves the smaller invertible integral equation, and $W_x=0$ outside the\nsmaller ball makes both global integrals identical. Hence the compact-support\nmodel determines $\\Psi$ and $f_k$ from $V,k,d$; arbitrary exterior fields\nand arbitrary interior extensions are excluded. For the general resolvent-tail\nclass these requirements define a conditional reading only, without an\nexistence or uniqueness assertion.\nThe **scattered physical field** is\n$\\Phi_{\\mathrm{sc}}:=\\sqrt{n_{\\mathrm{inc}}}(\\Psi-u_{\\mathrm{inc}})$.\nThe physical scattered radial current density, obtained by applying the\nquadratic current formula to this scattered component, is\n$$j_r^{\\mathrm{sc}}(r,\\Omega)=n_{\\mathrm{inc}}\\left(\\frac{\\hbar k}{m}\\frac{|f_k(\\Omega)|^2}{r^2}+O(r^{-3})\\right)\\qquad(r\\to\\infty),$$\nin $\\mathrm{m^{-2}s^{-1}}$ for the fixed declared density scale. The leading\nunit-shape current is clause (c) of\n[[lem-nrqm-scattering-three-dimensional-current-and-flux]]; the current of\nthe remainder is controlled by the stationary theorem. The definition uses\nthe scattered component, with no assumption that incident/scattered\ninterference in the total field vanishes.\nA generalized stationary solution is generally not an element of $H_{0}$; it\nis not a preparation of the model, and clause (d) of\n[[def-nrqm-scattering-framework-short-range-model]] does not apply to it. It\nis used only through the declared flux quantities computed from it, and every\nsuch use states the idealization.\n\n**(d) Differential and total cross-section readings.** Under the declared\n**equal-channel elastic flux assumptions** -- that the incident and outgoing\nreadings refer to the same elastic channel at the same energy, that the\nincident flux density is spatially uniform over the transverse extent of the\ntarget and the outgoing current is read far outside the support of $V$ -- the\nmodel declares the **differential cross section**\n$$\\frac{d\\sigma}{d\\Omega}(\\Omega):=\\lim_{r\\to\\infty}\\frac{r^{2}\\,j^{\\mathrm{sc}}_{r}(r,\\Omega)}{J_{\\mathrm{inc}}} =|f_{k}(\\Omega)|^{2},$$\nread in $\\mathrm{m^{2}\\,sr^{-1}}$, and the **total cross section**\n$$\\sigma:=\\int_{S^{2}}\\frac{d\\sigma}{d\\Omega}(\\Omega)\\,d\\Omega =\\int_{S^{2}}|f_{k}(\\Omega)|^{2}\\,d\\Omega,$$\nread in $\\mathrm{m^{2}}$, whenever the displayed limit and integral exist; the\nexistence and finiteness of both are proved on this page for the bounded\ncompact-support class at $k>0$. No stationary existence or finite\ncross-section result is asserted here for the general resolvent-tail class. The common positive density factor cancels in the ratio,\n$r^2j_r^{\\mathrm{sc}}/J_{\\mathrm{inc}}\\to |f_k|^2$, so the cross sections are\nindependent of the declared $n_{\\mathrm{inc}}$. This normalization supplies\nno relation to the energy outcome law of P2 and no assertion that an $L^2$\npreparation realizes either stationary field.\n\n**(e) Status and scope.** Clauses (b)-(d) are declarations of the pair under\nP1, P2, P4 and P7 together with the short-range model; they are an idealized\nstationary reading and not a model of an apparatus. The new density scale is\na symbolic normalization parameter of that reading, not a measured sample\nvalue; multiplication by $\\sqrt{n_{\\mathrm{inc}}}$ does not turn the\nstationary shape into an $L^2$ preparation. In particular no beam\nsource, target density, collimation, detector acceptance, efficiency,\nbackground or counting procedure appears, and the symbols\n$d\\sigma/d\\Omega$ and $\\sigma$ denote model quantities computed from the\ndeclared solution. An identification of a measured count cross section with\nthese quantities requires a separate preparation and detection model and\ncarries the uncertainty of that identification.",
research/plan-spec.json:71150:          "statement": "**What the page establishes, and what it does not.**\n\n- *Model deductions.* The page proves, conditionally on the declared\n  short-range model and the cited mathematics, the existence of the wave\n  operators, their isometry and intertwining\n  ([[pthm-nrqm-scattering-wave-operator-existence-and-free-continuity]]), the\n  asymptotic completeness and unitarity of the scattering operator for the\n  compact-support and resolvent-tail classes\n  ([[pthm-nrqm-scattering-compact-support-asymptotic-completeness]],\n  [[pthm-nrqm-scattering-resolvent-tail-asymptotic-completeness]]), the\n  stationary outgoing solution with its amplitude and the cross-section\n  readings ([[pthm-nrqm-scattering-stationary-wave-amplitude-and-flux]]), the controlled\n  Born approximation with its operator-norm condition\n  ([[pthm-nrqm-scattering-controlled-born-approximation]]), the phase-shift\n  cross-section series ([[pthm-nrqm-scattering-partial-wave-cross-section]]),\n  the on-shell operator identification\n  ([[pthm-nrqm-scattering-stationary-dynamical-identification]]), and the\n  conditional resonance-model algebra\n  ([[pthm-nrqm-scattering-single-channel-resonance-cross-section]]). Every one of these is a logical\n  consequence of the stated postulates, model declarations, hypotheses and\n  mathematics; none of them is an observation.\n- *No observations.* The page reports no experiment: it contains no apparatus,\n  no calibration, no sample, no count, no uncertainty budget and no measured\n  cross section. Nothing on the page should be read as a measurement of $V$,\n  $\\kappa$, a phase shift or a resonance parameter.\n- *What a comparison would require.* To compare the model quantities\n  $d\\sigma/d\\Omega$ or $\\sigma$ with data one needs a declared target\n  preparation (target density and composition), an incident fluence with its\n  own uncertainty, a stated scattering geometry, a detector acceptance and\n  efficiency model, and a background model, together with a declared\n  statistical model for the counts. The equal-channel elastic flux assumptions\n  of the beam model ([[def-nrqm-scattering-stationary-beam-and-cross-section-model]]\n  clause (d)) must be justified for the particular apparatus; inelastic or\n  multichannel processes lie outside the model and would require separate\n  channels.\n- *Interpretation.* Agreement of a measured count rate with the model reading\n  is evidence for the combined model-plus-apparatus assumptions; it is not a\n  deduction that the declared model, its potential class or the framework\n  describes nature. Disagreement would not by itself falsify the whole\n  framework, and a finite dataset can be compatible with a model without\n  confirming it.\n\n**Scope of the model declarations.** The potential is prescribed, not derived;\nthe Coulomb coupling $\\kappa$ and the resonance parameters $E_{r},\\Gamma$ are\nmodel parameters, not measured constants; and the modified Dollard comparison\nof the Coulomb model is a declared construction whose phase convention changes\nonly asymptotic channel phases\n([[def-nrqm-scattering-coulomb-dollard-model]] clauses (c), (d)).",
research/plan-spec.json:72448:          "statement": "Adopt the canonical equilibrium-ensemble postulate\n[[post-nrqm-many-body-canonical-equilibrium-ensemble]] for the mode models\ndeclared below, the state and probability postulates P1, P2\n([[post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-observable-and-probability]]) in the readings of\n[[def-nrqm-observables-framework-observable-and-outcome-law]] and\n[[pthm-nrqm-observables-preparation-moments]], and the\nidentical-particle and spin postulate P6\n([[post-nrqm-framework-identical-particles-and-spin]]); assume the Axiom of\nCountable Choice and the Axiom of Choice ([[def-countable-choice]],\n[[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Temperatures are assigned as in\n[[def-nrqm-many-body-framework-entropy-and-free-energy]], so that\n$\\beta=1/(k_BT)$ in $\\mathrm J^{-1}$. Declare the occupation readouts as assigned sharp observables $(N,1)$ and $(N_-,1)$, with unit particle-count, which is dimensionless. The total particle-number operator $\\mathcal N$ in the countable-mode reading has the same unit; every occupation mean and higher number moment below is read in the corresponding dimensionless count unit. Then:\n\n(a) *single bosonic mode*: let $F(\\mathbb C)$ be the one-mode Fock space with\nnumber operator $N$ and orthonormal number basis $(e_n)$\n([[thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-fixed-mode-number-law]],\n[[thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-number-operator-domain]]), let\n$\\varepsilon>0$ and let the model declare the energy operator $H:=\\varepsilon N$\non $F(\\mathbb C)$; the model's canonical preparation at temperature $T$ is\n$\\rho_\\beta=e^{-\\beta H}/Z$ with $Z=(1-e^{-\\beta\\varepsilon})^{-1}$, the\nnumber readout $N$ is an assigned sharp observable with finite thermal mean\n$$\\langle N\\rangle_\\beta=\\frac1{e^{\\beta\\varepsilon}-1}=\\frac1{e^{\\varepsilon/(k_BT)}-1},$$\nand all moments $\\langle N^k\\rangle_\\beta$, $k\\ge1$, are finite;\n\n(b) *single fermionic orbital and grand-canonical shift*: on\n$\\mathfrak h=\\mathbb C^2$ with occupation operator $N_-$ and\n$\\varepsilon_1,\\mu\\in\\mathbb R$, the model's canonical (grand-canonical)\npreparation $F=Z^{-1}\\sum_{k=0}^1e^{-\\beta(\\varepsilon_1-\\mu)k}T_{u_k,u_k}$\nhas $Z=1+e^{-\\beta(\\varepsilon_1-\\mu)}$, $\\operatorname{tr}F=1$, and occupation mean\n$$\\langle N_-\\rangle=\\frac1{e^{\\beta(\\varepsilon_1-\\mu)}+1};$$\nthe same formula is the model's thermal mean when $\\mu=0$ and\n$\\varepsilon_1>0$;\n\n(c) *countably many independent modes*: let $(\\varepsilon_j)_{j\\ge1}\\subseteq(0,\\infty)$\nand $\\mu\\in\\mathbb R$, and let the model declare the free-mode energies\n$\\varepsilon_j$ (one mode case of clause (a)) with the product reading of\n[[thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-countable-mode-product-criterion]]; then the\nbosonic product partition is finite exactly when\n$\\sum_je^{-\\beta\\varepsilon_j}<\\infty$ and $\\mu<\\inf_j\\varepsilon_j$, and\nthe fermionic product partition is finite exactly when\n$\\sum_je^{-\\beta\\varepsilon_j}<\\infty$; in the finite cases the products\nequal $\\prod_j(1-e^{-\\beta(\\varepsilon_j-\\mu)})^{-1}$ and\n$\\prod_j(1+e^{-\\beta(\\varepsilon_j-\\mu)})$ respectively;\n\n(d) *dilute remainders*: for $q=e^{-\\beta(\\varepsilon-\\mu)}$ the model's\noccupation means satisfy the exact identities\n$$\\frac q{1-q}=q+\\frac{q^2}{1-q}\\quad(\\text{bosonic},\\ 0<q<1),\\qquad \\frac q{1+q}=q-\\frac{q^2}{1+q}\\quad(\\text{fermionic},\\ q>0),$$\nso both differ from $q$ by at most $q^2/(1-q)$ and $q^2/(1+q)$\nrespectively;\n\n(e) *scope*: clauses (a)-(d) are conditional statements about the declared\nmode models together with the adopted ensemble assignment; they assert no\nthermodynamic limit, no continuum of radiation modes, no Bose-Einstein\ncondensation, no measured occupation number, and no thermalisation of a\nsample. All numerical equalities are exact identities of the declared\nfinite-mode (or product) models at the assigned temperature.",
research/plan-spec.json:73371:          "statement": "**(a) Adopted readings.** Adopt the state and composition postulate P1, the\nobservable and probability postulate P2, the ideal conditional-measurement\npostulate P3, the preparation and apparatus discipline P7\n([[post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-observable-and-probability]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-ideal-conditional-measurement]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-preparation-and-apparatus]]) and the radiation\nextension postulate\n[[post-nrqm-photons-radiation-extension-and-transverse-mode-assignment]] in\nits clause (i). Read the state vocabulary in\n[[def-nrqm-state-geometry-framework-state-space]], the observable vocabulary\nin [[def-nrqm-observables-framework-observable-and-outcome-law]], the\nmeasurement vocabulary in\n[[def-nrqm-measurement-framework-declared-measurements-and-instruments]], and\nthe units of angles in [[def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units]]: angles and\nphases are dimensionless under the radian convention. Assume Countable Choice,\nDependent Choice and Choice ([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-dependent-choice]],\n[[def-axiom-of-choice]]), including the hypotheses inherited from the adopted\nradiation postulate and its Fock-space suppliers.\n\n**(b) Declared fixed-mode data.** A **declared fixed-mode polarisation model**\nconsists of:\n\n1. a nonzero **propagation label** $k_{0}\\in\\mathbb R^{3}\\setminus\\{0\\}$, read\n   in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$ in a declared inertial chart, which is a parameter of the\n   model and not a state;\n2. a declared **transverse frame**, that is a pair $v_j=\\zeta u_j$ for\n   $j\\in\\{1,2\\}$, where $(u_1,u_2)$ is a real orthonormal basis of\n   $k_0^\\perp:=\\{u\\in\\mathbb R^3:k_0\\cdot u=0\\}$ and $\\zeta\\in\\mathbb C$\n   is one common phase factor, $|\\zeta|=1$. Here $k_0\\cdot u$ is the real\n   Euclidean dot product, and the Hermitian form on $\\mathbb C^3$ is\n   $\\langle x,y\\rangle=\\sum_{\\ell=1}^3 x_\\ell\\overline{y_\\ell}$, linear\n   in its first argument; thus $\\langle v_i,v_j\\rangle=\\delta_{ij}$ and\n   $k_0\\cdot v_j:=\\sum_{\\ell=1}^3(k_0)_\\ell(v_j)_\\ell=0$;\n3. the **polarisation space** $V:=\\mathbb Cv_{1}\\oplus\\mathbb Cv_{2}$ with the\n   restricted Hermitian inner product and the orthonormal basis\n   $(e_{1},e_{2}):=(v_{1},v_{2})$.\n\nThe model's **state space** is $D(V)$, the set of density operators of $V$; the\n**pure preparations** of the model are the unit rays $[\\psi]$ of $V$, that is\nthe preparations assigned the pure state $\\widehat\\rho(\\psi)$ of\n[[def-nrqm-state-geometry-pure-mixed-states-and-purity]], and a **mixed\npreparation** is a statistical mixture of preparations in the sense of P1.\nA pure ray is **linearly polarised** when it has a nonzero real representative\nin $k_0^\\perp$. The map\n$\\theta\\mapsto\\psi_{\\theta}:=\\cos\\theta\\,e_{1}+\\sin\\theta\\,e_{2}$ assigns\nto each angle $\\theta\\in\\mathbb R$ a distinguished pure preparation, the\n**linear polarisation preparation at angle $\\theta$** in the declared\ntransverse frame. In particular,\n$\\psi_\\theta=\\zeta(\\cos\\theta\\,u_1+\\sin\\theta\\,u_2)$ has that ray, whose\nreal direction is the unoriented line\n$\\mathbb R(\\cos\\theta\\,u_1+\\sin\\theta\\,u_2)$; hence the physical angle\nlabel is modulo $\\pi$. General complex unit vectors of $V$ remain allowed as\npure preparations and need not be linearly polarised.\n\n**(c) Declared polarisation analyser.** For each angle $\\theta\\in\\mathbb R$ the\n**declared polarisation analyser at angle $\\theta$** is the declared ideal\nsharp measurement of clause (a) of\n[[def-nrqm-measurement-framework-declared-measurements-and-instruments]] with\ntwo outcomes labelled $+1$ and $-1$ and outcome structure\n$$(P_{\\theta},\\,P_{\\theta}^{\\perp}),\\qquad P_{\\theta}x=\\langle x,e_{\\theta}\\rangle e_{\\theta},\\quad e_{\\theta}:=\\cos\\theta\\,e_{1}+\\sin\\theta\\,e_{2},\\qquad P_{\\theta}^{\\perp}:=I-P_{\\theta},$$\nwhose projections $P_{\\theta},P_{\\theta}^{\\perp}$ are those of\n[[lem-nrqm-photons-analyzer-projections-and-overlaps]]. The **outcome law** of\nthe analyser in a preparation $\\rho$ is the reading of P2 for the projections\nof the outcome structure, and the labels are dimensionless with the reading\n\"$+1$: transmitted\", \"$-1$: blocked\". Where a conditional post-measurement\nstate is used, it is the Lueders update of clause (b) of\n[[def-nrqm-measurement-framework-declared-measurements-and-instruments]]; the\nframework does not assert that a laboratory absorber leaves an outgoing\none-photon polarisation state, and the absorption outcome is used only through\nits discarded-outcome law unless a separate instrument is declared (clause (v)\nof P3).\n\n**(d) Frame covariance and angle labels.** For another complex orthonormal transverse pair $(e_1',e_2')$ (not necessarily a transverse frame of clause (b)), let $U:V\\to V$ be the unitary map with $Ue_i=e_i'$, explicitly $Ux=\\sum_i\\langle x,e_i\\rangle e_i'$. Its transported sharp-measurement family is $P_\\theta'=UP_\\theta U^*$, because $Ue_\\theta=\\cos\\theta e_1'+\\sin\\theta e_2'$. Under the simultaneous transport of a preparation to $\\rho'=U\\rho U^*$ the Born probabilities agree: $\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho'P_\\theta')=\\operatorname{tr}(U\\rho P_\\theta U^*)=\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho P_\\theta)$. The last equality follows by evaluating the finite trace in a transported orthonormal basis; the same argument preserves trace one, and conjugation preserves positivity and self-adjointness of $\\rho$. Thus covariance concerns the transported states and projectors together. A general complex basis change need not leave the original linear-analyser family fixed: replacing $e_2$ by $ie_2$ gives rays of $\\cos\\theta e_1+i\\sin\\theta e_2$, generally absent from the original family, and does not satisfy clause (b). For two transverse frames of clause (b), the bases differ by a common phase times a real orthogonal two-by-two matrix. Write its first column as $(\\cos\\chi,\\sin\\chi)$; its second column is $(-\\sin\\chi,\\cos\\chi)$ or $(\\sin\\chi,-\\cos\\chi)$ according to whether its determinant is $+1$ or $-1$. The families are equal with the angle reparametrisation $\\theta\\mapsto\\chi+\\theta$ or $\\chi-\\theta$, respectively, by the sine and cosine addition formulas. Arbitrary complex basis transport gives a valid sharp measurement but need not give a linear polariser. The value of $|k_0|$ enters no polarisation probability: it enters the separate mode frequency and energy assignments of the radiation postulate.\n\n**(e) Scope.** The model is an idealisation of a transverse propagation mode\nwith a definite mode label. It is a finite-dimensional internal-factor model\nin the sense of P1 and P6: its state space is $D(V)$, its pure preparations are\nrays, and its analyser is a declared ideal sharp measurement. It contains no\nposition observable, no momentum observable, no spatial wave function and no\nlocalisation claim, and it is not the massive one-particle model of P5 with\n$m=0$ and is not obtained from that model by a mass-to-zero limit; no such\nlimit is used or claimed. The mode label $k_{0}$ is not a\nnormalisable state of any sector of the framework, and a plane wave is not\nintroduced as a state. Whether a laboratory polariser and source realize this\ndeclared model, with what efficiency, loss and background, is a tested\nidentification with uncertainty under P7, not a consequence of this\ndefinition.\n\n**(f) Status.** Clauses (a)-(e) fix the model data, its outcome vocabulary and\nits scope; they add no assumption beyond the cited postulates and the cited\nfinite-dimensional mathematics. The mathematical properties of the projections\nare the content of\n[[lem-nrqm-photons-analyzer-projections-and-overlaps]]; the conditional outcome law of the declared analyser is proved on this page. No empirical claim is made here.",
research/plan-spec.json:73578:          "statement": "**(a) Adopted readings.** Adopt the state and composition postulate P1, the\nobservable and probability postulate P2, the ideal conditional-measurement\npostulate P3 and the preparation and apparatus discipline P7\n([[post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-observable-and-probability]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-ideal-conditional-measurement]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-preparation-and-apparatus]]), the radiation extension\npostulate\n[[post-nrqm-photons-radiation-extension-and-transverse-mode-assignment]] and\nthe radiation sector definition\n[[def-nrqm-photons-framework-radiation-sector-and-quantum-assignments]], in\nthe measurement vocabulary of\n[[def-nrqm-measurement-framework-declared-measurements-and-instruments]].\nAssume Countable Choice, Dependent Choice and Choice\n([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-dependent-choice]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]]),\nincluding the hypotheses inherited from the radiation postulate and the cited\nmeasurement mathematics.\n\n**(b) Declared ideal total-number detector.** The **declared ideal\ntotal-number detector** of the radiation sector is the declared ideal sharp\nmeasurement with outcome set $\\mathbb N$, outcome projection\n$$P_{n}:=\\Pi_{n}\\qquad(n\\in\\mathbb N),$$\nthe sector projections of the Fock space $F(\\mathfrak h)$; the family\n$(\\Pi_{n})$ consists of mutually orthogonal orthogonal projections with strong\nsum $I$ ([[lem-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-fock-sector-projections]] clauses\n(a),(b),(c)). Its **count law** in a declared radiation preparation $\\rho$ is\nthe reading of clause (ii) of P2,\n$$p_{n}(\\rho)=\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho\\Pi_{n}),\\qquad \\sum_{n\\in\\mathbb N}p_{n}(\\rho)=1,$$\nand its **number readout** is the assigned total-number observable $(N,1)$ of\n[[thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-number-operator-domain]] read through the atom\nlaw of P2. The conditional post-count state and the discarded-outcome state are\nthe Lueders updates of the measurement definition\n([[lem-nrqm-measurement-selective-luders-update]],\n[[lem-nrqm-measurement-nonselective-luders-map]]). The word \"count\" refers to\nthe quanta of the declared model; no laboratory counter, no absorption\nprocess, no detection efficiency, no dark count, no dead time and no\nbackground is declared here.\n\n**(c) Declared ideal energy-window detector.** For a Borel set\n$B\\subseteq[0,\\infty)$ read in joules the **declared ideal energy-window\ndetector of window $B$** is the declared ideal sharp measurement with outcome\nstructure\n$$\\bigl(E_{H_f}(B),\\ I-E_{H_f}(B)\\bigr),$$\nwhere $E_{H_f}$ is the spectral projection-valued measure of the assigned\nfree-field energy observable $(H_f,\\mathrm J)$; its **window law** in the\npreparation $\\rho$ is $p_{B}(\\rho)=\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho E_{H_f}(B))$ and its\ncomplement probability is $\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho(I-E_{H_f}(B)))$, the reading\nof P2. A finite-resolution detector is described by such a window or by a\ndeclared finite or countably infinite family $(B_a)_{a\\in A}$ of pairwise\ndisjoint Borel windows covering $[0,\\infty)$, with outcome projections\n$(E_{H_f}(B_a))_{a\\in A}$. Countable additivity of the spectral PVM and\n$E_{H_f}([0,\\infty))=I$ give their strong sum $I$, so this is an admissible\ndeclared ideal sharp measurement. The\nstatement that a laboratory spectrometer realizes a declared window, with what\nresolution and calibration, is an identification under P7.\n\n**(d) Declared analyser detection.** The **declared polarisation-analyser\ndetection** of the fixed propagation mode is the analyser family\n$\\{P_{\\theta},P_{\\theta}^{\\perp}\\}$ of\n[[def-nrqm-photons-framework-fixed-mode-polarization-model]], read as a\ndeclared ideal sharp measurement of the polarisation factor; its law and its conditional update are proved on this page. It detects the polarisation alternative of the model and is not a photon counter.\n\n**(e) Repetition requires a separate sampling model.** The declaration of a\ndetector gives the outcome law of a single declared measurement. A prediction\nfor repeated trials, a count rate, or any statistical comparison with data\nrequires in addition a declared preparation-and-apparatus model and a sampling\nmodel in the sense of P7 -- for instance an identically-and-independently\nrepeated preparation with a declared number of trials -- which this page does\nnot supply. A single measurement outcome law is not a frequency and no\nfrequency is asserted.\n\n**(f) Destructive detectors are outside the declared class.** A\nphotoabsorption detector that destroys the absorbed quantum, and any detector\nwhose post-outcome state of the radiation field is not the Lueders update,\nrequires its own declared instrument, that is its own outcome effects together\nwith its own conditional updates (clause (v) of P3, clause (d) of the\nmeasurement definition). No such instrument is declared on this page, and the\n\"blocked\" outcome of the analyser is used only through its discarded-outcome\nlaw.\n\n**(g) Status.** Clauses (b)-(d) fix vocabulary and read the cited postulates;\nthey add no physical assumption beyond P1-P3, P7 and the radiation model, and\nthey assert no detection mechanism, no response function and no empirical\nresult. In particular, the declaration of an ideal counting model is not a\nclaim that any physical device counts photons, and the energy-window model is\nnot a claim that any physical spectrometer measures exact energy.",
research/plan-spec.json:73624:          "physical_scope": "The declared ideal detection models of the radiation sector: the ideal total-number counting model with the Fock sector projections as outcome structure and the Born law of P2, the ideal energy-window model with the spectral projections of the free-field energy, and the polarisation analyser of the fixed-mode model. The declaration fixes vocabulary only; it asserts no laboratory counter, no absorption instrument, no efficiency, no dark counts, no dead time, no background, no calibration and no sampling model, and repeated trials require a separately declared statistical model.",
research/plan-spec.json:73723:          "statement": "**What the page adopts.** The page adopts the radiation extension of the\nframework, [[post-nrqm-photons-radiation-extension-and-transverse-mode-assignment]]:\na fixed propagation-mode one-photon polarisation model with a two-dimensional\ntransverse internal space, a quantised free transverse radiation sector with\nbosonic Fock space, and the mode assignments $\\omega=c|k|$,\n$E=\\hbar\\omega$ and $p=\\hbar k$ in a declared inertial chart. These are\nadditional model assumptions of the framework; they are not derived from P1-P7,\nnot derived from classical Maxwell theory or special relativity, and not\nobtained as a limit of the massive one-particle model. The page also adopts the\npreparation and apparatus discipline P7\n([[post-nrqm-framework-preparation-and-apparatus]]) and the observable and\nprobability postulate P2 ([[post-nrqm-framework-observable-and-probability]])\nfor every reading of a detector outcome.\n\n**What the page proves conditionally.**\n[[def-nrqm-photons-framework-fixed-mode-polarization-model]] and\n[[def-nrqm-photons-framework-radiation-sector-and-quantum-assignments]] fix the\nmodel vocabulary, units and domains.\n[[pthm-nrqm-photons-fixed-mode-polarization-outcome-law]] proves the Born\noutcome law $\\cos^{2}(\\theta-\\varphi)$, $\\sin^{2}(\\theta-\\varphi)$ of the\ndeclared ideal analyser, its affinity over mixtures, its phase invariance and\nthe conditional update of a repeated ideal analyser.\n[[pthm-nrqm-photons-one-photon-number-energy-and-momentum-laws]] proves that a\nnormalisable one-photon preparation has the sharp number readout one, the\nfrequency and energy laws given by the push-forwards of $\\|f(k)\\|^{2}\\,d^{3}k$\nunder $c|k|$ and $\\hbar c|k|$ (a unit packet coordinate $f(k)$ has units\n$\\mathrm m^{3/2}$ under $d^{3}k$), their means under the first-moment condition, and\nthe corresponding momentum-component laws and means under absolute\nfirst-moment integrability. Frequency, energy and momentum variances are\nstrictly positive and finite on the corresponding multiplier domains; the\nsingleton probabilities vanish for every normalisable unit vector without a\nmoment-domain hypothesis.\n[[pthm-nrqm-photons-fixed-mode-number-and-energy-statistics]] proves the sharp\ncount of the $n$-quanta mode state, the Poisson count law and moments of the\nmode-coherent preparation, the energy means and variances of those\npreparations, and that $e^{-\\beta H_f}$ is not trace class for the full\ncontinuum sector when $\\beta>0$ has unit $\\mathrm J^{-1}$. Thus its\ntrace-normalized full-sector Gibbs density is unavailable; other preparations\nare outside the theorem. All these are conditional consequences of P1, P2 and\nthe adopted radiation model in the declared measurement models. The repeated\nideal-analyser conditional update additionally adopts P3\n([[post-nrqm-framework-ideal-conditional-measurement]]); its use is restricted\nto outcomes of positive probability. Their units are recorded in\n[[def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units]] and in the assigned observables\nof the model.\n\n**What the page does not claim.** The page does not derive the radiation\nextension from Maxwell's equations, does not derive a Lorentz transformation\nlaw for mode labels, polarisations or energies, and does not assert Galilean\ncovariance of the radiation sector; the two polarisation states of the photon\nare adopted, and their relativistic description by Lorentz-group\nrepresentations is outside this non-relativistic extension. It does not\nintroduce a photon position wave function, a photon localisation operator or a\ngeneral photon-position distribution; the sharp-mode label $k$ is a spectral\nparameter and not a state, and no normalisable one-photon vector has sharp\nfrequency, energy or momentum. It does not supply a light-matter Hamiltonian, a\ndipole coupling, selection rules, absorption or stimulated emission, and it\ndoes not supply vacuum spontaneous emission; those belong to the later pair\non light-matter and spectral transitions, where the coupling and environment\nassumptions are declared separately.\n\n**What the page's idealisations are.** The declared analyser, the declared\ntotal-number detector and the declared energy-window detector are ideal\nprojection-valued measurements of the model\n([[def-nrqm-photons-framework-ideal-counting-and-detection-model]]). No\nlaboratory polariser, counter or spectrometer is identified with them; no\nefficiency, dark count, dead time, background, calibration or resolution is\ndeclared, and a destructive absorption detector would require its own\ninstrument. A single measurement law is not a frequency: repeated trials\nrequire a separately declared preparation and sampling model in the sense of\nP7, which this page does not supply.\n\n**Empirical status.** The page records no experiment, no measured count, no\ncalibration, no uncertainty budget and no statistical test, and none is\ninvented. The Born ratio $\\cos^{2}(\\theta-\\varphi)$ of the declared analyser\ncoincides with the intensity ratio of the declared classical transverse-field\nmodel of the companion page; the coincidence is a property of the two declared\nmodels and does not establish field quantisation, and classical\nelectromagnetic waves also interfere. Whether a given source, polariser or\ndetector is described by the declared fixed-mode model is a tested\nidentification with uncertainty under P7; agreement of the model's conditional\npredictions with reported data would be evidence about the combined model and\napparatus assumptions, not a deductive proof of the model or of the framework.\n\n**Mathematical hypotheses.** The conditional deductions summarized here retain the choice hypotheses of their mathematical suppliers; in particular, the transverse free-field Fock supplier assumes Countable Choice, Dependent Choice and Choice. [[def-countable-choice]], [[def-dependent-choice]] and [[def-axiom-of-choice]] record these assumptions.",
research/plan-spec.json:74296:          "statement": "**(a) Adopted readings.** Adopt the state and composition postulate P1, the\nobservable and probability postulate P2, the ideal conditional-measurement\npostulate P3 and the preparation and apparatus discipline P7\n([[post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-observable-and-probability]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-ideal-conditional-measurement]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-preparation-and-apparatus]]), the finite-level cutoff\ndipole model\n[[def-nrqm-light-matter-framework-finite-level-coupled-model]], the radiation\nsector definition\n[[def-nrqm-photons-framework-radiation-sector-and-quantum-assignments]] and\nthe declared ideal counting and energy-window detection models\n[[def-nrqm-photons-framework-ideal-counting-and-detection-model]], in the\nreadings of [[def-nrqm-state-geometry-framework-state-space]],\n[[def-nrqm-state-geometry-pure-mixed-states-and-purity]],\n[[def-nrqm-observables-framework-observable-and-outcome-law]] and\n[[def-nrqm-measurement-framework-declared-measurements-and-instruments]], with\nthe units of [[def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units]]. Assume the Axioms of\nCountable Choice and of Choice ([[def-countable-choice]],\n[[def-axiom-of-choice]]).\n\n**(b) Declared level pair and transition energy.** A **declared level pair**\n$(a,b)$ of the model is an ordered pair of orthonormal eigenvectors $v_{a},\nv_{b}\\in\\mathbb C^{d}$ of the compressed matter energy $H_{a}$\n([[def-orthonormal-family-complete-orthonormal-system-and-hilbert-basis]],\n[[def-inner-product-space]]). Its **transition energy** is\n$\\Delta:=E_{a}-E_{b}$, read in joules; the pair is **upward** when\n$\\Delta>0$, meaning level $a$ lies above level $b$ in the model's energy\nordering.\n\n**(c) Dipole coefficients and single-vertex photon density.** For an upward\ndeclared level pair $(a,b)$ and the couplings $D_j^{\\mathrm{ch}},g_{j},\\eta_{0}$ of\n[[def-nrqm-light-matter-framework-finite-level-coupled-model]] clauses (c),(d),\nthe model's **dipole coefficients** are the complex numbers\n$$c_{j}:=\\bigl\\langle D_j^{\\mathrm{ch}}v_{a},\\,v_{b}\\bigr\\rangle_{\\mathbb C^{d}} \\qquad(j=1,\\dots,J),$$\nthe model's **single-vertex mode vector** is\n$$M:=\\sum_{j=1}^{J}c_{j}\\,g_{j}\\in\\mathfrak h,$$\nand the model's **single-vertex photon density** is the finite Borel measure on\n$\\mathbb R$\n$$\\nu_{b}(B):=\\int_{\\mathbb R^{3}}\\mathbf 1_{B}\\bigl(\\varepsilon(k)\\bigr)\\, \\|M(k)\\|_{\\mathbb C^{3}}^{2}\\,d^{3}k,\\qquad B\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\ \\text{Borel},$$\nread in the model's units and supported in $(0,\\Lambda]$. The pairing is linear in its first argument and conjugate-linear in its\nsecond. Thus $c_j$ is the actual coefficient of $v_b$ in $D_j^{\\mathrm{ch}}v_a$,\nand Hermiticity gives $\\langle D_j^{\\mathrm{ch}}v_b,v_a\\rangle=\\overline{c_j}$.\nThese are exactly the coefficients of\n[[thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-golden-rule-coefficient-approximate-identity]]\nclause (c), with no conjugation or change of the transverse mode vectors;\nits mode vector and measure are the present $M$ and $\\nu_b$.\n\n**(d) Single-vertex emission vector and coefficient.** For $t\\ge0$ the model's\n**single-vertex emission vector** is the element of $\\mathfrak h$\n$$A_{b}(\\cdot,t):\\quad k\\mapsto-\\frac{i\\eta_{0}}{\\hbar}M(k) \\int_{0}^{t}e^{i(\\varepsilon(k)-\\Delta)s/\\hbar}\\,ds ,$$\nand the model's **single-vertex emission coefficient** is\n$$\\gamma_{b}(t):=\\|A_{b}(\\cdot,t)\\|_{\\mathfrak h}^{2}.$$\nThe vector and the coefficient are **defined model quantities**: the model\nassigns them through this clause and through the cutoff dipole mathematics, and\nthey are not asserted to be the exact probability of any measurement of the\nmodel. In particular $\\gamma_{b}(t)$ is dimensionless and is not a rate; the\nreading of a rate coefficient requires a separate limit statement, which is not\npart of this definition.\n\n**(e) Normalised single-vertex photon-energy law and ideal detection.** For\n$t>0$ and $M\\ne0$ the model's **normalised single-vertex photon-energy law** is\nthe Borel probability measure on $\\mathbb R$\n$$P_{b,t}(B):=\\frac{\\int_{B}K_{t}(E-\\Delta)\\,d\\nu_{b}(E)} {\\int_{\\mathbb R}K_{t}(E-\\Delta)\\,d\\nu_{b}(E)},\\qquad K_{t}(x):=\\frac{1}{\\hbar^{2}t}\\Bigl|\\int_{0}^{t}e^{ixs/\\hbar}\\,ds\\Bigr|^{2},$$\nwhose denominator is finite and strictly positive by\n[[lem-nrqm-light-matter-first-order-photon-energy-law]] clause (b). This\nprobability law is defined independently of $\\eta_0$, including $\\eta_0=0$.\nIts amplitude-based preparation reading additionally requires $A_b(\\cdot,t)\\ne0$.\nFor $t>0$ and $M\\ne0$, this is equivalent to $\\eta_0\\ne0$, since\n$\\|A_b(\\cdot,t)\\|^2=\\eta_0^2t\\int K_t(E-\\Delta)\\,d\\nu_b(E)$ and the integral\nis positive. Under this condition the **declared single-vertex\none-photon preparation** is the unit vector $v_{b}\\otimes(A_{b}(\\cdot,t)/\\|A_{b}(\\cdot,t)\\|_{\\mathfrak h})$\nof $\\mathbb C^{d}\\otimes\\mathfrak h\\subseteq\\mathbb C^{d}\\otimes\nF(\\mathfrak h)$, and for a Borel window $B\\subseteq[0,\\infty)$ the declared\nideal energy-window detector of window $B$ of\n[[def-nrqm-photons-framework-ideal-counting-and-detection-model]] clause (c)\nhas the Born probability $P_{b,t}(B)$ in that preparation, because the energy\nlaw of a unit one-photon vector $f$ is the push-forward of\n$\\|f(k)\\|_{\\mathbb C^{3}}^{2}d^{3}k$ under $k\\mapsto\\varepsilon(k)$\n([[def-nrqm-photons-framework-radiation-sector-and-quantum-assignments]] clause\n(e)). When $\\eta_0=0$, $A_b(\\cdot,t)=0$ and no preparation obtained by\nnormalizing that emission amplitude is declared; the formal law $P_{b,t}$\nremains the probability measure defined above. A **detected photon energy** is therefore a spectral reading of the\nassigned free-field energy observable $(H_{f},\\mathrm J)$, not a sharp energy\nof a normalisable state and not a quantum number of the model's mode labels.\n\n**(f) Scope.** Clauses (b)-(e) fix the model's spectral vocabulary. They assert\nno sharp spectral line: the energy law of clause (e) is spread over the support\nof $\\nu_{b}$. A point mass at $\\Delta$ (the probability measure assigning\n$\\mathbf 1_B(\\Delta)$ to a Borel set $B$) is the limiting idealisation of\n[[lem-nrqm-light-matter-first-order-photon-energy-law]] clause (c) only under\nits hypotheses: $\\nu_b=J_b\\lambda$, where $\\lambda$ is Lebesgue measure on\n$\\mathbb R$ and $J_b$ is an integrable nonnegative density representative\nvanishing almost everywhere off $(0,\\Lambda]$, continuous at\n$0<\\Delta<\\Lambda$ and satisfying $J_b(\\Delta)>0$. Under these hypotheses,\n$\\int f\\,dP_{b,t}\\to f(\\Delta)$ as $t\\to\\infty$ for every bounded continuous\n$f:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb C$; no such limit is asserted for other allowed\nlevel pairs or densities. In particular, if $\\Delta>\\Lambda$, the finite-time\nlaws assign all mass to $(0,\\Lambda]$ and cannot converge to the point mass\nat $\\Delta$.\nThey supply no spectrometer, no calibration, no detector response beyond the\ndeclared ideal window, no efficiency, no background, no sampling model, no\ncount and no measurement record; the identification of a laboratory\nspectrometer with a declared window is a tested identification with uncertainty\nunder P7. The single-vertex quantities of clauses (d)-(e) are conditional model\ndata; their relation to the model's exact outcome probabilities is a separate\nproved statement.",
research/plan-spec.json:74366:          "statement": "Adopt, as additional model assumptions of this pair, the following clauses. It\nis adopted together with the state and composition postulate P1, the observable\nand probability postulate P2, the autonomous-dynamics postulate P4 and the\npreparation and apparatus discipline P7\n([[post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-observable-and-probability]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-autonomous-dynamics]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-preparation-and-apparatus]]), the radiation extension\npostulate\n[[post-nrqm-photons-radiation-extension-and-transverse-mode-assignment]], the\nstatic finite-level dipole postulate\n[[post-nrqm-approximations-static-finite-level-dipole-coupling]] and the\ncoupled model\n[[def-nrqm-light-matter-framework-finite-level-coupled-model]], in the\nreadings of [[def-nrqm-photons-framework-radiation-sector-and-quantum-assignments]],\n[[def-nrqm-state-geometry-framework-state-space]],\n[[def-nrqm-observables-framework-observable-and-outcome-law]] and\n[[def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units]]. Assume the Axioms of Countable\nChoice and of Choice, the choice hypotheses of the cited mathematics\n([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]]).\n\n**(i) Vacuum preparation.** Let $(a,b)$ be a declared upward level pair of\n[[def-nrqm-light-matter-framework-spectral-transition-and-detection]] clause\n(b), with $\\Omega$ the vacuum of the radiation sector. The postulate declares\nthat a preparation described as **the declared finite-level matter in level\n$a$ with no quanta in the declared transverse radiation sector** is represented\nby the ray of the unit vector $v_{a}\\otimes\\Omega$ of\n$\\mathbb C^{d}\\otimes F(\\mathfrak h)$, and that its evolution is the model's\nautonomous evolution $t\\mapsto e^{-itH_{\\mathrm{LM}}/\\hbar}(v_{a}\\otimes\\Omega)$.\nThis representation of a physical preparation is an adopted model assumption;\nit is not derived from P1-P7, from the radiation extension or from the\nCoulomb model.\n\n**(ii) The emission question.** The postulate declares that the model's\n**vacuum-initiated emission question** for the pair is read through the\ndeclared ideal sharp measurement with outcome projection\n$Q_{b}=|v_{b}\\rangle\\langle v_{b}|\\otimes\\Pi_{1}$ (or through any other\ndeclared measurement of [[def-nrqm-photons-framework-ideal-counting-and-detection-model]]\nwhose outcome structure is explicitly declared), with its Born probability\nread as in P2; the model's exact probability of that outcome is the quantity\nstudied by the emission items of this page. The identification of this\noutcome with the registration of an emitted photon by a laboratory apparatus\nis a tested identification with uncertainty under P7.\n\n**(iii) The entire field and environment content.** The declared field content\nof the model is the quantized transverse sector with its vacuum $\\Omega$, the\nmode vectors $g_{1},\\dots,g_{J}$ with cutoff $\\Lambda$ and the coupling\n$\\eta_{0}$ of\n[[def-nrqm-light-matter-framework-finite-level-coupled-model]] clauses (c),(d).\nThe postulate adopts **no** thermal radiation state, no additional modes, no\nreservoir or environment, no measurement apparatus and no noise model. In\nparticular the reading of $\\Omega$ as the field's zero-point (vacuum) state is\na model reading, and the term \"spontaneous\" refers to the emission process\ninitiated by this declared vacuum state, not to a derivation of the vacuum\nfrom classical electrodynamics or of the coupling from a current.\n\n**(iv) What a rate claim would require.** The postulate explicitly does not\ndeclare a rate, a lifetime, a linewidth or an exponential decay law. Such a\nclaim would require additional adopted assumptions — for instance a declared\nweak-coupling window, a declared mode-density or environment model, and a\ndeclared measurement and sampling model — together with the corresponding\nmathematics; the theorems of this page state only the limits of the model's\nexplicit single-vertex coefficient and the exact-model control of one\nprojection probability, and they record those limits as model quantities.\n\n**(v) Contrast with the prescribed drive.** The prescribed-field dipole-drive\nmodel of [[def-nrqm-light-matter-framework-dipole-drive-and-long-wavelength]]\nis a different model class: its field is an external datum unaffected by the\nmatter, it contains no vacuum state and no photon number, and it supplies only\nstimulated transitions. The present postulate is the additional assumption\nunder which an emission process at all is represented by the model; the two\nclasses are not interchangeable, and no derivation of one from the other is\nasserted.\n\n**(vi) Scope.** Clauses (i)-(v) are adopted assumptions and readings. They\nassert no laboratory source, atom, mode content, cutoff or coupling value; no\ndetector response, efficiency, background or calibration; no observed\nspectrum, count, rate or lifetime; and no statistical inference. The\nidentification of a laboratory system and apparatus with the model and its\noutcome structure is a tested identification with uncertainty under P7, and\nthe empirical support of the radiation extension or of the coupling is the\nnonlogical relation recorded on this page, not a proof of the postulate.",
research/plan-spec.json:74657:          "statement": "Adopt the declared charged pair of\n[[post-nrqm-approximations-static-finite-level-dipole-coupling]] clauses\n(i)-(ii): the relative-coordinate factor $L^{2}(\\mathbb R^{3};\\mathbb C)$ of the\ndeclared two-body Coulomb model\n([[def-nrqm-central-framework-coulomb-and-hydrogen-model]]), the Cartesian coordinate\nmultipliers $X_j$ (the operators denoted $Q_j$ in that supplier), with maximal domains $D(X_j)=\\{\\psi:\\xi_j\\psi\\in L^2\\}$ and Cartesian dipole components $D_j=-q_{\\mathrm{el}}X_j$, where $q_{\\mathrm{el}}>0$ is the charge magnitude in coulombs.\nLet $(\\psi_{nlm})$ be the bound eigenfunctions of the Coulomb Hamiltonian of\n[[pthm-nrqm-central-coulomb-bound-spectrum-and-degeneracy]] with their angular\nlabels $l\\ge0$, $m\\in\\{-l,\\dots,l\\}$. Write\n$Q_0:=X_3$, $Q_\\pm:=(X_1\\pm iX_2)/\\sqrt2$ and $D_q:=-q_{\\mathrm{el}}Q_q$ for\n$q\\in\\{-1,0,1\\}$ (the dimensionless spherical-component index), with\n$D(Q_q):=\\{u\\in L^2:Q_q(x)u(x)\\in L^2\\}$. Thus\n$D(Q_0)=D(X_3)$ and $D(Q_\\pm)=D(X_1)\\cap D(X_2)$;\n$D(D_q)=D(Q_q)$ since $q_{\\mathrm{el}}>0$. Let $(Y_{lm})$ be the spherical harmonics and\n$u_{nl}$ the reduced radial functions of\n[[thm-nrqm-coulomb-atomic-dipole-matrix-element-domain]]. Assume the Axioms of\nCountable Choice and of Choice ([[def-countable-choice]],\n[[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Then:\n\n(a) (domain and finiteness) every bound state satisfies $\\psi_{nlm}\\in D(X_{j})$\nfor $j=1,2,3$, and for all labels and every $q\\in\\{-1,0,1\\}$ the matrix element\n$\\langle\\psi_{n'l'm'},D_{q}\\psi_{nlm}\\rangle$ is absolutely convergent;\n\n(b) (factorisation and angular condition) for all labels and every\n$q\\in\\{-1,0,1\\}$,\n$$\\langle\\psi_{n'l'm'},D_{q}\\psi_{nlm}\\rangle =-q_{\\mathrm{el}}\\Bigl(\\int_{0}^{\\infty}r\\,u_{n'l'}(r)\\overline{u_{nl}(r)}\\,dr\\Bigr) \\Bigl(\\int_{S^{2}}Y_{l'm'}(\\Omega)\\overline{Q_{q}(\\Omega)Y_{lm}(\\Omega)} \\,d\\sigma(\\Omega)\\Bigr),$$\nand the angular factor vanishes unless\n$$l'=l+1\\ \\text{and}\\ m'=m+q,\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad l\\ge1,\\ l'=l-1\\ \\text{and}\\ m'=m+q;$$\n\n(c) (necessary conditions only) consequently a nonvanishing matrix element\nrequires $m'-m=q\\in\\{0,\\pm1\\}$ and $|l'-l|=1$; these are necessary conditions\non the matrix element: the radial factor may vanish for an allowed angular\npair, and no transition probability, rate, oscillator strength, line intensity\nor claim that an \"allowed\" transition occurs is asserted;\n\n(d) (parity consequence) let $(\\Pi_{\\mathrm{par}}\\psi)(x):=\\psi(-x)$ on\n$L^{2}(\\mathbb R^{3};\\mathbb C)$; then\n$\\Pi_{\\mathrm{par}}\\psi_{nlm}=(-1)^{l}\\psi_{nlm}$ and\n$\\Pi_{\\mathrm{par}}$ is a unitary involution with\n$\\Pi_{\\mathrm{par}}D(Q_q)=D(Q_q)$, and\n$\\Pi_{\\mathrm{par}}Q_q\\Pi_{\\mathrm{par}}=-Q_q$ as operators on\n$D(Q_q)$ for $q\\in\\{-1,0,1\\}$; hence\na nonvanishing matrix element $\\langle\\psi_{n'l'm'},D_{q}\\psi_{nlm}\\rangle$\nrequires $(-1)^{l'}=-(-1)^{l}$, i.e. opposite parity of the two bound states\n(the Laporte condition), consistently with $|l'-l|=1$;\n\n(e) (transfer to the compressed model matrices) if the declared\nfinite-dimensional subspace $W$ of\n[[def-nrqm-light-matter-framework-finite-level-coupled-model]] clause (b) is\nthe span of a declared finite subfamily of the bound states, with an\northonormal basis chosen from that subfamily, define the spherical\ncompression $D_q^W:=\\Pi_WD_q|_W$; then for all $w,w'\\in W$\n$$\\bigl\\langle w',D^{W}_{q}w\\bigr\\rangle=\\bigl\\langle w',D_{q}w\\bigr\\rangle,$$\nso the model's compressed dipole matrices inherit every vanishing condition of\nclauses (b)-(d) entry by entry;\n\n(f) (scope) clauses (a)-(e) are conditional statements about the declared\nCoulomb pair and its compressions. They say nothing about transition rates,\nlineshapes, observed spectra or selection-rule violations by higher-order\nterms; the neglected next-order terms of the long-wavelength expansion and the\nmagnetic-moment interaction are outside this theorem, and no empirical\nspectrum is claimed.",
research/plan-spec.json:74736:          "statement": "## Scope of the page\n\n**What the page adopts.** The page works inside the adopted radiation\nextension\n[[post-nrqm-photons-radiation-extension-and-transverse-mode-assignment]] (a\nquantized free transverse sector with the mode assignments $\\omega=c|k|$,\n$E=\\hbar\\omega$ and $p=\\hbar k$, adopted rather than derived from Maxwell\ntheory or special relativity) and the declared finite-level cutoff dipole\nmodel\n[[def-nrqm-light-matter-framework-finite-level-coupled-model]], whose matter\nfactor is a declared finite-dimensional subspace of the two-body Coulomb\nrelative-coordinate model with compressed dipole matrices and compressed\nCoulomb energy, and whose channel dipole coupling $\\eta_0\\sum_jD_j^{\\mathrm{ch}}\\otimes\\phi(g_j)$\nto a finite family of cutoff mode vectors, with declared real Cartesian channel weights, is declared model data. It also\nadopts the prescribed dipole-drive model\n[[def-nrqm-light-matter-framework-dipole-drive-and-long-wavelength]] with its\nlong-wavelength regime, and the vacuum-preparation and quantized-field\nassumption\n[[post-nrqm-light-matter-vacuum-preparation-and-emission-assumption]]. The\nidentification of a laboratory system, field or apparatus with any of these\nmodels is a tested identification with uncertainty under P7\n([[post-nrqm-framework-preparation-and-apparatus]]).\n\n**What the page proves conditionally.**\n[[lem-nrqm-light-matter-first-order-photon-energy-law]] is the page's\nmathematics: the explicit factorisation of the single-vertex emission\nkernel, the normalised photon-energy law, its concentration at the transition\nenergy for a continuous positive mode density, and the strict positivity of\nthe law on windows of positive density.\n[[pthm-nrqm-light-matter-first-order-emission-and-absorption-coefficients]]\nproves the single-vertex emission and absorption amplitudes of the model, the\nexact-model control of the one-photon level-$b$ projection probability by the\nexplicit Dyson tail, and the exact mirror relation between emission into and\nabsorption from a unit one-photon vector.\n[[pthm-nrqm-light-matter-golden-rule-limit-and-rate-reading]] proves the\nlimit of the single-vertex coefficient per unit time and declares the model\nrate coefficient $\\Gamma_{b}=2\\pi\\eta_{0}^{2}J_{b}(\\Delta)/\\hbar$ under a\ncontinuous positive mode density, with the above-cutoff case tending to zero.\n[[pthm-nrqm-light-matter-stimulated-dipole-drive-transitions]] proves the\nfirst-order amplitudes of the prescribed classical drive, the equal\nstimulated emission and absorption coefficients for the same physical gap and prescribed real field, and\nthe exact identification of declared two-level Pauli data with the circular\nand linear two-level drive models of the driven-dynamics page.\n[[pthm-nrqm-light-matter-dipole-selection-rules]] proves the necessary\nangular and parity conditions on the uncompressed dipole matrix elements, and\ntransfers their vanishing to compressed matrices when the declared finite-level\nsubspace is spanned by a finite subfamily of bound states and its declared\northonormal basis is chosen from that same subfamily. All of these are\nconditional consequences of the cited postulates, mathematics and model data;\ntheir units are those of\n[[def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units]].\n\n**What the page does not claim.** The page derives no Maxwell equation, no\nmode assignment, no Lorentz transformation law, no massless limit of the\nmassive model and no coupling constant. It contains no experiment: there is no\nreported laboratory apparatus or preparation protocol, calibration, count,\nfrequency, efficiency, background or uncertainty budget anywhere on the page, and none is invented. It asserts\nno measured spectral line, no measured rate, lifetime or linewidth, and no\nexponential decay law; the model rate coefficient of\n[[pthm-nrqm-light-matter-golden-rule-limit-and-rate-reading]] is a limit of an\nexplicit model quantity, and its use as a decay constant over a time window\nwould require additional adopted assumptions, as the sources record\n(Binney §9.3.1 on the continuum and long-time approximations; Box 9.2 on\nspontaneous emission as a zero-point-field mechanism added to the classical\ntreatment). The page also does not claim that the coincidence of any model\nnumber with a measured ratio proves the framework, and it reports no\nstatistical comparison: a comparison with data would test the combined model\nand apparatus assumptions, would require a declared sampling and measurement\nmodel, and would carry finite-sample fluctuations and systematic\nuncertainties.\n\n**Empirical status.** An emission-spectrum or lifetime experiment is a\ncandidate for a separate reported-experiment item, not a record of this page.\nAny such item would need an authoritative full report with the apparatus,\npreparation, calibration, measured outputs, trial counts where reported,\nresolution, background and the reported statistical analysis; sources that\nsupply only historical line formulas or illustrative estimates do not\nauthorize quantitative empirical claims. The relation of the adopted\npostulates and of the model's predictions to empirical support is recorded as\na nonlogical support relation, not as a proof premise of any item on this\npage.",
research/plan-spec.json:74966:          "statement": "Adopt the declared finite-level cutoff dipole model\n[[def-nrqm-light-matter-framework-finite-level-coupled-model]] and its\nspectral-transition reading\n[[def-nrqm-light-matter-framework-spectral-transition-and-detection]], the\ndeclared ideal energy-window detection model\n[[def-nrqm-photons-framework-ideal-counting-and-detection-model]] clause (c)\nand the preparation and apparatus discipline P7\n([[post-nrqm-framework-preparation-and-apparatus]]). Assume the Axioms of\nCountable Choice and of Choice ([[def-countable-choice]],\n[[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Declare **three models**, all with the same matter\ndata $H_{a}$, $D_j^{\\mathrm{ch}}$ and the same cutoff $\\Lambda$ and coupling\n$\\eta_{0}$, and with a declared upward level pair $(a,b)$, $\\Delta\\in(0,\\Lambda)$:\n\n1. model $M_{1}$: cutoff mode data whose single-vertex photon density is\n   $\\nu^{(1)}_b=J_1\\lambda$ with a declared nonnegative Lebesgue-measurable integrable representative $J_1:\\mathbb R\\to[0,\\infty)$ of its $L^1(\\lambda)$ class, $J_1=0$\n   $\\lambda$-almost everywhere off $(0,\\Lambda]$ and $J_{1}$ continuous at\n   $\\Delta$ with $J_{1}(\\Delta)>0$;\n2. model $M_{2}$: same matter and cutoff data, single-vertex photon density\n   $\\nu^{(2)}_b=J_2\\lambda$, with a declared representative $J_2:\\mathbb R\\to[0,\\infty)$ having the same properties and\n   $J_{2}(\\Delta)=J_{1}(\\Delta)$;\n3. model $L$ (the **declared ideal sharp-line model**): a declared spectral\n   model in which the energy of an emitted and detected quantum is the value\n   $\\Delta$ with probability one, so that every energy window\n   $B\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ has probability $\\mathbf 1_{B}(\\Delta)$.\n\nAssume in addition that $J_{1}$ and $J_{2}$ are **not proportional**: there is\nno $\\gamma\\ge0$ with $J_1=\\gamma J_2$ $\\lambda$-almost everywhere. The single-vertex laws below are the formal normalized spectral laws of the cited definition for all real $\\eta_0$. Their reading as normalized one-photon preparations uses $\\eta_0\\ne0$; at zero coupling the physical single-vertex vector is zero and no such preparation is formed. No zero-coupling conditioning is asserted. Then:\n\n(a) (equal rate coefficients) the model rate coefficients of clause (c) of\n[[pthm-nrqm-light-matter-golden-rule-limit-and-rate-reading]] coincide,\n$$\\Gamma_{1}=\\frac{2\\pi\\eta_{0}^{2}}{\\hbar}J_{1}(\\Delta) =\\frac{2\\pi\\eta_{0}^{2}}{\\hbar}J_{2}(\\Delta)=\\Gamma_{2};$$\nin particular a comparison of the leading rate coefficients alone does not\ndiscriminate $M_{1}$ from $M_{2}$;\n\n(b) (different formal laws and conditional detected-energy reading) for every $t>0$ the normalised\nsingle-vertex photon-energy laws $P^{(1)}_{t}$ and $P^{(2)}_{t}$ of clause (e)\nof [[def-nrqm-light-matter-framework-spectral-transition-and-detection]]\ndiffer: there is a Borel set $B\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ with\n$P^{(1)}_{t}(B)\\ne P^{(2)}_{t}(B)$; when $\\eta_0\\ne0$, these are distinct predicted ideal energy-window probabilities in the declared normalized single-vertex one-photon preparations. This is a distinction between specified model laws, with no finite-sample discrimination guarantee; at $\\eta_0=0$ only the formal laws are compared;\n\n(c) (the sharp line is a limit, not a finite-time property) if $J_{1}>0$\n$\\lambda$-almost everywhere on a Borel set $B$ of positive Lebesgue measure\nwith $\\Delta\\notin B$, then $P^{(1)}_{t}(B)>0$ for every $t>0$ whereas\n$L$ assigns probability $0$ to $B$; on the other hand\n$P^{(1)}_{t}(B)\\to0$ as $t\\to\\infty$ for every Borel $B$ bounded away from\n$\\Delta$. Thus the declared sharp-line model is the idealisation that the\nsingle-vertex law approaches in the limit, not a property of the\nfinite-time law;\n\n(d) (what the comparison is) clauses (a)-(c) are statements about the three\ndeclared models. They construct no laboratory comparison and report no data:\nno laboratory apparatus or preparation protocol, calibration, detector response, efficiency,\nbackground, trial count, frequency or uncertainty budget is supplied, and\nnone is invented. Comparing any of these predictions with reported data would\ntest the combined model and apparatus assumptions under P7, would require a\ndeclared sampling and measurement model, and would carry finite-sample\nfluctuations and systematic uncertainties; it would not prove a model.",
research/plan-spec.json:78334:          "statement": "Adopt as physical assumptions of this framework, extending the ideal-clock\nreading rule of [[post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light]] to the general worldline\ncalculus of this page:\n\n**(i) Free trajectories.** A massive test particle that is subject to no\ninteraction (a **free particle**) has as its worldline a straight future\ntimelike line of the affine model ([[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]]): there are\nan event $p \\in A$ and a future timelike vector $v \\in V$ with\n$x(s) = p + (s-s_0)v$ for $s\\in I$, where $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ is a nondegenerate interval and $s_0\\in I$ ([[def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline]]).\nInteractions, forces and collisions are not part of this assumption: a particle\nthat is not free may have any future timelike worldline admitted by its own\ndynamical model, and no force law is adopted here. The kinematical consequences\nof this shape for the four-velocity are derived, not assumed, in\n[[pthm-sr-four-velocity-norm-and-orthogonality]].\n\n**(ii) Clock hypothesis.** An ideal clock carried along a future timelike\nworldline of the model, including an accelerated one and a piecewise $C^{1}$ one\nwith finitely many kinks, reads between any two parameter values the proper-time\ndifference of [[def-sr-proper-time]] for $C^{1}$ histories and of\n[[def-sr-piecewise-proper-time]] for weak piecewise $C^{1}$ histories, with no\nadditional term depending on acceleration, jerk or any higher derivative. A\nkink is an idealization of a finite short acceleration phase whose contribution\nis included through the integral on the smooth pieces, and the reading does not\ndepend on an increasing regular change of parameter admitted by the curve\ndefinition — its derivatives on smooth pieces and all finite one-sided endpoint\nderivatives are positive, as in\n[[lem-sr-proper-time-reparametrization-invariance]] and\n[[lem-sr-piecewise-proper-time-reparametrization-invariance]]. Zero-tangent\ncompositions are not worldlines of this model.\n\n**(iii) Scope and qualification.** The assumptions concern ideal point\nparticles, ideal clocks and flat classical spacetime. They are adopted, not\nproved; they are not consequences of the affine geometry, and no experiment is\nclaimed here to establish them. They inherit the idealizations of\n[[post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light]]: real clocks have finite resolution,\ncalibration and apparatus limitations, and real particles are not exactly free.\nThe coordinate and unit conventions are those of\n[[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]], and inertial charts are those\nof [[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]].",
research/plan-spec.json:81656:          "statement": "## Scope caveats for the twin reunion\n\nThis remark delimits the interpretation of the proper-time comparison of the\ntwin reunion ([[texp-sr-piecewise-inertial-twin-reunion]]) in the adopted model, with the ideal clock assumptions of [[post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light]] and\n[[post-sr-free-trajectories-and-clock-hypothesis]] and the SI conventions of\n[[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]]. It proves nothing new; it\nrecords what the earlier items do and do not assert.\n\n## Acceleration is not an additional aging term\n\nIn the model the reading of an ideal clock between two events of its history is\nthe proper-time integral of its worldline, by clause (i) of\n[[post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light]] for $C^1$ histories and by clause (ii) of\n[[post-sr-free-trajectories-and-clock-hypothesis]] for the weak piecewise $C^1$\nclass ([[def-sr-piecewise-proper-time]]); no term depending on the\nfour-acceleration ([[def-sr-four-velocity-and-four-acceleration]]) or on any\nhigher derivative of the history appears, and the clock hypothesis is precisely\nthe exclusion of such terms. The corner of the piecewise inertial history is an\nidealization: a physical turnaround occupies a finite parameter interval, the\nworldline is then $C^1$ or smooth there, and its proper time is still the\nintegral. The endpoint bound and strictness clause of\n[[thm-sr-endpoint-proper-time-bound-and-flat-maximum]] apply to every weak\npiecewise future timelike history with the same endpoints $p,q$. Thus any\nsmoothing of the corner that remains in that class, preserves $p,q$, and\nretains at least one event outside the straight segment still gives a strictly\nsmaller reading than the stay-at-home clock. No existence or convergence\nclaim for a smoothing family, or equality with the sharp-turn value\n$2T/\\gamma$, is asserted.\n\n## The comparison is not symmetric in the model\n\nOne history of the reunion is the straight segment from $p$ to $q$; the other\ncontains the corner event $r$ of [[texp-sr-piecewise-inertial-twin-reunion]], and\nthe three events $p,r,q$ are not collinear: in the chart of that item their\ncoordinate columns are $(0,0,0,0)$, $(cT,vT,0,0)$ and $(2cT,0,0,0)$ with\n$vT\\ne0$. Every change of inertial chart is an affine bijection\n([[lem-inertial-chart-transitions-are-affine-lorentz]]) and maps collinear\ntriples to collinear triples, so no change of inertial frame maps the image of\nthe stay-at-home history onto the image of the traveler's history, nor fixes both\nendpoints and exchanges the two histories. The two twins are therefore not\nrelated by an inertial change of description, and the difference of readings is\na model consequence of the different histories, not a consequence of who is\n'really moving': the symmetric time-dilation relation of\n[[lem-sr-boost-time-length-and-velocity-formulas]] (i) concerns the two frames,\nwhile the reunion comparison concerns the two clock readings at the single\ncommon event $q$.\n\n## Synchronization bookkeeping at the turn\n\nIn each momentary inertial rest frame of the traveler the stay-at-home clock runs\nslow by the factor $1/\\gamma$, and the traveler may legitimately apply the\ntime-dilation relation using successive pairs of simultaneous events, one\non each clock history in that momentary frame ([[lem-sr-boost-time-length-and-velocity-formulas]] (i),\n[[def-sr-simultaneity-slice-and-rest-distance]]). But the two legs use different\ninertial frames. With $\\gamma=(1-v^2/c^2)^{-1/2}$ and the turn event $r$ of\n[[texp-sr-piecewise-inertial-twin-reunion]], choose both rest-frame charts with origin $p$ as in\n[[texp-sr-piecewise-inertial-twin-reunion]]; the outbound chart\n$x'$ then satisfies $x'^0=\\gamma\\bigl(x^0-\\frac vc x^1\\bigr)$, so its slice through\n$r$ has $x'^0=\\gamma cT(1-v^2/c^2)=cT/\\gamma$ and meets the stay-at-home\nworldline $x^1=0$ at $x^0=\\frac{1}{\\gamma}\\cdot\\frac{cT}{\\gamma}=cT(1-v^2/c^2)$,\nthat is at coordinate time $t=T(1-v^2/c^2)=T/\\gamma^2$; the inbound rest frame's\nchart $x''$ satisfies $x''^0=\\gamma\\bigl(x^0+\\frac vc x^1\\bigr)$, and its slice\nthrough $r$ meets the same worldline at $x^0=cT(1+v^2/c^2)$, that is at\ncoordinate time $t=T(1+v^2/c^2)$. The two slices differ by\n$2Tv^2/c^2$ in coordinate time, and the identity\n$$2T=\\frac{T}{\\gamma^2}+\\frac{2Tv^2}{c^2}+\\frac{T}{\\gamma^2},$$\nwhich only uses $1/\\gamma^2=1-v^2/c^2$, inserts this gap into the traveler's two\nleg-by-leg accounts and reproduces the stay-at-home reading $2T$ at the reunion,\nin agreement with the conditional comparison result\n[[pthm-sr-same-endpoint-clock-comparison]]. The naive reciprocity statement\n'each twin judges the other's clock to run slow' therefore compares different\npairs of events for the two travelers — events simultaneous in different\nmomentary frames — and does not apply to the reunion comparison, which concerns\nthe two clock readings at the single common event $q$.\n\n## Empirical qualification\n\nEverything above is a conditional statement about ideal clocks in the adopted\nmodel: the clocks are ideal with no acceleration-dependent reading term, the\nhistories are prescribed, spacetime is flat and $c$ is fixed\n([[post-sr-free-trajectories-and-clock-hypothesis]],\n[[post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light]]). No measurement, apparatus, calibration,\nsampling or uncertainty model is involved, and no claim is made about real\nclocks or about nature; real clock comparisons would additionally require\napparatus and uncertainty analysis and are outside this remark.",
research/plan-spec.json:84028:          "strategy": "Definition of symmetric C^k type-(2,0) fields for k>=0, and of their divergence only for k>=1, with purely numerical component conventions. Symmetry is chart-independent by the rank-two tensor transformation law of [[thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation]], clause (iii). For k>=1, under an affine inertial transition xprime=Lambda x+a the constant inverse Jacobian in partialprime_a cancels the first Lambda factor in Tprime^{ab}; hence partialprime_a Tprime^{ab}=Lambda^b_d partial_c T^{cd}, clause (iv). This proves chart-independence of the divergence field. The constants and component families in (b) are numerical definitions, not assertions of measured energy or momentum. No conservation, positivity, matter model, slice convergence or choice principle is asserted. The C0 case is well-defined by the existing tensor-field supplier, which permits k=0; the rank-two component law and symmetry use finite linear evaluation and no derivative of T. The observer decomposition expands delta=h-n tensor n pointwise, so its original continuous-T scope and all contraction/uniqueness conclusions are retained. The C1 and higher cases keep exactly the original divergence and balance claims.",
research/plan-spec.json:84182:          "statement": "Let $\\Omega\\subseteq A$ be a nonempty open set with an inertial chart $x$, let $c,K>0$ be numerical constants, let $V:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ be smooth and let $\\varphi:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ be a smooth real scalar field ([[def-ck-tensor-field-on-minkowski-space]]). For every smooth compactly supported $h:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ define the first variation $$\\delta S(\\varphi)[h]:=-\\frac{K}{c}\\int_\\Omega\\Big(\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\varphi\\,\\partial_bh+V'(\\varphi)h\\Big)\\,d^4x,$$ a finite Riemann integral by compact support of $h$ ([[lem-sr-compact-variation-and-fundamental-lemma]]\\,(c)). Then (a) $\\delta S(\\varphi)[h]=\\dfrac{K}{c}\\int_\\Omega\\big(\\Box\\varphi-V'(\\varphi)\\big)h\\,d^4x$, where $\\Box\\varphi:=\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\partial_b\\varphi$; (b) $\\delta S(\\varphi)[h]=0$ for every such $h$ if and only if $\\Box\\varphi=V'(\\varphi)$ on $\\Omega$. The equation in (b) is called the scalar-field equation of this model. The statement is the first variation of the action on compactly supported variations; it asserts nothing about convergence of $S$ on unbounded domains, no boundary term, no dynamics beyond the equivalence in (b) and no empirical fact. No choice principle is used. Precisely, with $L(\\phi):=\\tfrac12\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\phi\\partial_b\\phi+V(\\phi)$, define the relative action $S_h(s):=-(K/c)\\int_\\Omega[L(\\varphi+sh)-L(\\varphi)]\\,d^4x$. Its integrand has support in $\\operatorname{supp}h$, so it is defined for every real $s$ even when either background action diverges; $S_h\\prime(0)=\\delta S(\\varphi)[h]$.",
research/plan-spec.json:84183:          "strategy": "For a fixed compactly supported h, the density difference L(phi+sh)-L(phi) and its s-derivative are supported in supp(h). On each bounded parameter interval they are continuous with continuous s-derivative, so [[lem-sr-compact-variation-and-fundamental-lemma]], clause (c), differentiates the relative action and gives exactly the stated first variation. Apply clause (b) to h and eta^{ab} partial_a phi, noting the metric components are constant, to obtain delta S=(K/c) integral(Box phi-Vprime(phi))*h. Mixed partial symmetry gives the displayed Box formula. If the residual is zero, stationarity follows. Conversely stationarity against every compactly supported h makes the continuous residual vanish by clause (a), including both signs. Neither a full-domain background action nor a boundary condition is used. This is the compact-support flat specialization of Tong (4.42)-(4.43) and its equation-of-motion calculation, with the relative functional made explicit; no choice principle is used.",
research/plan-spec.json:84235:          "statement": "Let $\\Omega\\subseteq A$ be any nonempty open set with inertial chart $x$, let $c,K>0$ be numerical constants, and let $V:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ and $\\varphi:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ be smooth. For each smooth symmetric $(0,2)$ tensor $h$ with compact support, set $g_{ab}(s)=\\eta_{ab}+s h_{ab}$. Choose $s_0>0$ so that these matrices are nondegenerate with negative determinant for $|s|<s_0$; outside $\\operatorname{supp}h$ they equal $\\eta$. Put $D_h(s,x):=[\\tfrac12 g^{ab}(s,x)\\partial_a\\varphi(x)\\partial_b\\varphi(x)+V(\\varphi(x))]\\sqrt{-\\det g(s,x)}$ and define the relative action $$\\mathcal A_h(s):=-\\frac Kc\\int_\\Omega[D_h(s,x)-D_h(0,x)]\\,d^4x.$$ This is the integral of a compactly supported density difference, without assuming either background density has a finite integral. (a) $\\mathcal A_h$ is differentiable at $0$ and $$\\mathcal A_h\\prime(0)=\\frac1{2c}\\int_\\Omega T^{ab}h_{ab}\\,d^4x,\\qquad T^{ab}:=K[\\partial^a\\varphi\\partial^b\\varphi-\\eta^{ab}(\\tfrac12\\partial_d\\varphi\\partial^d\\varphi+V(\\varphi))],$$ with indices raised by $\\eta$. (b) $T$ is smooth and symmetric, and is the unique continuous symmetric $(2,0)$ field for which $\\int_\\Omega T^{ab}h_{ab}\\,d^4x=2c\\mathcal A_h\\prime(0)$ for every such $h$. It is called the Hilbert stress-energy tensor of this scalar-field density. (c) The result holds on unrestricted open $\\Omega$, uses only compactly supported metric variations, and imposes no convergence or boundary condition at infinity. If the full background action happens to exist, the relative action is its variation difference and gives the same derivative. No choice principle is used.",
research/plan-spec.json:84236:          "strategy": "On the compact support of h its entries are bounded. The determinant is a polynomial in s with continuous coefficients, and det(eta)=-1; compactness supplies a uniform s0 for which det(g)<0. The adjugate formula then makes the inverse entries smooth in s and x, as is the square root. Outside supp(h), g=eta. Thus D_h(s)-D_h(0) and its parameter derivative have a common compact support; the compact-variation lemma applies to the relative integral.\nDifferentiate g^{ac}g_cb=delta^a_b to obtain delta g^{ab}=-eta^{ac}eta^{bd}h_cd=-h^{ab}. Cofactor expansion gives delta det(g)=det(eta)*eta^{ab}h_ab=-eta^{ab}h_ab, so delta sqrt(-det(g))=+(1/2)eta^{ab}h_ab. Therefore delta D=-(1/2)h^{ab}partial_a phi partial_b phi+(1/2)eta^{ab}h_ab L(phi). Multiplication by -K/c gives exactly +(1/(2c))integral T^{ab}h_ab. Symmetry and smoothness follow from the formula. For uniqueness, take a continuous symmetric difference Q: a test with one diagonal component psi gives integral Q^{aa}psi=0; a test with h_ab=h_ba=psi for a<b gives 2 integral Q^{ab}psi=0. The signed fundamental lemma yields every component Q^{ab}=0. Tong (4.46) uses the inverse-metric variation convention; converting it to the covariant variation delta g_ab=h_ab yields the positive sign here. This convention change, compact density difference, and explicit finite-dimensional derivation preserve the source-derived formula without presuming a convergent background action. No choice principle is used.",
research/plan-spec.json:84592:          "strategy": "Adopted physical premise; no proof obligation (the schema records postulates with formulation, scope and sources, not a deduction). The mathematical content used in the formulation is confined to the model equations and the displayed tensors; conservation is deliberately NOT part of this postulate. Sources: the scalar action and equation of motion are 4.5.1 of Tong; the fluid equations are 4.5.4 of Tong and the displayed tensors are (4.53) of Tong, (1.106)-(1.110) of Carroll and 9.2.3-9.2.6 of Crowell; the choice to adopt, rather than derive, the matter dynamics follows the source statements that conservation follows from each model's own equations of motion (Carroll p. 30) and that matter actions and equations of state are separate model assumptions (Tong 4.5). No empirical support is claimed. The observer component reading removed from mathematical definitions/lemmas is explicitly adopted in(v), including the exact c factors: $\\partial_0=c^{-1}\\partial_t$ turns the tensor balance into the two displayed SI balances, while the observer-decomposition lemma provides only the numerical contractions/components. This is preservation of the framework's stated interpretation, not a new empirical law or a deduction of an observer measurement. Local relative actions, not a possibly divergent background integral over all Omega, formulate(i).",
research/plan-spec.json:86626:          "procedure": "**Count and fit objects.** A spectrum in this account is a finite list of frequency settings $f_i\\in\\mathbb R$ with detected pulse counts $n_{i,h}\\in\\mathbb N_0$ in each switching configuration $h\\in\\{1,2,3,4\\}$ and window. Fluorescence denotes the emitted light counted by the photomultipliers; a dark count is a registered pulse without applied excitation. Subtracted or averaged spectra are real-valued lists, not nonnegative integer counts or independent trials. A Lorentzian peak plus linear background means a fitted function $L:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ of the form $L(f)=A/[1+4(f-f_0)^2/w^2]+b_0+b_1(f-f_0)$ with $A>0$, $w>0$; $f_0,w$ have frequency units, and the peak term has half height at $f_0\\pm w/2$. Residuals are the differences of processed values and the fitted values at the recorded settings. A weighted average of fitted centers has form $\\sum_i w_i f_{0,i}/\\sum_i w_i$ for nonnegative weights with positive sum; the report does not give the weights or their construction. Frequency locking means controlling a laser reading relative to a specified reference or offset; second-harmonic generation denotes conversion to twice the optical frequency. Optical-optical double resonance is the selected condition that both listed transitions are resonant for the same ion velocity class; it is an apparatus selection model, not a measured probability law.\n\nThe parallel beam was generated by doubling a $772\\,\\mathrm{nm}$ Ti:Sa laser locked to an iodine marker previously calibrated with a frequency comb against a rubidium clock. The antiparallel diode laser was offset-locked to a rubidium reference, with a $600\\,\\mathrm{MHz}$ tuning range. Polarization-maintaining fibers transported the beams. Motorized stages controlled angles and positions to $20\\,\\mu\\mathrm{rad}$ and $50\\,\\mu\\mathrm{m}$; scrapers established laser/ion overlap within $80\\,\\mu\\mathrm{rad}$. Photomultipliers counted fluorescence in four successive $100\\,\\mu\\mathrm{s}$ configurations: both excitation beams, antiparallel only, parallel only, and neither. Dark counts were subtracted, then the separate-beam background was subtracted from the both-beam spectrum. Optical-optical double resonance selected ions for which both Lambda legs were resonant at the same velocity. To compensate metastable depletion, an $81$-point scan was paused after $69$ points for injection and cooling. Figure 2 shows an example averaged over $44$ scans, with $49\\,\\mathrm{MHz}$ laboratory linewidth and $35\\,\\mathrm{MHz}$ rest-frame linewidth. The authors fitted $40$ spectra with Lorentzians on linear backgrounds and formed a weighted average. Raw count tables, fit weights, a covariance matrix and a complete likelihood are not published in this report.",
research/plan-spec.json:86628:          "uncertainty": "The Gouy phase is the additional spatial phase of a focused beam relative to a plane-wave phase; for a $C^1$ phase correction $\\psi:J\\to\\mathbb R$ on a proper-time interval $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$, the phase-reading convention assigns a frequency correction $-(2\\pi)^{-1}d\\psi/d\\tau$ under the stipulated phase-reading interpretation. No beam profile $\\psi$ is reconstructed here. The $1\\sigma$ notation below is the source\\'s label for an uncertainty scale; without its full estimation model it specifies neither a guaranteed interval nor a sampling distribution.\n\nTable I labels its uncertainties $1\\sigma$. In MHz, the aggregate entries are $0.43$ for each rest frequency, $1.6$ for the antiparallel frequency and $1.25$ for the parallel frequency. Antiparallel contributions are: weighted-fit statistical uncertainty $0.6$, rubidium reference $0.05$, diode stabilization $0.64$, background linearization $0.5$, Gouy phase $1.0$, angular alignment $0.6$, and ion divergence $0.4$. Parallel contributions are: iodine reference $0.06$, Ti:Sa stabilization $0.12$, Gouy phase $1.0$, alignment $0.6$, and divergence $0.4$. The inferred Gouy correction for each laser was $0.44\\pm1.0\\,\\mathrm{MHz}$, from experimentally validated numerical simulation using measured beam parameters. The authors reported no significant residual asymmetry; simulated background linearization was assigned $0.5\\,\\mathrm{MHz}$. The excess linewidth, about five times the expected width, was attributed mainly to drift-induced fluorescence. The $0.6\\,\\mathrm{MHz}$ statistical entry comes from the weighted analysis of forty fitted spectra, not forty independent raw clock readings. These reported statistical and systematic scales are preserved without inventing independence, a Gaussian total sampling law, fit weights, covariance or coverage.",
research/plan-spec.json:86629:          "interpretation": "The measured observable tests the combined Doppler/time-dilation, active atomic-transition and vacuum-wave invariance assumptions together with resonance selection, calibration, beam alignment and correction models. The source reports no resolved deviation at its uncertainty level and quotes $|\\alpha|\\le2.0\\times10^{-8}$ for $\\alpha=\\epsilon/\\beta^2$. That quotation is recorded as the source's test-theory sensitivity statement, rather than a newly certified hard bound or confidence interval. RMS and SME parameter translations and future higher-boost prospects remain contextual research topics; they are not prerequisites or claims of the comparison theorem.",
research/plan-spec.json:86632:            "uncertainty": "Reported u_epsilon = 2.3 x 10^-9; includes fitting, calibration, background, Gouy-phase, alignment and divergence uncertainty scales; no probability or coverage guaranteed.",
research/plan-spec.json:86636:          "statement": "## Setup\n\n**Quantities and apparatus notation.** The laboratory speed parameter is $\\beta=v/c$, with $v$ the selected ion-class speed in an approximately inertial laboratory description; its quoted value is nominal. Frequencies $\\nu_1,\\nu_2,\\nu_p,\\nu_a>0$ are scalar estimates in hertz of the two rest transitions and the parallel/antiparallel laser readings, respectively; the ideal-model convention is $\\nu=\\omega/(2\\pi)$ and $\\lambda=c/\\nu$ from [[def-sr-null-wave-covector-and-observer-frequency]], while the real apparatus has finite-width spectra. Here $\\mathrm{MHz}=10^6\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}$, $\\mathrm{nm}=10^{-9}\\,\\mathrm m$, and $\\mu\\mathrm{rad}=10^{-6}$ radians. For a single-peaked nonnegative profile, its full width at half maximum (FWHM) is the separation of the two frequencies (or speeds) at half its peak height, when those crossings exist; the reported $\\delta v/v$ uses the speed width divided by the selected speed. Beam divergence and alignment are angles between ion trajectories and the specified beam axis. A Lambda system here means three source-labelled atomic levels, two lower and one common upper, with the two listed driven transitions; the symbols $3S_1$, $3P_2$ and $F$ distinguish the report's atomic levels, rather than supplying a quantum-state model. Metastable lifetime means the reported characteristic persistence time of the prepared level; no survival distribution is inferred. The Penning source, ESR (Experimental Storage Ring), electron cooling, lasers and optics below are the report's named preparation and control apparatus; their performance is reported empirically, not derived from SR. The energy label $\\mathrm{MeV/u}$ is energy per atomic-mass unit: $1\\,\\mathrm{MeV}=10^6$ electronvolts, with one electronvolt the energy acquired by elementary charge across one volt; it is not used here to deduce the nominal speed.\n\nThe experiment used $^7\\mathrm{Li}^+$ ions from a Penning ion gauge source, accelerated to $58.6\\,\\mathrm{MeV/u}$ and stored in the ESR at nominal $\\beta=0.338$. Electron cooling reduced the reported longitudinal velocity spread to $\\delta v/v\\approx9\\times10^{-6}$ (FWHM) and transverse divergence to less than $100\\,\\mu\\mathrm{rad}$. About $10^7$ to $10^8$ ions were stored, with an estimated metastable fraction below $1\\%$. The metastable $3S_1$ lifetime was $15$–$30\\,\\mathrm{s}$ in the ring, compared with $50\\,\\mathrm{s}$ in vacuum. The Lambda system used the transitions $3S_1(F=3/2)\\to3P_2(F=5/2)$ and $3S_1(F=5/2)\\to3P_2(F=5/2)$. Parallel and antiparallel beams near $386\\,\\mathrm{nm}$ and $780\\,\\mathrm{nm}$ addressed its two legs; an additional $780\\,\\mathrm{nm}$ beam repumped ions into the system.\n\n## Procedure\n\n**Count and fit objects.** A spectrum in this account is a finite list of frequency settings $f_i\\in\\mathbb R$ with detected pulse counts $n_{i,h}\\in\\mathbb N_0$ in each switching configuration $h\\in\\{1,2,3,4\\}$ and window. Fluorescence denotes the emitted light counted by the photomultipliers; a dark count is a registered pulse without applied excitation. Subtracted or averaged spectra are real-valued lists, not nonnegative integer counts or independent trials. A Lorentzian peak plus linear background means a fitted function $L:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ of the form $L(f)=A/[1+4(f-f_0)^2/w^2]+b_0+b_1(f-f_0)$ with $A>0$, $w>0$; $f_0,w$ have frequency units, and the peak term has half height at $f_0\\pm w/2$. Residuals are the differences of processed values and the fitted values at the recorded settings. A weighted average of fitted centers has form $\\sum_i w_i f_{0,i}/\\sum_i w_i$ for nonnegative weights with positive sum; the report does not give the weights or their construction. Frequency locking means controlling a laser reading relative to a specified reference or offset; second-harmonic generation denotes conversion to twice the optical frequency. Optical-optical double resonance is the selected condition that both listed transitions are resonant for the same ion velocity class; it is an apparatus selection model, not a measured probability law.\n\nThe parallel beam was generated by doubling a $772\\,\\mathrm{nm}$ Ti:Sa laser locked to an iodine marker previously calibrated with a frequency comb against a rubidium clock. The antiparallel diode laser was offset-locked to a rubidium reference, with a $600\\,\\mathrm{MHz}$ tuning range. Polarization-maintaining fibers transported the beams. Motorized stages controlled angles and positions to $20\\,\\mu\\mathrm{rad}$ and $50\\,\\mu\\mathrm{m}$; scrapers established laser/ion overlap within $80\\,\\mu\\mathrm{rad}$. Photomultipliers counted fluorescence in four successive $100\\,\\mu\\mathrm{s}$ configurations: both excitation beams, antiparallel only, parallel only, and neither. Dark counts were subtracted, then the separate-beam background was subtracted from the both-beam spectrum. Optical-optical double resonance selected ions for which both Lambda legs were resonant at the same velocity. To compensate metastable depletion, an $81$-point scan was paused after $69$ points for injection and cooling. Figure 2 shows an example averaged over $44$ scans, with $49\\,\\mathrm{MHz}$ laboratory linewidth and $35\\,\\mathrm{MHz}$ rest-frame linewidth. The authors fitted $40$ spectra with Lorentzians on linear backgrounds and formed a weighted average. Raw count tables, fit weights, a covariance matrix and a complete likelihood are not published in this report.\n\n## Observations\n\nTable I gives the frequency estimates, in MHz, $\\nu_1=546455143.0$, $\\nu_2=546474960.7$, $\\nu_a=384225534.98$ and $\\nu_p=777210326.98$. The rest-frame inputs $\\nu_1,\\nu_2$ come from earlier measurements cited as references 21–22; those papers were not retrieved or read here. Equation (4) reports $\\epsilon=\\sqrt{\\nu_a\\nu_p/(\\nu_1\\nu_2)}-1=(1.5\\pm2.3)\\times10^{-9}$ at nominal $\\beta=0.338$. These are estimates with the source-labelled $1\\sigma$ uncertainty budget, not exact values or guaranteed bounds. The rounded Table I centers yield approximately $1.4744849565\\times10^{-9}$, consistent with the reported rounded estimate, without reconstructing unrounded data.\n\n## Uncertainty\n\nThe Gouy phase is the additional spatial phase of a focused beam relative to a plane-wave phase; for a $C^1$ phase correction $\\psi:J\\to\\mathbb R$ on a proper-time interval $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$, the phase-reading convention assigns a frequency correction $-(2\\pi)^{-1}d\\psi/d\\tau$ under the stipulated phase-reading interpretation. No beam profile $\\psi$ is reconstructed here. The $1\\sigma$ notation below is the source\\'s label for an uncertainty scale; without its full estimation model it specifies neither a guaranteed interval nor a sampling distribution.\n\nTable I labels its uncertainties $1\\sigma$. In MHz, the aggregate entries are $0.43$ for each rest frequency, $1.6$ for the antiparallel frequency and $1.25$ for the parallel frequency. Antiparallel contributions are: weighted-fit statistical uncertainty $0.6$, rubidium reference $0.05$, diode stabilization $0.64$, background linearization $0.5$, Gouy phase $1.0$, angular alignment $0.6$, and ion divergence $0.4$. Parallel contributions are: iodine reference $0.06$, Ti:Sa stabilization $0.12$, Gouy phase $1.0$, alignment $0.6$, and divergence $0.4$. The inferred Gouy correction for each laser was $0.44\\pm1.0\\,\\mathrm{MHz}$, from experimentally validated numerical simulation using measured beam parameters. The authors reported no significant residual asymmetry; simulated background linearization was assigned $0.5\\,\\mathrm{MHz}$. The excess linewidth, about five times the expected width, was attributed mainly to drift-induced fluorescence. The $0.6\\,\\mathrm{MHz}$ statistical entry comes from the weighted analysis of forty fitted spectra, not forty independent raw clock readings. These reported statistical and systematic scales are preserved without inventing independence, a Gaussian total sampling law, fit weights, covariance or coverage.\n\n## Interpretation\n\nThe measured observable tests the combined Doppler/time-dilation, active atomic-transition and vacuum-wave invariance assumptions together with resonance selection, calibration, beam alignment and correction models. The source reports no resolved deviation at its uncertainty level and quotes $|\\alpha|\\le2.0\\times10^{-8}$ for $\\alpha=\\epsilon/\\beta^2$. That quotation is recorded as the source's test-theory sensitivity statement, rather than a newly certified hard bound or confidence interval. RMS and SME parameter translations and future higher-boost prospects remain contextual research topics; they are not prerequisites or claims of the comparison theorem.",
research/plan-spec.json:86706:              "uncertainty": "Reported u_epsilon = 2.3 x 10^-9; includes fitting, calibration, background, Gouy-phase, alignment and divergence uncertainty scales; no probability or coverage guaranteed.",
research/plan-spec.json:89879:          "statement": "This remark separates the mathematics of\n[[thm-gr-point-normal-and-exponential-coordinate-charts]] and\n[[thm-gr-local-geodesics-and-exponential-map]] from the physical reading\nusually attached to them, and is bookkeeping: it establishes no mathematical\nresult and no physical law.\n\n- **What the construction gives.** For every event $p$ there is a chart with\n  $g_{ab}(p)=\\eta_{ab}$, $\\Gamma^a{}_{bc}(p)=0$ and\n  $\\partial_ag_{bc}(p)=0$, and in the exponential chart of an orthonormal\n  basis the geodesics through $p$ are the straight coordinate lines through\n  the origin\n  ([[thm-gr-point-normal-and-exponential-coordinate-charts]]). The identities\n  are first order at the single point $p$.\n\n- **What it does not give.** The construction asserts nothing about the second\n  and higher derivatives of the metric, and therefore nothing about the\n  curvature of the model; a chart of this kind is not an isometry and does not\n  make any neighborhood of $p$ identical with a region of Minkowski space.\n  The remark makes no curvature claim here; the second-order behavior is the\n  subject of the curvature material of this track, which is a later page of\n  the same category.\n\n- **No global inertial frame, no congruence.** A chart with the normal-form\n  identities at one event is not a global label of events by inertial\n  coordinates, and the exponential charts at different points are not\n  compatible frames. An \"observer\" in this library is a single future unit\n  timelike vector at a point, and a \"congruence\" is a field of such vectors\n  on an open set ([[def-gr-observer-and-rest-space]],\n  [[def-gr-observer-congruence-and-adapted-charts]]); a freely falling\n  observer is one timelike geodesic, not a global reference frame. Charts\n  adapted to a congruence are a different construction from the normal\n  coordinates of a geodesic through a point.\n\n- **The physical reading is adopted, not derived.** The statement that an\n  ideal freely falling test body or observer, using normal coordinates around\n  an event of its own worldline, finds the local form of the metric to be that\n  of Minkowski space at first order — and the associated local\n  inertial-frame reading of the equivalence principle — depends on the\n  adopted clock and free-fall assumptions of\n  [[post-gr-ideal-clocks-and-free-fall]]. It is a statement about the model in\n  the test-body idealization, not a derivation from the geometry alone and not\n  an empirical claim that real experiments cannot detect a gravitational\n  field locally. The limitations recorded in the postulate — finite-size and\n  tidal couplings of extended bodies, self-force, and nonlocal measurements —\n  are precisely what the first-order identities cannot exclude.",
research/plan-spec.json:96332:                "title": "D. Tong, General Relativity (Cambridge Part III Mathematical Tripos, Michaelmas 2019), section 1.3.1: the geodesic equations in the Schwarzschild background and the constants of motion, printed pp. 29-31 (PDF pp. 36-38); section 6.1: the Killing vector partial_t, printed p. 232 (PDF p. 239)",
research/plan-spec.json:97064:                "title": "D. Tong, General Relativity (Cambridge Part III Mathematical Tripos, Michaelmas 2019), sections 1.3.1-1.3.2: the geodesic equations for massive particles in the Schwarzschild background, circular orbits, the innermost stable circular orbit at r = 6GM and the photon sphere, printed pp. 29-33 (PDF pp. 36-40); section 6.1: the same results in the black-hole chapter, printed pp. 232-235 (PDF pp. 239-242)",
research/plan-spec.json:97566:          "statement": "This item assumes $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ as inherited through the Riemannian\nitems cited below ([[def-countable-choice]]); no further choice principle is\nused.\n\n**(a) The datum.** Let $\\Sigma$ be a connected smooth three-manifold without\nboundary ([[def-smooth-manifold]]) and let $\\gamma$ be a Riemannian metric on\n$\\Sigma$ ([[def-riemannian-metric-and-riemannian-manifold]]). Let\n$k\\in\\{-1,0,1\\}$. The pair $(\\Sigma,\\gamma)$ is a **spatial model of constant\nnormalized curvature $k$**, with **normalized curvature parameter** $k$, when\n\n$$\\operatorname{Ric}(\\gamma)=2k\\,\\gamma\\qquad\\text{at every point of }\\Sigma ,$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{Ric}(\\gamma)$ is the Ricci curvature of $\\gamma$\n([[def-ricci-curvature]]). We then call $(\\Sigma,\\gamma,k)$ a **spatial model\ndatum**. The signs are named for orientation only: $k=+1$ is called the\n**closed** or **positively curved** case, $k=0$ the **flat** case and $k=-1$\nthe **open** or **negatively curved** case; no topological content is attached\nto these names by this definition.\n\n**(b) Status of the condition.** The condition in (a) is pointwise and local.\nIt neither asserts nor implies that $\\Sigma$ is complete, compact, simply\nconnected or geodesically complete, and it does not determine the global\nmodel: this definition proves no classification theorem and this page assumes\nnone. In dimension three the condition is equivalent to the statement that\n$\\gamma$ has constant sectional curvature $k$; the companion lemma on\nconstant-curvature three-manifolds, recorded in the Remarks, proves that\nequivalence. What is used by the cosmological deductions of this page is\nexactly the Ricci form displayed in (a).\n\n**(c) Normalization and scale.** The condition (a) fixes the numerical\ncurvature to $k\\in\\{-1,0,1\\}$. For a constant $\\lambda>0$, put\n$\\gamma'=\\lambda^2\\gamma$. Then\n\n$$\\operatorname{Ric}(\\gamma')=\\operatorname{Ric}(\\gamma)=2k\\gamma=2(k/\\lambda^2)\\gamma',$$\n\nand $K_{\\gamma'}=k/\\lambda^2$\n([[lem-gr-constant-rescaling-preserves-levi-civita-and-curvature]]).\nThe sign is unchanged, but for $k\\ne0$ the normalized condition (a) is\npreserved only when $\\lambda=1$; the flat condition is preserved for every\n$\\lambda>0$. Conversely, if $\\operatorname{Ric}(h)=2\\kappa h$ for a real\nconstant $\\kappa\\ne0$, the metric $\\gamma=|\\kappa|h$ satisfies\n$\\operatorname{Ric}(\\gamma)=2\\operatorname{sgn}(\\kappa)\\gamma$ by the same\nRicci invariance, so it restores the normalization. A physical model assigns\nits spatial length scale through the further factor in the model postulate.\n\n**(d) Standard models.** For each $k\\in\\{-1,0,1\\}$ at least one complete,\nconnected, boundaryless, simply connected model satisfying (a) is available:\n\n- For $k=+1$, take the round unit sphere $S^3\\subset\\mathbb R^4$ with its\n  induced metric. Its constant curvature $+1$, completeness and connected\n  boundaryless Riemannian structure are supplied by\n  [[prop-round-sphere-model-geometry]] with $n=3$, $R=1$ (the curvature\n  calculation is also [[ex-the-round-sphere-has-positive-constant-sectional-curvature]]);\n  its simple connectedness is [[thm-higher-dimensional-spheres-are-simply-connected]].\n- For $k=0$, take $\\mathbb R^3$ with the standard metric. Its Riemannian\n  structure and vanishing curvature are [[ex-euclidean-space-has-zero-curvature]].\n  It is nonempty and convex, hence simply connected by\n  [[thm-convex-subsets-have-trivial-fundamental-group]], and thus connected by\n  [[thm-path-connected-implies-connected]]. For completeness, the constant\n  metric coefficients give zero Christoffel symbols\n  ([[prop-christoffel-formula-for-the-levi-civita-connection]]); for every\n  initial position $p$ and velocity $v$, the all-time curve $t\\mapsto p+tv$\n  solves the coordinate geodesic equation\n  ([[prop-coordinate-geodesic-equation]]). Uniqueness of the maximal geodesic\n  ([[thm-existence-uniqueness-and-smooth-dependence-of-geodesics]]) identifies\n  it with that curve, so every maximal geodesic has domain $\\mathbb R$.\n  Hopf–Rinow ([[thm-hopf-rinow]]) then gives metric completeness; its\n  hypotheses hold because $\\mathbb R^3$ is nonempty, connected and boundaryless.\n- For $k=-1$, take the upper half-space $\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^3:x^3>0\\}$ with\n  $y=x^3$ and metric $y^{-2}\\sum_{a=1}^3dx^a\\otimes dx^a$.\n  Its complete, connected, boundaryless, simply connected Riemannian\n  structure and constant curvature $-1$ are exactly the $n=3$, $a=1$\n  instance of [[prop-half-space-model-geometry]].\n\nBy the companion lemma recorded in the Remarks, constant sectional curvature\n$k$ in dimension three gives $\\operatorname{Ric}(\\gamma)=2k\\gamma$, so each\nmodel satisfies (a) and each sign is realized. A model datum restricts to one\nof these global standard models only when its postulate says so.",
research/plan-spec.json:97578:            "thm-higher-dimensional-spheres-are-simply-connected",
research/plan-spec.json:97596:            "thm-higher-dimensional-spheres-are-simply-connected": "mathematical-premise",
research/plan-spec.json:98111:          "statement": "Adopt as the geometry and matter content of a **homogeneous cosmological\nmodel** of the classical gravitational framework, to be used together with\n[[post-gr-spacetime-and-classical-domain]],\n[[post-gr-field-and-matter-assumptions]] and the field law of\n[[post-gr-einstein-field-law]], the following assumptions.\n\n**(i) Spacetime geometry.** The model's spacetime is an FLRW model datum\n$(I,\\Sigma,\\gamma,k,a)$ with the framework's conversion constant $c>0$, its\nmetric $g=-c^2dt^2+a(t)^2\\gamma$ and its comoving congruence\n$n=c^{-1}\\partial_t$ ([[def-gr-flrw-model-data]],\n[[lem-gr-flrw-metric-and-comoving-data]]). The spatial model\n$(\\Sigma,\\gamma,k)$ is one of the standard complete simply connected models of\n[[def-gr-flrw-spatial-model]] clause (d), with normalized curvature parameter\n$k\\in\\{-1,0,1\\}$: the round unit three-sphere for $k=+1$, Euclidean space for\n$k=0$, or hyperbolic three-space for $k=-1$; by\n[[lem-gr-constant-curvature-three-manifold-ricci]] each of these carries\nconstant sectional curvature $k$ and $\\operatorname{Ric}(\\gamma)=2k\\gamma$.\nThe name **homogeneous and isotropic cosmological model** denotes here the\nexplicit standard-spatial-model FLRW ansatz just specified. Its operative\ngeometric assumptions are the chosen global standard model, the displayed\nmetric and the curvature condition; the deductions do not use an additional\nundefined symmetry premise. The identification of the coordinate\n$t$ with the readings of ideal comoving clocks is not part of this clause: it\nis the separate clock rule of [[post-gr-ideal-clocks-and-free-fall]], applied\nonly where a physical clock reading is asserted. The\nmathematical content used by the deductions of this page is the FLRW form\ndisplayed above together with the curvature condition; no classification\ntheorem, and no transitivity statement about isometry groups, is proved or\nused here.\n\n**(ii) Matter content.** Exactly one of the following two sectors is adopted.\n\n- **Vacuum sector.** No matter is present: the model's stress-energy tensor is\n  the zero tensor, $\\varepsilon=p=0$.\n- **Comoving perfect-fluid sector.** The model's matter is a perfect-fluid\n  model $(\\varepsilon,p,n_F)$ on the matter domain $U:=M$, the model's whole\n  spacetime, in the sense of [[def-gr-perfect-fluid-matter-model]]: that is,\n  $U=M$ is the nonempty open domain, $\\varepsilon$ and $p$ are the smooth\n  rest energy density and pressure on it, and the fluid's unit timelike\n  four-velocity field $n_F$ is assumed to be the comoving congruence\n  $n=c^{-1}\\partial_t$ of clause (i). The density and pressure are assumed\n  **homogeneous**, that is, functions of cosmic time alone:\n  $\\varepsilon=\\varepsilon(t)$ and $p=p(t)$ in the product charts, with\n  values in the units of [[def-gr-si-unit-and-coordinate-conventions]]. The\n  model additionally adopts an **explicit equation of state**, one of:\n  a barotropic relation $p=P(\\varepsilon)$ with $P$ a smooth real function\n  defined on an open interval containing the range of $\\varepsilon$; or the\n  constant-parameter relation $p=w\\varepsilon$ with a real constant $w$ and\n  the stated domain of positive $\\varepsilon$. The equation of state is an\n  adopted constitutive restriction, not a consequence of the field law.\n  The fluid stress-energy $T_{ab}=(\\varepsilon+p)n_an_b+pg_{ab}$ is the\n  gravitating source in $G_{ab}+\\Lambda g_{ab}=(8\\pi G_N/c^4)T_{ab}$\n  ([[post-gr-einstein-field-law]]). The scale factor and fluid data must\n  jointly satisfy that law when a solution is claimed; the fluid is not\n  treated as test matter on a separately prescribed background.\n\n**(iii) Status, scope and exclusions.** Clauses (i) and (ii) are adopted\nassumptions of the model. They are not derived from the field law, from\nmathematical symmetry, or from observation; the symmetry of the ansatz\nmotivates but does not prove them, and no claim is made that any actual\ncosmological system is exactly homogeneous, isotropic, comoving or\nbarotropic. The model contains no observed value: the scale factor, the\ndensities, the parameters $k,\\Lambda,w$ and the spacetime interval are model\ndata, and every conclusion conditional on the model inherits the idealizations\nlisted in the physical scope of this item. Anisotropic stresses, heat flow and\ndissipation, spatial inhomogeneities and perturbations, distributional or\nlower-dimensional sources, non-smooth fields and quantum effects lie outside\nthe model and require additional content. Comparisons of the model's\npredictions with reported observations test the combined model — geometry,\nmatter content, field law and the observation and analysis assumptions — and\nagreement is evidence about that combination, not a proof of this postulate.",
research/plan-spec.json:99220:          "physical_scope": "Conditional deduction inside the classical gravitational framework: a one-parameter family of models of the adopted field law on an open set of the flat model, with smooth metric and stress-energy families, expanded to first order about the flat metric. The conclusion is the linearized equation with the first-order stress coefficient as its source, its vacuum and harmonic-gauge reductions, and, on the zero-source Lambda = 0 branch, a compact-set bound on the exact vacuum residual of a small-amplitude metric. The all-space harmonic-gauge existence clause additionally assumes the Axiom of Countable Choice. Excludes nonlinear back-reaction, non-smooth or distributional sources, curved backgrounds, boundaries, quantum fields and any comparison with observation; the stress-energy family is model input, not derived from the metric perturbation.",
research/plan-spec.json:99366:          "physical_scope": "Conditional model deductions inside the classical gravitational framework with the adopted field law in its vacuum branch Lambda = 0: the first-order perturbation of a family of vacuum model spacetimes solves the linearized vacuum equations; transverse-traceless plane waves with arbitrary bounded smooth profiles are solutions of those equations and have a compact-set exact-vacuum residual of order lambda squared in the small-amplitude chart-control regime; and, at a fixed null wave covector, each real cosine or sine amplitude coefficient modulo plane-wave gauge transformations has a two-dimensional quotient coordinatized by transverse-traceless amplitudes in a chosen screen basis. The full real cosine-and-sine coefficient pair has four real coordinates, representing two polarization types with independent phase coefficients; its first-order curvature tensor is gauge invariant and injective on that full quotient. The item also states the SI coordinate speed and angular-frequency conversion for that frame. Excludes nonlinear waves, radiation from sources, energy and flux statements, curved backgrounds, quantization and any comparison with observation; no measured amplitude, frequency or curvature is asserted.",
research/plan-spec.json:99482:          "statement": "This note fixes what the physics items of the linearized-waves page do and do\nnot claim; it proves nothing by itself.\n\n- **What is proved.** [[pthm-gr-linearized-field-equations]] derives the\n  linearized field equation, its vacuum branch, the harmonic-gauge wave\n  reduction and the quadratic residual bound from the adopted field law\n  [[post-gr-einstein-field-law]] for a family of solutions. The mathematical\n  existence statement for the vacuum plane waves is\n  [[thm-gr-tt-plane-wave-solutions]]: for every pair of bounded smooth\n  profiles there is an exact solution of the linearized vacuum equations in\n  harmonic gauge, whose exact metric satisfies the vacuum field law only up\n  to a residual bounded by a constant times the square of the amplitude on\n  compact sets. [[pthm-gr-plane-wave-polarizations]] reads the amplitude\n  classification of [[lem-gr-plane-wave-gauge-decomposition]] as the\n  statement that the model has two polarizations at fixed null wave covector,\n  with the gauge-invariant first-order curvature as their observable content.\n  [[pthm-gr-plane-wave-tidal-detector-response]] derives the model's\n  relative acceleration of freely falling test bodies and the proper-\n  separation strain of the ideal two-body detector of\n  [[def-gr-linearized-wave-regime-and-detector-coupling]].\n\n- **What is conditional.** Every physical statement on the page is a\n  deduction from the adopted postulates of\n  [[post-gr-spacetime-and-classical-domain]],\n  [[post-gr-field-and-matter-assumptions]],\n  [[post-gr-einstein-field-law]] and\n  [[post-gr-ideal-clocks-and-free-fall]], together with the explicit\n  hypotheses of the mathematical items. The postulates are adopted model\n  assumptions, not derived results; no item on the page establishes the\n  postulates, and no item compares a prediction with a measurement. The\n  metric family $\\eta+\\lambda h$ is an exact solution of the field law only\n  at zeroth order in the amplitude, so the polarization and response\n  statements are statements of the linearized model.\n\n- **Idealizations of the detector.** The \"detector\" of\n  [[def-gr-linearized-wave-regime-and-detector-coupling]] is two freely\n  falling test bodies together with the proper separation of their worldlines\n  at equal inertial-chart time. It has no rigidity, elasticity, internal\n  resonance, readout, noise, light propagation or calibration. The strain\n  function is a model quantity; identifying it with a recorded signal of a\n  real instrument would require an apparatus model with its own assumptions,\n  empirical premises with uncertainties and a statistical analysis, none of\n  which occurs on this page. In particular no numerical value of an\n  amplitude, frequency, strain or event rate is asserted anywhere in the\n  batch.\n\n- **Frame and gauge caveats.** The transverse-traceless form and the\n  amplitudes $A_+,A_\\times$ are defined with respect to an adapted\n  orthonormal frame ([[def-gr-wave-screen-and-tt-projection]],\n  [[lem-gr-plane-wave-gauge-decomposition]]); the pair $(A_+,A_\\times)$\n  transforms under screen rotations as in\n  [[lem-gr-tt-screen-projector]] clause (d), and the space of classes, not\n  the particular representative, is the frame-independent content. The\n  first-order curvature is gauge invariant\n  ([[thm-gr-tt-plane-wave-curvature]] clause (b)). The projection to the\n  physical screen is two-dimensional; a trace subtraction with the half\n  weight of a three- or four-dimensional projector is not the\n  transverse-traceless projection\n  ([[lem-gr-tt-screen-projector]] remark and the counterexample recorded\n  there).\n\n- **What is deferred.** The following are outside this page and are not\n  proved, assumed or silently used: the construction of solutions of the\n  exact vacuum equations with prescribed initial data or from specified\n  sources; the linearization about a non-flat background; radiation from\n  localized slow sources, the quadrupole formula, the energy and angular\n  momentum carried by waves, asymptotic and ADM-type charges, and the\n  far-zone scale separation; the propagation of light in a wave background\n  and the optical response of an interferometer; post-Newtonian and\n  nonlinear corrections; the existence and properties of exact strong-field\n  solutions; quantum aspects of gravitational waves and any gravitational\n  quantum theory. Sources and detectors are experiment-level material and\n  require retrieved primary reports, apparatus and sampling models,\n  uncertainties and a stated statistical inference before any reported\n  observation can be recorded in the library.",
research/plan-spec.json:102322:            "def-higher-derivatives-and-smoothness",
