research/plan-spec.json:105788:          "statement": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be a time-oriented smooth Lorentzian four-manifold\n([[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]) with metric-selected connection $\\nabla$\n([[def-gr-metric-selected-connection]],\n[[thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative]]) and let\n$U\\subseteq M$ be an open set carrying a fixed smooth **background metric**\n$\\bar g$ with Levi-Civita connection $\\bar\\nabla$\n([[thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative]]), whose Christoffel\nsymbols in any chart are $\\bar\\Gamma^a{}_{bc}$\n([[def-christoffel-symbols-of-an-affine-connection]]); the same symbol\n$\\Gamma^a{}_{bc}$ denotes those of $g$. Let $\\kappa>0$ and $\\Lambda\\in\\mathbb R$\nbe constants and let $T$ be a smooth symmetric covariant $2$-tensor field on $U$\n([[def-gr-smooth-covariant-two-tensor-field]]), the **matter stress-energy\ntensor**, with $T=g^{ab}T_{ab}$ its metric trace\n([[def-gr-inverse-metric-and-musical-isomorphisms]]).\n\n**(a) Gauge vector.** The **harmonic-gauge vector field** of the pair\n$(g,\\bar g)$ is the smooth vector field\n$$H^a:=g^{bc}\\bigl(\\Gamma^a{}_{bc}-\\bar\\Gamma^a{}_{bc}\\bigr) .$$\nThe difference of the connection coefficients is a $(1,2)$-tensor, so $H$ is a\nwell-defined smooth vector field independent of the chart used to express the\nsymbols ([[def-gr-tensor-fields-and-components]]).\n\n**(b) Reduced Einstein equation.** The **reduced Einstein equation** for the\npair $(g,T)$ with respect to $\\bar g$ is the second-order quasilinear system\n$$\\operatorname{Ric}_{ab}-\\nabla_{(a}H_{b)}=\\kappa\\bigl(T_{ab}-\\tfrac12Tg_{ab}\\bigr)+\\Lambda g_{ab},$$\nwhere $\\operatorname{Ric}_{ab}$ is the Ricci tensor of $g$\n([[def-gr-curvature-tensor-and-derived-tensors]]),\n$H_b=g_{bc}H^c$ and $\\nabla_{(a}H_{b)}=\\tfrac12(\\nabla_aH_b+\\nabla_bH_a)$.\n\n**(c) Reduced solutions.** A **reduced solution** on $U$ is a pair $(g,T)$ of a\nsmooth Lorentzian metric and a smooth symmetric $2$-tensor on $U$ satisfying\n(b) for the fixed background $\\bar g$.\n\n**(d) Einstein residual.** The **Einstein residual** of a pair $(g,T)$ is the\nsmooth symmetric $2$-tensor\n$$E_{ab}:=G_{ab}+\\Lambda g_{ab}-\\kappa T_{ab},\\qquad G_{ab}=\\operatorname{Ric}_{ab}-\\tfrac12Sg_{ab},$$\nwith $S=g^{ab}\\operatorname{Ric}_{ab}$ the scalar curvature\n([[def-gr-curvature-tensor-and-derived-tensors]]). The pair solves the Einstein\nequation exactly when $E=0$.\n\n**(e) Constraint equations.** If $g$ is defined on a neighborhood of a spacelike\nhypersurface $\\Sigma$ with future unit normal field $\\mathfrak n$ and tangential\nframe $(e_i)$ ([[def-gr-spacelike-initial-data-and-constraints]]), the\n**constraint residuals** are the functions $E(\\mathfrak n,\\mathfrak n)$ and\n$E(\\mathfrak n,e_i)$ on $\\Sigma$; that these are the normal and\nnormal-tangential projections of the field equation, and that they agree with\nthe Hamiltonian and momentum constraint equations of the initial data, is\nrecorded in [[lem-gr-constraint-projections]] and\n[[def-gr-spacelike-initial-data-and-constraints]].\n\nThe definition introduces the objects only. The principal part of (b), its\nfirst-order symmetric hyperbolic reduction, the identity expressing $E$ through\n$H$, the wave equation satisfied by $H$ and the propagation of the constraint\nresiduals are proved in [[lem-gr-reduced-einstein-principal-part]],\n[[thm-gr-reduced-einstein-local-existence]],\n[[lem-gr-einstein-residual-and-subsidiary-identity]] and\n[[thm-gr-harmonic-constraint-propagation]].",
research/plan-spec.json:105968:                "title": "H. Friedrich and A. D. Rendall, The Cauchy Problem for the Einstein Equations, Living Reviews in Relativity 3 (2000), article 1 (arXiv:gr-qc/0002074), Section 3.1 (Symmetric Hyperbolic Systems): the definition of a quasilinear symmetric hyperbolic system, the finite-dimensional mollifier approximation (3.1) solved by ODE theory, the energy and Moser product/composition estimates, the higher-order energy inequality, uniqueness, the Rellich/Ascoli compactness step and the passage to a classical solution, with Remarks 1-6 on smooth persistence, the continuation criterion, Cauchy stability and the non-identity time matrix",
research/plan-spec.json:105979:          "title": "Harmonic initial data from geometric data: prescribing the metric jet of a spacelike slice in a time-independent background",
research/plan-spec.json:105980:          "statement": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be a time-oriented smooth Lorentzian four-manifold, let\n$\\Sigma\\subseteq M$ be a spacelike embedded hypersurface with induced metric\n$h$, future unit normal field $n$ and second fundamental form\n$K(V,W)=g(\\nabla_Vn,W)$ ([[def-gr-spacelike-hypersurfaces-and-cauchy-sets]],\n[[lem-gr-spacelike-hypersurface-normal-and-second-fundamental-form]]). Fix\nsmooth product coordinates $(s,x^1,x^2,x^3)$ on a neighborhood of $\\Sigma$ such\nthat $s=0$ exactly on $\\Sigma$ and $\\partial_s|_\\Sigma=n$. Let $\\pi_\\Sigma$ denote the spatial projection of this product neighborhood\nonto $\\Sigma$, and set $h_0:=\\pi_\\Sigma^*h$. Thus $h_0$ is a smooth\ncovariant tensor with zero time and mixed components, $h_0|_\\Sigma=h$\nand $\\partial_sh_0=0$. Put $\\bar g:=-ds^2+h_0$ as background metric. Define a smooth\nmetric germ $g_0$ on $\\Sigma$ by the initial jet\n$$g_{ij}=h_{ij},\\qquad g_{00}=-1,\\qquad g_{0i}=0,\\qquad \\partial_sg_{ij}=2K_{ij},\\qquad \\partial_sg_{0i}=0,\\qquad \\partial_sg_{00}=-2\\operatorname{tr}_hK .$$\nThen:\n\n**(a)** $g_0$ is the germ of a smooth Lorentzian metric for which $\\Sigma$ is\nspacelike with induced metric $h$, future unit normal $n=\\partial_s$ and second\nfundamental form $K$;\n\n**(b)** the harmonic-gauge vector of $(g_0,\\bar g)$ vanishes on $\\Sigma$:\n$H^a|_\\Sigma=0$.",
research/plan-spec.json:106084:                "title": "H. Friedrich and A. D. Rendall, The Cauchy Problem for the Einstein Equations, Living Reviews in Relativity 3 (2000), article 1 (arXiv:gr-qc/0002074), Section 3.1 (Symmetric Hyperbolic Systems): the definition of a quasilinear symmetric hyperbolic system, the finite-dimensional mollifier approximation (3.1) solved by ODE theory, the energy and Moser product/composition estimates, the higher-order energy inequality, uniqueness, the Rellich/Ascoli compactness step and the passage to a classical solution, with Remarks 1-6 on smooth persistence, the continuation criterion, Cauchy stability and the non-identity time matrix",
research/plan-spec.json:106139:                "title": "H. Friedrich and A. D. Rendall, The Cauchy Problem for the Einstein Equations, Living Reviews in Relativity 3 (2000), article 1 (arXiv:gr-qc/0002074), Section 3.1 (Symmetric Hyperbolic Systems): the definition of a quasilinear symmetric hyperbolic system, the finite-dimensional mollifier approximation (3.1) solved by ODE theory, the energy and Moser product/composition estimates, the higher-order energy inequality, uniqueness, the Rellich/Ascoli compactness step and the passage to a classical solution, with Remarks 1-6 on smooth persistence, the continuation criterion, Cauchy stability and the non-identity time matrix",
research/plan-spec.json:106212:          "statement": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be a time-oriented smooth Lorentzian four-manifold with\nmetric-selected connection $\\nabla$, let $\\bar g$ be a smooth background\nmetric, let $H^a=g^{bc}(\\Gamma^a{}_{bc}-\\bar\\Gamma^a{}_{bc})$ be the\nharmonic-gauge vector and let $E_{ab}=G_{ab}+\\Lambda g_{ab}-\\kappa T_{ab}$ be\nthe Einstein residual of a smooth symmetric tensor $T$\n([[def-gr-harmonic-gauge-and-reduced-equations]]). Then:\n\n**(a)** On every open set where $(g,T)$ solves the reduced Einstein equation, the identity\n$$E_{ab}=\\tfrac12\\bigl(\\nabla_aH_b+\\nabla_bH_a-g_{ab}\\nabla_cH^c\\bigr)$$\nholds on that set; and\n\n**(b)** if $(g,T)$ solves the reduced Einstein equation on an open set $U$ and\n$\\nabla^aT_{ab}=0$ on $U$, then the gauge vector satisfies the homogeneous\nlinear wave equation\n$$\\Box_gH_b+\\operatorname{Ric}_{ba}H^a=0\\qquad\\text{on }U,$$\nwhere $\\Box_gH_b=\\nabla^a\\nabla_aH_b$.",
research/plan-spec.json:106253:          "statement": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be a time-oriented smooth Lorentzian four-manifold, let $\\bar g$\nbe a smooth background metric with Levi-Civita connection $\\bar\\nabla$, and let\n$H^a=g^{bc}(\\Gamma^a{}_{bc}-\\bar\\Gamma^a{}_{bc})$ be the harmonic-gauge vector\nof [[def-gr-harmonic-gauge-and-reduced-equations]]. Then:\n\n**(a) [principal part]** Let $\\hat g$ be a smooth Lorentzian metric on an open\nset $U\\subseteq M$ and let $h$ be a smooth symmetric covariant $2$-tensor on\n$U$. The derivative at $\\hat g$ in the direction $h$ of the operator\n$g\\mapsto\\operatorname{Ric}_{ab}[g]-\\bar\\nabla_{(a}H_{b)}[g]$ equals\n$$-\\tfrac12\\hat g^{cd}\\,\\bar\\nabla_c\\bar\\nabla_d h_{ab}+L_{ab}(h,\\bar\\nabla h;\\hat g,\\bar g),$$\nwhere $L$ is a smooth expression linear in $h$ and its first\n$\\bar\\nabla$-derivatives with coefficients built from $\\hat g,\\hat g^{-1}$, its first and second\n$\\bar\\nabla$-derivatives, $\\bar g$ and the background curvature. Thus the\ncoefficients depend on the full second jet of the linearization metric. In particular the principal symbol is\n$-\\tfrac12\\hat g^{cd}\\xi_c\\xi_d$ times the identity on the metric slot at every\ncovector $\\xi$.\n\n**(b) [first-order reduction]** Fix a chart $(x^0,x^1,x^2,x^3)$ with\n$x^0=t$. Introduce the metric variables\n$$u:=\\bigl(g_{ab},\\ p_{ab}:=\\bar\\nabla_0g_{ab},\\ q_{abi}:=\\bar\\nabla_ig_{ab}\\bigr)$$\n(symmetry in $a,b$ understood) and the relation variables $\\bar\\nabla_0g_{ab}-p_{ab}=0$ and $\\bar\\nabla_0q_{abi}-\\bar\\nabla_ip_{ab}=\\mathcal C_{abi}$, where $\\mathcal C_{abi}=-\\bar R^c{}_{a0i}g_{cb}-\\bar R^c{}_{b0i}g_{ac}$. Second covariant derivatives include their derivative-slot connection. These known background-curvature terms are zeroth order in the first-order state and vanish for the flat coordinate background. Then the reduced Einstein equation can be solved for the second time derivatives in the form\n$$\\bar\\nabla_0p_{ab}=-(g^{00})^{-1}\\Bigl[2g^{0i}\\bar\\nabla_ip_{ab}+g^{ij}\\bar\\nabla_iq_{abj}+R_{ab}(u;\\bar g)\\Bigr],$$\nand the resulting first-order system for $u$ is symmetric hyperbolic on every\nregion where $g^{00}<0$ and the spatial block $(g^{ij})$ is positive definite:\nthe coefficient of $\\bar\\nabla_0u$ is the block diagonal\n$\\operatorname{diag}(I,-g^{00}I,g^{ij}I)$ (on the three slots\n$g,p,q$), which is positive definite there, and the coefficients of the spatial\nderivatives are symmetric.",
research/plan-spec.json:106290:                "title": "H. Friedrich and A. D. Rendall, The Cauchy Problem for the Einstein Equations, Living Reviews in Relativity 3 (2000), article 1 (arXiv:gr-qc/0002074), Section 3.1 (Symmetric Hyperbolic Systems): the definition of a quasilinear symmetric hyperbolic system, the finite-dimensional mollifier approximation (3.1) solved by ODE theory, the energy and Moser product/composition estimates, the higher-order energy inequality, uniqueness, the Rellich/Ascoli compactness step and the passage to a classical solution, with Remarks 1-6 on smooth persistence, the continuation criterion, Cauchy stability and the non-identity time matrix",
research/plan-spec.json:106343:                "title": "H. Friedrich and A. D. Rendall, The Cauchy Problem for the Einstein Equations, Living Reviews in Relativity 3 (2000), article 1 (arXiv:gr-qc/0002074), Section 3.1 (Symmetric Hyperbolic Systems): the definition of a quasilinear symmetric hyperbolic system, the finite-dimensional mollifier approximation (3.1) solved by ODE theory, the energy and Moser product/composition estimates, the higher-order energy inequality, uniqueness, the Rellich/Ascoli compactness step and the passage to a classical solution, with Remarks 1-6 on smooth persistence, the continuation criterion, Cauchy stability and the non-identity time matrix",
research/plan-spec.json:106404:          "statement": "Fix one scalar model with smooth potential $V$ and fixed stress normalization $K_\\phi$ as in [[def-gr-scalar-field-matter-model]]. On a smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold its equations and stress are\n$$\\Box_g\\varphi-V'(\\varphi)=0,\\qquad T_{ab}=K_\\phi\\left[\\partial_a\\varphi\\partial_b\\varphi-g_{ab}\\left(\\tfrac12g^{cd}\\partial_c\\varphi\\partial_d\\varphi+V(\\varphi)\\right)\\right].$$\nFix a chart and a smooth background $\\bar g$ for the reduced Einstein equation of [[def-gr-harmonic-gauge-and-reduced-equations]]. On the open state set where $g^{00}<0$ and $(g^{ij})$ is positive definite, the reduced Einstein equation coupled to this scalar equation has a smooth first-order symmetric hyperbolic reduction with variables\n$$z=(g_{ab},p_{ab}=\\partial_0g_{ab},q_{abi}=\\partial_ig_{ab},\\varphi,P=\\partial_0\\varphi,Q_i=\\partial_i\\varphi).$$\nThe time matrix is block diagonal with metric block $\\operatorname{diag}(I,-g^{00}I,g^{ij}I)$ and scalar block $\\operatorname{diag}(1,-g^{00},g^{ij})$. Matter and metric cross-coupling is zeroth order in derivatives of $z$. The partial-derivative metric variables and the background-covariant ones are related by an invertible smooth zeroth-order change of variables; either gives the reduction.\n\nFor complete initial scalar data $(\\varphi_0,\\pi_0)$ in an initially orthogonal chart with $\\partial_0=n$, prescribe $(\\varphi,P,Q_i)=(\\varphi_0,\\pi_0,\\partial_i\\varphi_0)$. The auxiliary constraints propagate, the reconstructed scalar solves its full equation on the reduced metric, and its stress is conserved there before imposing the unreduced Einstein equation. Conversely every smooth physical scalar solution extracts to the same system. No potential positivity or mass sign is required; local arguments do not require a global orientation.",
research/plan-spec.json:106441:                "title": "H. Friedrich and A. D. Rendall, The Cauchy Problem for the Einstein Equations, Living Reviews in Relativity 3 (2000), article 1 (arXiv:gr-qc/0002074), Section 3.1 (Symmetric Hyperbolic Systems): the definition of a quasilinear symmetric hyperbolic system, the finite-dimensional mollifier approximation (3.1) solved by ODE theory, the energy and Moser product/composition estimates, the higher-order energy inequality, uniqueness, the Rellich/Ascoli compactness step and the passage to a classical solution, with Remarks 1-6 on smooth persistence, the continuation criterion, Cauchy stability and the non-identity time matrix",
research/plan-spec.json:106453:          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]), carrying the hypotheses of the analytic suppliers used below.\n\nLet $(M,g,N)$ be a smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold, let\n$\\Sigma\\subseteq M$ be a smooth spacelike hypersurface\n([[def-gr-spacelike-hypersurfaces-and-cauchy-sets]]) with future unit normal\nfield $\\mathfrak n$ and tangential frame $(e_i)$\n([[lem-gr-spacelike-hypersurface-normal-and-second-fundamental-form]]), and let\n$U$ be an open neighborhood of $\\Sigma$ carrying a smooth background metric\n$\\bar g$ and a smooth symmetric tensor $T$ with $\\nabla^aT_{ab}=0$ on $U$. Let\n$H$ and $E$ be the gauge vector and Einstein residual of\n[[def-gr-harmonic-gauge-and-reduced-equations]]. Suppose:\n\n1. $(g,T)$ solves the reduced Einstein equation on $U$;\n2. $H=0$ on $\\Sigma$;\n3. the constraint residuals vanish on $\\Sigma$: $E(\\mathfrak n,\\mathfrak n)=0$\n   and $E(\\mathfrak n,e_i)=0$ for all $i$.\n\nThen there is an open neighborhood $W\\subseteq U$ of $\\Sigma$ on which $H=0$ and\n$E=0$; consequently $G_{ab}+\\Lambda g_{ab}=\\kappa T_{ab}$ on $W$, so $g$ solves\nthe Einstein equation with the prescribed matter tensor there.",
research/plan-spec.json:106510:          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]), carrying the hypotheses of the analytic suppliers used below.\n\nLet $M$ be a smooth four-manifold carrying a chart $(x^0=t,x^1,x^2,x^3)$ whose\ndomain $U$ contains the slice $\\Sigma:=\\{t=0\\}$, let $\\bar g$ be a smooth\nbackground metric on $U$ with its Levi--Civita connection $\\bar\\nabla$, and let\n$T$ be a smooth symmetric covariant $2$-tensor on $U$ together with the\nbackground-covariant derivatives needed below (the matter field data). Suppose\nsmooth functions $g^0_{ab},g^1_{ab}$ on $\\Sigma\\cap U$ are given such that:\n\n1. $g^0$ is a smooth field of Lorentzian bilinear forms on the restricted\n   spacetime tangent bundle $TM|_{\\Sigma\\cap U}$, whose restriction to\n   $T\\Sigma$ is positive definite. Write $g^0_{ab}$ for its components in the\n   given spacetime chart; its inverse components satisfy $g^{00}_0<0$ and the\n   spatial block $(g_0^{ij})$ is positive definite;\n2. $g^1_{ab}$ is symmetric and agrees on $\\Sigma$ with the components of\n   $\\bar\\nabla_0g_{ab}$ for some smooth extension $g$ of $g^0$ to $U$;\n3. the harmonic-gauge condition $H^a=0$ holds on $\\Sigma\\cap U$ for the pair\n   $(g,\\bar g)$ ([[def-gr-harmonic-gauge-and-reduced-equations]]).\n\nThen there is an open neighborhood $V\\subseteq U$ of $\\Sigma\\cap U$ and a smooth\nmetric $g$ on $V$ with $g|_{\\Sigma\\cap U}=g^0$ and\n$\\bar\\nabla_0g_{ab}|_{\\Sigma\\cap U}=g^1_{ab}$, solving the **reduced Einstein\nequation** with the given background $\\bar g$ and matter tensor $T$\n([[def-gr-harmonic-gauge-and-reduced-equations]]) on $V$.",
research/plan-spec.json:106547:                "title": "H. Friedrich and A. D. Rendall, The Cauchy Problem for the Einstein Equations, Living Reviews in Relativity 3 (2000), article 1 (arXiv:gr-qc/0002074), Section 3.1 (Symmetric Hyperbolic Systems): the definition of a quasilinear symmetric hyperbolic system, the finite-dimensional mollifier approximation (3.1) solved by ODE theory, the energy and Moser product/composition estimates, the higher-order energy inequality, uniqueness, the Rellich/Ascoli compactness step and the passage to a classical solution, with Remarks 1-6 on smooth persistence, the continuation criterion, Cauchy stability and the non-identity time matrix",
research/plan-spec.json:107095:                "title": "H. Friedrich and A. D. Rendall, The Cauchy Problem for the Einstein Equations, Living Reviews in Relativity 3 (2000), article 1 (arXiv:gr-qc/0002074), Section 3.1 (Symmetric Hyperbolic Systems): the definition of a quasilinear symmetric hyperbolic system, the finite-dimensional mollifier approximation (3.1) solved by ODE theory, the energy and Moser product/composition estimates, the higher-order energy inequality, uniqueness, the Rellich/Ascoli compactness step and the passage to a classical solution, with Remarks 1-6 on smooth persistence, the continuation criterion, Cauchy stability and the non-identity time matrix",
research/plan-spec.json:107111:          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]), carrying the integration hypothesis of [[def-gr-periodic-torus-and-sobolev-spaces]].\n\nRefuted statement: \"At every constant rest state with $H_0=\\epsilon_0+p(\\epsilon_0)>0$, the fixed-flat-background linearized barotropic fluid equations admit a periodic $C^1$ solution on some positive time interval for every smooth periodic initial datum, and their solution operators are continuous in each fixed integer Sobolev topology $H^r$, $r\\ge0$, at every fixed positive time.\"\n\nFor $p'(\\epsilon_0)=-a^2<0$, both assertions fail: the explicit smooth datum of step 2.2 has no such solution on any positive time interval, and the smooth single-mode sequence of step 2.1 proves failure of $H^r$ continuity for every integer $r\\ge0$ at every fixed positive time. These conclusions concern the specified linearized equations; nonlinear Einstein-fluid illposedness with constrained data remains an open prerequisite.",
research/plan-spec.json:108854:                "title": "D. H. Fremlin, Measure Theory, Volume 2, Chapter 4A: second countability, bases and countability of subfamilies (background for the basis argument)",
research/plan-spec.json:108935:                "title": "H. Ringstrom, On the Topology and Future Stability of the Universe, Oxford University Press 2013: the Cauchy problem and geometric uniqueness, Chapter 8 (background)",
research/plan-spec.json:109254:                "title": "J. K. Hunter, Notes on Partial Differential Equations (UC Davis lecture notes): the standard countable basis of R^n by rational balls, background for the verification",
research/plan-spec.json:110250:          "strategy": "Direct verification of the affine concave entropy and of relative local maximality, followed by an explicit higher global value.",
research/plan-spec.json:115340:          "title": "Finite entropy integral does not force a heat-capacity power bound of order one or higher",
research/plan-spec.json:115685:                "locator": "Functional-analysis background; the local proof below uses the explicitly listed published sequence-space and complex-completeness interfaces. No new full-text reading is attested here."
research/plan-spec.json:123633:          "statement": "Choose a Galilean inertial chart $\\mathcal F$ as defined in [[def-td-galilean-rest-frame]] for the specified fixed closed trap-and-bath body, assumed to have positive total mass and the center-of-mass map required there, and supply its componentwise differentiable center-of-mass trajectory on the whole modeled time interval. Assume that its center-of-mass velocity is one constant vector on that interval, so the single constant boost of that supplier selects $\\mathcal F$ with zero center-of-mass velocity throughout. Take $\\mathcal F$ to be this common rest chart. The trap-and-bath background is held fixed in this idealization; center-of-mass recoil or accelerating-frame dynamics are not part of the scalar process. In this chart let $\\mathbf r_c\\in\\mathbb R^3$ be the fixed trap-center position and let $\\mathbf e\\in\\mathbb R^3$ be a fixed unit vector along the modeled trap axis. For a particle position $\\mathbf r\\in\\mathbb R^3$, define the signed displacement $x=\\langle\\mathbf r-\\mathbf r_c,\\mathbf e\\rangle$ in metres; the process $X_t$ in [[def-tdmath-ornstein-uhlenbeck-equation]] is this coordinate of the particle position at time $t$. Thus $U(x)=kx^2/2$ is the harmonic potential relative to the trap center, not an absolute spatial coordinate. Using the SI conventions and exact $k_{\\mathrm B}$ of [[def-td-si-quantities]], take stiffness $k>0$ in joules per square metre, constant mobility $\\mu>0$ in square metres per joule-second, and bath temperature $T_b>0$ kelvin. Adopt the one-dimensional overdamped model $dX_t=-\\gamma X_t\\,dt+\\sigma\\,dB_t$, interpreted through the integral equation of [[def-tdmath-ornstein-uhlenbeck-equation]], with $\\gamma=\\mu k$ in seconds$^{-1}$ and $\\sigma^2=2\\mu k_{\\mathrm B}T_b$ in square metres per second. Here $B$ is standard Brownian motion with its stated filtration and increment hypotheses, and the bath noise is independent of the initial preparation. This Einstein noise relation and the linear Markov model are physical assumptions. They do not follow from equilibrium thermodynamics alone and do not report a measured spectrum or calibration."
research/plan-spec.json:127152:          "statement": "For $z\\in[-1,1]$ define the real polynomial $P_l(z)=\\pi^{-1}\\int_0^\\pi\\bigl[z+i\\sqrt{1-z^2}\\,\\cos\\theta\\bigr]^l\\,d\\theta$, $l\\in\\mathbb N_0$. Then $P_l$ is a real polynomial of degree at most $l$ with $|P_l(z)|\\le1$ on $[-1,1]$, $P_l(1)=1$, and for $|q|<1$ the series $\\sum_{l\\ge0}q^lP_l(z)$ converges absolutely and uniformly on $[-1,1]$ with\n\n$$(1-2qz+q^2)^{-1/2}=\\sum_{l=0}^{\\infty}q^lP_l(z),$$\n\nwhere the branch is the analytic square root equal to $1$ at $q=0$ ([[def-series-of-real-functions]], [[thm-weierstrass-m-test-for-function-series]]). Consequently, for $x\\ne0$, $r:=|x|>|y|$ and $n=x/|x|$ and $N\\in\\mathbb N_0$,\n\n$$\\bigl|\\,|x-y|^{-1}-\\sum_{l=0}^{N}|y|^l|x|^{-l-1}P_l(n\\cdot y/|y|)\\bigr|\\le|y|^{N+1}|x|^{-N-2}(1-|y|/|x|)^{-1},$$\n\nuniformly on $|y|\\le a<r$ (with $y=0$ interpreted by the zero higher terms), and after integrating against an $L^1$ source the corresponding multipole expansion converges with that error times its $L^1$ norm. For the integrated $L^1$-source and every-spatial-derivative assertions, assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]) through the Lebesgue and harmonic-estimate interfaces. On an observation compact set disjoint from $\\overline{B_a(0)}$ every fixed spatial derivative series converges uniformly, uniformly also over $|y|\\le a$, by harmonicity of the coefficient terms and [[thm-interior-derivative-estimates-for-harmonic-functions]]. The initial finite-Riemann polynomial/generating assertions are choice-free. This lemma supplies the convergent zonal kernel; it does not by itself prove a spherical $L^2$ basis or boundary convergence.",
research/plan-spec.json:127183:            "thm-parametrized-implicit-function-theorem-with-higher-regularity",
