research/plan-spec.json:127217:          "statement": "Let $c>0$, $0\\le v_*<c$, and $z:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be $C^1$ with $|z'(s)|\\le v_*$ for all $s$. For every $(x,t)\\in\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$ there is exactly one real root $s=s(x,t)$ of $t-s=|x-z(s)|/c$, and $s\\le t$. Equality holds exactly when $x=z(t)$. Thus on the open off-instantaneous-worldline domain $D=\\{(x,t):x\\ne z(t)\\}$ one has $s<t$, $r=|x-z(s)|=c(t-s)>0$, and the root is $C^1$. With $n=(x-z(s))/r$, $\\beta=z'(s)/c$, and $\\kappa=1-n\\cdot\\beta$, its derivatives are $\\partial_t s=1/\\kappa$ and $\\nabla_xs=-n/(c\\kappa)$, where $1-v_*/c\\le\\kappa\\le1+v_*/c$. If $z$ is $C^k$, for an integer $k\\ge1$, the root is $C^k$ on $D$; in particular a $C^3$ trajectory gives a $C^3$ root. A smooth trajectory gives a smooth root. No higher regularity follows from a merely $C^1$ trajectory. These claims concern an all-time uniformly subluminal prescribed history; no finite-history or nonuniform-light-speed-limit assertion is made.",
research/plan-spec.json:127899:          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), with the exact meaning of [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $P_l$ denote the real vector space of homogeneous polynomials of degree $l$ on $\\mathbb R^3$, $H_l=\\ker(\\Delta:P_l\\to P_{l-2})$ with $P_k=\\{0\\}$ for $k<0$, and let spherical harmonics of degree $l$ be the restrictions of $H_l$ to $S^2$, with unnormalised surface measure $d\\Omega$ of total mass $4\\pi$. Then $P_l=H_l\\oplus|x|^2P_{l-2}$, $\\dim H_l=2l+1$, harmonics of distinct degree are orthogonal in $L^2(S^2,d\\Omega)$, and the union over $l$ of the spaces of spherical harmonics admits a complete orthonormal basis of $L^2(S^2,d\\Omega)$ ([[def-orthonormal-family-complete-orthonormal-system-and-hilbert-basis]]). For any real orthonormal basis $Y_{lm}$, $m=1,\\dots,2l+1$, of degree-$l$ harmonics the addition theorem holds: $$\\sum_{m=1}^{2l+1}Y_{lm}(n)Y_{lm}(n')=\\frac{2l+1}{4\\pi}P_l(n\\cdot n'),$$ where $P_l$ is the Legendre polynomial of the convergent zonal kernel lemma ([[lem-em-convergent-legendre-kernel]]). The Laplacian is the Euclidean one ([[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]]). This supplies the harmonic decomposition, orthogonality, completeness and exact addition kernel; it does not assert higher boundary regularity. The purely polynomial decomposition and finite dimension identities use no Choice; the surface/L2/completeness claims retain the stated Countable Choice.",
research/plan-spec.json:128330:            "thm-higher-order-boundary-regularity-for-dirichlet-problems",
