research/plan-spec.json:128458:            "thm-higher-order-sobolev-embedding": "mathematical-premise",
research/plan-spec.json:128467:          "strategy": "The existence claims use the AC-qualified prescribed Dirichlet and weak Neumann suppliers; that assumption is transported explicitly rather than inferred from the word \"weak solution\". Local estimate. In an interior patch, or in a flattened smooth boundary/interface patch, write the weak equation in divergence form $\\int a_{ij}\\partial_ju\\,\\partial_iv=\\int fv+\\int G_i\\partial_iv$ with the transformed coefficients $\\tilde a=|\\det D\\Phi^{-1}|\\,D\\Phi\\,a\\,D\\Phi^{\\mathsf T}$; positivity of the Jacobian bounds and the bounds on $D\\Phi$ preserve uniform ellipticity. Dirichlet data are removed by subtracting the smooth lift $g(y)\\eta(t)$ built from a finite subordinate smooth partition and a normal cutoff; smooth Neumann flux is converted to smooth volume data by a collar vector field $G$ with $G\\cdot n=g$ and the divergence theorem. For a tangential direction $k$, put $w:=\\delta_h^ku$, and take a cutoff $\\eta$ supported in a smaller patch; test the weak identity with $v=-\\delta_{-h}^k(\\eta^2w)$; change of variables and the product identity give the principal bound $\\alpha\\|\\eta\\nabla w\\|_2^2$ against cutoff terms $C\\|u\\|_{H^1}[\\|\\eta\\nabla w\\|_2+\\|w\\|_2]$, the crucial increment estimates $\\|\\delta_hu\\|_{L^2}\\le\\|\\partial_ku\\|_{L^2}$ (from the ACL line identity $\\delta_h^ku=\\int_0^1\\partial_ku(x+\\theta he_k)d\\theta$, $L^2$ Cauchy–Schwarz on the unit $\\theta$ interval and Fubini; the derivative norm is taken on the larger patch containing the shifted segments) and $\\|\\delta_ha\\|_\\infty\\le\\|\\partial_ka\\|_\\infty$, and $f,G$ terms; Cauchy-Schwarz and $2AB\\le(\\alpha/2)A^2+2B^2/\\alpha$ ([[cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two]]) absorb the gradient terms, giving uniform difference-quotient bounds. The weak second derivatives that are purely tangential (or mixed interior) exist through the bounded functional obtained by passing to the limit of the difference quotients; the Hilbert Riesz representation ([[thm-riesz-representation-for-hilbert-space]]) supplies an $L^2$ representative, with no pointwise derivative or weak subsequence selection. With compactly supported tests the weak identity is $-\\operatorname{div}(a\\nabla u)=f-\\operatorname{div}G$ distributionally. Expanding that divergence, at a boundary or interface the normal second derivative is recovered from $a_{33}\\partial_{33}u=\\operatorname{div}G-f-\\sum_{(i,j)\\ne(3,3)}a_{ij}\\partial_{ij}u-\\sum_{ij}(\\partial_ia_{ij})\\partial_ju$ with $a_{33}\\ge\\alpha>0$. A finite cover of the compact closure gives global $H^2$ bounds on each declared side. The higher-order induction differentiates the weak equation by tangential multi-indices; the exact coefficient commutator is finite and $H^1$, so the same local estimate raises the regularity by two orders of tangential/mixed derivatives, and the normal-recovery equation raises the remaining normal counts one by one; Dirichlet tangential derivatives keep zero trace and natural tests stay admissible. Classical representative: the Sobolev extension theorem ([[thm-extension-theorem-for-bounded-smooth-domains]]) extends each piecewise $H^m$ class to a compactly supported $H^m(\\mathbb R^3)$ function, and the Fourier characterisation of integer-order Sobolev spaces ([[thm-fourier-characterisation-of-integer-order-hilbert-sobolev-spaces]]) with Fourier inversion ([[thm-l-two-fourier-inversion]]) converts $W^{m,2}$ membership with $m>j+3/2$ into a $C^j$ representative via the absolutely convergent inverse integral. Derivatives agree with the weak ones by testing and Fubini, representatives agree by continuity, and arbitrarily large $m$ gives a $C^\\infty$ representative on the closure whose continuous trace is the prescribed one. Existence of the weak solutions is supplied by the prescribed-data and weak-Neumann lemmas ([[lem-em-prescribed-fractional-dirichlet-existence]], [[lem-em-weak-neumann-and-transmission-existence]])."
research/plan-spec.json:128550:            "thm-higher-order-sobolev-embedding",
research/plan-spec.json:128567:            "thm-higher-order-sobolev-embedding": "mathematical-premise",
research/plan-spec.json:128579:          "strategy": "1. Bilinear boundary identity in the chosen completion. Write $\\nu$ for the outward unit normal, extend it smoothly as a unit field in a collar, put $\\kappa=\\operatorname{div}\\nu$ on the boundary, and write $u_n=u\\cdot\\nu$. Polarizing the identity proved in [[lem-em-smooth-pec-cavity-spectral-closure]] gives, for smooth PEC $u,v$,\n$$\\int_\\Omega(\\operatorname{curl}u\\cdot\\operatorname{curl}v+\\operatorname{div}u\\operatorname{div}v)=\\int_\\Omega\\nabla u:\\nabla v+\\int_{\\partial\\Omega}\\kappa u_n v_n.$$\nFor clarity the integration-by-parts boundary term before imposing the traces is $\\sum_{i,j}\\int_{\\partial\\Omega}(\\nu_j u_j\\partial_i v_i-\\nu_j u_i\\partial_i v_j)$; at the boundary $u=u_n\\nu$, $v=v_n\\nu$, the tangential derivatives of $v$ and $\\nu\\cdot\\partial_i\\nu=0$ reduce it to $\\kappa u_n v_n$. Thus the sign is positive for an outward convex sphere ($\\kappa=2/R$); no convexity is assumed. The $H^1$ norm equivalence of that supplier and the bounded trace [[thm-lp-trace-operator-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain]] extend this identity from smooth PEC approximants to every pair in the actual graph completion $X$. Since $\\operatorname{div}u=0$, the assumed full-$X$ eigenidentity therefore gives\n$$\\int_\\Omega\\nabla u:\\nabla v+\\int_{\\partial\\Omega}\\kappa u_n v_n=\\lambda\\int_\\Omega u\\cdot v\\qquad(v\\in X).$$\nThis is the weak mixed boundary identity; no classical normal derivative has yet been assumed.\n\n2. Flattening and actual admissibility. Shrink smooth boundary charts before taking a finite cover of the compact boundary, with nested half-boxes and cutoffs away from artificial sides. In each chart choose a smooth orthonormal frame $Q=(\\tau_1,\\tau_2,\\nu)$ on the boundary, by normalizing a graph tangent and putting $\\tau_2=\\nu\\times\\tau_1$, and extend it smoothly into the collar. In flattened coordinates $(y_1,y_2,s)$ with $s>0$ inside, write $u=QU$. The $H^1$ trace has $TU_1=TU_2=0$; $TU_3$ is free. Every localized $H^1$ triple $V$ with these two zero traces and compact support away from the artificial sides pulls back to an element of the original $X$: extend $V_1,V_2$ oddly and $V_3$ evenly across $s=0$. These are $H^1$ extensions. Indeed integrating against a smooth test on both half-spaces cancels the interface term for equal one-sided traces; the odd extensions have matching traces because their traces are zero, and the even extension has matching traces automatically. This is the first-order gluing calculation in [[lem-em-dirichlet-resolvent-h-two-and-smooth-test-density]] and the half-space trace integration argument of [[thm-wkp-extension-from-a-half-space]]. Mollify with a compact kernel even in $s$; convolution preserves these parities, converges in $H^1$, and makes the first two traces exactly zero. Multiply by an even smooth cutoff equal to one on the localized support and pull back through the smooth frame and chart. Extend by zero outside the chart. The resulting fields are smooth on the closure, have $\\nu\\times v_j=0$ exactly, and converge to the localized field in $H^1$, hence in the curl/div graph norm. This explicitly supplies the smooth PEC approximants required by the definition of $X$. Interior compactly supported tests are obtained by ordinary mollification. This local construction is used below; no equality with an unspecified maximal tangential-trace realization is asserted.\n\n3. The local coupled weak system. Expanding $\\nabla(QU)$ in the identity of step 1 and transporting the volume measure gives a real symmetric smooth principal matrix $a^{ij}$, uniformly positive on the smaller half-box, that is scalar in the three component indices. The frame derivative terms have smooth matrix coefficients and at most one derivative of $U$. Thus the local equation for all admissible triples $V$ is\n$$\\sum_m\\int a^{ij}\\partial_jU_m\\partial_iV_m+\\int_B\\beta U_3V_3=\\int f\\cdot V+\\sum_i\\int G_i\\cdot\\partial_iV,$$\nwhere $B=\\{s=0\\}$, $\\beta=\\kappa J_B$ is smooth (possibly signed), $f=\\lambda JU+\\sum_i C_i\\partial_iU+C_0U\\in L^2$, and $G_i=B_iU\\in H^1$ for smooth matrix coefficients $C_i,C_0,B_i$; $J$ and $J_B$ are the positive volume and surface densities. This follows directly by expanding the finite products: the only two differentiated factors produce the scalar $a^{ij}$ because $Q^TQ=I$; terms with a derivative of $Q$ on the test factor enter $G$, and those with a derivative of $Q$ on the solution factor enter $f$. Multipliers obey [[lem-cutoff-difference-quotient-commutator-estimate]]. More generally allow a boundary datum $\\int_B hV_3$ with $h\\in H^1(B)$ for the induction below. Localization $Z=\\eta U$ has the same principal and Robin terms and exactly $f_Z=\\eta f+\\sum_i G_i\\partial_i\\eta-\\sum_{i,j}a^{ij}\\partial_jU\\partial_i\\eta$, $G_{Z,i}=\\eta G_i+\\sum_j a^{ij}U\\partial_j\\eta$, and $h_Z=\\eta h$. In particular the divergence-type cutoff/frame terms are retained rather than silently assigning zero conormal data.\n\n4. Tangential mixed $H^2$ estimate. Let $k=1,2$, set $W=\\delta_h^k U=(U(y+he_k,s)-U(y,s))/h$, and test with $V=-\\delta_{-h}^k(\\eta^2W)$ on a nested patch. For each fixed small $h$, its first two traces vanish; step 2 makes its pullback an admissible element of $X$ by actual smooth PEC approximation. Discrete integration by parts and the shifted product rule give the principal term $\\sum_m\\int\\eta^2 a^{ij}(x+he_k)\\partial_jW_m\\partial_iW_m\\ge\\alpha\\|\\eta\\nabla W\\|_2^2$. The other principal terms involve $\\delta_h a$, $\\nabla\\eta$, $\\nabla U$ and $W$ and are bounded by $\\epsilon\\|\\eta\\nabla W\\|_2^2+C_\\epsilon\\|U\\|_{H^1}^2$ on the larger patch. The elementary line formula $\\delta_h U=\\int_0^1\\partial_kU(x+the_k)dt$, valid on a.e. ACL line, and Cauchy--Schwarz in $t$ followed by Fubini give $\\|W\\|_2\\le C\\|\\partial_kU\\|_2$ on the enlarged patch; it also bounds $\\|\\delta_{-h}(\\eta^2W)\\|_2$ by $C(\\|\\eta\\nabla W\\|_2+\\|W\\|_2)$. Hence the $f$ term is bounded without differentiating $f$. Transfer the quotient in the $G$ pairing to $G$; $\\|\\delta_hG\\|_2\\le C\\|G\\|_{H^1}$ gives the same bound. On $B$ the exact quotient of the Robin product gives $\\int_B\\beta(y+h)\\eta^2|W_3|^2+\\int_B(\\delta_h\\beta)U_3\\eta^2W_3$. The normal-line proof in G3 gives the localized trace estimate $\\|Tz\\|_2^2\\le\\epsilon\\|\\nabla z\\|_2^2+C_\\epsilon\\|z\\|_2^2$. It absorbs the first term even when $\\beta$ has either sign; bounded $\\delta_h\\beta$, $\\|TU_3\\|_2\\le C\\|U\\|_{H^1}$ and Young bound the second in the same way. The extra $h$ pairing transfers to $\\delta_h h$ and is bounded by $\\|h\\|_{H^1(B)}\\|T(\\eta^2W_3)\\|_2$, again absorbed by the trace estimate and Young. Choosing the finitely many epsilons small relative to $\\alpha$ yields\n$$\\|\\eta\\nabla\\delta_h^kU\\|_2\\le C(\\|U\\|_{H^1}+\\|f\\|_2+\\|G\\|_{H^1}+\\|h\\|_{H^1(B)}),$$\nuniformly for small $h$. These estimates apply to all three components and explicitly bound the coupled lower-order and Robin terms; a scalar Dirichlet theorem has not been substituted for this system.\n\n5. Recover normal derivatives. From the last bound each distributional tangential derivative of $\\partial_iU_m$ is $L^2$: for a smooth test $\\psi$ its pairing is the limit of $\\int\\delta_h^k(\\partial_iU_m)\\psi$, a bounded $L^2$ functional, represented by an $L^2$ class (Hilbert Riesz, available through the variational supplier in G3). Interior quotient tests give the same conclusion away from the boundary. The interior equation is $-\\partial_i(a^{ij}\\partial_jU_m)=f_m-\\partial_iG_{i,m}$. Therefore\n$$a^{33}\\partial_{33}U_m=\\partial_iG_{i,m}-f_m-\\sum_{(i,j)\\ne(3,3)}a^{ij}\\partial_{ij}U_m-\\sum_{i,j}(\\partial_i a^{ij})\\partial_jU_m.$$\nEvery term on the right is $L^2$, and $a^{33}\\ge\\alpha>0$, so all three normal second derivatives are $L^2$. This proves local $H^2$ with a bound on a smaller patch. It does not require a pre-existing classical Robin derivative.\n\n6. Full bootstrap with boundary commutators. Suppose $U\\in H^{r+1}$ on the larger patch, $r\\ge1$. Its actual lower-order data satisfy $f\\in H^r$ and $G\\in H^{r+1}$, since the equation's eigenvalue term is $\\lambda JU$ and all coefficients are smooth; no initially smooth right-hand side is presumed. For a tangential multi-index $\\alpha$ of order $r$, put $Z=D_y^\\alpha U\\in H^1$. Its first two traces are still zero. To justify trace commutation, extend any localized $H^{r+1}$ class by the integer-order half-space extension [[thm-wkp-extension-from-a-half-space]], mollify in that norm, and use the continuous $H^1$ trace for every derivative of order at most $r$; the smooth tangential trace identity passes to the limit. Apply tangential integration by parts to the weak system of step 3. Its principal term remains $a^{ij}\\partial_j Z_m\\partial_iV_m$, its boundary principal term remains $\\beta Z_3V_3$, its bulk datum is $D_y^\\alpha f\\in L^2$, and its divergence-type datum is\n$$\\widetilde G_i=D_y^\\alpha G_i-\\sum_{0<\\gamma\\le\\alpha}{\\alpha\\choose\\gamma}(D_y^\\gamma a^{ij})\\partial_jD_y^{\\alpha-\\gamma}U\\in H^1.$$\nThe boundary commutator is $\\widetilde h=-\\sum_{0<\\gamma\\le\\alpha}{\\alpha\\choose\\gamma}(D_y^\\gamma\\beta)T D_y^{\\alpha-\\gamma}U_3\\in H^1(B)$. Indeed $|\\alpha-\\gamma|\\le r-1$, so the indicated derivative of $U$ is $H^2$; its trace and each tangential derivative trace are $L^2$ by the $H^1$ trace and its just-proved commutation. This proves the claimed $H^1(B)$ bound without assuming a stronger trace theorem. Step 2 again makes localized tests for $Z$ admissible by smooth PEC approximation; differentiation of the weak identity is justified first by difference quotients and then distributional limits. Steps 4--5 give $Z\\in H^2$, controlling derivatives of total order $r+2$ with at most two normal derivatives. For the remaining derivatives, differentiate the normal-recovery equation of step 5 and induct on the number of normal derivatives at this fixed total order. The non-$(3,3)$ principal terms have strictly fewer normal derivatives; all differentiated coefficient terms use derivatives of $U$ of order at most $r+1$, and differentiated $f$ and $\\operatorname{div}G$ have that same available order. Dividing by positive $a^{33}$ recovers the next normal derivative. Thus $U\\in H^{r+2}$. Use nested patches for each finite order and the finite boundary/interior cover to obtain $u\\in H^m(\\Omega)$ for every integer $m$, with $\\|u\\|_{H^m}\\le C_{m,\\Omega,\\lambda}\\|u\\|_{H^1}$; no estimate uniform in $\\lambda$ is asserted.\n\n7. Classical conclusion. For each $j$ choose $m>j+3/2$ and apply [[thm-extension-theorem-for-bounded-smooth-domains]] and [[thm-higher-order-sobolev-embedding]] componentwise. They yield continuous derivatives through order $j$ on the closure, compatible for different $j$ by weak derivative uniqueness and continuity on the interior. All jets therefore have continuous one-sided boundary limits. The fixed smooth extension [[lem-em-parameter-preserving-smooth-extension-on-a-smooth-domain]] applied to the constant-in-time field gives one actual smooth ambient representative near the entire closure, rather than unrelated finite-order extensions. The interior variational equation gives $\\operatorname{curl}\\operatorname{curl}u=\\lambda u$ pointwise, and the tangential trace is classically zero. Now integration by parts in step 1 with arbitrary smooth compactly supported normal boundary traces, admissible by step 2, gives $\\partial_\\nu u_n+\\kappa u_n=0$ pointwise. The unit-normal collar extension ensures $\\partial_\\nu u_n=(\\partial_\\nu u)\\cdot\\nu$ when the normal is extended constant along its normal lines. This agrees with the classical identity $\\operatorname{div}u=\\partial_\\nu u_n+\\kappa u_n$ for zero tangential trace. The argument covers $\\lambda=0$, disconnected smooth domains, and every topology allowed by G3; it makes no smoothness assertion for arbitrary finite-energy modal sums. Full AC is carried from G3, the trace, extension, Hilbert and Sobolev suppliers. Hunter's bounded scalar Dirichlet excerpt supports the difference-quotient method only; the vector coupling, actual graph-class tests and Robin bootstrap above are proved locally here.",
