research/plan-spec.json:130556:                "locator": "Sections6.4.1–6.4.3 printed158–164 supply the potential/field background. The additional proper-history distributional wave/gauge and C1 cutoff proof is explicit here against exact library suppliers; no distributional point-source theorem is attributed to this text."
research/plan-spec.json:130560:          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let c>0, epsilon0>0, q in R, mu0=(epsilon0 c²)^-1, and let z:R->R³ be C³ with |z'(s)|<=v*<c for all s. These are mathematical parameters. On Q=R³ times R let rho,J and Phi,Upsilon be exactly the proper point-current and retarded-history distributions of [[lem-em-point-current-continuity]] and [[lem-em-point-retarded-potential-and-fields]], with the kernel K of [[lem-em-retarded-wave-kernel]] and no independent free addition. Put W=c^-2 partial_t²-Delta. Then (a)\n$$W\\Phi=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0,\\qquad W\\Upsilon=\\mu_0J,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}\\Upsilon+c^{-2}\\partial_t\\Phi=0\\quad\\text{in }\\mathcal D'(Q).$$\n(b) Define the vector distributions $\\mathcal E=-\\nabla\\Phi-\\partial_t\\Upsilon$ and $\\mathcal B=\\operatorname{curl}\\Upsilon$ using componentwise [[def-distributional-derivative]] and the usual finite-coordinate cross/curl convention [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]. They satisfy\n$$\\operatorname{div}\\mathcal E=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0,\\quad\\operatorname{div}\\mathcal B=0,\\quad\\operatorname{curl}\\mathcal E=-\\partial_t\\mathcal B,\\quad\\operatorname{curl}\\mathcal B=\\mu_0J+c^{-2}\\partial_t\\mathcal E.$$\n(c) On the complement of Gamma={(z(s),s)}, these distributions restrict to the exact C1 Lienard-Wiechert fields of the earlier point-potential lemma, with all its higher-regularity branches retained. The zero extensions of those E,B formulas on Gamma belong to L1loc(Q), and their regular distributions are precisely mathcal E,mathcal B ([[def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function]]). The off-worldline C1 fields also satisfy the source-free classical Maxwell equations there, because the point pair restricts to zero and each continuous regular residual is zero. Thus the equations in (b) are equations for locally integrable fields with the exact point source, not merely arbitrary field distributions. No field value on Gamma is physically assigned; zero extension is a representative convention on a null set. No physical adoption, nonlinear distribution product, finite self-energy, self-force, global acceleration bound, or incoming-field uniqueness theorem is asserted.",
research/plan-spec.json:130699:            "thm-parametrized-implicit-function-theorem-with-higher-regularity"
research/plan-spec.json:130720:            "thm-parametrized-implicit-function-theorem-with-higher-regularity": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json:130724:          "strategy": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Fix $c>0$, $v_*<c$, $q\\in\\mathbb R$, $\\varepsilon_0>0$, $C=q/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0)$ and a $C^3$ worldline $z$ with $|z'|\\le v_*$ everywhere. (a) Fix $(x,t)$ with $x\\ne z(t)$ and put $f(s):=t-s-|x-z(s)|/c$. Then $f$ is continuous, $f(t)=-|x-z(t)|/c<0$, and for $s_2>s_1$ the integral representation of $z(s_2)-z(s_1)$ with the integral triangle inequality ([[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]], [[thm-integral-triangle-inequality]]) gives $|z(s_2)-z(s_1)|\\le v_*(s_2-s_1)$, hence $f(s_2)-f(s_1)\\le-(1-v_*/c)(s_2-s_1)<0$: $f$ is strictly decreasing. Since $|z(s)|\\le|z(0)|+v_*|s|$ makes $f(s)\\to+\\infty$ as $s\\to-\\infty$, the intermediate value theorem ([[thm-intermediate-value]]) on an interval $[s_1,t]$ with $f(s_1)>0$ produces a root $s<t$, and strict decrease makes it unique. This is the $C^3$ branch of [[lem-em-global-retarded-root]] applied in its corrected event form, which clauses (b)-(d) need because the root must be available at every event of $\\Omega\\setminus\\Gamma$, including events whose spatial point $x$ lies on the worldline at another time. At the root $r=c(t-s)>0$, so $s'\\mapsto|x-z(s')|$ is near $s$ a composition of the $C^3$ map $s'\\mapsto x-z(s')$ with the smooth norm off the origin ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]); therefore $F(x,t,s):=t-s-|x-z(s)|/c$ is $C^3$ near $(x,t,s)$ and $\\partial_sF=-\\kappa\\le-(1-v_*/c)<0$ there. The parametrized implicit function theorem with $C^3$ regularity ([[thm-parametrized-implicit-function-theorem-with-higher-regularity]]) supplies a local $C^3$ root map with $Ds=-(\\partial_sF)^{-1}D_{(x,t)}F$, and global uniqueness identifies the local maps, so $s$ is $C^3$ on $\\Omega\\setminus\\Gamma$. Differentiating $F=0$ gives $\\partial_ts=\\kappa^{-1}$ and $\\nabla_xs=-n/(c\\kappa)$ ([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]], [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]). The emission-time derivative is the one-variable chain rule applied to $s'\\mapsto s'+|x-z(s')|/c$: its derivative is $1-n(s')\\cdot\\beta(s')$, which at $s'=s$ equals $\\kappa$; and $|n\\cdot\\beta|\\le|\\beta|\\le v_*/c<1$ gives $\\kappa\\in[1-v_*/c,1+v_*/c]$ ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]]). (b) is exactly clause (b) of [[lem-em-point-retarded-potential-and-fields]], which supplies the finiteness of the pairings, the pairing identities and the identification of $\\Phi,\\Upsilon$ as the regular distributions of the locally integrable zero extensions of the $C^2$ potentials $\\varphi=C/(\\kappa r)$ and $A=\\beta\\varphi/c$ (its proof bounds the explicit history integrals on a fixed-support observation cylinder, uses integrability of $1/|y|$ near zero, and applies the actual nonnegative-Borel and L1 $C^1$ change-of-variables suppliers; the exact consumed statement is the displayed pairing identity, since the library does not define $\\delta\\circ f$). (c) is clause (c) of the same item together with the elementary bounds: $|1-|\\beta|^2|\\le1$, $|n-\\beta|\\le1+v_*/c$ and $\\kappa^3\\ge(1-v_*/c)^3$ give $|E_{\\mathrm{vel}}|\\le |C|(1+v_*/c)(1-v_*/c)^{-3}r^{-2}$; the split and $B=n\\times E/c$ are read off that clause, and the cross-product identities used to recognise the acceleration numerator are in [[def-cross-product-in-r3]] and [[lem-cross-product-is-bilinear-alternating-and-orthogonal]]. (d) For fixed $s_0$ and unit $n$, the same Lipschitz bound gives $|z(t_r)-z(s_0)|\\le v_*(t_r-s_0)=v_*r/c<r=|x_r-z(s_0)|$, so $x_r\\ne z(t_r)$ and $(x_r,t_r)\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\Gamma$; since $t_r-s_0=r/c=|x_r-z(s_0)|/c$, the time $s_0$ is a root at that event, and uniqueness from (a) gives $s(x_r,t_r)=s_0$. Evaluating (c) at that event with $\\beta=\\beta_0$, $\\dot\\beta=\\dot\\beta_0$, $\\kappa=\\kappa_0$ and retarded distance $r$ gives the displayed expansion with remainder $E_{\\mathrm{vel}}$, whose bound is that of (c); multiplying the field error by $r$ gives the equivalent uniform limit. No uniformity in $s_0$ is claimed because $z''$ is not assumed bounded, and no clause asserts a self-force, radiation reaction, finite energy or physical Maxwell coupling. For the all-time history the point-current proper-graph hypothesis is automatic: compact ambient support bounds its time projection. The signed constant C remains in every field formula; absolute-value estimates use |C|. The witness q=−4pi epsilon0,z=0 gives |E_vel|=r^−2 while a signed-C upper bound would be negative.",
research/plan-spec.json:135222:                "locator": "Section 3.4.1 printed pp. 146-149 (Example 3.10 and Eqs. (3.91)-(3.96), the multipole expansion of the potential in powers of 1/r, whose leading two terms give the monopole-dipole splitting; the higher terms die off more rapidly with increasing r). Read in the stamped scanned full text, OCR cross-checked."
research/plan-spec.json:135527:                "locator": "Section 3.4.1 printed pp. 146-147 (Example 3.10: the exact potential of two opposite charges and its approximation by the dipole potential) and Section 3.4.2 printed pp. 149-151 (p = qd for the physical dipole and the remark that the dipole potential is only the approximate potential, higher multipole contributions being present). Read in the stamped scanned full text, OCR cross-checked."
research/plan-spec.json:135931:          "statement": "Let the compact smooth current class and the declared ideal models be those of [[def-em-steady-current-regime]], with the units of [[def-em-si-conventions]].\n\n**(a) [Compact smooth currents].** Let $J\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^3)$ with $\\operatorname{div}J=0$, extended by zero to $\\mathbb R^3$. The **magnetic dipole moment** of $J$ is the vector\n$$m:=\\frac12\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}y\\times J(y)\\,dy\\in\\mathbb R^3,$$\nof unit A m$^2$. The integral is computed componentwise over any nondegenerate closed bounding rectangle $Q$ containing $\\operatorname{supp}J$, using the continuous functions $y\\mapsto(y\\times J(y))_i$ on $Q$ and their zero extension outside $Q$. Its value is independent of the chosen larger bounding rectangle by finite rectangular subdivision and vanishing of the integrand off its support; the support itself need not be Jordan measurable. Thus this is a Riemann integral of continuous compactly supported functions ([[def-riemann-integral-over-a-jordan-set]], [[thm-continuous-functions-on-compact-jordan-sets-are-integrable]], [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]); it is absolutely finite because $J$ is bounded with compact support.\n\n**(b) [Declared ideal thin loop].** For a closed embedded $C^2$ curve $\\gamma:\\mathbb R/L\\mathbb Z\\to\\mathbb R^3$ with $|\\gamma'|\\equiv1$ and $I\\in\\mathbb R$, the **magnetic moment** of the ideal loop current of [[def-em-steady-current-regime]](c1) is\n$$m:=\\frac I2\\int_0^L\\gamma(s)\\times\\gamma'(s)\\,ds,$$\nagain a componentwise Riemann integral over the compact interval $[0,L]$ of continuous functions; for the circle $\\gamma(s)=a(\\cos(s/a),\\sin(s/a),0)$ traversed in the positive orientation this is $m=I\\pi a^2e_3$.\n\n**(c) [Status and scope].** In both clauses $m$ is a source moment of the declared current model: it is not a rest mass, not an intrinsic spin and not an observed quantity. The definition asserts no far-field property of $m$ (that is the content of [[pthm-em-magnetic-dipole-field]] under its hypotheses), no agreement between the two clauses in a common limit, and no moment for currents outside the two classes; no higher multipole is defined here. The finite Riemann moment definitions and circular-loop evaluation themselves are choice-free; no distribution-embedding or surface-measure theorem is used to compute these moments.",
research/plan-spec.json:136114:          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), with the exact meaning of [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $J\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^3)$ with $\\operatorname{div}J=0$ and $\\operatorname{supp}J\\subset B_a(0)$ for some $a>0$, let $A$ be the vector potential of [[pthm-em-magnetostatic-vector-potential]] and $B=\\operatorname{curl}A$ its magnetostatic field, and let $m=\\frac12\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}y\\times J(y)\\,dy$ be the magnetic moment of [[def-em-magnetic-moment]](a). Then for $r=|x|\\ge2a$, uniformly in the unit vector $n=x/r$,\n$$A(x)=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\frac{m\\times x}{r^3}+O\\!\\left(\\frac{\\mu_0a^2\\|J\\|_1}{r^3}\\right),\\qquad B(x)=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\frac{3n(m\\cdot n)-m}{r^3}+O\\!\\left(\\frac{\\mu_0a^2\\|J\\|_1}{r^4}\\right),$$\nwhere $\\|J\\|_1=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}|J|\\,d\\lambda_3$ and the constants implicit in the $O$ terms are absolute: one constant works for all $r\\ge2a$ and all unit $n$. This is a controlled two-term far-field estimate: nothing is asserted for $r<2a$ or at the support, no higher multipole is defined here, and the leading dipole term is not claimed to be exact.",
research/plan-spec.json:136874:          "physical_scope": "The causal polarisation response of the memory branch of the material-closure postulate, with the transfer function, its regularity and growth properties, the principal-value dispersion relations (unsubtracted, instantaneous-subtracted, finitely Taylor-subtracted and constitutive-polynomial branches) and the monochromatic work formula together with the exact positive-semidefiniteness criterion for passivity. Conditional on the declared kernel or transfer-function hypotheses and on AC_omega (and Dependent Choice in the integrable branches); passivity is not inferred from causality; no material, microscopic or empirical claim. For arbitrary real instantaneous matrices, storage is assigned only to the symmetric quadratic part and the antisymmetric part is retained in work and in the total frequencywise passivity criterion. Each finite subtraction order requires its own growth and regularity hypotheses; higher allowed orders are retained.",
research/plan-spec.json:137156:          "statement": "Let the laboratory frame and units be those of [[def-em-lab-geometry]] and [[def-em-si-conventions]], with spatial coordinates $x=(x_1,x_2,x_3)$, $z=x_3$, $r=|x|$ and the applied field along $e_3$. The potentials below are functions on the full ball and full exterior in $\\mathbb R^3$; their physical fields are restricted to the laboratory region, while (c)-(d) concern the full exterior. Fix a radius $a>0$ in metres with $\\overline B_a\\subseteq\\Omega$, so that the interface $S=\\{r=a\\}$ below lies in the laboratory region, a field magnitude $E_0>0$ in $\\mathrm V\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}$ and constants $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}},\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}\\in(0,\\infty)$ (the interior and exterior permittivities of the elementary branch of [[post-em-material-closure]], the material being at rest in the laboratory frame), and put\n\n$$C:=\\frac{3\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}}{\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}}+2\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}},\\qquad d:=\\frac{E_0a^3(\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}}-\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}})}{\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}}+2\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}}.$$\n\nConsider the time-independent potentials\n\n$$\\varphi_{\\mathrm{in}}(x):=-C\\,E_0\\,z\\quad(r<a),\\qquad \\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}}(x):=-E_0\\,z+\\frac{d\\,z}{r^3}\\quad(r>a),$$\n\nwith fields $E:=-\\nabla\\varphi$ and $D:=\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}}E$ inside, $D:=\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}E$ outside ($E$ being of the class of [[def-em-fields]] restricted to this time-independent configuration). The coefficient $C$ is dimensionless and $d$ has units $\\mathrm V\\,\\mathrm m^2$, so both potentials carry volts. Write $S=\\{r=a\\}$, $n=e_r:=x/r$ for the outward unit normal, and $\\partial_r\\varphi:=n\\cdot\\nabla\\varphi$. For a trace $F$, its normal and tangential parts are $n\\cdot F$ and $F-(n\\cdot F)n$. Then:\n\n**(a) [Harmonicity]** $\\Delta\\varphi_{\\mathrm{in}}=0$ on $r<a$ and $\\Delta\\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}}=0$ on $r>a$ in the classical sense of [[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]], and the potentials and their first derivatives extend continuously to the closed sides of $S$.\n\n**(b) [Interface conditions]** With one-sided traces at $r=a$, $\\varphi_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}}$ and $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}}\\,\\partial_r\\varphi_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}\\,\\partial_r\\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}}$ on $S$. Potential-trace equality implies tangential-electric continuity, and the weighted radial-derivative equality is equivalent to normal-$D$ continuity. Tangential-electric continuity alone determines the potential-trace difference only up to an additive constant; the displayed potentials have that constant fixed to zero. These electric matching identities hold independently of any magnetic completion. In addition, declare the static completion $B=H=0$, $\\rho_f=J_f=0$, with any constant symmetric positive-definite permeability and the zero-conductivity branch $\\sigma=0$, $J_{\\mathrm{ext}}=0$ of [[post-em-material-closure]]. The four fields then satisfy all four thin-interface macroscopic equations distributionally with $\\sigma_f=K_f=0$, and the joint one-sided regularity at the compact sphere verifies the hypotheses of [[pthm-em-material-stationary-jumps]]. Assume its inherited $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ convention ([[def-countable-choice]]) for this distribution/surface reading.\n\n**(c) [Asymptotics]** $\\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}}(x)+E_0z=dz/r^3$ and $\\nabla(\\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}}+E_0z)=O(r^{-3})$ as $r\\to\\infty$: the difference from the applied uniform potential decays like $r^{-2}$ and its gradient like $r^{-3}$.\n\n**(d) [Uniqueness in the declared class]** Assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]), as required by the Green and harmonic derivative-estimate interfaces used in this branch. Let $(\\tilde\\varphi_{\\mathrm{in}},\\tilde\\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}})$ be $C^2$ on the closed ball $\\overline{B_a}$ respectively on every closed exterior region $\\{a\\le r\\le R\\}$, harmonic on $r<a$ and $r>a$, satisfying the same interface conditions (b), and satisfying $|\\tilde\\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}}+E_0z|\\le M/r$ for some $M$ and all sufficiently large $r$. Then $\\tilde\\varphi_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\varphi_{\\mathrm{in}}$ and $\\tilde\\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}}=\\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}}$. Hence the displayed pair is the unique solution of the dielectric-sphere transmission problem in that class, existence being exhibited explicitly; the uniqueness proof uses only the Green identity, the matching conditions and the decay, with no maximum principle.\n\n**(e) [Fields, polarisation and induced dipole]** On $r<a$ the field is uniform, $E=C E_0e_3$ with $D=\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}}CE_0e_3$; outside, the field is the applied uniform field 
