research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:130560:          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let c>0, epsilon0>0, q in R, mu0=(epsilon0 c\u00b2)^-1, and let z:R->R\u00b3 be C\u00b3 with |z'(s)|<=v*<c for all s. These are mathematical parameters. On Q=R\u00b3 times R let rho,J and Phi,Upsilon be exactly the proper point-current and retarded-history distributions of [[lem-em-point-current-continuity]] and [[lem-em-point-retarded-potential-and-fields]], with the kernel K of [[lem-em-retarded-wave-kernel]] and no independent free addition. Put W=c^-2 partial_t\u00b2-Delta. Then (a)\n$$W\\Phi=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0,\\qquad W\\Upsilon=\\mu_0J,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}\\Upsilon+c^{-2}\\partial_t\\Phi=0\\quad\\text{in }\\mathcal D'(Q).$$\n(b) Define the vector distributions $\\mathcal E=-\\nabla\\Phi-\\partial_t\\Upsilon$ and $\\mathcal B=\\operatorname{curl}\\Upsilon$ using componentwise [[def-distributional-derivative]] and the usual finite-coordinate cross/curl convention [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]. They satisfy\n$$\\operatorname{div}\\mathcal E=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0,\\quad\\operatorname{div}\\mathcal B=0,\\quad\\operatorname{curl}\\mathcal E=-\\partial_t\\mathcal B,\\quad\\operatorname{curl}\\mathcal B=\\mu_0J+c^{-2}\\partial_t\\mathcal E.$$\n(c) On the complement of Gamma={(z(s),s)}, these distributions restrict to the exact C1 Lienard-Wiechert fields of the earlier point-potential lemma, with all its higher-regularity branches retained. The zero extensions of those E,B formulas on Gamma belong to L1loc(Q), and their regular distributions are precisely mathcal E,mathcal B ([[def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function]]). The off-worldline C1 fields also satisfy the source-free classical Maxwell equations there, because the point pair restricts to zero and each continuous regular residual is zero. Thus the equations in (b) are equations for locally integrable fields with the exact point source, not merely arbitrary field distributions. No field value on Gamma is physically assigned; zero extension is a representative convention on a null set. No physical adoption, nonlinear distribution product, finite self-energy, self-force, global acceleration bound, or incoming-field uniqueness theorem is asserted.",
