research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:138382:          "strategy": "(a) is [[lem-em-gauge-wave-reachability]] with its hypotheses and countable choice carried verbatim; the pair of the framework enters only through the vocabulary, so nothing is reproved. (b) The fixed-time smooth Dirichlet and compatible Neumann interfaces are those of [[thm-em-elliptic-boundary-existence]], and their parameter-compatible ambient smoothness, exact boundary lifts, complete natural-Neumann estimates and normalized uniqueness are supplied by the earlier pure-mathematical [[lem-em-parameter-dependent-smooth-poisson-boundary-solvers]]. Use the original open neighbourhood of the potentials and of the boundary family to choose an open time interval $J$ containing $\\overline I$ by compactness of each fixed spatial/boundary fibre; only $\\overline\\Omega\\times K$, with compact $K\\subset J$, is used for derivative bounds, never compactness of all $\\overline I$. Put $F_t=\\varepsilon_0\\operatorname{div}A(\\cdot,t)$. In the Dirichlet case the supplier constructs a fixed finite-chart spatial lift $H_t=L_Dg_D(\\cdot,t)$ and the zero-trace operator $R$ for $-\\varepsilon_0\\Delta$ from its exact weak solver, including every $L^2$ source, with its higher-order bounds. Set $\\psi_t=H_t+R(F_t+\\varepsilon_0\\Delta H_t)$. Differentiation includes the lift at every order: $\\partial_t^j\\psi_t=L_D\\partial_t^jg_D+R(\\partial_t^jF_t+\\varepsilon_0\\Delta L_D\\partial_t^jg_D)$. Thus the spatial equation is $-\\varepsilon_0\\Delta\\psi_t=F_t$ and the Dirichlet family is exact. In the Neumann case use the same supplier's compatible normalized weak solver and natural-boundary estimates. Compatibility is imposed only for $t\\in I$: on an open $J$, define $c_j(t)=\\int_{\\Omega_j}F_t+\\int_{\\partial\\Omega_j}\\kappa_t$ and the auxiliary bulk extension $\\widehat F_t=F_t-c_j(t)/|\\Omega_j|$ on each of the finitely many components. It is smooth, compatible for all $t\\in J$ and agrees with $F_t$ on $I$; no equation for the gauge outside the physical interval is promised. Apply the parameter solver to $(\\widehat F,\\kappa)$ with zero mean on each component. In either case locally uniform compact-time difference-quotient estimates give all mixed derivatives up to the boundary, and the supplier's fixed parameter-preserving Seeley construction supplies one actual ambient $C^\\infty$ continuation, not merely separate finite-order Sobolev representatives. The resulting generator obeys $\\Delta\\psi=-\\operatorname{div}A$ on every physical time, with the requested boundary data, so it is Coulomb gauge and preserves the fields by [[pthm-em-gauge-invariance]]. Elliptic uniqueness gives the stated normalized uniqueness on $\\Omega\\times I$; subtracting any two Coulomb generators gives $\\Delta\\chi=0$ and the listed Dirichlet/Neumann residual freedom. This argument retains the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice hypotheses of the variational, chart and Sobolev suppliers, and imposes no global time bounds or extra regularity restriction. (c) Put $\\delta\\varphi=\\tilde\\varphi-\\varphi$ and $\\delta A=\\tilde A-A$. Equality of the field pairs gives $\\operatorname{curl}\\delta A=0$ and $\\nabla\\delta\\varphi+\\partial_t\\delta A=0$. Apply the corrected scalar lemma to $C=\\delta A$ and denote its negative-gradient witness by $\\phi_{\\delta A}(x,t)=-\\int_0^1\\langle\\delta A(a+s(x-a),t),x-a\\rangle ds$. Then $\\nabla\\phi_{\\delta A}=-\\delta A$ and $\\phi_{\\delta A}$ is jointly $C^2$. Take $$\\chi_0=-\\phi_{\\delta A}=\\int_0^1\\langle\\delta A(a+s(x-a),t),x-a\\rangle ds,$$ so $\\nabla\\chi_0=\\delta A$. This additional negation is essential; using the lemma's negative-gradient witness itself as the gauge generator would reverse the vector-potential difference. Mixed partials of the $C^2$ function $\\chi_0$ now give $\\nabla(\\delta\\varphi+\\partial_t\\chi_0)=\\nabla\\delta\\varphi+\\partial_t\\delta A=0$. For fixed t, restrict this $C^1$ scalar to each radial segment $a+s(x-a)$; the chain rule gives zero derivative, and cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant gives its common value $h(t)=\\delta\\varphi(a,t)+\\partial_t\\chi_0(a,t)$. Since $\\chi_0(a,t)=0$ for every t, the time derivative there is zero, so $h(t)=\\delta\\varphi(a,t)$ (in particular h is C1; indeed C2 under the stated hypotheses). For nondegenerate I, fix $t_*\\in I$ and set $$\\eta(t)=-\\int_{t_*}^{t}h(u)du.$$ cor-primitives-of-a-continuous-function gives $\\eta'=-h$ on I, with relative endpoint derivatives; since h is C1, eta is C2. Put $\\chi=\\chi_0+\\eta$. Then $\\nabla\\chi=\\delta A$ and $\\partial_t\\chi=\\partial_t\\chi_0-h=-\\delta\\varphi$, so $T_\\chi(\\varphi,A)=(\\tilde\\varphi,\\tilde A)$. Finally the unchanged exact gauge-shift identity in pthm-em-lorenz-gauge-wave-form(b) gives $0=G(\\tilde\\varphi,\\tilde A)=G(\\varphi,A)-W\\chi=-W\\chi$, hence $W\\chi=0$. The corrected radial construction gives chi0 as a C2 restriction from an open radial-admissibility neighbourhood. At the centre, the data extension gives h(t)=delta phi(a,t) on an open time neighbourhood; take the interval component containing I and construct the time primitive there. Adding this primitive to the chi0 extension yields a C2 extension on the intersection neighbourhood of Omega x I. Thus the asserted generator retains the same regularity convention at included endpoints of nondegenerate time intervals. Degenerate interval restrictions do not supply unique time jets: any use of this time-derivative branch there carries chosen ambient extensions and the same-field/Lorenz hypotheses on an ambient time neighbourhood, to which the nondegenerate construction applies before restriction. On an empty restriction there are no pointwise equations to prove. The physical laboratory branch already uses a nonempty open time interval.  This residual construction uses no choice principle; the preexisting Choice assumptions in branches (a)-(b) remain exactly as stated. The linearity of $\\nabla$ and $\\partial_t$ used throughout is [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]] and [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]; $H^m$ notation is [[def-sobolev-space-wkp-and-its-norm]], and the elliptic problems use the domains of [[def-bounded-c-k-domain-and-boundary-charts]]. The declared Choice assumptions enter only through the elliptic existence theorem and the wave supplier and are carried in the statement. No claim beyond (a)-(c) is made. Dirichlet and normalized Neumann uniqueness concern generator values for arbitrary I; they imply transformed-pair uniqueness only when I has at least two elements, as Proof 3.1 now establishes.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:138407:                "locator": "\u00a74.12 printed pp. 116-117, Theorem 4.31 and Corollary 4.32: the higher-order H^{k+2} Dirichlet estimate and the C^infty regularity for smooth data used in the parameter-smoothness argument of branch (b)"
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:138465:            "thm-higher-dimensional-spheres-are-simply-connected",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:138513:            "thm-higher-dimensional-spheres-are-simply-connected": "mathematical-premise",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:138534:          "strategy": "(a) Differentiate the components of $F$ by the quotient rule ([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]) to get $\\operatorname{curl}F=0$; parametrize the unit circle, use the pullback identity for vector line integrals and the sine/cosine derivative and Pythagorean identities to get $F(\\gamma(t))\\cdot\\gamma'(t)=1$, and evaluate $\\int_0^{2\\pi}1\\,dt=2\\pi$ by the fundamental theorem ([[thm-ftc-second-part]]). The gradient theorem for a $C^2$ potential gives zero circulation around the closed curve $\\gamma$, so $F$ is not a gradient ([[thm-gradient-theorem-for-line-integrals]], [[def-closed-and-exact-c1-vector-fields]]). (b) Compute $\\operatorname{div}B=\\sum_i\\partial_i(x_i\\|x\\|_2^{-3})=3\\|x\\|_2^{-3}-3\\|x\\|_2^{-5}\\sum_ix_i^2=0$ by the chain and product rules ([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives]]). For the flux, differentiate $\\Phi$ to get $\\Phi_\\phi\\times\\Phi_\\theta=\\sin\\phi\\,\\Phi$ ([[def-cross-product-in-r3]], [[lem-cross-product-is-bilinear-alternating-and-orthogonal]], [[thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives]]); since $B(\\Phi)=\\Phi$ on the unit sphere, the integrand is $\\sin\\phi$, and Fubini with the fundamental theorem evaluate the double integral as $4\\pi$ ([[thm-jordan-fubini-by-sections]], [[thm-ftc-second-part]], [[def-riemann-integral-over-a-jordan-set]]). Suppose $A$ is $C^1$ on the open set $U_2$ with $\\operatorname{curl}A=B$. Each hemisphere rectangle is a finite elementary Green region with its positive boundary chain and the spherical parametrization restricted to it is a $C^2$ regular patch whose image lies in $U_2$, so the classical Stokes theorem applies to each ([[def-type-i-type-ii-and-elementary-green-regions]], [[def-positive-orientation-for-elementary-region-boundaries]], [[def-the-induced-boundary-chain-of-a-c2-surface-patch]], [[thm-the-classical-stokes-theorem-for-a-c2-surface-patch]]). In each hemisphere the induced boundary chain has four entries: one equator arc, two meridian arcs with the same image and opposite orientations, and one degenerate polar arc; the two meridian integrals cancel by reversal ([[thm-line-integrals-under-reversal-and-concatenation]]) and the degenerate one is zero. Hence the north circulation is the equator integral and the south circulation is its reverse, so their sum is zero; summing the two Stokes identities gives zero for the total flux of $B$ over the sphere, contradicting $4\\pi\\ne0$, and no such $A$ exists. (c) For the basepoint reduction, $L$ and $L^{-1}$ are linear maps of $\\mathbb R^3$, hence bounded and continuous on the finite-dimensional space ([[cor-linear-maps-with-finite-dimensional-domain-are-bounded]], [[thm-bounded-linear-operator-equivalences]]), so a null-homotopy of $L\\circ\\gamma$ composed with $L^{-1}$ is one of $\\gamma$; for a basepoint on the unit sphere, $H$ is the continuous radial homotopy of [[thm-radial-straight-line-map-on-punctured-rn]] fixing the unit sphere, so it deforms the loop to a loop in $S^2$ rel basepoint ([[def-homotopy-relative-and-path-homotopy]], [[def-based-loops-and-fundamental-group]]), which is null-homotopic rel basepoint by simple connectivity of $S^2$ ([[thm-higher-dimensional-spheres-are-simply-connected]]); composing with the inclusion and concatenating gives the required null-homotopy in $\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$. Path connectedness of $\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$ is seen by routing through the unit sphere ([[cor-euclidean-spheres-are-path-connected]], [[def-path-connected]]). (d) is the comparison of the two mechanisms: circulation versus flux. All suppliers are published items used at their stated hypotheses; no choice principle is used.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:139877:          "statement": "Let $K>0$, $\\omega:=cK$, let $\\alpha\\in(0,\\pi/2)$ (half the angle between the\nbeams) and $\\varphi\\in\\mathbb R$, and take the two plane waves of\n[[def-em-plane-wave-definition]] with\n$$k_1=K(\\sin\\alpha\\,e_1+\\cos\\alpha\\,e_3),\\qquad k_2=K(-\\sin\\alpha\\,e_1+\\cos\\alpha\\,e_3),$$\n$$e_1^{\\mathrm{amp}}=E_0e_2,\\qquad e_2^{\\mathrm{amp}}=E_0e^{i\\varphi}e_2,$$\nwhere $E_0>0$ carries $\\mathrm V\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}$ and both amplitudes are\npolarized along $e_2$, perpendicular to both wavevectors; with\n$b_j=k_j\\times e_j^{\\mathrm{amp}}/\\omega$ and $|k_j|=\\omega/c$ both pairs\nsatisfy the amplitude equations of [[pthm-em-plane-wave-maxwell]], so the\nsuperposition is a source-free smooth Maxwell solution\n([[pthm-em-classical-interference]]). Write\n$\\delta(x):=(k_2-k_1)\\cdot x=-2K\\sin\\alpha\\,x_1$. Then:\n\n(i) [Averaged energy density]\n$\\langle u\\rangle(x)=\\varepsilon_0E_0^2\\bigl[1+\\cos^2\\alpha\\,\\cos(2K\\sin\\alpha\\,x_1-\\varphi)\\bigr]$\nfor every $x$, by clause (b) of [[pthm-em-classical-interference]] with\n$e_1^{\\mathrm{amp}}\\cdot\\overline{e_2^{\\mathrm{amp}}}=E_0^2e^{-i\\varphi}$ and\n$(\\hat k_1\\times e_1^{\\mathrm{amp}})\\cdot(\\hat k_2\\times\\overline{e_2^{\\mathrm{amp}}})=E_0^2e^{-i\\varphi}\\cos2\\alpha$.\n\n(ii) [Fringes and visibility] At fixed $x_2,x_3$ the averaged energy density is\nperiodic in $x_1$ with period $\\pi/(K\\sin\\alpha)=\\lambda/(2\\sin\\alpha)$, where\n$\\lambda=2\\pi/K$; its maximum and minimum are\n$\\varepsilon_0E_0^2(1\\pm\\cos^2\\alpha)$, so the visibility\n$V:=\\frac{\\langle u\\rangle_{\\max}-\\langle u\\rangle_{\\min}}{\\langle u\\rangle_{\\max}+\\langle u\\rangle_{\\min}}$\nequals $\\cos^2\\alpha=\\frac12(1+\\hat k_1\\cdot\\hat k_2)$. In particular\n$V\\to1$ as $\\alpha\\downarrow0$ through crossing beams, and $V\\to0$ as\n$\\alpha\\to\\pi/2$. At $\\alpha=0$ with fixed relative phase the profile is\nspatially constant: its spatial visibility is $0$ when its value is positive,\nand the displayed ratio is undefined when the field vanishes.\n\n(iii) [The promised copropagating amplitude law] For $k_1=k_2=k$ with $|k|=K$ and $k\\cdot e_2=0$, retain the common linear\npolarization $e_2$ and amplitudes $E_0e_2$, $E_0e^{i\\Delta}e_2$ (so that the\nsum is the single plane wave with vector amplitude $E_0(1+e^{i\\Delta})e_2$),\n$\\langle u\\rangle=\\varepsilon_0E_0^2(1+\\cos\\Delta)=2\\varepsilon_0E_0^2\\cos^2(\\Delta/2)\\propto|1+e^{i\\Delta}|^2$,\nthe form used in the double-slit discussion of the content model. Scanning\n$\\Delta$ gives phase-scan visibility $1$, distinct from spatial visibility at\nfixed phase.\n\n(iv) [Statistical qualifications] This is a deterministic prediction of the\nclassical wave model for the specified coherent two-wave preparation, at every\npoint of the ideal plane-wave field; a *measured* fringe pattern or detector\nhistogram would additionally require a declared measurement model (detector\nresponse, finite bin width, background, integration time, sampling) and a\nstatistical analysis. The example reports no observation, no finite-sample\nclaim and no visibility estimate from data; in particular classical\nelectromagnetic waves interfere, so the prediction alone does not discriminate\nbetween classical and quantum descriptions of light. All quantities are\nidealized field quantities of the framework.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:143222:          "statement": "Adopt the laboratory region with its inertial chart and fixed-time reading of\n[[def-em-lab-geometry]], the electric and magnetic fields of [[def-em-fields]],\nthe charge parameter, frame charge and current densities and charge integrals\nof [[def-em-sources]], and the SI units and constants of\n[[def-em-si-conventions]]. This definition fixes the framework's report-record\nvocabulary; it selects no experiment, records no value, evaluates no indicator\nand asserts no measurement.\n\n(a) [Primary source] A *primary source* is a declared bibliographic\nidentification of a report (author, title, date, publication, and the\nidentification of the passages actually inspected) together with the declared\nlimits of the reading: which parts of the report were read, which were not, and\nany carrier defects (for instance corrupted optical-character-recognition text\nof formulae or tables).\n\n(b) [Comparison quantity and measurand] Let $d\\ge1$ be an integer. A\n*comparison quantity* of dimension $d$ is a declared map from a declared\ncondition set to $\\mathbb R^d$, with declared units, whose components are\nfinite expressions in specified framework quantities. These may include the\nvalues of $E,B,\\rho,J$ at declared events, the charge parameter $q$, and the\ndeclared charge or current integrals of [[def-em-sources]]; declared field\ncirculations and surface fluxes on the admitted fixed geometries of\n[[def-em-fixed-test-geometry]]; and declared finite-time or period averages of\nspecified derived field quantities, including $u$ and $S$ of\n[[def-em-vacuum-energy-flux]]. An integral or average is admitted only with its\ndomain, orientation where needed, integration convention and actual\nwell-definedness hypotheses declared. For example, a component $Q(t)$\ncontinuous on a declared compact interval $[a,b]$ inside the laboratory time\ndomain, with $a<b$, has the finite Riemann average\n$(b-a)^{-1}\\int_a^b Q(t)\\,dt$ by [[thm-continuous-implies-integrable]]; a\nperiod average additionally declares the period and interval. Field fluxes\ninherit the regularity, compactness, boundary presentation and measure/choice\nconventions of their fixed-test supplier. A declared detector-response\nintegral may be used only when its declared model and integral suppliers\nestablish a finite real value. An extended nonnegative integral with value\n$+\\infty$ is not a component of this real-valued comparison map or a recorded\nfinite rate. No existence or finiteness of an arbitrary field integral,\ndistributional product or detector response is asserted. A *measurand* is a\ncomparison quantity together with a declared condition description under which\nit is to be read. All quantities carry the SI conventions of\n[[def-em-si-conventions]].\n\n(c) [Apparatus and preparation description] An *apparatus-and-preparation\ndescription* is a declared finite record of the apparatus, the preparation,\nthe controls and the environment in the report's own terms, with the\nidentification of the passage of the primary source of (a) from which each\nitem of the description is taken. The definition asserts only that the\ndescription is declared and finite; it asserts nothing about its correctness\nor completeness.\n\n(d) [Recorded data and reported observations] For an integer $d\\ge1$, a\n*recorded datum* is a vector $z_k\\in\\mathbb R^{d}$ with declared units\ntogether with a *condition annotation* $a_k$, a declared finite text\nidentifying the declared run condition of the datum and, when the datum is\ntaken from a report, its locator in the primary source. A *reported\nobservation* is a declared finite text recording a qualitative observation of\nthe report. A *record* is a finite tuple of recorded data and reported\nobservations together with the declared condition annotations. The record\ncontains no probability space, random variable, distribution, sampling law,\nindependence assumption, likelihood or coverage statement.\n\n(e) [Calibration model] A *calibration model* is a tuple $(Z,c,\\delta)$ in\nwhich $Z$ is a declared nonempty set of *apparatus-output values*,\n$c:Z\\to\\mathbb R^d$ is a declared *calibration map* and\n$\\delta\\in[0,\\infty)$ is a declared *calibration-error bound*: it declares\nthat for every output $o\\in Z$ obtained under the declared conditions, the\nvalue of the comparison quantity of (b) lies in the closed box\n$\\prod_{j=1}^d[c_j(o)-\\delta,\\,c_j(o)+\\delta]$ in the declared units. A\ncalibration model is a declaration; the definition asserts no accuracy beyond\nthe bound $\\delta$ and no statistical property of $c$ or $\\delta$. A declared\n*missing* calibration is recorded as missing, and no value or bound is then\navailable from this clause.\n\n(f) [Uncertainty record] An *uncertainty record* is a finite tuple of declared\nbounds, each carrying a declared *source label* from the finite list\n$\\{calibration,\\ resolution,\\ systematic,\\ sampling-variability,\\ rounding,\\ other\\}$\nand a declared value in the declared units; each entry is a declared\nconstraint of the form of (e), and the record may state that a bound is\nunavailable. No entry is a standard-deviation convention, a coverage\nprobability, a confidence level, a $p$-value, a posterior or a probability\nlaw.\n\n(g) [Analysis assumptions] An *analysis-assumption list* is a finite tuple of\ndeclared assumptions used to obtain the recorded data or to compare them with\na model (binning, background model, selection rules, corrections, fitting or\nestimation procedure, independence or dependence of entries, and the\ntreatment of the sources of uncertainty); declared omissions are recorded as\nomissions and are not filled in by the definition.\n\n(h) [Interpretation] An *interpretation* is a declared statement that\nidentifies a specified model prediction and declares the result of comparing\nthe record with it (support, constraint, failure to distinguish, or no\nconclusion), conditional on (a)-(g). The interpretation is part of the report\naccount and not of the data; it asserts no probability, no significance level\nand no truth of any framework, and it records a comparison of a specified\nmodel, not of a whole theory.\n\n(i) [Well-definedness and scope] All components of (a)-(h) are finite tuples,\nreal numbers, declared maps on declared sets and finite text labels; forming\nthis finite vocabulary uses no choice principle. Evaluating a comparison\nquantity inherits every hypothesis and axiom of its actual suppliers,\nincluding countable choice when the selected fixed-test surface or\nLebesgue-measure interface requires it; no measurability, integrability,\ncompleteness or differentiability is inferred merely from the record. A\ncomparison quantity is evaluated only where the cited items supply its\ningredients and a finite value. Missing source detail stays missing: no\nuniversal sampling law, precision, independence, coverage or calibration\ncorrectness is part of the definition, and none may be inferred from it. The\ndefinition is the framework-local vocabulary for the reports of this page; it\ndoes not assert that any laboratory realises a measurand or that any reported\nobservation is correct.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:143269:          "statement": "**Primary source and reading limits.** Michael Faraday, *Experimental Researches in\nElectricity*, Volume I, First Series (read 24 November 1831), 1849 collected\nsecond edition, read in the Project Gutenberg transcription derived from\nBnF/Gallica images; the First Series paragraphs 1-33 were read completely, and\nno claim is made to have read the whole volume. This is an original report\nreprint transmitted by a later transcription, not a newly repeated experiment.\nThe account below is the report's apparatus descriptions and reported\nobservations, structured by the record contract of\n[[def-em-experiment-record-contract]]; it is not a deduction, and 'no perceived\ndeflection' is everywhere the report's finite-resolution verbal observation.\n\n## Setup\n\nInsulated copper helices wound on a wooden cylinder, each containing about\ntwenty-seven feet of wire and kept from touching by interposed twine, twelve\nhelices superposed in the same direction with the first, third, fifth, seventh,\nninth and eleventh joined into one helix and the remainder into a second\n(paragraph 6). A pair of two-hundred-and-three-foot coils interleaved as a\nspiral on a wooden block with metallic contact prevented by twine (paragraph\n10). A small hollow helix wound on a glass tube into which a steel needle could\nbe inserted (paragraph 13). Insulated zigzag wire boards moved relatively to\none another (paragraph 18). A welded soft-iron ring, seven-eighths of an inch\nthick and six inches in external diameter, with three insulated inducing\nhelices A of about twenty-four feet each and a separate helix B made from\nabout sixty feet of wire in two pieces. The A helices were joined end to end\nand connected to a battery of ten pairs of plates; B was connected to a\ngalvanometer three feet from the ring (paragraphs 27-28). Paragraph 31 reports\nthe ring trial with one hundred pairs; paragraph 11 reports a separate\ncoil trial with one hundred and twenty pairs.\n\n## Procedure\n\nMaking and breaking the battery circuit of the inducing helix while the\ndetector loop is kept complete (paragraphs 10-11); removing the inserted needle\nbefore breaking the battery contact, and inserting it after make but before\nbreak (paragraphs 13-14);\ncompleting the detector circuit before the battery contact is made or broken\n(paragraph 17); moving the zigzag boards together and apart with a steady\nbattery current (paragraphs 18-19); reversing the direction of the battery\nconnection (paragraph 30); repeating with larger batteries and with charcoal\ncontacts (paragraphs 31-32).\n\n## Observations\n\nA slight deflection of the galvanometer needle at the moment of completing the\nconnection, always in one direction, with the opposite deflection at breaking,\nand no sustained perceived deflection under the continued steady battery\nconnection (paragraphs 11 and 28-31); magnetization of an inserted steel needle,\nwith opposite poles at make and at break and near-cancellation when both\ntransients act on the same inserted needle (paragraphs 13-17); opposite\ntransient deflections as the boards approach and recede, with return to the\nordinary position when the movement ceases (paragraphs 18-20); a minute spark\nat the secondary helix with charcoal contacts (paragraph 32); and the control\nthat, after the first effect, the galvanometer needle resumes its position\nunder the continued battery current (paragraphs 28 and 33). These are reported\nfinite-resolution qualitative observations.\n\n## Uncertainty\n\nThe read passages identify deflection direction, needle-pole comparison,\nbattery reversal and background and timing controls, but supply no calibrated\nconversion from deflection to current, no detector response or transfer\nfunction, no trial-level dataset, no declared sampling law, no error bars and\nno formal significance test; dimensions, wire lengths and battery plate counts\nare historical approximate apparatus descriptions, not modern precision\nestimates. The sign interpretation of the induced current depends on\ngalvanometer and needle response conventions. Missing information is retained\nas missing; no error bars, precision or independence are invented.\n\n## Interpretation\n\nThe reported observations support a transient induction model for the described\napparatus under the stated apparatus assumptions and are qualitatively\nconsistent with conditional framework predictions of transient induction where\nthose are instantiated for the described circuits; they do not establish a\nuniversal exact induction law, do not isolate all auxiliary assumptions\n(magnetic inertia, switching transients, leakage, current history, geometry)\nand supply no quantitative model comparison. The empirical support is a\nnonlogical relation to the induction predictions, not a proof of any\npostulate, and the source limitations of the reading travel into any downstream\nconclusion.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:143521:          "statement": "Let the record vocabulary be that of [[def-em-experiment-record-contract]], and\nadopt a declared affine response model in which the model prediction for a\nrecorded comparison quantity is $$y(s,b):=G\\,s+b,$$ where $G>0$ is a declared\nresponse coefficient (with declared units [recorded]/[source]), $s\\in\\mathbb R$\na declared source-strength parameter and $b\\in\\mathbb R$ a declared background\nparameter in the units of the recorded quantity; the model is part of the\nrecord's analysis assumptions. Then:\n\n(i) [Exact ambiguity] For every $\\delta\\in\\mathbb R$,\n$$y(s+\\delta,\\,b-G\\delta)=G(s+\\delta)+(b-G\\delta)=Gs+b=y(s,b),$$ by the field\narithmetic of $\\mathbb R$ ([[thm-reals-ordered-field]] makes $\\mathbb R$ a\ntotally ordered field). Hence the two parameter pairs $(s,b)$ and\n$(s+\\delta,b-G\\delta)$ produce the same predicted value at every recorded\ncoordinate, and every recorded value or declared closed bound interval\ncompatible with the prediction of one pair is compatible with the prediction\nof the other.\n\n(ii) [Nonidentifiability] When $G\\ne0$ and $\\delta\\ne0$ the two pairs are\ndistinct, yet their model predictions coincide; consequently comparison through\nthe affine model alone constrains only the combination $Gs+b$.\nFor an exact recorded equality $y(s,b)=z$ it fixes that combination to $z$;\nfor a declared bound $|y(s,b)-z|\\le r$, $r\\ge0$, it only constrains it to\n$[z-r,z+r]$. It cannot distinguish the pairs without additional independent\ninformation that distinguishes them. Independently fixing $b$ or $s$ is\nsufficient, though other independently declared constraints can also\ndistinguish the pairs.\n\n(iii) [Explicit instance] With $G=2$, $s=3$, $b=1$ and $\\delta=1$ one has\n$y(3,1)=2\\cdot3+1=7$ and $y(4,-1)=2\\cdot4-1=7$, so the pairs $(s,b)=(3,1)$ and\n$(4,-1)$ are indistinguishable by the recorded value $7$ alone; the same holds\nfor every declared bound interval, since the two predictions coincide exactly.\n\n(iv) [Status] This is a deterministic algebraic nonidentifiability statement\nabout the declared affine response model. It asserts no property of any\napparatus, no historical value, no dataset and no statistical or probabilistic\nclaim; it records that a discrepancy (or an agreement) can test the auxiliary\nbackground and calibration parameters as well as the field or source\nassumptions of a model, so that attributing a recorded outcome to one premise\nrequires independent information resolving the ambiguity.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:143522:          "strategy": "Direct substitution and declared assumptions (the recorded research argument X8 of `worked-example-verifications.md`). In (i), expand and rearrange in the field $\\mathbb R$ ([[thm-reals-ordered-field]] supplies the ordered-field, hence field, axioms for $\\mathbb R$: distributivity gives $G(s+\\delta)=Gs+G\\delta$, associativity and commutativity of addition regroup $(Gs+G\\delta)+(b-G\\delta)$ as $Gs+(G\\delta-G\\delta)+b$, and $G\\delta-G\\delta=0$ with $Gs+0+b=Gs+b$). The same identity shows that any condition, bound interval or comparison expression depending only on the predicted value is unchanged, which proves (i) and the distinctness/nonidentifiability claim (ii) for $G\\ne0$, $\\delta\\ne0$. Clause (iii) is the explicit arithmetic instance $2\\cdot3+1=7=2\\cdot4-1$. Clause (iv) records the scope: no apparatus, historical, dataset or probabilistic claim is made, and the example uses only the record vocabulary and the real field arithmetic. An independent background or source-strength declaration, when available, is an additional hypothesis of any consumer, not a consequence of this example.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:143537:                "locator": "First Series paragraphs 1-33; an instance of a report with background, timing and detector-response auxiliaries that are not calibrated, motivating the declared affine model's ambiguity"
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:143541:          "physical_scope": "Elementary conditional statement about a declared affine response model inside the framework's record vocabulary: the parameter pairs $(s,b)$ and $(s+\\delta,b-G\\delta)$ produce identical predictions, so comparison through this model alone constrains only $Gs+b$ and cannot separate the pairs without additional independent information that distinguishes them. Independently fixing background or source strength is sufficient. It asserts nothing about any apparatus, no historical claim, no dataset, no sampling law and no probability; it is an exact algebraic nonidentifiability of the declared model."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:143606:          "statement": "Adopt the two-beam data and conclusions of [[pthm-em-classical-interference]]:\nfor $v\\in\\mathbb C^3$ write $|v|^2:=\\sum_{j=1}^3v_j\\overline{v_j}\n=\\sum_{j=1}^3|v_j|^2$, using the scalar conjugation and modulus of\n[[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]]; this agrees with\nthe Euclidean squared norm on real vectors. Use\nthe complex electric amplitude $\\alpha(x)=a_1e_1+a_2e_2e^{i\\delta(x)}$ and\nmagnetic amplitude $\\beta(x)=a_1b_1+a_2b_2e^{i\\delta(x)}$ of its clause (a),\nwith $\\delta(x)=(k_2-k_1)\\cdot x$, $b_j=k_j\\times e_j/\\omega$\n([[def-cross-product-in-r3]]) and the plane-wave solutions $(E_j,B_j)$ of\n[[def-em-plane-wave-definition]]; and its clause (b) averaged energy density\n$$\\langle u\\rangle(x)=\\frac{\\varepsilon_0}{4}|\\alpha(x)|^2+\\frac{1}{4\\mu_0}|\\beta(x)|^2$$\nwith the SI constants of [[def-em-si-conventions]], and assume the Axiom of\nCountable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, [[def-countable-choice]]) exactly as\ninherited by the Lebesgue-measure and integral items cited in clause (ii),\nwhich are stated under that hypothesis; no other choice principle is used.\nAssume dimensionless $a_1=a_2=:a>0$, $e_1=e_2=:e$ with real amplitude vector $e$, and\n$k_1\\ne k_2$ with $(k_2-k_1)\\times e\\ne0$; let $x_0$ be a point with\n$\\delta(x_0)\\equiv\\pi\\pmod{2\\pi}$.\n\n(i) [Ideal prediction and its vanishing] At such $x_0$ one has\n$\\alpha(x_0)=ae(1+e^{i\\delta(x_0)})=0$\n([[cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity]]),\nhence the electric field of the superposition vanishes at $x_0$ for every $t$\nand the electric term of the averaged energy density vanishes there. The full\naveraged energy density need not vanish:\n$\\beta(x_0)=a(b_1-b_2)=a\\bigl((k_1-k_2)\\times e\\bigr)/\\omega\\ne0$, so\n$\\langle u\\rangle(x_0)=|a(b_1-b_2)|^2/(4\\mu_0)>0$\n([[def-euclidean-inner-product]] for the positive-definite norm). Thus a claim\nthat 'the ideal intensity vanishes at $x_0$' must name the quantity that\nvanishes.\n\n(ii) [Declared bin response and its finite-rate branch] Adopt a declared\ndetector model with a Lebesgue-measurable bin $W\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$,\n$x_0\\in W$ and $\\lambda_3(W)>0$, a finite real response coefficient\n$\\kappa\\ge0$, and a declared nonnegative density $b:W\\to[0,\\infty)$ whose zero\nextension $\\tilde b$ (equal to $b$ on $W$ and to $0$ off $W$) is Lebesgue\nmeasurable; this measurability of the zero extension is a declared hypothesis\nof the detector model. The declared response functional uses numerical values\nin fixed declared units:\nspatial coordinates are in metres, so $\\lambda_3$ carries $\\mathrm m^3$;\n$\\alpha$ carries $\\mathrm V\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}$ as in\n[[def-em-plane-wave-definition]]. Fix a declared SI unit $\\mathsf U$ of\naccumulated response (possibly dimensionless); a response rate means response\nper unit time, in $\\mathsf U\\,\\mathrm s^{-1}$. Declare $\\kappa$ in\n$\\mathsf U\\,\\mathrm s^{-1}\\,\\mathrm V^{-2}\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}$ and $b$ (including\nany constant $b_0$) in $\\mathsf U\\,\\mathrm s^{-1}\\,\\mathrm m^{-3}$. Thus\n$\\kappa|\\alpha|^2+b$ is a response-rate density in\n$\\mathsf U\\,\\mathrm s^{-1}\\,\\mathrm m^{-3}$, and $R(W)$ carries\n$\\mathsf U\\,\\mathrm s^{-1}$. This is a declared response model, without an\nempirical identification with a detector output. It is defined by the nonnegative\nLebesgue integral\n$$R(W):=\\int_W\\bigl(\\kappa|\\alpha(x)|^2+b(x)\\bigr)\\,d\\lambda_3\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\nHere the continuous real function $|\\alpha|^2$ is Borel measurable by\n[[cor-continuous-functions-are-borel-measurable]] and Lebesgue measurable by\n[[thm-borel-sets-are-lebesgue-measurable]]; closure under nonnegative scaling\nand addition is supplied by\n[[prop-closure-properties-of-measurable-functions-used-by-the-integral]], and\n[[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]] defines the extended integral. No\nboundedness or finite-volume condition on $W$ is imposed. If\n$\\int_W b\\,d\\lambda_3>0$, monotonicity gives\n$$R(W)\\ge\\int_W b\\,d\\lambda_3>0$$ even though $|\\alpha(x_0)|^2=0$. For a\nconstant $b=b_0>0$ this lower bound is $b_0\\lambda_3(W)$ by\n[[def-integral-of-a-nonnegative-simple-function]],\n[[prop-the-nonnegative-integral-agrees-with-the-simple-integral]] and\n[[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]; it may be\n$+\\infty$. The *finite predicted rate* branch additionally requires\n$R(W)<\\infty$, in the response-rate units just declared. A concrete sufficient case is\n$0<\\lambda_3(W)<\\infty$ and constant $0<b_0<\\infty$: the bound\n$|\\alpha|^2\\le4a^2|e|^2$ gives\n$$0<b_0\\lambda_3(W)\\le R(W)\\le(4\\kappa a^2|e|^2+b_0)\\lambda_3(W)<\\infty.$$\nFor $\\kappa=0$ the field contribution is the zero function, without forming\n$0\\cdot(+\\infty)$. Identifying a finite predicted response with a recorded\ncomparison quantity still requires the apparatus, calibration and analysis\ndeclarations of [[def-em-experiment-record-contract]]. Thus a finite positive\nbin prediction is compatible with the ideal pointwise zero, but an extended\ninfinite response is not a recorded finite rate, and neither response is an\nevent probability.\n\n(iii) [Record versus prediction; statistical qualification] The data of\n[[def-em-experiment-record-contract]] are a finite tuple of real recorded\nvalues or reported observations with declared conditions, calibration and\nuncertainty. The predictions of (i)-(ii) are deterministic field quantities or\na declared extended response functional, with a finite real rate only on its\nstated finite branch. Here an ideal fringe pattern describes spatial variation\nof a specified intensity or response; a single detection and a finite data\ntuple are separate records. Whether a finite record displays a specified\nvisibility or pattern requires a declared criterion, calibration, binning,\nbackground and sampling assumptions, none supplied by the field calculation\nalone. No sampling law, probability of failing a visibility criterion,\ndetection statistics or measured histogram is asserted. This example\ndiscriminates no classical or quantum ontology: classical electromagnetic\nwaves interfere and the coherence calculation is a deterministic prediction of\nthe classical model.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:143607:          "strategy": "Direct substitution and declared assumptions. (i): substitute the stated amplitude data into clause (a) of [[pthm-em-classical-interference]]; at $\\delta(x_0)\\equiv\\pi$ the factor $1+e^{i\\pi}=0$ ([[cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity]]), giving $\\alpha(x_0)=0$ and vanishing electric field at $x_0$ for every $t$; the magnetic amplitude is $a(b_1-b_2)$ with $b_1-b_2=((k_1-k_2)\\times e)/\\omega\\ne0$ by the bilinearity of the cross product ([[def-cross-product-in-r3]]) and the nondegeneracy of the Euclidean norm ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]]), so the magnetic term of the averaged density is positive at $x_0$. (ii): $\\alpha$ is a smooth plane-wave amplitude, so $|\\alpha|^2$ is continuous. The cited continuous-to-Borel and Borel-to-Lebesgue suppliers, under the already stated $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, supply its measurability. Restrict to measurable $W$; the explicitly measurable $b$ extends by zero, with positive superlevels measurable in the ambient space. The closure supplier makes the nonnegative sum measurable. Monotonicity applies in $[0,+\\infty]$ and proves the lower bound without finiteness. A positive constant background integrates to $b_0\\lambda_3(W)$ by the simple-integral suppliers. In the finite-volume constant-background branch, $\\alpha=ae(1+e^{i\\delta})$ and the unit modulus of the exponential and subadditivity of its scalar complex modulus ([[lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws]]) give $|\\alpha|\\le2a|e|$. Compare the entire integrand with the constant $4\\kappa a^2|e|^2+b_0$ and use the same monotonicity and simple-integral formula for the finite upper bound; this uses no unproved integral additivity and handles $\\kappa=0$ as the zero function. Unbounded or infinite-measure bins retain the extended response contract; only a separately verified finite response can enter the real-valued record vocabulary. (iii): the record structure is that of [[def-em-experiment-record-contract]], and the vanishing pointwise value is not by itself the bin response; no sampling model, probability, dataset or observation is introduced, and the classical-wave interference character of the prediction is that of [[pthm-em-classical-interference]] clause (d). The only choice assumption is $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, declared for the Lebesgue-measure and integral interface used in clause (ii); no other choice principle is used.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:143622:                "locator": "Clause 3.3.2 (enumerated sources of uncertainty, including finite resolution and incomplete definition of the measurand, printed p. 6) and clause 4.1.1 (the measurement model, printed pp. 8-9), read in the complete PDF; used for the declared-response and background vocabulary of the bin model"
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:143626:          "physical_scope": "Conditional classical-wave example separating an ideal pointwise electric zero from a declared measurable bin response with background. All measurable bins of positive measure and nonnegative measurable backgrounds remain allowed, with a response in $[0,+\\infty]$; a finite-rate interpretation requires a finite response and the declared apparatus/calibration dictionary. The finite-volume constant-positive-background case provides an explicit finite positive response despite the point zero. No data, sampling law, event probability or classical/quantum discrimination is inferred."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:144370:          "statement": "Let $\\omega>0$, $k:=\\omega/c$ with $c$ the vacuum speed of [[def-em-si-conventions]], $\\mu_0>0$ the vacuum permeability of [[def-em-si-conventions]], and $p_0\\in\\mathbb C^3$ (the complex dipole-moment amplitude, in $\\mathrm C\\,\\mathrm m$). Define, on the punctured space $\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$ and all times $t\\in\\mathbb R$, $$E_{\\rm d}(x,t):=\\operatorname{Re}\\Bigl(-\\frac{\\mu_0\\omega^2}{4\\pi r}\\,n\\times(n\\times p_0)\\,e^{i(kr-\\omega t)}\\Bigr),\\qquad B_{\\rm d}(x,t):=\\frac1c\\,n\\times E_{\\rm d}(x,t),$$ where $r:=|x|$ and $n:=x/r$ ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]), with the real part and complex exponential of [[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]] and [[def-complex-exponential]] and the componentwise real-part encoding convention of [[def-em-complex-plane-wave]] (the spatial amplitude here varies with x; no plane-wave instance is asserted). Call $(E_{\\rm d},B_{\\rm d})$ the **ideal harmonic electric-dipole radiation model of frequency $\\omega$ and amplitude $p_0$**, and call $S_{\\rm d}:=E_{\\rm d}\\times B_{\\rm d}/\\mu_0$ its model flux ([[def-em-vacuum-energy-flux]]); the model fields are read as an electric and a magnetic field of [[def-em-fields]]. [Adopted status] This is an explicitly adopted ideal radiation-law model, fixed by the following declarations: (i) it is the leading transverse far-zone field of [[pthm-em-dipole-radiation]](c) evaluated at the harmonic transverse moment $V(u)=\\operatorname{Re}(-\\omega^2p_0e^{-i\\omega u})$, i.e. at $p''$ for the waveform $p(u)=\\operatorname{Re}(p_0e^{-i\\omega u})$; (ii) the model contains no independent magnetic-dipole or higher-multipole radiation, no near-zone or static field and no other radiating component; (iii) it is not asserted to solve the vacuum Maxwell equations at finite $r$, to be the field of any actual source, to satisfy any boundary condition or to have finite total energy on $\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$; (iv) for compact smooth conserved sources, comparison with these prescribed model fields uses the actual source's moment p_act(u), the harmonic waveform p_h(u)=Re(p0 exp(-i omega u)), their second-derivative mismatch M(u)=|p_act''(u)-p_h''(u)|, and the explicit D,Gamma bounds of [[pthm-em-dipole-radiation]]. A bound depending only on that source's D and Gamma is valid when M0 on the observation window; the source's moment is not automatically the independently prescribed p0. These actual-source comparison uses assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]) carried by the cited theorem. The explicit ideal fields themselves and their pointwise flux definition remain choice-free. No measured, empirical or material-response content is adopted: $p_0$ is prescribed model data, not a derived polarizability.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:144395:          "physical_scope": "Explicit ideal radiation-law model of a harmonic electric dipole of complex amplitude $p_0$ in vacuum: the leading transverse far-zone fields $E_{\\rm d},B_{\\rm d}$ on the punctured space, their flux $S_{\\rm d}$, and the declared absence of magnetic-dipole and higher-multipole content. Excluded: exact Maxwell solutions at finite radius, near-zone and static fields, any actual source construction, boundary conditions, finite total energy, self-force and radiation reaction, material response laws, and every measured or empirical statement; the relation to actual compact smooth sources is only through the explicit error bounds of the dipole theorem.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:144676:          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$) as carried by [[pthm-em-dipole-radiation]] and [[lem-em-sphere-zonal-moments]] ([[def-countable-choice]]); the period-average and cross-section algebra itself uses no choice. Adopt the ideal harmonic electric-dipole radiation model of [[def-em-ideal-harmonic-dipole-model]] with frequency $\\omega>0$, wavenumber $k=\\omega/c$ and amplitude $p_0\\in\\mathbb C^3$; let $E_{\\rm d},B_{\\rm d},S_{\\rm d}$ be its fields and model flux. Let $\\kappa\\in\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$ with $|\\kappa|=\\omega/c$, $e\\in\\mathbb C^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$ with $\\kappa\\cdot e=0$, $b:=\\kappa\\times e/\\omega$ ([[def-cross-product-in-r3]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]), and let $(E_{\\rm inc},B_{\\rm inc})$ be the corresponding plane-wave fields of [[def-em-plane-wave-definition]] on $\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$; by [[pthm-em-plane-wave-maxwell]] they satisfy the smooth-branch source-free vacuum Maxwell equations, and by [[pthm-em-wave-energy-average]](b) their period-averaged energy flux has magnitude $I_{\\rm inc}=\\frac{\\varepsilon_0c}{2}|e|^2=\\frac{|e|^2}{2\\mu_0c}$ ([[def-em-si-conventions]]). Define the **model differential scattering cross section** by $$\\frac{d\\sigma}{d\\Omega}(n):=\\frac{r^2\\langle S_{\\rm d}(rn,\\cdot)\\cdot n\\rangle}{\\langle S_{\\rm inc}\\cdot\\hat\\kappa\\rangle},\\qquad n\\in S^2,\\ r>0,$$ where $\\hat\\kappa:=\\kappa/|\\kappa|$, $S_{\\rm inc}:=E_{\\rm inc}\\times B_{\\rm inc}/\\mu_0$ ([[def-em-vacuum-energy-flux]]), and $\\langle f\\rangle:=\\frac1T\\int_0^Tf(s)\\,ds$, $T=2\\pi/\\omega$, is the period average at fixed observation point. Then: (a) [Angular pattern] the ratio is independent of $r$ and of the averaging start, and for every $n\\in S^2$ $$\\frac{d\\sigma}{d\\Omega}(n)=\\frac{\\omega^4|n\\times p_0|^2}{16\\pi^2\\varepsilon_0^2c^4|e|^2}=\\frac{\\mu_0^2\\omega^4|n\\times p_0|^2}{16\\pi^2|e|^2}.$$ (b) [Total cross section] with $d\\sigma_{S^2}$ the surface measure of the unit sphere, $\\sigma:=\\int_{S^2}\\frac{d\\sigma}{d\\Omega}\\,d\\sigma_{S^2}$ equals $$\\sigma=\\frac{\\omega^4|p_0|^2}{6\\pi\\varepsilon_0^2c^4|e|^2}=\\frac{\\mu_0^2\\omega^4|p_0|^2}{6\\pi|e|^2};$$ if $p_0=\\alpha e$ for a complex scalar $\\alpha$ (the scalar polarizability, in $\\mathrm F\\,\\mathrm m^2$), then $\\sigma=\\frac{\\omega^4|\\alpha|^2}{6\\pi\\varepsilon_0^2c^4}$ and $\\frac{d\\sigma}{d\\Omega}(n)=\\frac{\\omega^4|\\alpha|^2}{16\\pi^2\\varepsilon_0^2c^4}|n\\times\\hat e|^2$ with $\\hat e:=e/|e|$. (c) [Fidelity for arbitrary actual sources and matched windows] Let an actual compact smooth conserved source satisfy the exact hypotheses of [[pthm-em-dipole-radiation]], with spatial support radius a>0, source-time interval [tau0,tau1], moment p_act(u), variation bound D(u) and constants Gamma_E,Gamma_B. These source data are independent of the prescribed model amplitude p0. Put\n$$p_h(u):=\\operatorname{Re}(p_0e^{-i\\omega u}),\\qquad M(u):=|p_{\\rm act}''(u)-p_h''(u)|.$$\nFor n in S2,u in[tau0,tau1],r>=2a, define\n$$H(u,r):=\\frac{\\Gamma_E}{r^2}+\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi r}[D(u)+M(u)].$$\nThe actual retarded fields and the model fields satisfy, uniformly in n,\n$$|E_{\\rm act}(rn,u+r/c)-E_{\\rm d}(rn,u+r/c)|\\le H(u,r),$$\n$$|B_{\\rm act}(rn,u+r/c)-B_{\\rm d}(rn,u+r/c)|\\le\\frac{\\Gamma_B+\\Gamma_E/c}{r^2}+\\frac{H(u,r)}c.$$\nAlso retain the sharper direct source-leading-field consequence\n$$|B_{\\rm act}-B_{\\rm d}|\\le\\frac{\\Gamma_B}{r^2}+\\frac{\\mu_0[D(u)+M(u)]}{4\\pi cr}$$\nat the same events. If p_act''=p_h'' on a specified finite window, M0 there and these give the full matched dipole bounds; in particular they imply the older coarser electric bound Gamma_E/r\u00b2+mu0 D/(2pi r). Neither moment matching nor D0 is automatic. A sound sufficient matched branch is p_act=p_h+a0+b0 u on an open neighborhood of the window (any fixed real vectors a0,b0). Such a branch has actual compact realizations over any finite window: choose a smooth time cutoff eta equal1 near the window, let p_eta=eta(p_h+a0+b0 u), choose a nonnegative smooth spatial bump sigma with support inside B_a and integral1, and set rho=-p_eta dot grad sigma,J=p_eta' sigma. Then continuity holds and the actual moment is p_eta; outside the window it is tapered. Apply the actual theorem with the resulting D and Gamma, retaining every finite-radius/source-variation error. No eternal harmonic moment of a compact-time source is asserted. (d) [Scope] conditional predictions of the adopted ideal model: no measured cross section, no frequency-domain response law (the amplitude $p_0$ is prescribed model data), no magnetic-dipole or higher-multipole content, no self-force, material response, finite-size or empirical claim; the scalar-$\\alpha$ clause records a declared proportional response, not a derived polarizability. In all complex-amplitude formulas |v|\u00b2=sum_i |v_i|\u00b2 is the Hermitian norm square; n cross v is complex-linear for real n. The p0=0 case gives zero scattered fields and cross sections while the nonzero incident intensity still defines the ratios. Actual-source fidelity assumes only the explicitly quantified mismatch and source hypotheses; it does not turn the model cross section into a measured or self-consistent scattering prediction.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:144701:          "physical_scope": "Conditional cross sections of the ideal harmonic electric-dipole model in a prescribed incident plane wave: the differential pattern $\\frac{\\omega^4|n\\times p_0|^2}{16\\pi^2\\varepsilon_0^2c^4|e|^2}$, the total cross section and its scalar-polarizability specialization, together with the explicit absolute fidelity bounds to actual compact smooth sources inherited from the dipole theorem. Excluded: measured or source-backed cross sections, derived polarizabilities or response laws, magnetic-dipole and higher-multipole content, self-force, radiation reaction, material and finite-size models, and all empirical or statistical claims.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:145929:          "statement": "Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open interval of time and let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be a nonempty open set of Cartesian spatial coordinates $x=(x_1,x_2,x_3)$, with the standard right-handed orientation. A vector field $u:I\\times\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R^3$ is in the regularity class used here if $u$, its first time partial derivatives, its first spatial partial derivatives, and its second spatial partial derivatives all exist and are continuous. A pressure field is a map $p:I\\times\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ with continuous first spatial partial derivatives; a density field is a map $\\rho:I\\times\\Omega\\to(0,\\infty)$ with continuous first time and first spatial partial derivatives. The scalar field $q:I\\times\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ used in the rotating system below has continuous first spatial partial derivatives. A scalar function of one or several displayed real variables is $C^1$ when its first partial derivatives exist and are continuous; piecewise-$C^1$ paths have the meaning in the line-integral definition below. For a scalar field $f$, set $\\nabla f=(\\partial_{x_1}f,\\partial_{x_2}f,\\partial_{x_3}f)$; for a vector field $u=(u_1,u_2,u_3)$, set $\\operatorname{div}u=\\sum_{j=1}^3\\partial_{x_j}u_j$, $\\operatorname{curl}u=(\\partial_{x_2}u_3-\\partial_{x_3}u_2,\\partial_{x_3}u_1-\\partial_{x_1}u_3,\\partial_{x_1}u_2-\\partial_{x_2}u_1)$, and $\\Delta f=\\sum_{j=1}^3\\partial_{x_j}^2f$, with $\\Delta u$ applied componentwise. For $v,w\\in\\mathbb R^3$, use the right-handed cross product $v\\times w=(v_2w_3-v_3w_2,v_3w_1-v_1w_3,v_1w_2-v_2w_1)$. Define the row-component Jacobian $D_xu=(\\partial_{x_j}u_i)_{i,j}$ and $(u\\cdot\\nabla)u=\\sum_{j=1}^3u_j\\partial_{x_j}u$.\n\nFor constants $\\rho_0>0$ and $\\mu>0$, define the kinematic-viscosity parameter $\\nu=\\mu/\\rho_0$. The formal constant-density incompressible Navier--Stokes system is\n\n$$\\partial_tu+(u\\cdot\\nabla)u+\\rho_0^{-1}\\nabla p-\\nu\\Delta u=0,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}u=0.$$\n\nLet $I_3$ denote the $3\\times3$ identity matrix. The associated formal Cauchy stress matrix is $\\sigma=-pI_3+\\mu(D_xu+(D_xu)^{\\mathsf T})$. The corresponding steady Stokes system is $\\rho_0^{-1}\\nabla p-\\nu\\Delta u=0$ together with $\\operatorname{div}u=0$; it omits both the time derivative and convective term. These are definitions of pointwise mathematical systems and do not assert that a physical fluid has a solution with the stated regularity.\n\nFor a barotropic branch, meaning a pressure prescribed as a function of density alone, let $P:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ have two continuous derivatives and let $V:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ have continuous spatial partial derivatives through order two. The formal compressible Euler system for $u$ and positive density $\\rho$ is\n\n$$\\partial_t\\rho+\\operatorname{div}(\\rho u)=0,\\qquad \\partial_tu+(u\\cdot\\nabla)u=-\\rho^{-1}\\nabla(P(\\rho))-\\nabla V.$$\n\nAt a constant background density $\\rho_0>0$ with $P'(\\rho_0)>0$, the positive parameter $c$ defined by $c^2=P'(\\rho_0)$ is the sound-speed parameter of this mathematical branch. A separate rotating linear system, for constant $\\Omega\\in\\mathbb R^3$ and scalar field $q$, is $\\partial_tu+2\\Omega\\times u=-\\nabla q$ and $\\operatorname{div}u=0$.\n\nThe vorticity field is $\\operatorname{curl}u$. For every fixed $t\\in I$, define the spatial sections $u^{[t]}:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R^3$, $u^{[t]}(x)=u(t,x)$, and $\\sigma^{[t]}:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R^{3\\times3}$, $\\sigma^{[t]}(x)=\\sigma(t,x)$. If $\\gamma:[a,b]\\to\\Omega$ is a closed piecewise-$C^1$ oriented curve, with $\\gamma(a)=\\gamma(b)$, its circulation at time $t$ is $\\oint_\\gamma u^{[t]}\\cdot d\\mathbf r$. If $S$ is a compact oriented regular surface patch in $\\Omega$ with unit normal $n_S$, its volume-flux observable at time $t$ is $\\int_S u^{[t]}\\cdot n_S\\,dS$. The traction at $(t,x)$ for $x\\in S$ is $\\sigma^{[t]}(x)n_S(x)$ and the formal resultant force at time $t$ is the componentwise surface integral $\\int_S\\sigma^{[t]}n_S\\,dS$. When time is fixed, $u(x)$ and $\\sigma(x)$ may denote these spatial sections; for time-independent fields this notation is independent of the chosen time. A steady velocity field is a map $u_0:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R^3$ for which $u(t,x)=u_0(x)$ for all $t\\in I$ and $x\\in\\Omega$; a streamline of $u_0$ is a $C^1$ curve $x:J\\to\\Omega$ on a nondegenerate real interval $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ (open, closed, half-open or unbounded) satisfying $x'(s)=u_0(x(s))$. Here $C^1$ means that $x$ is continuous on $J$, is continuously differentiable on its interior, and at each included finite endpoint has the corresponding one-sided derivative to which the interior derivative extends continuously. The displayed equation is imposed in the interior and with that one-sided derivative at any included endpoint. The variable $s$ is a curve parameter, not time; no curve-existence assertion is made. A closed material loop on a time interval $J\\subseteq I$ is a $C^1$ map $X:J\\times[0,1]\\to\\Omega$ with $X(t,0)=X(t,1)$ and $\\partial_tX(t,\\theta)=u(t,X(t,\\theta))$; the definition asserts no existence of such a map. For the following limit, an exterior spatial domain $\\Omega$ means that there is $R_0>0$ such that $\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^3:|x|>R_0\\}\\subseteq\\Omega$. For a scalar or vector field $f:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ or $f:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R^3$, respectively, and $f_\\infty$ in the same target space, $f(x)\\to f_\\infty$ uniformly in direction as $|x|\\to\\infty$ means $\\sup_{|e|=1}|f(re)-f_\\infty|\\to0$ in the scalar case and $\\sup_{|e|=1}\\|f(re)-f_\\infty\\|_2\\to0$ in the vector case, with $r\\to\\infty$.\n\nFor one optional SI interpretation, the dimensions are $[x]=\\mathrm m$, $[t]=\\mathrm s$, $[u]=\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, $[\\rho]=\\mathrm{kg\\,m^{-3}}$, $[p]=\\mathrm{Pa}$, $[\\mu]=\\mathrm{Pa\\,s}$, $[\\nu]=\\mathrm{m^2\\,s^{-1}}$, $[V]=[q]=\\mathrm{m^2\\,s^{-2}}$, and $[\\Omega]=\\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. Thus $[\\operatorname{curl}u]=\\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, circulation has units $\\mathrm{m^2\\,s^{-1}}$, volume flux has units $\\mathrm{m^3\\,s^{-1}}$, stress has units Pa, and resultant force has units N. In this interpretation $\\rho_0,\\mu,P,V,\\Omega$ are inputs to the displayed formulas, while $\\nu=\\mu/\\rho_0$ and $c^2=P'(\\rho_0)$ are derived quantities relative to those formulas; this classification is not asserted to be universal across physical theories.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:151205:          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $d\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$, let $s\\ge4$ be an integer, let $\\nu\\ge0$, and let $u_0:\\mathbb T^d\\to\\mathbb R^d$ be smooth, real, and divergence-free, where $\\mathbb T^d=(\\mathbb R/2\\pi\\mathbb Z)^d$ has normalized Lebesgue measure and divergence-free means $\\sum_{j=1}^d\\partial_j(u_0)_j=0$. For a smooth periodic $f$, set $\\widehat f(k)=(2\\pi)^{-d}\\int_{[0,2\\pi]^d}f(x)e^{-ik\\cdot x}\\,dx$, $w_k=(1+|k|^2)^{1/2}$, and $\\|f\\|_{H^m}^2=\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z^d}w_k^{2m}|\\widehat f(k)|^2$. Define the Leray multiplier by $P_0=I$ and $P_k=I-kk^{\\mathsf T}/|k|^2$ for $k\\ne0$. Then there is $T>0$, depending only on $d$, $s$, and $\\|u_0\\|_{H^s}$ and independent of $\\nu$ and all higher Sobolev norms of $u_0$, and a unique smooth real field $u:[0,T]\\times\\mathbb T^d\\to\\mathbb R^d$ satisfying\n$$u(0,\\cdot)=u_0,\\qquad \\partial_tu+P((u\\cdot\\nabla)u)=\\nu\\Delta u,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}u=0.$$\nThe same time $T$ works for every integer Sobolev order $m\\ge s$, and $\\sup_{0\\le t\\le T}\\|u(t)\\|_{H^m}<\\infty$ for each such $m$. There is a unique smooth mean-zero scalar field $\\pi$ for which the equivalent full equation $\\partial_tu+(u\\cdot\\nabla)u=-\\nabla\\pi+\\nu\\Delta u$ holds.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:152172:          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $d\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$, let $L>0$, $U_*>0$, and $\\rho_*>0$, and fix an integer $s\\ge4$. Use SI units: $L$ is measured in metres, $U_*$ in metres per second, physical position $X$ in metres, time $t$ in seconds, mass density $\\rho_*$ in kilograms per cubic metre, velocity $u$ in metres per second, pressure $p$ and stress $\\sigma$ in pascals, and dynamic shear viscosity $\\mu$ in pascal seconds. To specialize the Newtonian/Fourier closure, fix an auxiliary constant temperature $T_*>0$ in kelvins and an open state set $\\mathcal S\\subset(0,\\infty)^2$ containing $(\\rho_*,T_*)$; take the auxiliary constitutive state throughout the branch to be the constant pair $(\\rho,T)=(\\rho_*,T_*)$ and the shear-viscosity state law to have value $\\mu$ there. The bulk coefficient may be any admissible value because $\\nabla_d\\cdot u=0$, and any admissible conductivity law gives zero Fourier flux for this constant temperature. No total-energy or entropy-balance solution is asserted. Let $I\\subset\\mathbb R$ be an open time interval containing $0$ and $\\mathbb T_L^d=(\\mathbb R/(2\\pi L\\mathbb Z))^d$; this is the physical periodic cell for $d=3$ and the reduced coordinate torus for $d=1,2$, whose physical cell is $\\mathbb T_L^3$. The smooth model fields are $u\\in C^\\infty(I\\times\\mathbb T_L^d;\\mathbb R^d)$ and $p\\in C^\\infty(I\\times\\mathbb T_L^d;\\mathbb R)$. Torus fields are understood as smooth periodic lifts to Euclidean coordinate space when the local continuum equations are applied. Adopt a force-free incompressible Newtonian model with constant density $\\rho_*$, constant shear viscosity $\\mu\\ge0$, velocity field $u$, pressure multiplier $p$ enforcing $\\nabla_d\\cdot u=0$, and reduced stress\n$$\\sigma^{(d)}=-pI_d+2\\mu D_du,\\qquad D_du=\\tfrac12\\bigl(\\nabla_du+(\\nabla_du)^{\\mathsf T}\\bigr).$$\nOn the retained $d$ coordinates, the local momentum equation and constraint are\n$$\\rho_*\\bigl(\\partial_tu+(u\\cdot\\nabla_d)u\\bigr)=\\nabla_d\\cdot\\sigma^{(d)},\\qquad \\nabla_d\\cdot u=0.$$\nFor $d=3$, this is the direct physical equation on $\\mathbb T_L^3$ with stress $\\sigma^{(3)}=-pI_3+2\\mu D_3u$. For $d=1,2$, $\\mathbb T_L^d$ is the reduced coordinate torus and the corresponding physical cell is $\\mathbb T_L^3$. Let $J_d:\\mathbb T_L^3\\to\\mathbb T_L^d$ be $J_d(X_1,X_2,X_3)=(X_1,\\ldots,X_d)$, with coordinates understood modulo $2\\pi L$. A reduced velocity $u:\\mathbb T_L^d\\to\\mathbb R^d$ and pressure $p:\\mathbb T_L^d\\to\\mathbb R$ lift to\n$$\\widetilde u(X)=\\bigl(u_1(J_dX),\\ldots,u_d(J_dX),0,\\ldots,0\\bigr),\\qquad \\widetilde p(X)=p(J_dX).$$\nThe physical three-dimensional stress is $\\widetilde\\sigma=-\\widetilde p I_3+2\\mu D_3\\widetilde u$, where $D_3\\widetilde u=\\tfrac12(\\nabla_3\\widetilde u+(\\nabla_3\\widetilde u)^{\\mathsf T})$. For $\\nabla_d\\cdot u=0$, direct differentiation gives\n$$\\nabla_3\\cdot\\widetilde u=(\\nabla_d\\cdot u)\\circ J_d,\\qquad (\\widetilde u\\cdot\\nabla_3)\\widetilde u=\\bigl((u\\cdot\\nabla_d)u,0,\\ldots,0\\bigr)\\circ J_d,\\qquad \\nabla_3\\cdot\\widetilde\\sigma=\\bigl((\\nabla_d\\cdot\\sigma^{(d)}),0,\\ldots,0\\bigr)\\circ J_d.$$\nThus the reduced equation is exactly the restriction of the three-dimensional equation to fields independent of the omitted coordinates and with the omitted velocity components zero. Conversely, every smooth solution in this invariant class reduces to the displayed $d$-dimensional equation. When $d=3$, no lift or reduction is used.\nAssume $u_0:\\mathbb T_L^d\\to\\mathbb R^d$ is smooth, real, periodic, and divergence-free. Set $x=X/L\\in\\mathbb T^d=(\\mathbb R/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z))^d$, $\\tau=tU_*/L$, $U_0(x)=u_0(Lx)/U_*$, $\\bar\\nu=\\mu/(\\rho_*U_*L)$, and $\\Pi=p/(\\rho_*U_*^2)$. For a smooth periodic field $f$, use normalized measure $d\\mu_x=(2\\pi)^{-d}dx$, coefficients $\\widehat f(k)=\\int_{\\mathbb T^d}f(x)e^{-ik\\cdot x}\\,d\\mu_x$, and\n$$\\|f\\|_{H^s}^2=\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z^d}(1+|k|^2)^s|\\widehat f(k)|^2.$$\nFor this integer $s$, there is $T>0$, depending only on $d$, $s$, and $\\|U_0\\|_{H^s}$, and independent of $\\bar\\nu$ and all higher Sobolev norms of $U_0$, such that the normalized system has a unique smooth solution $(U,\\Pi)$ on $[0,T]\\times\\mathbb T^d$:\n$$\\partial_\\tau U+(U\\cdot\\nabla_x)U=-\\nabla_x\\Pi+\\bar\\nu\\Delta_xU,\\qquad \\nabla_x\\cdot U=0,\\qquad U(0,\\cdot)=U_0,$$\nwith $\\Pi$ fixed by zero spatial mean at each time. Every integer Sobolev order $m\\ge s$ remains bounded on this same interval. Returning to physical variables gives a unique smooth model evolution on $0\\le t\\le (L/U_*)T$. When $\\mu=0$, the adopted reduced stress is $-pI_d$ (its 3D lift is $-\\widetilde pI_3$) and the mechanical model is inviscid Euler; when $\\mu>0$, it is viscous Navier\u2013Stokes. This is a conditional model deduction and makes no claim of empirical validity, wall evolution, variable-viscosity evolution, or thermal evolution.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:155375:          "statement": "Use the signature $(-,+,+,+)$ throughout. In the flat case, **Minkowski spacetime** means the affine model $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ of [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]]: $A$ is an affine space, $\\eta$ is a nondegenerate Lorentz form on its four-dimensional displacement space, and $C^+$ selects the future cone. In the curved case, a **background spacetime** means a prescribed smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold $(M,g,N)$ of [[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]; the metric and time orientation are fixed data, not dynamical unknowns here. No field equation or completeness assumption is included.\n\nA **massive worldline** on an open interval $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ is a $C^2$ curve $x:I\\to M$ with future-directed timelike tangent $T=dx/ds$ at every parameter value. This is the $C^2$ subclass of [[def-gr-timelike-worldline-and-proper-time]]. A **null worldline** is a $C^2$ curve with nonzero future-directed null tangent everywhere. Its tangent has no proper-time normalization: $g(T,T)=0$, so the massive proper-time formula does not apply. In the flat model these are affine curves into $A$ with tangent in the selected future timelike or null cone from [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]].\n\nAn **observer at an event** $p\\in M$ is a future unit timelike vector $n\\in T_pM$ with $g_p(n,n)=-1$, as in [[def-gr-observer-and-rest-space]]. Its instantaneous rest space is $n^\\perp=\\{v:g_p(v,n)=0\\}$. This is a pointwise vector-space construction; the definition does not assign a clock reading or a moving frame to a lone observer.\n\nAn **observer congruence** on an open set $U\\subseteq M$ is a smooth field of future unit timelike vectors on $U$, as in [[def-gr-observer-congruence-and-adapted-charts]]; its observer curves are its integral curves. A **tetrad** is a chosen orthonormal frame field, as in [[def-gr-local-frame-and-tetrad-fields]]. A **chart** is a coordinate map; an adapted chart is a chart whose spatial coordinates are constant along the congruence's integral curves. These objects are distinct: a chart need not be inertial, a tetrad need not be coordinate induced, and a congruence is a field of observers rather than a chart or a frame. This distinction agrees with [[def-sr-chart-tetrad-and-congruence-distinction]].\n\nThe coordinates and units used later are SI: in an inertial chart $x^0=ct$ and $x^i$ are metres, $t$ is seconds, $c>0$ is metres per second, and proper time is measured in seconds. In a general chart, coordinate components inherit their coordinate units; invariant equations are tensorial. No global inertial chart, global tetrad, or global observer congruence is asserted to exist.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:155510:          "id": "post-rpm-test-clock-and-background",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:155513:          "title": "Ideal clocks on a prescribed Lorentzian background",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:155514:          "statement": "**Adopted convention.** On a prescribed background $(M,g,N)$ from [[def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline]], let $x:[a,b]\\to M$, $a<b$, be a continuous piecewise-$C^1$ future timelike curve in the sense of [[def-gr-timelike-worldline-and-proper-time]]: it has a finite partition $a=s_0<\\cdots<s_k=b$, each segment is $C^1$ up to its endpoints with future timelike one-sided tangents there, and the two tangents at a corner need not agree. If $s$ denotes its parameter and $T_i=dx/ds$ on the $i$th segment, an ideal clock carried along it records the increment\n\n$$\\Delta\\tau=\\frac1c\\sum_{i=1}^k\\int_{s_{i-1}}^{s_i}\\sqrt{-g(T_i,T_i)}\\,ds.$$\n\nThe increment is independent of the admissible finite partition, by additivity of the integral, and is additive across corners. On each smooth segment this is the proper-time parameter with $d\\tau=c^{-1}\\sqrt{-g(dx/ds,dx/ds)}\\,ds$. The flat-spacetime specialization is inherited from [[post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light]]; extending that convention to the curved prescribed background is a separate adoption of this framework.\n\nWhen the parameter $s$ is measured in seconds, $x^0=ct$ is in metres, $c$ is in metres per second, and $\\tau$ is in seconds. This is a model postulate, not a theorem or a reported measurement. It does not assert that a physical clock realizes the idealization exactly, that the background solves gravitational field equations, or that the test body affects the background.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:155542:          "physical_scope": "Adopted classical test-particle framework on a prescribed smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold; the curved-background clock reading convention extends the flat ideal-clock postulate and is not an empirical accuracy claim.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:155605:          "statement": "Let $a,b\\in\\mathbb R$ with $a<b$, and let $x:[a,b]\\to M$ be a $C^2$ future timelike curve on the prescribed background of [[post-rpm-test-clock-and-background]]. Let $H:(-\\epsilon_0,\\epsilon_0)\\times[a,b]\\to M$ be a $C^2$ variation, for some $\\epsilon_0>0$, such that each $H(\\epsilon,\\cdot)$ is future timelike, $H(0,s)=x(s)$, and $H(\\epsilon,a)=x(a)$ and $H(\\epsilon,b)=x(b)$. For a spinless, structureless massive test body of constant primitive mass $m>0$, measured in kilograms, and the SI conversion constant $c>0$ metres per second, adopt the action on this finite segment\n\n$$S_m[x]=-mc\\int_a^b\\sqrt{-g(dx/ds,dx/ds)}\\,ds=-mc^2\\Delta\\tau,$$\n\nwhere $\\Delta\\tau:=c^{-1}\\int_a^b\\sqrt{-g(dx/ds,dx/ds)}\\,ds$ is the ideal-clock increment specified by [[post-rpm-test-clock-and-background]] on this smooth segment. The model's free trajectories are precisely the curves stationary under the fixed-endpoint $C^2$ variations just specified; this is the local variation class in [[lem-rpm-length-action-first-variation]]. When $s$ is measured in seconds, $T$ has units metres per second, and the action has units joule-seconds. This is an adopted model premise, not a consequence of the metric or clock postulate. It excludes self-gravity, radiation reaction, spin, finite-size structure, particle creation, and quantum effects; it does not assert empirical adequacy for any particular body.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:155607:            "post-rpm-test-clock-and-background",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:155612:            "post-rpm-test-clock-and-background": "formulation-prerequisite",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:155633:          "physical_scope": "Classical spinless structureless point test body of constant primitive mass m>0 on a prescribed smooth background; no self-field, radiation reaction, finite-size, quantum, or global-completeness claim.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:155696:          "statement": "Let $a,b\\in\\mathbb R$ with $a<b$. A configuration on $[a,b]$ consists of a $C^2$ curve $x:[a,b]\\to M$ and a smooth positive function $e:[a,b]\\to(0,\\infty)$ on the prescribed background of [[post-rpm-test-clock-and-background]]. Vary $x$ and $e$ independently: $x$ through $C^2$ variations $H:(-\\epsilon_0,\\epsilon_0)\\times[a,b]\\to M$ for some $\\epsilon_0>0$, with $H(0,s)=x(s)$ and $H(\\epsilon,a)=x(a)$, $H(\\epsilon,b)=x(b)$, and no causal restriction on the varied curves; and $e$ through jointly smooth positive variations $e_\\epsilon:[a,b]\\to(0,\\infty)$ whose variation field $\\delta e:=\\partial_\\epsilon e_\\epsilon|_{\\epsilon=0}$ is compactly supported in $(a,b)$. Adopt a distinct massless test-particle sector with primitive action\n\n$$S_0[x,e]=\\frac12\\int_a^b e(s)^{-1}g_{x(s)}(T(s),T(s))\\,ds,\\qquad T(s)=dx/ds,$$\n\nand stationary under all such independent variations. The variational equations and their null branch follow from [[lem-rpm-einbein-variation-and-equivalence]]: the constraint is $g(T,T)=0$, and the physical branch requires $T$ to be nonzero and future-directed. The associated momentum $P=T/e$ is nonzero future null; its positive scale is primitive initial data, not $mU$ for any $m=0$ substitution. Null curves have no proper-time parameter. With $s$ in seconds, $T$ in metres per second, and $e$ in kg$^{-1}$, the integral has units joule-seconds. For finite intervals $[a',b']$ and $[a,b]$, an orientation-preserving $C^\\infty$ diffeomorphism $s=\\phi(r):[a',b']\\to[a,b]$ with $\\phi'>0$ transforms $T_r(r)=\\phi'(r)T(\\phi(r))$ and $e_r(r)=\\phi'(r)e(\\phi(r))$; this leaves the functional invariant by [[lem-rpm-einbein-variation-and-equivalence]] and preserves the stated configuration class. Neither the scale nor a clock reading is inferred from the ray image alone.\n\nThis is an adopted classical model formulation, not an empirical observation or a theorem. Its scope excludes mass generation, interactions, and quantum field effects.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:155698:            "post-rpm-test-clock-and-background",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:155702:            "post-rpm-test-clock-and-background": "formulation-prerequisite",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:155768:          "statement": "On a prescribed smooth time-oriented Lorentzian background, stationarity for the massive action of [[post-rpm-massive-test-particle-action]] is defined on a finite segment $[a,b]$ with the stated fixed-endpoint $C^2$ variations. A stationary massive path on that segment satisfies $D_\\tau U=0$ in proper-time parameter, with $P=mU$ and $m>0$ constant. In the separately adopted null sector of [[post-rpm-null-test-particle-action]], stationarity for the einbein action is likewise defined on a finite segment, using independent fixed-endpoint $C^2$ variations of the curve and smooth interior variations of the einbein. Its nonzero future-null branch has momentum $P$ satisfying $D_\\lambda P=0$ in an affine parameter $\\lambda$ with $dx/d\\lambda=P$. These equations hold locally on the interiors of the finite segments; their ODE solutions extend uniquely to maximal solutions on open intervals, with no action integrated over an infinite interval. For any $\\tau_0\\in\\mathbb R$ and $p_0\\in M$, an initial vector $U_0\\in T_{p_0}M$ that is future timelike and satisfies $g_{p_0}(U_0,U_0)=-c^2$ determines a unique local massive solution with $x(\\tau_0)=p_0$, $U(\\tau_0)=U_0$, and a unique maximal solution on an open interval containing $\\tau_0$. For any $\\lambda_0\\in\\mathbb R$ and $p_0\\in M$, a nonzero future-null vector $P_0\\in T_{p_0}M$ determines a unique local null solution with $x(\\lambda_0)=p_0$, $P(\\lambda_0)=P_0$, and a unique maximal solution on an open interval containing $\\lambda_0$. Neither maximal interval is asserted to be all of $\\mathbb R$.\n\nMore explicitly, there are open intervals $J\\ni\\tau_0$ and $J'\\ni\\lambda_0$ such that the massive local solution is a $C^2$ curve $x:J\\to M$ with $U=dx/d\\tau$, and the null local solution is a $C^2$ curve $x:J'\\to M$ with $P=dx/d\\lambda$; their stated initial values are imposed at the indicated parameter values.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:155809:          "physical_scope": "Conditional model deduction for free spinless test bodies on a prescribed smooth background; local initial-value solutions only, with no geodesic completeness or empirical universality claim.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:155844:          "physical_scope": "A selected smooth external force law for a named massive test body of constant mass on a prescribed background; orthogonality is a compatibility condition preserving the mass shell, not a universal force principle.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:155852:          "statement": "Let $\\Omega\\subseteq M$ be an open region of the prescribed background and let $K$ be a smooth Killing field on $\\Omega$. For a massive force-postulate trajectory $x:I\\to M$, parameterized by proper time on a nonempty open interval $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ with $x(I)\\subseteq\\Omega$, constant $m>0$, $P=mU$, and $D_\\tau P=f$, the scalar $g(K,P)$ obeys\n\n$$\\frac{d}{d\\tau}g(K,P)=g(K,f).$$\n\nThus it is constant when the body is free ($f=0$). For a free null geodesic $y:J\\to M$ on a nonempty open interval $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$, with $y(J)\\subseteq\\Omega$, affine parameter $\\lambda$, and tangent momentum $P=dy/d\\lambda$, $g(K,P)$ is constant along $\\lambda$ as well. If $K$ is a future unit timelike observer field along the applicable curve, then $-c\\,g(K,P)$ has energy units and is the energy defined by that observer; for a general Killing field it is only a conserved scalar, not automatically an energy.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:156010:            "post-rpm-test-clock-and-background",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:156016:            "post-rpm-test-clock-and-background": "physical-assumption",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:156051:          "statement": "In a prescribed background, let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open interval and let $x:I\\to M$ be an affinely parametrized nonzero future-null free geodesic with momentum $P=dx/d\\lambda$. For any constant $a>0$, on the rescaled open interval $I/a:=\\{\\lambda/a:\\lambda\\in I\\}$ the same ray image admits momentum $P_a=aP$ using the affine parameter $\\lambda_a=\\lambda/a$.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:156699:                "locator": "Author-hosted Chapter 1 excerpt; \u00a71.1 fixed-endpoint Euler\u2013Lagrange variation and \u00a71.2.3, equation (1.20), printed pp. 8\u201328. Equation (1.20) uses the interaction sign -q A_mu dx^mu; this library adopts +q A(U) with its own electromagnetic tensor convention, so the source is contextual and does not supply the library sign or curved-background assumption. The curved local variation is proved from inspected mathematical suppliers."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:156795:                "locator": "Author-hosted Chapter 1 excerpt; \u00a71.1 fixed-endpoint Euler\u2013Lagrange variation and \u00a71.2.3, equation (1.20), printed pp. 8\u201328. Equation (1.20) uses the interaction sign -q A_mu dx^mu; this library adopts +q A(U) with its own electromagnetic tensor convention, so the source is contextual and does not supply the library sign or curved-background assumption. The cited section gives action context only; the local gauge-boundary identity is proved in this item."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:156813:          "statement": "Use the smooth, time-oriented Lorentzian background $(M,g,N)$, signature $(-,+,+,+)$, and the distinct chart, observer, congruence, tetrad and worldline meanings of [[def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline]]. The metric and time orientation are prescribed data, not dynamical variables. A **charged spinning test body** in this definition consists of the following model quantities along a massive worldline $z:I\\to M$:\n\n- $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ is an open interval whose parameter is proper time $\\tau$ in seconds; $z$ is $C^2$, future-directed and timelike, and $U:=dz/d\\tau$ obeys $g(U,U)=-c^2$. The SI conversion constant is $c>0$ metres per second; in a flat specialization use the affine Minkowski model and the proper-time decomposition of [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]] and [[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]].\n- The constant rest-mass parameter $m>0$ has unit kilogram, and the constant charge parameter $q\\in\\mathbb R$ has unit coulomb. They are primitive model parameters; this definition does not derive them from the background field or from another framework.\n- The **kinetic four-momentum** is the derived vector $P:=mU$ along $z$, with unit kilogram metre per second. It is distinct from the potential-dependent covector defined in [[lem-rpm-charge-gauge-boundary]].\n- The **spin or polarization vector** is a $C^1$ tangent vector field $s(\\tau)\\in T_{z(\\tau)}M$ constrained by $g(s,U)=0$, so it lies in the positive-definite instantaneous rest space $U^\\perp$. Its unit is angular momentum, kilogram metre squared per second. This is a classical model vector; no quantization or quantum spin state is defined here.\n- The gyromagnetic ratio $g_s\\in\\mathbb R$ is a dimensionless primitive species parameter. In the instantaneous rest space the model defines the magnetic-moment vector by $\\mu=(g_s q/2m)s$, with unit ampere metre squared. No value $g_s=2$ is assumed, and this constitutive relation is not deduced from Lorentz covariance or classical electromagnetism.\n- The prescribed external electromagnetic field is a $C^1$ alternating covariant two-tensor $F$ on an open region of $M$, independent of the unknown curve. Define $F^\\sharp:T M\\to T M$ by $g(F^\\sharp(v),w)=F(w,v)$. In the flat specialization, a named inertial chart with $x^0=ct$ and $x^i$ in metres uses $F_{0i}=-E_i/c$ and $F_{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$, matching [[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]]. On the curved background, $F$ is only a two-form; at an event its components in a chosen orthonormal tetrad use the same algebraic convention. Such a tetrad is a basis field, not a coordinate chart or an observer congruence, as distinguished in [[def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline]]. The components in an orthonormal tetrad have tesla units. This is an explicit curved-background model input. The cited EM supplier is a flat Minkowski definition and does not derive a curved field or curved Maxwell system.\n- On any open potential domain where a $C^2$ covector field $A$ exists, $F=dA$ with components $F_{ab}=\\partial_aA_b-\\partial_bA_a$ and $A$ has tesla metre components in the stated coordinates. No global potential is assumed, and no Maxwell equation or constitutive law is imposed here.\n\nThis is a test-body model on a fixed background: self-gravity, gravitational backreaction, self-field evaluation, radiation reaction, collisions, electric dipole moments, field-gradient translational forces and higher multipoles are outside this definition. Units follow the framework-local SI conventions of [[def-em-si-conventions]] and do not identify quantities across frameworks.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:156841:                "locator": "Author-hosted Chapter 1 excerpt; \u00a71.1 fixed-endpoint Euler\u2013Lagrange variation and \u00a71.2.3, equation (1.20), printed pp. 8\u201328. Equation (1.20) uses the interaction sign -q A_mu dx^mu; this library adopts +q A(U) with its own electromagnetic tensor convention, so the source is contextual and does not supply the library sign or curved-background assumption. The cited section gives action context only; this definition adopts its charged-body quantities separately and treats the curved field as prescribed model data."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:156857:          "physical_scope": "Definitions of one classical massive charged and spinning test body on a prescribed smooth time-oriented Lorentzian background: constant positive mass and fixed charge/gyromagnetic parameter, proper-time worldline, kinetic momentum, orthogonal polarization and magnetic-moment convention, and prescribed external two-form with local potential. These are ideal model quantities and SI conventions, not a field equation, motion law, measured gyromagnetic value or quantum-spin construction. Self-gravity, self-field evaluation, radiation reaction, collisions, electric dipole forces, field-gradient translational forces and higher multipoles are excluded as stated in the Definition."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:156880:                "locator": "Author-hosted Chapter 1 excerpt; \u00a71.1 fixed-endpoint Euler\u2013Lagrange variation and \u00a71.2.3, equation (1.20), printed pp. 8\u201328. Equation (1.20) uses the interaction sign -q A_mu dx^mu; this library adopts +q A(U) with its own electromagnetic tensor convention, so the source is contextual and does not supply the library sign or curved-background assumption. The cited section gives action context only; this postulate separately adopts a prescribed smooth external potential and makes no empirical claim."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:156890:          "physical_scope": "Adopted dynamics for one massive charged test body on a prescribed smooth time-oriented Lorentzian background, on finite timelike curve segments contained in a local potential domain. The background metric and external electromagnetic two-form are fixed; the model excludes self-field evaluation, self-gravity, radiation reaction, variable mass or charge, and any global-potential or global-solution claim.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:156898:          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ for the canonical tangent-bundle smooth structure, and let $\\nabla$ be the metric-selected Levi\u2013Civita connection of [[thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative]]. Let $U$ and the prescribed external two-form $F$ be as in [[def-rpm-charge-spin-model]], and let the translational motion obey the adopted action law [[post-rpm-external-charge-action]]. For a $C^1$ spin or polarization vector $s$ along the worldline with $g(s,U)=0$, define $G:=g_s/2$ and $K:=F(s,U)=g(s,F^\\sharp(U))$. Adopt the transport law\n\n$$\\nabla_U s=\\frac{q}{m}\\left[G F^\\sharp(s)-(G-1)\\frac{U}{c^2}K\\right].$$\n\nHere $\\nabla_U s$ denotes the covariant derivative of the section $s$ along the specified curve, rather than differentiation of an unspecified ambient extension. More generally, for any $C^1$ vector field $V$ along this $C^2$ worldline $z$, define\n\n$$(D_\\tau V)^a:=\\frac{dV^a}{d\\tau}+\\Gamma^a{}_{bc}(z(\\tau))U^bV^c,\\qquad \\nabla_U s:=D_\\tau s.$$\n\nThe components of $V$ are $C^1$ functions of $\\tau$ in each smooth chart, and the displayed derivative is continuous. This is the pullback-connection derivative on $z^*TM$. To verify that the component definition is independent of the chart, let $y=y(x)$ and $J^\\alpha{}_a:=\\partial y^\\alpha/\\partial x^a$. The connection transformation law gives\n\n$$\\Gamma'^\\alpha{}_{\\beta\\gamma}J^\\beta{}_bJ^\\gamma{}_c=J^\\alpha{}_a\\Gamma^a{}_{bc}-\\partial_bJ^\\alpha{}_c.$$\n\nSince $V'^\\alpha=J^\\alpha{}_aV^a$, the ordinary chain and product rules give $\\dot V'^\\alpha=J^\\alpha{}_a\\dot V^a+(\\partial_bJ^\\alpha{}_c)U^bV^c$. The two transition-derivative terms cancel, leaving $(D_\\tau V)'^\\alpha=J^\\alpha{}_a(D_\\tau V)^a$. Thus no smoothness of the curve beyond $C^2$, or of the section beyond $C^1$, is required. Metric compatibility also gives $\\frac d{d\\tau}g(V,W)=g(D_\\tau V,W)+g(V,D_\\tau W)$ for $C^1$ sections $V,W$: insert the component formulas and $\\partial_bg_{ac}=\\Gamma^d{}_{ba}g_{dc}+\\Gamma^d{}_{bc}g_{ad}$. These conventions specify the mathematical derivative in the adopted transport law; they add no further physical motion assumption.\n\nThis is an ideal classical external-field dipole model with constant $q,m,g_s$. Its flat homogeneous-field specialization is the BMT polarization law in the stated RPM convention. The formula on a curved background, and its use for a spatially varying prescribed field, are separately adopted model assumptions; neither follows from the flat BMT source or from general covariance alone.\n\nThe model excludes an electric dipole moment, field-gradient or Stern\u2013Gerlach translational forces, spin backreaction, radiation reaction, collisions and higher multipoles. The parameter $g_s$ is not fixed to $2$ and no measured value is asserted. Using this classical vector to represent a real quantum particle's polarization is a correspondence assumption; the postulate does not construct quantum spin or claim empirical validation.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:156925:                "locator": "PDF pp. 6\u20137: the covariant Thomas\u2013Bargmann\u2013Michel\u2013Telegdi transport equation and its Fermi\u2013Walker derivative in a curved background, with a separate electric-dipole term. The source uses the opposite metric signature and field-index convention; the present postulate states its own converted convention and does not import the source as a proof."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:156978:                "locator": "Author-hosted Chapter 1 excerpt; \u00a71.1 fixed-endpoint Euler\u2013Lagrange variation and \u00a71.2.3, equation (1.20), printed pp. 8\u201328. Equation (1.20) uses the interaction sign -q A_mu dx^mu; this library adopts +q A(U) with its own electromagnetic tensor convention, so the source is contextual and does not supply the library sign or curved-background assumption. The curved local variation is proved from inspected mathematical suppliers."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:156988:          "physical_scope": "A conditional equivalence for finite fixed-endpoint timelike curve variations in a local potential domain of the prescribed curved background model: stationarity of the adopted external charged-particle action is equivalent to the covariant Lorentz-force equation with the RPM SI sign convention. It asserts no global potential, local or global trajectory existence, self-field, radiation reaction, or empirical claim.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:157034:          "physical_scope": "Local solutions of the external-field charged test-body model with constant $m>0$ and $q$ preserve the future timelike four-velocity shell in a fixed curved Lorentzian background. The laboratory three-force and power identities are asserted only in the flat Minkowski specialization with a named inertial SI chart and the compatible EM tensor convention. No global completeness, self-force, radiation reaction, measurement or empirical claim is included.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:157238:          "id": "def-rpm-h-background-and-model-quantities",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:157241:          "title": "Lorentz background, particle quantities and parameter units",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:157358:          "statement": "Let $I\\subset\\mathbb R$ be an open interval, and let $x:I\\to M$ be a $C^2$ future-directed timelike worldline on a prescribed smooth, time-oriented Lorentzian background $(M,g,T)$ of signature $(-+++)$, with $m>0$ a fixed primitive mass in kg and $c>0$ in m/s. For any compact segment $[a,b]\\subset I$ with $a<b$, adopt the stationary-action model on that segment\n\n$$S_{m,[a,b]}[x]=-mc\\int_a^b\\sqrt{-g(x',x') }\\,d\\lambda.$$\n\nHere $I\\subset\\mathbb R$ is an open interval, $\\lambda$ is its real coordinate, and $x'$ denotes $dx/d\\lambda$. The future-timelike condition makes $x'$ nowhere zero. On a neighborhood of $[a,b]$, $x$ is $C^2$ (so its tangent is $C^1$). If $\\widetilde\\lambda=f(\\lambda)$ is an increasing $C^1$ reparametrization with $f'>0$, then $\\sqrt{-g(dx/d\\widetilde\\lambda,dx/d\\widetilde\\lambda)}\\,d\\widetilde\\lambda=\\sqrt{-g(x',x')}\\,d\\lambda$ by the chain rule. An admissible variation is a family $x_\\epsilon(\\lambda)$ defined for $\\epsilon\\in(-\\delta,\\delta)$, $\\delta>0$, and $\\lambda$ in a common neighborhood of $[a,b]$, with $x_0=x$, that is $C^2$ jointly in $(\\epsilon,\\lambda)$, has fixed endpoint events $x_\\epsilon(a)=x(a)$ and $x_\\epsilon(b)=x(b)$, and remains future timelike for sufficiently small $|\\epsilon|$. The adopted motion principle is that on every such segment $[a,b]$, the physical worldline $x$ satisfies $\\left.\\frac{d}{d\\epsilon}\\right|_{\\epsilon=0}S_{m,[a,b]}[x_\\epsilon]=0$ for every admissible variation through $x$. The integrand is the proper-time length density, so $S_{m,[a,b]}=-mc^2\\int_a^b d\\tau$; its dimension is action (J s). The adopted premise concerns this ideal free test body only. It is neither a theorem about all matter nor an empirical report, and supplies no backreaction or completeness claim. The flat ideal-clock specialization agrees with [[post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light]]; adopting this action on a curved background remains a separate premise.\n\nThe cotangent phase-space carrier convention assumes countable choice AC\u03c9 only; full choice is not assumed.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:157361:            "def-rpm-h-background-and-model-quantities",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:157368:            "def-rpm-h-background-and-model-quantities": "formulation-prerequisite",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:157390:          "physical_scope": "Classical spinless structureless point test body with fixed primitive mass m>0 on a prescribed background; excludes self-field, radiation reaction, finite size, spin and quantum effects.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:157398:          "statement": "On a prescribed background $(M,g,T)$, choose an open parameter interval $I$ and a compact segment $[a,b]\\subset I$ with $a<b$. In a spacetime chart containing the curve and sufficiently small variations, let $x:I\\to M$ be smooth near $[a,b]$, let $p(\\lambda)\\in T^*_{x(\\lambda)}M$ be a smooth covector field along $x$, and let $e$ be smooth and positive near $[a,b]$. In this chart, write $g_{ab}=g(\\partial_a,\\partial_b)$ for $a,b\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}$, let $(g^{ab})$ be the inverse matrix, and define $p^\\sharp=\\sum_{a,b=0}^3g^{ab}p_b\\partial_a$; equivalently, $g(p^\\sharp,w)=p(w)$ for every tangent vector $w$. Adopt the distinct massless classical particle action on this segment\n\n$$S_{0,[a,b]}[x,p,e]=\\int_a^b\\left[p_a x'^a-eC_0(x,p)\\right]d\\lambda,\\qquad C_0=\\tfrac12g^{ab}p_ap_b.$$\n\nAdmissible variations are smooth one-parameter families of chart fields $(x_\\epsilon,p_\\epsilon,e_\\epsilon)$ on a common neighborhood of $[a,b]$, with $x_\\epsilon(a)=x(a)$ and $x_\\epsilon(b)=x(b)$; the $p_\\epsilon$ and $e_\\epsilon$ variations are independent and have no endpoint constraints, while $e_\\epsilon>0$ for sufficiently small $|\\epsilon|$. Impose the physical branch only on stationary solutions after these variations: $p\\ne0$ and $p^\\sharp$ is future null. With all spacetime coordinates measured in metres and dimensionless $\\lambda$, $p_a$ has units kg m/s, $C_0$ has units kg\u00b2m\u00b2/s\u00b2 and $e$ has units s/kg, making the action J s. Other parameter units scale $e$ accordingly. This is a separate adopted model: null curves have no proper-time parameter, the model does not arise by setting $m=0$ in $P=mU$, and its positive momentum scale is additional initial data. On the nonzero branch, the vertical differential is $d_pC_0=p^\\sharp\\ne0$: nondegeneracy of $g^{-1}$ and $p\\ne0$ give $p^\\sharp\\ne0$. Hence the shell is a regular primary constraint in the sense of [[def-rpm-h-regular-constraint-system]]. This postulate is not an empirical observation or a deduction from the massive model.\n\nThe cotangent phase-space carrier convention assumes countable choice AC\u03c9 only; full choice is not assumed.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:157401:            "def-rpm-h-background-and-model-quantities",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:157408:            "def-rpm-h-background-and-model-quantities": "formulation-prerequisite",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:157694:          "statement": "Let $(M,g,T)$ be a smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold. At $x\\in M$, for $p\\in T_x^*M$, define $p^\\sharp$ as the unique vector satisfying $g_x(p^\\sharp,W)=p(W)$ for every $W\\in T_xM$, and set $g_x^{-1}(p,r):=g_x(p^\\sharp,r^\\sharp)$. In a chart, $p^\\sharp=g^{ab}p_b\\partial_a$, where $(g^{ab})$ is the inverse matrix of $g_{ab}=g(\\partial_a,\\partial_b)$. A covector on this background is future when its metric dual is future-directed. Let $m,c>0$ and $L(x,V)=-mc\\sqrt{-g(V,V)}$ on the future timelike tangent cone. Its fibre derivative is\n\n$$p_a=mc\\frac{g_{ab}V^b}{s},\\qquad s=\\sqrt{-g(V,V)}>0.$$\n\nThe velocity Hessian is $mc(g_{ab}/s+V_aV_b/s^3)$, has kernel exactly $\\mathbb RV$, and rank three. The image consists exactly of the future shell $\\Sigma_m^+=\\{(x,p)\\in T^*M:g_x^{-1}(p,p)=-m^2c^2,\\ p^\\sharp\\text{ future}\\}$. Each fibre is a connected positive ray, and the Legendre map is a surjective submersion onto this embedded image. Its energy $p(V)-L$ is zero; the map has no local inverse in the four-velocity direction.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:157881:          "statement": "Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval with nonempty interior, and let $\\gamma:I\\to M$ be a $C^1$ future-timelike curve on a prescribed smooth Lorentzian background, parameterized by $\\lambda$; set $V=d\\gamma/d\\lambda$. For the massive square-root Lagrangian $L=-mc\\sqrt{-g(V,V)}$ with $m,c>0$, its fibre derivative is $p=mc\\,V^\\flat/\\sqrt{-g(V,V)}$. If $\\tau$ is proper time along $\\gamma$, then $U=d\\gamma/d\\tau=cV/\\sqrt{-g(V,V)}$, so the raised momentum is $p^\\sharp=mU$. In flat spacetime this agrees with the adopted special-relativistic mechanical momentum convention. This is a model identity, not a measurement.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:157884:            "def-rpm-h-background-and-model-quantities",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:157900:            "def-rpm-h-background-and-model-quantities": "formulation-prerequisite",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:157931:            "def-rpm-h-background-and-model-quantities",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:157938:            "def-rpm-h-background-and-model-quantities": "formulation-prerequisite",
