research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:158138:          "physical_scope": "Conditional flat-metric coordinate example using the adopted normal-observer energy convention and the stated future massive shell; no measurement, empirical inference or curved-background claim.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:158536:          "title": "Conditional free Poincare and background Killing charges",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:158537:          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ for the moment-map supplier. Adopt either the massive free test-particle action or the distinct massless shell action of [[post-rpm-h-free-test-particle-action]] and [[post-rpm-h-massless-shell-action]]. Conditional on those model choices and the corresponding future free-shell equations, the affine Poincar\u00e9 charges are conserved on flat spacetime; in a fixed-time Hamiltonian description the boost charge includes its explicit partial-time derivative. On a prescribed curved background, every mechanical scalar $p_a\\xi^a$ for a Killing field is conserved along the adopted free shell. These are model deductions. They do not establish the empirical adequacy of either action, and they do not extend to arbitrary interacting potentials.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:158574:          "physical_scope": "Under the exact action and background symmetry, adopted mechanical charge observables are conserved; time-dependent boosts include partial-time cancellation.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:159221:          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice as inherited from [[def-rpm-body-moments]]. For a neutral test body, let $z:I\\to M$ be a $C^2$ future timelike curve parametrized by proper time, let $U=dz/d\\tau$, and let $p^a$ and $S^{ab}=-S^{ba}$ be the momentum and spin moments of [[def-rpm-body-moments]]. Adopt the pole-dipole evolution equations\n$$D_\\tau p^a=-\\frac12 R^a{}_{bcd}U^bS^{cd},\\qquad D_\\tau S^{ab}=p^aU^b-p^bU^a.$$\nA spin-supplementary condition (SSC) is an additional equation $S^{ab}n_b=0$ for a specified future unit timelike field $n$ along $z$; this postulate does not select one SSC, and $p$ is an independently evolved moment rather than a primitive assumption $p=mU$.\n\nFor a retained quadrupole or higher-multipole model, adopt\n$$D_\\tau p^a=-\\frac12 R^a{}_{bcd}U^bS^{cd}+f_{\\rm extra}^a,\\qquad D_\\tau S^{ab}=p^aU^b-p^bU^a+N_{\\rm extra}^{ab},$$\nwhere $f_{\\rm extra}$ is a specified smooth force map along the state and $N_{\\rm extra}^{ab}=-N_{\\rm extra}^{ba}$ is a specified smooth torque map. A quadrupole convention that may be adopted is\n$$f_{\\rm quad}^a=-\\frac16J^{bcde}\\nabla^aR_{bcde},\\qquad N_{\\rm quad}^{ab}=\\frac43R^{[a}{}_{cde}J^{b]cde},$$\nwith $J^{bcde}$ satisfying the algebraic symmetries of the curvature tensor, carrying units $\\mathrm{kg\\,m^4\\,s^{-2}}$, and brackets including the factor $1/2$. Any evolved $J$ or higher moment needs its own transport or constitutive law; without it the multipole equations are not a closed initial-value model.\n\nThe pole-dipole and multipole equations are adopted finite-dimensional truncations, not derivations from the moment integrals above. Their use as approximations for a material body is restricted to regimes in which its size is small compared with the relevant curvature and background-variation lengths and omitted moment effects are negligible on the stated finite interval. These conditions are qualitative applicability assumptions here: this postulate supplies no uniform error bound, no convergence of truncations, and no global existence claim. No experimental result or measurement is asserted.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:159236:          "physical_scope": "Adopted neutral pole-dipole and explicitly closed multipole test-body truncations on a prescribed smooth Lorentzian background. They exclude self-gravity, self-force, arbitrary stress-integral derivation, and any unqualified error or convergence theorem."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:161298:          "statement": "Let $\\mathcal D\\subseteq\\mathbb R^8$ be an open coordinate-state domain with state $z=(x,U)\\in\\mathbb R^4\\times\\mathbb R^4$, and let $G:\\mathcal D\\to\\mathbb R^8$ be the smooth autonomous coordinate vector field of the shell-tangent force system in [[lem-rpm-shell-tangent-force-ivp]]. Let $z:(a,b)\\to\\mathcal D$ be its unique maximal solution. If $b<\\infty$ and there are $b_0\\in(a,b)$ and a compact set $K\\subset\\mathcal D$ such that $z([b_0,b))\\subseteq K$, then the solution extends beyond $b$, contradicting maximality. The analogous statement holds at a finite left endpoint. Therefore a finite maximal endpoint can occur only if the state eventually leaves every compact subset of its open coordinate-state domain.\n\nThis is a mathematical continuation criterion for the coordinate ODE. It asserts neither that a solution exists for all proper times nor that the background spacetime is geodesically complete.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:161449:          "physical_scope": "Conditional local dynamics of one massive test body with constant primitive mass on a prescribed smooth time-oriented Lorentzian background, under a separately adopted smooth external force orthogonal to its future unit velocity. It proves local motion and a chart-local compact-continuation criterion only; it makes no universal-force, global-completeness, self-force, or empirical claim.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:161459:          "statement": "The following are two separate conditional reductions, with no identification of the flat inertial quantities in clause (a) with the curved coordinate quantities in clause (b).\n\n**(a) Flat inertial kinematics.** In a named inertial chart of the flat massive-particle model [[post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics]], use the mechanical energy and momentum of [[def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy]]. Let $C>0$, $0<\\lambda\\le\\lambda_*$, and $\\rho=C^2\\lambda_*<1$. For any massive state with constant $m>0$, speed $v$, and $|v|\\le C\\sqrt\\lambda\\,c$, the bounds of [[lem-rpm-low-speed-uniform-remainders]] hold with $\\varepsilon=\\lambda$: the relativistic momentum and kinetic energy have the stated uniform Newtonian remainders, and the coordinate-time action density after subtracting the rest term differs from $\\frac12m|v|^2$ by at most $mC^4c^2\\lambda^2/[8(1-\\rho)^{3/2}]$. This is a kinematic assertion; it assumes no force law and proves no trajectory convergence.\n\n**(b) Curved weak-field free motion.** Adopt the separate massive test-particle action on a prescribed smooth background ([[post-rpm-massive-test-particle-action]]). Let the stationary weak-field metric family, scale $L$, spatial potential $\\Phi=\\varepsilon c^2\\varphi$, massive geodesic, compact spatial region, speed bound, Newtonian comparison solution, and Hessian bound satisfy exactly hypotheses (H1)\u2013(H3) of [[thm-gr-weak-field-scaled-trajectory-limit]]. If the massive particle's free worldline is the geodesic of that theorem, then for the scaled trajectories $(X_\\varepsilon,W_\\varepsilon)$ and $(X_*,W_*)$,\n\n$$D(S):=\\|X_\\varepsilon(S)-X_*(S)\\|_2+\\|W_\\varepsilon(S)-W_*(S)\\|_2\\le\\bigl(D(0)+C_3\\varepsilon S_*\\bigr)e^{(1+K_2)S_*}\\qquad(0\\le S\\le S_*),$$\n\nwith $C_3,K_2,S_*$ exactly as in that theorem. In particular, for identical scaled initial data, $\\sup_{0\\le S\\le S_*}D(S)\\le C_3\\varepsilon S_*e^{(1+K_2)S_*}=O(\\varepsilon)$ on this fixed scaled interval. The corresponding coordinate-time horizon is $T_\\varepsilon=LS_*/(\\sqrt\\varepsilon c)$; this is not a large-time limit at fixed $\\varepsilon$.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:161462:            "post-rpm-test-clock-and-background",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:161473:            "post-rpm-test-clock-and-background": "physical-assumption",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:162121:          "design_state": "reserved-EMG-C01 A result; exact fixed-background residual only, no solution-error theorem",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:162445:          "physical_scope": "Conditional flat-specialization identity for the smooth vacuum Maxwell subsystem and the local Lorentz force/power density in the selected SI (-+++) dictionary. It does not assert that a nonzero field on the flat background solves the coupled Einstein equation, that curved spacetime has a global inertial chart, or that any adopted postulate is empirically true.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:162476:          "statement": "Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open interval, $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ a nonempty open region, and $U=I\\times\\Omega$ a flat inertial Minkowski model with metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, orientation $dx^0\\wedge dx^1\\wedge dx^2\\wedge dx^3$, and coordinates $x^0=ct$ and $x^i$ in metres. Let $E,B:U\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be jointly $C^1$, with every additional derivative displayed below existing and continuous, and let $\\rho,J$ be continuous. Use $F_{0i}=-E_i/c$, $F_{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$, $F^{0i}=E_i/c$, $F^{i0}=-E_i/c$, $F^{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$, $j^0=c\\rho$, $j^i=J^i$, and $\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ as fixed by [[def-emg-geometry-units]].\n\nOn this flat specialization, the tensor systems from the smooth curved postulate are\n\n$$(\\mathrm{TM1})\\quad \\partial_\\mu F^{\\mu\\nu}=-\\mu_0j^\\nu\\quad(\\nu=0,1,2,3),\\qquad (\\mathrm{TM2})\\quad \\partial_\\lambda F_{\\mu\\nu}+\\partial_\\mu F_{\\nu\\lambda}+\\partial_\\nu F_{\\lambda\\mu}=0\\quad(\\lambda,\\mu,\\nu=0,1,2,3).$$\n\nThey hold if and only if the four adopted smooth SI vacuum Maxwell equations hold on $U$:\n\n$$\\operatorname{div}E=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}B=0,\\qquad \\operatorname{curl}E=-\\partial_tB,\\qquad \\operatorname{curl}B=\\mu_0J+c^{-2}\\partial_tE.$$\n\nHere $\\partial_t$ is at fixed spatial position and $\\operatorname{div}$ and $\\operatorname{curl}$ use the positively oriented Euclidean spatial basis. In addition, for the tensor force density $f^\\nu:=F^{\\nu\\lambda}j_\\lambda$, its time and spatial components satisfy\n\n$$f^0=(J\\cdot E)/c,\\qquad f^i=\\rho E_i+(J\\times B)_i.$$\n\nThus they reproduce the adopted power density $J\\cdot E$ divided by $c$ and force density $\\rho E+J\\times B$ of [[post-em-lorentz-coupling]]. The tensor systems have exactly eight independent scalar equations: four sourced components and four independent homogeneous components. All these are conditional component identities for the selected smooth model; no empirical premise is used. The result checks only the Maxwell subsystem on a flat background and the local force dictionary, not the coupled Einstein equation."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:162514:          "physical_scope": "An exact source-free constant electromagnetic two-form on a prescribed flat Minkowski background, with its electric and magnetic components in one observer tetrad. It verifies only the Maxwell subsystem and makes no claim that the nonzero field and flat metric solve the coupled Einstein equation or represent an observation.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:162535:          "statement": "Let $E_0\\in\\mathbb R$ and $B_0\\in\\mathbb R$ be constant parameters with units $\\mathrm{V\\,m^{-1}}$ and tesla, respectively. On $M=\\mathbb R^4$ use the flat Minkowski metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, the time orientation in which $\\partial_0$ is future-directed, and the orientation for which $dx^0\\wedge dx^1\\wedge dx^2\\wedge dx^3$ is positive. Use coordinates $x^0=ct,x^1,x^2,x^3$ in metres and the future unit observer $n=\\partial_0$. Set\n\n$$F:=-\\frac{E_0}{c}\\,dx^0\\wedge dx^1+B_0\\,dx^2\\wedge dx^3,\\qquad j:=0.$$\n\nThe covariant components are constant, with $F_{01}=-E_0/c$, $F_{23}=B_0$, and all other independent components zero. The observer electric and magnetic covectors have spatial components $(E_0,0,0)$ and $(B_0,0,0)$, respectively. This is a prescribed-background example of the source-free Maxwell subsystem. It does not assert that the pair $(\\eta,F)$ solves the coupled Einstein equation."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:162831:                "title": "J. Lebl, Basic Analysis II, Theorem 8.5.1 and higher-regularity discussion",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:164259:          "title": "Exact background scalar plane wave and derived current",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:164264:          "statement": "On $\\mathbb R^4$ with $x^0=ct$, Minkowski metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, and trivial bundle, set $A=0$, $V(u)=m_s^2u$ with $m_s>0$ in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$, and\n\n$$\\varphi(t,\\mathbf x)=C\\exp(-i\\omega t+i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x),$$\n\nwhere $C\\ne0$ is dimensionless, $\\mathbf k\\in\\mathbb R^3$ has units $\\mathrm m^{-1}$, and $\\omega:=c\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+m_s^2}>0$ has units $\\mathrm s^{-1}$. For the scalar action of [[def-emg-charged-scalar-functional]], this is an exact solution of the local background scalar equation, equivalently stationary under compactly supported scalar variations. The infinite-volume action value need not be finite; only the local equation and compactly supported variations are used. If the coupling $\\lambda\\ne0$, the same configuration has a nonzero derived current although $F=dA=0$, so it fails the sourced Maxwell equation of [[lem-emg-maxwell-current-forms]]. It is therefore a background scalar solution, not a solution of the coupled scalar\u2013Maxwell system. No relation to $\\hbar$ or empirical data is used.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:164302:                "locator": "\u00a72.5.2 supplies a classical complex-scalar gauge-covariant derivative; the flat background plane wave and sourced-current obstruction are calculated here."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:164835:          "id": "ex-emg-classical-scalar-background-limits",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:164853:          "physical_scope": "Exact smooth plane-wave calculations in the specified classical scalar model on a flat background. They distinguish a matter equation solution from a solution of the coupled equations and make no empirical or quantum claim.\n",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:164881:          "statement": "Take the trivial global-potential branch of the scalar model on\n$M=\\mathbb R^4$ with global coordinates $(x^0,\\mathbf x)$, where\n$x^0=ct$ and $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^3$, and Minkowski metric\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$. Use the trivial principal bundle\n$P=M\\times U(1)$ with its product connection, so the global physical\npotential is $A=0$, and take $\\varphi\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^4;\\mathbb C)$.\nSet\n$V(u)=m_s^2u$, where $m_s>0$ is a classical inverse-length parameter\n$[\\mathrm{m}^{-1}]$, not a rest mass. Let $C\\in\\mathbb C\\setminus\\{0\\}$ be\ndimensionless and let $\\mathbf k\\in\\mathbb R^3$ have components in\n$\\mathrm{m^{-1}}$. Define the angular frequency\n$\\omega:=c\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+m_s^2}$, with unit $\\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. The smooth field\n$\\varphi=C\\exp(-i\\omega t+i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x)$ solves the scalar\nequation for every allowed value of $\\lambda$, because $A=0$ makes\n$D_a=\\partial_a$. Its current, however, depends on $\\lambda$. The two cases\nbelow show why a solution of the scalar equation on a prescribed background\nneed not solve the coupled Einstein\u2013Maxwell system."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:164902:          "physical_scope": "A smooth exact counterexample showing that a source-free Maxwell solution on a prescribed flat background need not satisfy the coupled action-based Einstein equation. It uses no empirical data or singular field.\n",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:165375:          "id": "lem-emg-fixed-smooth-background-harmonic-data",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:165387:          "title": "Harmonic initial data relative to a fixed smooth background",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:165408:                "locator": "Section 2.4 describes the harmonic-map reduction; section 5.1 constructs reduced initial data from (h,K). The finite-regularity fixed-background correction is proved here."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:165417:          "statement": "Let $s\\ge5$ be an integer and $L_0>0$. Let the underlying periodic manifold be $\\Sigma=\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi L_0}$ with length coordinates $X^i$, and use the diffeomorphism $D_{L_0}:\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi}\\to\\Sigma$, $[\\theta]\\mapsto[L_0\\theta]$, to parametrize its component functions as in [[def-emg-periodic-high-sobolev-state]]. This map is well defined with smooth inverse $[X]\\mapsto[X/L_0]$. Let $(h,K,e,b)$ be periodic data of [[def-emg-constrained-electrovacuum-data]] with $h\\in H^{s+1}$, $K,e,b\\in H^s$ and a uniform positive lower eigenvalue bound for the continuous representative of $h$. Here Sobolev membership means membership of the length-frame component arrays $h_{ij},K_{ij}$ and the specified orthonormal arrays $e_A,b_A$, composed with $D_{L_0}$, in the spaces on the dimensionless torus; it does not mean the components of the pulled-back tensors. All spatial derivatives below are $\\partial_{X^i}=L_0^{-1}\\partial_{\\theta^i}$. Fix a smooth positive reference metric $\\gamma$ on $\\Sigma$ and use the product background $\\bar g=-(dx^0)^2+\\gamma_{ij}(X)dX^i dX^j$ near $\\{x^0=0\\}\\times\\Sigma$. Define\n\n$$Q^i:=h^{jk}\\bigl(\\Gamma(h)^i{}_{jk}-\\Gamma(\\gamma)^i{}_{jk}\\bigr),$$\n\nwhere the difference of the two spatial Levi-Civita connections is a tensor. Prescribe the spacetime metric Cauchy $1$-jet at $x^0=0$ by\n\n$$g_{ij}=h_{ij},\\quad g_{00}=-1,\\quad g_{0i}=0,\\quad \\partial_0g_{ij}=2K_{ij},\\quad \\partial_0g_{00}=-2\\operatorname{tr}_hK,\\quad \\partial_0g_{0i}=h_{ij}Q^j.$$\n\nFor this jet, let $g_0^{ab}=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,h^{ij})$ be the inverse metric block on the slice and define its Christoffel value by\n\n$$\\Gamma^a{}_{bc}[j^1_0g]:=\\tfrac12 g_0^{ad}\\bigl(\\partial_bg_{dc}+\\partial_cg_{db}-\\partial_dg_{bc}\\bigr)\\big|_{x^0=0}.$$\n\nDefine the initial-slice harmonic-gauge vector directly from the prescribed $1$-jet by\n\n$$H_0^a:=g_0^{bc}\\bigl(\\Gamma^a{}_{bc}[j^1_0g]-\\bar\\Gamma^a{}_{bc}\\bigr)\\big|_{x^0=0}.$$\n\nThis is the initial value of the harmonic-gauge vector of [[def-gr-harmonic-gauge-and-reduced-equations]] for every $C^1$ metric on a neighborhood realizing the prescribed jet; no neighborhood extension is needed to define $H_0$. Each metric value in the jet is $H^{s+1}$ and each prescribed first derivative is $H^s$, so the displayed algebraic expression has an $H^s$ representative. It vanishes on the initial slice. The background $\\gamma$ is required to be smooth; choosing $\\gamma=h$ is not asserted to be valid for finite-regularity $h$."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:165499:          "statement": "Let $M=\\mathbb R^{1,1}\\times\\mathbb T^2_L$ with flat product metric, where $\\mathbb T^2_L=(\\mathbb R/L\\mathbb Z)^2$ has length coordinates $x^1,x^2$ of period $L>0$. For $B_0\\ne0$, define the smooth two-form $F=B_0\\,dx^1\\wedge dx^2$, with $B_0$ in tesla in the SI interpretation. Then $F$ is closed and source-free on the flat background, and its period over the spatial two-torus is $B_0L^2\\ne0$. The field has local potentials but no single-valued global one-form potential on $M$. This example concerns the Maxwell field-strength sector on a fixed flat background; it is not asserted to solve the coupled Einstein equation with that flat metric."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:165646:            "lem-emg-fixed-smooth-background-harmonic-data",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:165655:            "lem-emg-fixed-smooth-background-harmonic-data": "mathematical-premise",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:165678:          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $s\\ge5$ be an integer and let $U=(g_{ab},p_{ab},q_{abi},e_i,b_i)$ be the finite-dimensional first-order state on the $2\\pi$-periodic three-torus, with $p_{ab}=\\bar\\nabla_0g_{ab}$ and $q_{abi}=\\bar\\nabla_i g_{ab}$ for a fixed smooth background connection $\\bar\\nabla$. The component Sobolev spaces $H^s$ are those of [[def-emg-periodic-high-sobolev-state]]. Assume an open admissible state set $O$ on which $g$ is Lorentzian, $g^{00}<0$, the inverse spatial block $(g^{ij})$ is positive definite, the lapse is positive, and the chosen state variables and coefficients are smooth. Let $K\\Subset\\operatorname{int}K'\\Subset O$ be compact sets and $R\\ge0$. Assume the draft local symmetric-hyperbolic supplier [[thm-emg-symmetric-hyperbolic-research-supplier]] with its stated integer-$s$, coefficient, compact-state, choice and norm hypotheses. Then the harmonic-coordinate reduced Einstein equation with source $T_{ab}=\\mu_0^{-1}(F_{ac}F_b{}^c-\\tfrac14g_{ab}F_{cd}F^{cd})$, coupled to the six source-free Maxwell field-strength evolution equations, is a smooth quasilinear symmetric-hyperbolic system. For every initial state $U_0\\in H^s$ taking values in $K$ and satisfying $\\|U_0\\|_{H^s}\\le R$, its reduced system has a local solution with the regularity and compact-state bounds of [[thm-emg-symmetric-hyperbolic-research-supplier]]. No Einstein or Maxwell constraint is assumed in this reduced-system statement."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:165690:            "lem-emg-fixed-smooth-background-harmonic-data",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:165700:            "lem-emg-fixed-smooth-background-harmonic-data": "mathematical-premise",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:165724:          "statement": "Let $(g,F)$ be a reduced Einstein\u2013Maxwell solution with $g\\in C^3$ and $F\\in C^2$ on a neighborhood of a smooth spacelike initial hypersurface $\\Sigma_0$. Let the initial metric and first derivatives be chosen by [[lem-emg-fixed-smooth-background-harmonic-data]], so the harmonic vector $H^a:=g^{bc}(\\Gamma^a{}_{bc}-\\bar\\Gamma^a{}_{bc})$ vanishes on $\\Sigma_0$. Assume the source-free Maxwell divergence constraints and the four Einstein Hamiltonian/momentum constraints hold on $\\Sigma_0$. Using the classical stress identity below and the stated normal constraint identities, $H=0$ on a neighborhood of each point of $\\Sigma_0$, and the reduced metric equation there is the full Einstein\u2013Maxwell equation."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:165734:            "lem-emg-fixed-smooth-background-harmonic-data",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:165751:            "lem-emg-fixed-smooth-background-harmonic-data": "mathematical-premise",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:165784:          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $s\\ge5$ be an integer, $\\kappa>0$, $\\mu_0>0$, and $\\Lambda\\in\\mathbb R$. Fix $L_0>0$ in metres and put $\\Sigma=\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi L_0}$ with length coordinates $X^i$. On $\\Sigma$, let $h\\in H^{s+1}$ be a symmetric Riemannian metric with $h\\ge c_0 I$ for some $c_0>0$, let $K\\in H^s$ be a symmetric covariant two-tensor, and let $e,b\\in H^s$ be spatial vector fields. Sobolev membership means that the length-frame arrays $h_{ij},K_{ij}$ and oriented orthonormal arrays $e_A,b_A$, composed with $D_{L_0}:\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi}\\to\\Sigma$, $[\\theta]\\mapsto[L_0\\theta]$, belong to the real componentwise Fourier spaces of [[def-emg-periodic-high-sobolev-state]]; these are not tensor pullbacks. All spatial derivatives in the geometric equations are $\\partial_{X^i}=L_0^{-1}\\partial_{\\theta^i}$. Use the length coordinate $t=x^0=ct_{\\rm SI}$, so $g,h$ are dimensionless, $K$ has units $\\mathrm m^{-1}$, and $e=E/c$ and $b=B$ both have units tesla in an oriented orthonormal frame; $\\kappa\\mu_0^{-1}|e|^2$ has units $\\mathrm m^{-2}$. Use the sign convention $K(X,Y)=g(\\nabla_Xn,Y)$ for the future unit normal. Assume\n$$D_ie^i=0,\\qquad D_ib^i=0,\\qquad R(h)+(\\operatorname{tr}_hK)^2-|K|_h^2=2\\Lambda+\\frac{\\kappa}{\\mu_0}(|e|_h^2+|b|_h^2),\\qquad D^jK_{ji}-D_i\\operatorname{tr}_hK=-\\frac{\\kappa}{\\mu_0}(e\\times_h b)_i,$$\nwhere $D$ is the Levi-Civita derivative of $h$. Then there are $T>0$ and a solution of the full source-free Einstein\u2013Maxwell system on $(-T,T)\\times\\Sigma$ with\n$$g\\in L^\\infty H^{s+1}\\cap C^0H^s,\\qquad \\partial_tg\\in L^\\infty H^s\\cap C^0H^{s-1},\\qquad F\\in L^\\infty H^s\\cap C^0H^{s-1}.$$\nThe initial two-form is reconstructed by $F(n,\\mathsf e_i)=-e_i$ and $F(\\mathsf e_i,\\mathsf e_j)=\\epsilon_{ijk}b_k$; no independent Maxwell normal derivative is prescribed. The metric remains Lorentzian with spacelike $t$-levels in a fixed compact admissible state neighborhood. Smooth data give a smooth solution on the same base-order interval. For a fixed harmonic background, on any common local interval with the stated compact-state and uniform $H^s$ bounds, the harmonic-reduced first-order state is unique and continuously dependent on its initial state in $C^0H^{s-1}$. In addition, on any smooth oriented three-manifold, smooth constrained data with these same equations have a smooth full development in a neighborhood of each initial point. For finite-Sobolev data these equations mean their classical pointwise form at the $C^3$ metric and $C^2$ field regularity proved below; smoothness is asserted only for smooth initial data. No endpoint regularity threshold, strong $C^0H^s$ stability, global continuation, or geodesic completeness is asserted. Here an affinely parametrized metric geodesic solves $\\ddot x^a+\\Gamma^a{}_{bc}\\dot x^b\\dot x^c=0$ on an interval; a maximal such solution admits no extension to a larger interval with the same affine parameter. Geodesic completeness means every maximal affine solution has parameter domain all of $\\mathbb R$."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:165838:          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ and the conditional local existence theorem [[thm-emg-local-high-sobolev-electrovacuum]]. Let $s\\ge5$, let a harmonic-reduced source-free Einstein\u2013Maxwell solution on $[t_0,T)$ have first-order state $U=(g,p,q,e,b)\\in L^\\infty H^s\\cap C H^{s-1}$ with $\\partial_tU\\in L^\\infty H^{s-1}$, and suppose its values stay in a fixed compact admissible state set and $\\sup_{t<T}\\|U(t)\\|_{H^s}<\\infty$. If $T$ is finite and lies inside the smooth coefficient-time domain, the reduced solution extends through $T$ to a larger interval. If the original state has $p=\\bar\\nabla_0g$, $q_i=\\bar\\nabla_i g$, vanishing harmonic vector $H$, and satisfies the full source-free Einstein\u2013Maxwell equations, the extension has these same properties and extends the full development. The same conclusion holds at a finite left endpoint. On every common interval where two reduced solutions have the same fixed harmonic background, compact-state range and uniform $H^s$ bound, their states are unique and satisfy an $H^{s-1}$ continuous-dependence estimate."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:165856:            "lem-emg-fixed-smooth-background-harmonic-data",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:165872:            "lem-emg-fixed-smooth-background-harmonic-data": "mathematical-premise",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:166555:          "statement": "Adopt the equation-based Einstein-Maxwell field postulate\n[[post-emg-einstein-maxwell-equations]] and the smooth material closure of\n[[post-emg-strict-charged-fluid-model]], with the SI, signature, and coordinate\nconventions of [[def-emg-geometry-units]]. Thus the spacetime field equations\nare $dF=0$, $\\nabla_aF^{ab}=-\\mu_0j^b$, and\n$$G_{ab}+\\Lambda g_{ab}=\\kappa(T_{m,ab}+T_{\\mathrm{EM},ab}),\\qquad \\kappa=8\\pi G_N/c^4,$$\nwhile the fluid contributes exactly its adopted $T_m,j$ and material balance\nequations. No matter action is assumed.\n\nAssume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ and Dependent Choice.\nLet $p=p(\\epsilon)$ be smooth on an open interval $J$, and let\n$K_\\epsilon\\Subset J$ be a compact interval containing the initial density\nvalues, on which $\\epsilon+p(\\epsilon)>0$ and $0<p'(\\epsilon)<1$. Take\n$\\kappa>0$, $\\mu_0>0$, $c>0$, and $\\Lambda\\in\\mathbb R$.\n\nLet $\\Sigma$ be a smooth oriented three-manifold without boundary, and let\n$\\mathfrak n$ be its future unit normal. Let $h$ be a Riemannian metric and\nlet $K\\in\\Gamma(\\operatorname{Sym}^2T^*\\Sigma)$ use the second fundamental\nform convention\n$$K(X,Y):=g(\\nabla_X\\mathfrak n,Y).$$\nThe initial normal bundle is\n$\\mathcal V:=\\mathbb R\\mathfrak n\\oplus T\\Sigma$ with\n$$g_0(a\\mathfrak n+X,b\\mathfrak n+Y):=-ab+h(X,Y).$$\nLet $\\epsilon_0,Z_0$ be smooth scalar fields, $u_0$ a smooth future unit\ntimelike section of $\\mathcal V$ satisfying $g_0(u_0,u_0)=-1$, and $e,b$\nsmooth spatial vector fields\nrepresenting $E/c$ and $B$ in an oriented orthonormal frame. Their units are\nJ m$^{-3}$, C, dimensionless, and tesla, respectively; $K$ has units m$^{-1}$.\nThe components of the smooth initial two-form $F_0$ are defined in every\npositively oriented $h$-orthonormal frame $(\\mathsf e_i)$ by\n$$e_i=F_0(\\mathsf e_i,\\mathfrak n),\\qquad b_i=\\tfrac12\\varepsilon_i{}^{jk}F_0(\\mathsf e_j,\\mathsf e_k), \\qquad \\varepsilon_{123}=1.$$\nThis fixes all six independent components of $F_0$. Set\n$j_0=cN(\\epsilon_0)Z_0u_0$ and $T_0=T_{m,0}+T_{\\mathrm{EM},0}$ from the\nadopted model.\n\nWrite $D$ for the Levi-Civita connection of $h$,\n$\\tau:=\\operatorname{tr}_hK=h^{ij}K_{ij}$,\n$|K|_h^2:=h^{ik}h^{jl}K_{ij}K_{kl}$,\n$(\\operatorname{div}_hK)_j:=h^{ik}(D_iK)_{kj}$, and $R(h)$ for the scalar\ncurvature of $h$. Assume the complete Einstein and sourced Maxwell initial\nconstraints\n$$R(h)+\\tau^2-|K|_h^2=2\\Lambda+2\\kappa T_0(\\mathfrak n,\\mathfrak n),\\qquad (\\operatorname{div}_hK)(X)-D_X\\tau=\\kappa T_0(\\mathfrak n,X)$$\nfor every tangent vector $X$, and\n$$\\operatorname{div}_h e=-\\mu_0g_0(j_0,\\mathfrak n),\\qquad \\operatorname{div}_h b=0.$$\n\nThen the adopted equation-based fluid model has the following conditional\nlocal developments.\n\n**(a) Torus Sobolev branch.** If\n$\\Sigma=\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi}=\\mathbb R^3/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)^3$ with the periodic\nSobolev spaces of [[def-gr-periodic-torus-and-sobolev-spaces]], assigning each\nspatial period length $2\\pi\\,\\mathrm m$ in the length-coordinate convention of\n[F6]. Assume $s\\ge5$ is an integer, $h\\in H^{s+1}$ and\n$K,e,b,\\epsilon_0,u_0,Z_0\\in H^s$, with the same strict compact\nadmissibility. Then there is $T>0$ and a solution on\n$(-T,T)\\times\\mathbb T^3$ in the harmonic gauge of\n[[def-gr-harmonic-gauge-and-reduced-equations]], relative to the fixed\nbackground used by [[thm-emg-local-charged-barotropic-development]]. In\nperiodic charts,\n$g\\in L^\\infty H^{s+1}\\cap C^0H^s$,\n$\\partial_tg\\in L^\\infty H^s\\cap C^0H^{s-1}$, and\n$F,\\epsilon,u,Z\\in L^\\infty H^s\\cap C^0H^{s-1}$. The equations hold\nclassically, and the reduced solution is unique on common intervals with common\ncompact admissible bounds.\n\n**(b) Smooth geometric branch.** For smooth data on any $\\Sigma$ above, there\nis a smooth time-oriented solution on an open variable-width neighborhood of\n$\\{0\\}\\times\\Sigma$, inducing exactly $(h,K,F_0,\\epsilon_0,u_0,Z_0)$. It is\nunique near the initial surface up to a data-fixing time-orientation-preserving\nisometry matching $F,\\epsilon,u,Z$. No uniform time width for noncompact\n$\\Sigma$, global existence, shock continuation, vacuum/free-boundary solution,\nor empirical validity is asserted.\n\nThis is a model deduction. It does not assert that the postulates describe\nnature."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:167218:          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ for the imported Sobolev and finite-domain integration results. Let $\\Sigma=\\{s=0\\}$ be a spacelike slice in a smooth time-coordinate region $U\\subset I\\times\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi}$, with future unit normal $n$ and induced metric $h$. Use the global-potential system of [[def-emg-trivial-potential-charged-scalar-system]] in temporal gauge $A_0=0$, including $\\partial_sA_i=F_{0i}$ and the scalar first-order equations of [[lem-emg-temporal-scalar-positive-symmetric-reduction]]. Treat $\\pi_a$ and $F_{ab}$ as independent evolution variables. Write $\\mathcal D_a\\varphi:=\\partial_a\\varphi-i\\lambda A_a\\varphi$ and $\\mathcal D_a\\pi_b:=\\nabla_a\\pi_b-i\\lambda A_a\\pi_b$ for the charged derivatives of the model suppliers. In this item $D$ denotes only the spatial Levi-Civita derivative of $h$; thus $D_i\\varphi=\\partial_i\\varphi$ on a scalar, while $\\mathcal D_i\\varphi=\\partial_i\\varphi-i\\lambda A_i\\varphi$.\n\nFix a smooth background metric $\\bar g$ and define\n\n$$H^a:=g^{bc}\\bigl(\\Gamma(g)^a{}_{bc}-\\Gamma(\\bar g)^a{}_{bc}\\bigr)$$\n\nas in [[def-gr-harmonic-gauge-and-reduced-equations]]. Let $D$ be the Levi\u2013Civita derivative of $h$, let $K(X,Y)=g(\\nabla_Xn,Y)$, and put $\\tau=\\operatorname{tr}_hK$. Define the scalar and magnetic-potential residuals\n\n$$C_i:=\\pi_i-\\partial_i\\varphi+i\\lambda A_i\\varphi,\\qquad B_{ij}:=F_{ij}-(\\partial_iA_j-\\partial_jA_i).$$\n\nAssume the reduced scalar, sourced Maxwell, and harmonic-reduced Einstein evolution equations hold on $U$. The metric is in the admissible region of [[lem-emg-temporal-scalar-positive-symmetric-reduction]], with $g^{00}<0$ and positive definite $(g^{ij})$. The Maxwell field-strength evolution equations are the normal contractions $\\iota_n(dF)=0$ and $\\iota_n(d\\star_gF-\\mu_0\\iota_j\\mathrm{vol}_g)=0$, with the algebraic current source added to the Maxwell block. If $\\partial_s=\\alpha n+\\beta^i\\partial_i$, their coordinate-time contractions can contain shift multiples of the all-spatial residuals; no coordinate-time residual is set to zero before those spatial constraints are recovered. Assume initially\n\n$$C_i=0,\\qquad B_{ij}=0,\\qquad D_i b^i=0,\\qquad D_i e^i=\\mu_0j^0,\\qquad H|_\\Sigma=0,$$\n\nwhere in a positively oriented future orthonormal frame $F_{0i}=-e_i$, $F_{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}b_k$, $\\epsilon_{123}=1$, and $j^0=-n_aj^a$. Assume also the total-stress Einstein constraints\n\n$$R(h)+\\tau^2-|K|_h^2=2\\Lambda+2\\kappa T(n,n),\\qquad (\\operatorname{div}_hK)(X)-D_X\\tau=\\kappa T(n,X)\\quad(X\\in T\\Sigma),$$\n\nwhere $(\\operatorname{div}_hK)(X):=h^{ij}(D_{\\partial_i}K)(\\partial_j,X)$. The momentum constraint is an equality of covectors, independent of the choice of spatial coordinates or orthonormal frame.\n\nFor the metric first-order state $p_{ab}=\\bar\\nabla_0g_{ab}$ and $q_{abi}=\\bar\\nabla_i g_{ab}$, require their initial values to equal the corresponding metric derivatives.\n\nThen on a neighborhood of every initial point:\n\n1. $C_i=0$ and $B_{ij}=0$ persist, so $\\pi_i=\\mathcal D_i\\varphi$ and $F=dA$.\n2. The scalar equation gives $\\nabla_a j^a=0$, and the sourced Maxwell evolution together with the two initial Gauss constraints gives $dF=0$ and $d\\star_gF=\\mu_0\\iota_j\\mathrm{vol}_g$.\n3. Before Einstein-equation recovery, $\\nabla^a(T^{\\mathrm{EM}}_{ab}+T^\\varphi_{ab})=0$.\n4. The metric first-order derivative variables remain derivatives of the metric, and harmonic gauge propagates; hence the reduced Einstein equation is the full Einstein equation.\n\nThese statements hold for smooth solutions. They also hold for the classical finite-regularity system of [[def-emg-classical-charged-scalar-finite-regularity-system]], with $g\\in C^3$, $A,\\varphi\\in C^2$ and the evolved $F,\\pi\\in C^2$ supplied at periodic Sobolev order $s\\ge5$. Smooth underlying coordinates and background are retained; smoothness of the physical fields is not inferred.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:167295:          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ and Dependent Choice $\\mathrm{DC}$ ([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-dependent-choice]]). Fix an integer $s\\ge5$, constants $c>0$, $G_N>0$, $\\mu_0>0$, $K_\\varphi>0$, $\\lambda,\\Lambda\\in\\mathbb R$, and a real $V\\in C^\\infty(\\mathcal U)$ on an open $\\mathcal U\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ containing the attained values of $|\\varphi|^2$. Use the system and units of [[def-emg-trivial-potential-charged-scalar-system]] for smooth fields and its explicit classical extension [[def-emg-classical-charged-scalar-finite-regularity-system]] for the finite-Sobolev branch.\n\nWrite $\\mathcal D_a\\varphi:=\\partial_a\\varphi-i\\lambda A_a\\varphi$ and $\\mathcal D_a\\pi_b:=\\nabla_a\\pi_b-i\\lambda A_a\\pi_b$ for the charged derivatives of the model suppliers. In this item $D$ denotes only the spatial Levi-Civita derivative of $h$; thus $D_i\\varphi=\\partial_i\\varphi$ on a scalar, while $\\mathcal D_i\\varphi=\\partial_i\\varphi-i\\lambda A_i\\varphi$.\n\nFor any initial three-manifold $\\Sigma$ with Riemannian metric $h$ below, use the abstract initial Lorentzian bundle $\\mathcal V_\\Sigma:=\\mathbb R n\\oplus T\\Sigma$ with metric $g_\\Sigma(an+X,bn+Y):=-ab+h(X,Y)$ for $a,b\\in\\mathbb R$ and $X,Y\\in T\\Sigma$, as in [[thm-gr-local-geometric-development]]. Here $n$ is the distinguished future unit generator, realized as the future unit normal in any spacetime development; the data definition does not presuppose that development. For the periodic Cauchy result let $\\Sigma=\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi}$ and orient it by its ordered periodic coordinates, as in [[def-emg-periodic-high-sobolev-state]]. Prescribe a real symmetric metric $h\\in H^{s+1}$ whose continuous representative satisfies $h\\ge c_0I$ for some $c_0>0$, a symmetric tensor $K\\in H^s$, spatial vector fields $e,b\\in H^s$, a global real spatial one-form $A_\\Sigma\\in H^s$, a complex scalar $\\varphi_0\\in H^{s+1}$, and an independent complex datum $\\pi_n\\in H^s$ for the normal component of the charged derivative, realized by $\\mathcal D_n\\varphi|_\\Sigma=\\pi_n$. All Sobolev spaces are real componentwise spaces with complex fields split into real and imaginary parts, as in [[def-emg-periodic-high-sobolev-state]]. Let $(\\mathfrak e_1,\\mathfrak e_2,\\mathfrak e_3)$ be any positively oriented $h$-orthonormal spatial frame. The associated initial two-form $F_\\Sigma$ on $\\mathcal V_\\Sigma$ is defined invariantly by\n\n$$F_\\Sigma(X,n)=h(e,X),\\qquad F_\\Sigma(X,Y)=\\mathrm{vol}_h(b,X,Y)\\quad(X,Y\\in T\\Sigma).$$\n\nThus, writing $e_i=h(e,\\mathfrak e_i)$ and $b_i=h(b,\\mathfrak e_i)$, its frame components are $F_\\Sigma(\\mathfrak e_i,n)=e_i$ and $F_\\Sigma(\\mathfrak e_i,\\mathfrak e_j)=\\epsilon_{ijk}b_k$, with $\\epsilon_{123}=+1$. Require $F_\\Sigma|_{T\\Sigma}=d_\\Sigma A_\\Sigma$, $\\pi_i=(\\mathcal D_i\\varphi_0)=\\partial_i\\varphi_0-i\\lambda A_{\\Sigma,i}\\varphi_0$, and $A(n)=0$. Fix a smooth positive reference metric $\\gamma$ and the product comparison metric $\\bar g=-(dx^0)^2+\\gamma$ near the initial slice. The harmonic-gauge vector is $H^a:=g^{bc}(\\Gamma(g)^a{}_{bc}-\\Gamma(\\bar g)^a{}_{bc})$; prescribe the following initial metric jet relative to this fixed smooth background:\n\n$$g_{ij}=h_{ij},\\quad g_{00}=-1,\\quad g_{0i}=0,\\quad \\partial_0g_{ij}=2K_{ij},\\quad \\partial_0g_{00}=-2\\operatorname{tr}_hK,\\quad \\partial_0g_{0i}=h_{ij}h^{k\\ell}(\\Gamma(h)^j{}_{k\\ell}-\\Gamma(\\gamma)^j{}_{k\\ell}).$$\n\nLet $D$ be the Levi\u2013Civita derivative of $h$, $\\tau=\\operatorname{tr}_hK$, and $T=T^{\\mathrm{EM}}+T^\\varphi$ be evaluated on the complete initial data. Define $j^0:=-n_aj^a$ in the normal frame and assume the sourced Maxwell and total-stress Einstein constraints\n\n$$D_i b^i=0,\\qquad D_i e^i=\\mu_0j^0,\\qquad R(h)+\\tau^2-|K|_h^2=2\\Lambda+2\\kappa T(n,n),\\qquad (\\operatorname{div}_hK)(X)-D_X\\tau=\\kappa T(n,X)\\quad(X\\in T\\Sigma),$$\n\nwhere $(\\operatorname{div}_hK)(X)=h^{ij}(D_{\\partial_i}K)(\\partial_j,X)$, $\\kappa=8\\pi G_N/c^4$, and $K(X,Y)=g(\\nabla_Xn,Y)$ in a spacetime realization. The constraint expressions are interpreted using their continuous representatives at the stated Sobolev orders. On the reduced coordinate domain require $g^{00}<0$ and the inverse spatial block $(g^{ij})$ to be positive definite. The real first-order state consists of the metric variables $(g_{ab},p_{ab},q_{abi})$ relative to a fixed smooth background, the six Maxwell variables $(e_i,b_i)$, the three potential components $A_i$, and the real and imaginary parts of $\\varphi,\\pi_0,\\pi_i$. For fixed reference scales $L_0>0$ metres and $F_0>0$ tesla, replace each first metric derivative $v$ and each $\\pi_a$ by $L_0v$ and $L_0\\pi_a$, each $e_i,b_i$ by $e_i/F_0,b_i/F_0$, and each $A_i$ by $A_i/(L_0F_0)$; $g_{ab}$ and $\\varphi$ are already dimensionless. These fixed invertible rescalings make the componentwise real Sobolev state dimensionless and add no equations. Let $u_0$ be the resulting state in the sense of [[def-emg-periodic-high-sobolev-state]], and suppose its continuous range is a compact $K_0$ contained in the interior of a compact $K_1$ inside an open admissible state set $O$, with $\\|u_0\\|_{H^s}\\le R$ and $R\\ge0$. On an open time interval $I\\ni0$, the complete periodic coefficient maps are smooth jointly on $I\\times\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi}\\times O$ as in [[def-gr-periodic-symmetric-hyperbolic-system]]; the potential range lies in $\\mathcal U$, and the combined time matrix is positive definite throughout $O$.\n\nThen there is $T_*>0$ and a local solution on $(-T_*,T_*)\\times\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi}$ of the full Einstein, sourced Maxwell, and classical charged-scalar equations. Its first-order metric and matter state has the $L^\\infty H^s\\cap C H^{s-1}$ regularity of [[thm-emg-symmetric-hyperbolic-research-supplier]]; the metric satisfies $g\\in L^\\infty H^{s+1}\\cap C H^s$ and $\\partial_0g\\in L^\\infty H^s\\cap C H^{s-1}$, while $F,\\pi,A_i\\in L^\\infty H^s\\cap C H^{s-1}$ and $\\varphi\\in L^\\infty H^{s+1}\\cap C H^s$. The constraints and first-order metric, scalar, and potential compatibility conditions propagate. Smooth data give a smooth development on the same local interval.\n\nMore generally, let $\\Sigma$ be any smooth oriented three-manifold without boundary. Prescribe a smooth positive-definite metric $h\\in C^\\infty(\\Sigma;\\operatorname{Sym}^2T^*\\Sigma)$, a smooth symmetric covariant two-tensor $K\\in C^\\infty(\\Sigma;\\operatorname{Sym}^2T^*\\Sigma)$, smooth spatial vector fields $e,b\\in C^\\infty(\\Sigma;T\\Sigma)$, a smooth global one-form $A_\\Sigma\\in C^\\infty(\\Sigma;T^*\\Sigma)$, a complex scalar $\\varphi_0\\in C^\\infty(\\Sigma;\\mathbb C)$, and an independent complex datum $\\pi_n\\in C^\\infty(\\Sigma;\\mathbb C)$ realized by $\\mathcal D_n\\varphi|_\\Sigma=\\pi_n$ on $\\mathcal V_\\Sigma$. Define $F_\\Sigma$ on $\\mathcal V_\\Sigma$ by the same $h,e,b$ convention in local positively oriented orthonormal frames, without requiring a global frame. Require $F_\\Sigma|_{T\\Sigma}=d_\\Sigma A_\\Sigma$, $\\pi_i=\\mathcal D_i\\varphi_0$, $A(n)=0$, and the sourced Maxwell and total-stress Einstein constraints displayed above at every point of $\\Sigma$. Restrict to neighborhoods where $|\\varphi|^2\\in\\mathcal U$. Then the data have a smooth local development near each initial point. Any two developments of this same fixed model and complete data are related near the initial surface by a data-fixing, time-orientation-preserving isometry and a global-potential gauge transformation\n\n$$A\\mapsto A+d\\chi,\\qquad \\varphi\\mapsto e^{i\\lambda\\chi}\\varphi,$$\n\nfor a smooth real $\\chi$ with $\\chi=0$ on the initial surface; under the formal units of [[def-emg-trivial-potential-charged-scalar-system]], $[\\chi]=\\mathrm{Wb}$ so $\\lambda\\chi$ is dimensionless. No nontrivial bundle sector, singular source, global-in-time existence, uniform noncompact time width, or geodesic completeness is asserted.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:167356:                "locator": "Complete 23-page PDF; \u00a7\u00a72 and 4.1\u20134.2, PDF pp. 3\u201320: charged-scalar current conventions and a flat-space Coulomb-gauge Cauchy comparison. Its elliptic scalar potential and fixed-background solution are not substituted for this coupled Einstein proof."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:167933:          "statement": "Let $p$ be an event in an open neighborhood $U$ on which a smooth\nfuture-directed unit timelike observer field $n$ is defined. At $p$ choose a\npositively oriented orthonormal tetrad with time vector $n(p)$ and signature\n$(-,+,+,+)$. Let $E_0\\in\\mathbb R$ have units $\\mathrm{V\\,m^{-1}}$ and\n$B_0\\in\\mathbb R$ have units tesla. For the pure-electric\nobserver field $\\mathbf E=(E_0,0,0)$, $\\mathbf B=0$, the SI stress components\nof [F1], with the stated Countable Choice background premise [A2], are\n\n$$T^{00}=\\frac{\\epsilon_0E_0^2}{2},\\qquad T^{0i}=0,\\qquad (T^{ij})=\\operatorname{diag}\\!\\left(-\\frac{\\epsilon_0E_0^2}{2}, \\frac{\\epsilon_0E_0^2}{2},\\frac{\\epsilon_0E_0^2}{2}\\right).$$\n\nFor the pure-magnetic observer field $\\mathbf E=0$,\n$\\mathbf B=(0,0,B_0)$, they are\n\n$$T^{00}=\\frac{B_0^2}{2\\mu_0},\\qquad T^{0i}=0,\\qquad (T^{ij})=\\operatorname{diag}\\!\\left(\\frac{B_0^2}{2\\mu_0}, \\frac{B_0^2}{2\\mu_0},-\\frac{B_0^2}{2\\mu_0}\\right).$$\n\nIn each case $T^a{}_a=0$. The entries display anisotropic spatial stress in the\nchosen observer frame; no perfect-fluid identification is made.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:168460:          "statement": "Let $M=\\mathbb R^4$ with global length coordinates $(x^0,x^1,x^2,x^3)$ and metric components $g_{ab}=\\eta_{ab}=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ in the coordinate frame $\\partial_a:=\\partial/\\partial x^a$. Use this metric to raise and lower indices and its Levi\u2013Civita connection $\\nabla$. Take orientation $dx^0\\wedge dx^1\\wedge dx^2\\wedge dx^3$, future time-orientation $N=\\partial_0$, and the positive parameters $c,\\mu_0$ of [[def-emg-matter-formal-systems]], with $x^0=ct$. Choose constants with SI units $[E_0]=\\mathrm{V\\,m^{-1}}$, $[\\chi]=\\mathrm{S\\,m^{-1}}$, and $[\\epsilon_i]=[p_0]=\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}$, with $E_0\\ne0$, $\\chi>0$, $\\epsilon_i>0$, and $p_0\\in\\mathbb R$. Let $I=(-\\epsilon_i/(2\\chi E_0^2),\\epsilon_i/(2\\chi E_0^2))$, which has units of seconds; put $t=x^0/c$ and restrict all fields to the open spacetime domain $\\mathcal D:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^4:t(x)\\in I\\}\\cong\\mathbb R^3\\times I$. The maps $\\mathbf E,\\mathbf B:\\mathcal D\\to\\mathbb R^3$ below give the spatial components relative to the positively oriented orthonormal frame $(\\partial_0,\\partial_1,\\partial_2,\\partial_3)$, with $\\mathbf e_x=(1,0,0)$. Define\n\n$$U=c\\partial_0,\\quad \\mathbf E=E_0\\mathbf e_x,\\quad \\mathbf B=0,\\quad \\epsilon(t)=\\epsilon_i+\\chi E_0^2t,\\quad p=p_0,\\quad \\sigma=0,\\quad q^a=(0,\\chi E_0,0,0).$$\n\nLet $F$ be the antisymmetric covariant two-tensor with $F_{01}=-E_0/c$ and all other independent components zero, and set $j=q$. All the displayed scalar, vector and tensor fields are smooth on $\\mathcal D$. The interval gives $\\epsilon_i/2<\\epsilon(t)<3\\epsilon_i/2$ throughout $\\mathcal D$. The imposed field is a fixed background for the matter calculation; the nonzero current is not claimed to solve its Maxwell equation.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:168723:          "statement": "Use the field, stress, matter, unit and curvature conventions of [[def-emg-matter-formal-systems]]. Let $U\\subseteq M$ be open, let $\\Lambda\\in\\mathbb R$ be constant, and define the Einstein residual for the source-free field model by\n\n$$ \\mathscr R_{ab}(g,F;\\Lambda):=G_{ab}(g)+\\Lambda g_{ab}-\\kappa T^{\\mathrm{EM}}_{ab}(g,F). $$\n\nSuppose $G_{ab}(g)+\\Lambda g_{ab}=0$ on $U$, and let $F_0$ be a smooth real two-form with $dF_0=0$ and $\\nabla_a(F_0)^{ab}=0$, the source-free form of the Maxwell equations in [[def-emg-matter-formal-systems]]. For every real dimensionless $\\delta$, the field $F_\\delta:=\\delta F_0$ obeys those same source-free equations and\n\n$$ \\mathscr R_{ab}(g,F_\\delta;\\Lambda)=-\\kappa\\delta^2 T^{\\mathrm{EM}}_{ab}(g,F_0). $$\n\nThis is an exact residual identity on the fixed metric. If $\\delta\\ne0$ and $F_0$ is nonzero at a point, its observer energy there is positive and the residual is nonzero there; this does not estimate the distance to a different coupled solution.\n\nSeparately, let $\\rho,\\sigma\\in C^1(\\Omega)$ and a $C^2$ future timelike field $U$ with $g(U,U)=-c^2$ define charged dust on an open $\\Omega\\subseteq U$, with $\\rho>0$, $T_m^{ab}=\\rho U^aU^b$, $j^a=\\sigma U^a$, and the formal matter-exchange equation of [[def-emg-matter-formal-systems]]. For any dimensionless $\\eta>0$, replacing $(\\rho,\\sigma)$ by $(\\eta\\rho,\\eta\\sigma)$ multiplies $T_m$ and $j$ by $\\eta$, leaves $\\alpha=\\sigma/\\rho$ unchanged, and leaves the dust acceleration equation $\\rho U^a\\nabla_aU^b=\\sigma F^b{}_cU^c$ unchanged when the prescribed $F$ and $U$ are held fixed. The sourced Maxwell equation must still match the changed current. If both $F$ and $j$ are scaled by $\\eta$, Maxwell's equations remain linear but the electromagnetic stress scales by $\\eta^2$, whereas the dust stress scales by $\\eta$; no general coupled-solution family follows.\n\nFor a flat background with $\\Lambda=0$ and $T_m=0$, a nonzero source-free Maxwell field cannot solve the exact Einstein equation with the unchanged Minkowski metric, because its observer energy density is positive somewhere.\n\nFinally, take $M=\\mathbb R^4$ with length coordinates $x^0=ct$ and $\\mathbf x=(x^1,x^2,x^3)$, Minkowski metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, standard orientation, and future direction $\\partial_0$. Let $\\Omega=\\mathbb R^3$, $I=\\mathbb R$, and use the Euclidean norm $|\\mathbf x|_2=(\\sum_{i=1}^3(x^i)^2)^{1/2}$ and Lebesgue volume $d^3x$ in these Cartesian coordinates. Consider only the prescribed Coulomb profile on the finite annulus $\\mathcal A_\\ell:=\\{\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^3:\\ell<|\\mathbf x|_2<2\\ell\\}$, where $\\ell>0$ is a length and $Q\\ne0$ is a charge parameter with units $\\mathrm C$. Put $r:=|\\mathbf x|_2$, $\\mathbf e_r:=\\mathbf x/r$, $\\mathbf E(\\mathbf x)=Q(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0r^2)^{-1}\\mathbf e_r$, and $\\mathbf B=0$. Its Maxwell energy on that annulus is\n\n$$ \\mathcal E_\\ell=\\int_{\\mathcal A_\\ell}\\frac{\\varepsilon_0}{2}|\\mathbf E|^2\\,d^3x =\\frac{Q^2}{16\\pi\\varepsilon_0\\ell}. $$\n\nThus this fixed-charge Coulomb cutoff family has diverging shell energy as $\\ell\\downarrow0$. This finite-annulus calculation asserts no point-source construction, self-field evaluation, or obstruction theorem for arbitrary regularizations.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:168782:          "statement": "Use the mathematical Lorentzian and SI conventions of [[def-emg-matter-formal-systems]]. Fix a length scale $L>0$, a speed $c>0$, a charge parameter $q\\in\\mathbb R$ with units $\\mathrm C$, and a mass parameter $m>0$ with units $\\mathrm{kg}$; set $\\alpha:=q/m\\ne0$, with units $\\mathrm{C\\,kg^{-1}}$. Choose $0<\\epsilon\\le\\epsilon_*\\le1$. Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be open and let $\\mathcal K\\Subset\\Omega$ be a compact convex set. Use the Cartesian coordinate domain $\\mathbb R_{x^0}\\times L\\Omega$ with standard orientation $dx^0\\wedge dx^1\\wedge dx^2\\wedge dx^3$, and use $x^0=ct$, $x^i=LX^i$ with dimensionless $X\\in\\Omega$. Assume a smooth stationary (independent of $x^0$) Lorentzian metric $g^{(\\epsilon)}$ of signature $(-,+,+,+)$ on an open neighborhood of $\\mathbb R_{x^0}\\times L\\mathcal K$ in this domain, with $\\partial_0$ chosen future timelike and $t$ increasing along the considered curves, whose components have the form\n\n$$ g_{00}^{(\\epsilon)}(X)=-(1+2\\epsilon\\phi(X))+r_{00}^{(\\epsilon)}(X),\\qquad g_{ij}^{(\\epsilon)}(X)=(1-2\\epsilon\\phi(X))\\delta_{ij}+r_{ij}^{(\\epsilon)}(X),\\qquad g_{0i}^{(\\epsilon)}(X)=r_{0i}^{(\\epsilon)}(X). $$\n\nHere $\\phi$ is dimensionless and smooth. A spatial multi-index is $\\beta=(\\beta_1,\\beta_2,\\beta_3)\\in\\mathbb N_0^3$, with $|\\beta|=\\beta_1+\\beta_2+\\beta_3$ and $\\partial_X^\\beta:=\\partial_{X^1}^{\\beta_1}\\partial_{X^2}^{\\beta_2}\\partial_{X^3}^{\\beta_3}$. For each such $\\beta$ with $|\\beta|\\le2$, assume the uniform bounds\n\n$$ |\\partial_X^\\beta r_{00}^{(\\epsilon)}|+ \\max_{i,j}|\\partial_X^\\beta r_{ij}^{(\\epsilon)}| \\le C_g\\epsilon^2,\\qquad \\max_i|\\partial_X^\\beta r_{0i}^{(\\epsilon)}|\\le C_g\\epsilon^{3/2} $$\n\non $\\mathcal K$, with a fixed finite nonnegative $C_g$. Let $\\mathbf e,\\mathbf b:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be smooth dimensionless fields whose values and first derivatives are bounded on $\\mathcal K$. Since $\\mathcal K$ is compact, the fixed smooth function $\\phi$ and its derivatives used below are also bounded there. Prescribe a smooth stationary (independent of $x^0$) real two-form $F^{(\\epsilon)}$ by its Cartesian components\n\n$$ F_{0i}^{(\\epsilon)}=-E_i^{(\\epsilon)}/c,\\qquad F_{ij}^{(\\epsilon)}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k^{(\\epsilon)},\\qquad \\alpha\\mathbf E^{(\\epsilon)}(X)=\\frac{\\epsilon c^2}{L}\\mathbf e(X),\\qquad \\alpha\\mathbf B^{(\\epsilon)}(X)=\\frac{\\sqrt\\epsilon\\,c}{L}\\mathbf b(X). $$\n\nNo Maxwell or Einstein equation for this prescribed background field or metric is assumed. Define the dimensionless potential $\\phi$ comparison system on $S\\in[0,S_*]$ by\n\n$$ \\frac{d\\mathbf X_N}{dS}=\\mathbf W_N,\\qquad \\frac{d\\mathbf W_N}{dS}=-\\nabla_X\\phi(\\mathbf X_N)+\\mathbf e(\\mathbf X_N)+\\mathbf W_N\\times\\mathbf b(\\mathbf X_N), $$\n\nwhere $(\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v)_i:=\\sum_{j,k}\\epsilon_{ijk}u_jv_k$. Let $f(\\mathbf X,\\mathbf W)$ denote the six-component right-hand side of this system. Assume that on a compact convex state set $\\mathcal Q\\subseteq\\mathcal K\\times\\mathbb R^3$, containing both trajectories, its derivative satisfies $\\|Df\\|_{1\\to1}\\le L_f$ for a finite nonnegative $L_f$. Here $\\|(\\mathbf X,\\mathbf W)\\|_1$ is the sum of the absolute values of its six real components, and $\\|Df\\|_{1\\to1}$ is the operator norm induced by this $\\ell^1$ norm.\n\nFor each $\\epsilon$, let $z_\\epsilon$ be a $C^2$ future timelike curve, parameterized by proper time $\\tau$ as in [[def-gr-timelike-worldline-and-proper-time]], that solves the prescribed external Lorentz equation\n\n$$ \\nabla_U U=\\alpha F^{(\\epsilon)\\sharp}(U),\\qquad g^{(\\epsilon)}(U,U)=-c^2,\\qquad U=\\frac{dz_\\epsilon}{d\\tau}. $$\n\nWrite its coordinate velocity as $\\mathbf w=d\\mathbf x/dt$ and assume $|\\mathbf w|\\le C_v\\sqrt\\epsilon\\,c$ on $[0,S_*]$ for a fixed finite $C_v$. Suppose the relativistic scaled trajectory $(\\mathbf X_\\epsilon,\\mathbf W_\\epsilon)$ and the comparison trajectory $(\\mathbf X_N,\\mathbf W_N)$ exist throughout $[0,S_*]$, remain in $\\mathcal Q$, and have the same initial data, where\n\n$$ \\mathbf X:=\\mathbf x/L,\\qquad \\mathbf W:=\\mathbf w/(\\sqrt\\epsilon\\,c),\\qquad S:=\\sqrt\\epsilon\\,ct/L. $$\n\nThen, after choosing $\\epsilon_*>0$ sufficiently small in terms of the stated uniform constants, the exact relativistic trajectory satisfies\n\n$$ \\frac{d\\mathbf X_\\epsilon}{dS}=\\mathbf W_\\epsilon,\\qquad \\frac{d\\mathbf W_\\epsilon}{dS} =-\\nabla_X\\phi(\\mathbf X_\\epsilon)+\\mathbf e(\\mathbf X_\\epsilon) +\\mathbf W_\\epsilon\\times\\mathbf b(\\mathbf X_\\epsilon)+\\mathbf r_\\epsilon(S), \\qquad \\|\\mathbf r_\\epsilon(S)\\|_1\\le C_r\\epsilon, $$\n\nwhere $C_r$ depends only on the displayed uniform bounds and the fixed coefficient bounds on $\\mathcal K$, and is independent of $\\epsilon$. Consequently,\n\n$$ \\sup_{0\\le S\\le S_*}\\left(\\|\\mathbf X_\\epsilon(S)-\\mathbf X_N(S)\\|_1+ \\|\\mathbf W_\\epsilon(S)-\\mathbf W_N(S)\\|_1\\right) \\le C_r\\epsilon S_*e^{L_fS_*}. $$\n\nThe estimate is conditional on existence and common compact containment through the specified finite scaled interval. It does not construct such a long-time family, solve Maxwell's equations for the prescribed fields, or produce a coupled Einstein\u2013Maxwell family. In particular the $\\sqrt\\epsilon$ magnetic scaling can fail a coupled Einstein stress hierarchy unless additional matter scales are supplied.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:168822:                "locator": "Equations (6.9)\u2013(6.10), PDF p. 63, give the curved-background Lorentz-force equation, and equations (6.12)\u2013(6.14), PDF pp. 63\u201364, derive null geodesic rays for geometric optics. Reall uses Gaussian units; the theorem states its SI component convention locally and proves its weak/slow estimate independently."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:168903:          "design_state": "Complete exact constant-magnetic relativistic orbit and finite-time scaled comparison; the field is a prescribed test background.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:168906:          "statement": "Let $M=\\mathbb R^4$ with global length coordinates $(x^0,x^1,x^2,z)$, $x^0=ct$, Minkowski metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, orientation $dx^0\\wedge dx^1\\wedge dx^2\\wedge dz$, and future direction $\\partial_0$. Use the SI convention of [[def-emg-matter-formal-systems]]. Write $\\mathbf e_z:=(0,0,1)$ and use the Euclidean product $\\mathbf u\\cdot\\mathbf v:=\\sum_{j=1}^3u_jv_j$, norm $|\\mathbf u|_2:=\\sqrt{\\mathbf u\\cdot\\mathbf u}$, and orthogonality convention $\\mathbf u\\perp\\mathbf v$ iff $\\mathbf u\\cdot\\mathbf v=0$. Let $\\Omega=\\mathbb R^3$ and $I=\\mathbb R$, and write $\\mathbf x=(x^1,x^2,z)$. Let $q\\in\\mathbb R$ be a charge parameter with units $\\mathrm C$ and $m>0$ a mass parameter with units $\\mathrm{kg}$; set $\\alpha:=q/m\\ne0$, with units $\\mathrm{C\\,kg^{-1}}$. Let dimensionless $b_0\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0\\}$ and length $L>0$. For dimensionless $0<\\epsilon\\le1$, define smooth fields $\\mathbf E,\\mathbf B:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ by\n\n$$ \\mathbf E(\\mathbf x,t)=0,\\qquad \\alpha\\mathbf B(\\mathbf x,t)=\\frac{\\sqrt\\epsilon\\,c}{L}b_0\\mathbf e_z,\\qquad 0<\\epsilon\\le1. $$\n\nHere $[\\mathbf E]=\\mathrm{V\\,m^{-1}}$ and $[\\mathbf B]=\\mathrm T$; the corresponding smooth two-form on $\\mathbb R^4$ is specified by $F_{0i}=-E_i/c$ and $F_{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$.\n\nChoose a dimensionless initial velocity $\\mathbf W_0\\perp\\mathbf e_z$ with $\\epsilon|\\mathbf W_0|_2^2\\le\\tfrac12$, and put $\\mathbf w_0=\\sqrt\\epsilon\\,c\\mathbf W_0$. Let the exact relativistic trajectory and the weak/slow comparison trajectory have the same initial position and velocity at $S=0$. On every finite interval $0\\le S\\le S_*$, where $S=\\sqrt\\epsilon\\,ct/L$ and both trajectories are followed, their dimensionless velocities and positions satisfy\n\n$$ |\\mathbf W(S)-\\mathbf W_N(S)|_2 \\le |b_0|\\epsilon|\\mathbf W_0|_2^3S,\\qquad |\\mathbf X(S)-\\mathbf X_N(S)|_2 \\le \\frac12|b_0|\\epsilon|\\mathbf W_0|_2^3S^2. $$\n\nHere $\\mathbf X=\\mathbf x/L$, $\\mathbf W=\\mathbf w/(\\sqrt\\epsilon c)$, a prime denotes $d/dS$, and the comparison equation is $\\mathbf X_N'=\\mathbf W_N$, $\\mathbf W_N'=b_0\\mathbf W_N\\times\\mathbf e_z$. For $\\vartheta\\in\\mathbb R$, define $R_z(\\vartheta):=\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos\\vartheta&-\\sin\\vartheta&0\\\\\\sin\\vartheta&\\cos\\vartheta&0\\\\0&0&1\\end{pmatrix}$. The uniform magnetic field is a prescribed test background: these orbit calculations do not assert an exact coupled Einstein\u2013Maxwell solution on unchanged Minkowski space.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:168952:                "locator": "Curved-background Lorentz equation and its flat-space special case; the constant-field orbit and its finite-time comparison are computed directly here."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:168957:                "locator": "Covariant point-charge Lorentz equation in a prescribed background and the warning that the particle's own singular field makes the unregularized self-field equation only formal; this example uses no self-field."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:169811:          "physical_scope": "Two exact pointwise electromagnetic-stress calculations in an orthonormal observer frame: a pure magnetic field has anisotropic principal stresses, and crossed equal-normalized electric and magnetic fields saturate the DEC flux bound. These are ideal local/test-background calculations, not measurements or full coupled Einstein\u2013Maxwell solutions.",
