research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:169930:          "physical_scope": "C4 real phase and C3 complex amplitudes under the exact four finite-order constraints; finite compact-chart component residual and leading-field bounds only; local affine null rays and the two-dimensional transverse polarization quotient. The construction is interpreted as a fixed-background Maxwell test field and gives no convergence, global caustic control, exact-solution error, Einstein equation, or backreaction estimate.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:169958:          "statement": "Use the fixed-background Maxwell test model of [[post-emg-background-maxwell-test]] on a smooth oriented, time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold with signature $(-,+,+,+)$ and its adopted length-coordinate SI conventions. Let $U\\subseteq M$ be open. Let $\\theta\\in C^4(U,\\mathbb R)$ be dimensionless, set $k=d\\theta$, and assume $k$ is nowhere zero and $g^{-1}(k,k)=0$. Let $a_0,a_1\\in C^3(U;T^*M\\otimes_\\mathbb R\\mathbb C)$, with covariant derivatives and contractions extended complex-linearly. Use $[k]=\\mathrm{m}^{-1}$, $[a_0]=[a_1]=\\mathrm{T\\,m}$, and dimensionless $\\epsilon$, as in [[lem-emg-controlled-maxwell-wkb]]. Define\n\n$$ (\\Box a)_b:=g^{ac}\\nabla_a\\nabla_c a_b,\\qquad (Pa)_b:=(\\Box a)_b-\\operatorname{Ric}_b{}^c a_c,\\qquad (La)_b:=2k^c\\nabla_ca_b+(\\nabla_ck^c)a_b. $$\n\nAssume the four pointwise constraints $g^{-1}(k,a_0)=0$, $La_0=0$, $iLa_1+Pa_0=0$, and $ik^a(a_1)_a+\\nabla^a(a_0)_a=0$. For $0<\\epsilon\\le1$, form the complex ansatz $A_\\epsilon=\\epsilon e^{i\\theta/\\epsilon}(a_0+\\epsilon a_1)$ and its real potential $\\widehat A_\\epsilon:=\\operatorname{Re}A_\\epsilon$ with real field $\\widehat F_\\epsilon:=d\\widehat A_\\epsilon$. Use the fixed-chart component max norm $\\|\\cdot\\|_{\\infty,K}$ of [[lem-emg-controlled-maxwell-wkb]], which is zero on an empty compact set. On a nonempty compact coordinate set $K$, assume finite constants $K_0,D_0,C_0,B_0,B_1$ satisfy $\\|k\\|_{\\infty,K}\\le K_0$, $\\|d_1\\|_{\\infty,K}\\le D_0$, $\\|Pa_1-\\nabla d_1\\|_{\\infty,K}\\le C_0$, $\\|da_0+ik\\wedge a_1\\|_{\\infty,K}\\le B_0$, and $\\|da_1\\|_{\\infty,K}\\le B_1$. The real field obeys $d\\widehat F_\\epsilon=0$ and\n\n$$ \\|\\nabla^a(\\widehat F_\\epsilon)_{ab}\\|_{\\infty,K}\\le\\epsilon K_0D_0+\\epsilon^2C_0,\\qquad \\|\\widehat F_\\epsilon-\\operatorname{Re}(e^{i\\theta/\\epsilon}ik\\wedge a_0)\\|_{\\infty,K}\\le\\epsilon B_0+\\epsilon^2B_1. $$\n\nEach local integral curve of $k^\\sharp$ is an affinely parameterized null geodesic. Along it $a_0$ is transported according to the supplied scalar transport amplitude, and the leading physical polarization is the class in $k^\\perp/\\operatorname{span}_\\mathbb C\\{k\\}$, a two-dimensional complex space with positive induced Hermitian form. These are exact finite-order deductions under the displayed ansatz constraints. The residual is a component norm in the chosen chart, not an observer-independent tensor norm. The conclusion asserts no convergent WKB series, existence of amplitudes across caustics, exact Maxwell solution for a nonzero residual, solution-distance estimate, or coupled Einstein\u2013Maxwell family."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:169972:            "post-emg-background-maxwell-test",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:169978:            "post-emg-background-maxwell-test": "physical-assumption",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:170016:          "statement": "Use the smooth oriented, time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold, Levi\u2013Civita connection $\\nabla$, and length-coordinate SI conventions of [[def-emg-matter-observables]] and the separately prescribed Maxwell test model of [[post-emg-background-maxwell-test]]. Here $U$ is open, $g$ is the fixed metric, a field strength $F$ is a smooth real two-form, and a prescribed current $j$ is a smooth real vector field. The formal stress tensor and constants are\n\n$$T_{\\mathrm{EM}}^{ab}(g,F)=\\frac1{\\mu_0}\\left(F^{ac}F^b{}_c-\\frac14g^{ab}F_{cd}F^{cd}\\right),\\qquad T^{\\mathrm{EM}}_{ab}=g_{ac}g_{bd}T_{\\mathrm{EM}}^{cd},\\qquad \\kappa=\\frac{8\\pi G_N}{c^4}>0.$$\n\nThe adopted Maxwell alternatives are $dF=0$, $d(\\star_gF)=0$ when source-free, or $dF=0$, $\\nabla_aF^{ab}=-\\mu_0j^b$ for a separately prescribed current. Let $g$ satisfy $G_{ab}(g)+\\Lambda g_{ab}=0$ on $U$, where $\\Lambda$ is constant. Define the electromagnetic stress residual relative to this unchanged vacuum metric, with no matter stress included, by\n\n$$\\mathscr R_{ab}(g,F;\\Lambda):=G_{ab}(g)+\\Lambda g_{ab}-\\kappa T^{\\mathrm{EM}}_{ab}(g,F).$$\n\nFirst let $F_0$ be a smooth source-free Maxwell two-form on $U$. For real dimensionless $\\delta$, $F_\\delta:=\\delta F_0$ remains source-free and\n\n$$\\mathscr R_{ab}(g,F_\\delta;\\Lambda)=-\\kappa\\delta^2T^{\\mathrm{EM}}_{ab}(g,F_0).$$\n\nThe same conclusion holds for an actual smooth sourced Maxwell pair: let $F_0$ be a smooth real two-form and $j_0$ a smooth real vector field on $U$ satisfying $dF_0=0$ and $\\nabla_a(F_0)^{ab}=-\\mu_0j_0^b$. With $F_\\delta:=\\delta F_0$ and $j_\\delta:=\\delta j_0$, the pair still satisfies the same sourced Maxwell equations, and\n\n$$\\mathscr R_{ab}(g,F_\\delta;\\Lambda)=-\\kappa\\delta^2T^{\\mathrm{EM}}_{ab}(g,F_0).$$\n\nFor a compact set $K$ in a fixed smooth coordinate chart contained in $U$ and a smooth covariant two-tensor $S$, define $\\|S\\|_{\\infty,K}:=\\max_{0\\le a,b\\le3}\\sup_{p\\in K}|S_{ab}(p)|$ when $K\\ne\\varnothing$, and set $\\|S\\|_{\\infty,\\varnothing}:=0$. In either Maxwell case,\n\n$$\\|\\mathscr R(g,F_\\delta;\\Lambda)\\|_{\\infty,K}=\\kappa\\delta^2\\|T^{\\mathrm{EM}}(g,F_0)\\|_{\\infty,K}.$$\n\nIf $\\delta\\ne0$ and $F_0$ is nonzero at a point, its observer energy is positive there, so this electromagnetic/vacuum-background residual is nonzero. In the sourced case this does not test a full Einstein\u2013Maxwell\u2013matter equation, because no source-matter stress is included in $\\mathscr R$. The residual is a consistency calculation, not an estimate of distance to a different metric solution.\n\nSeparately, on an open region where smooth charged-dust quantities have $\\rho>0$, $T_m^{ab}=\\rho U^aU^b$, $j^a=\\sigma U^a$, and the formal matter-exchange equation holds, replacing $(\\rho,\\sigma)$ by $(\\eta\\rho,\\eta\\sigma)$ for $\\eta>0$ scales matter stress and current linearly, leaves $\\sigma/\\rho$ unchanged, and preserves the dust force equation when the prescribed $F,U$ are held fixed. Maxwell's sourced equation must still match the scaled current. If instead both $F$ and $j$ are scaled by $\\eta$, the sourced Maxwell system is linear but electromagnetic stress scales as $\\eta^2$ while dust stress scales as $\\eta$; these degree differences provide no general coupled-solution family.\n\nFinally, for a fixed $Q\\ne0$ and $\\ell>0$, prescribe only on the finite Minkowski annulus $\\mathcal A_\\ell=\\{\\mathbf x:\\ell<|\\mathbf x|<2\\ell\\}$ the radial electric field $\\mathbf E=Q(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0r^2)^{-1}\\mathbf e_r$, $\\mathbf B=0$. Its field energy on that annulus is $Q^2/(16\\pi\\varepsilon_0\\ell)$ and diverges as $\\ell\\downarrow0$. This calculation defines no field or source at the origin and supplies no point-charge construction or regularization theorem."
