research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:170041:          "physical_scope": "Finite-time comparison for a massive charged test probe on a stationary weak metric with the exact C2 remainder bounds and prescribed electric/magnetic scalings of the mathematical supplier, with a smooth closed background two-form and the adopted action on short local-potential curve segments. It requires alpha nonzero, a sufficiently small parameter cutoff fixed by the uniform bounds, uniformly small speed, existing trajectories, common compact convex state containment in K x R3 and finite scaled duration. It imposes neither the inhomogeneous Maxwell equation nor Einstein equations and constructs no fully coupled background family.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:170052:                "locator": "Equations (6.9)\u2013(6.10), PDF pp. 61\u201363, give the curved-background Lorentz equation; equations (6.12)\u2013(6.14), PDF pp. 63\u201364, give the leading null-ray calculation. The finite weak/slow trajectory bound follows from the exact mathematical supplier, not from this source's leading-order discussion."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:170069:          "statement": "Use the charged-probe action sector of the fixed-background model of [[post-emg-background-maxwell-test]] and let $\\alpha=q/m\\ne0$, with constant $m>0$, constant charge $q$, length scale $L>0$, and speed constant $c>0$. When the Lorentz-force form is obtained from the adopted action, the inherited $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ hypothesis of that exact action-equivalence theorem is assumed; no stronger choice axiom is used. In SI, $[\\alpha]=\\mathrm{C\\,kg^{-1}}$, $[L]=\\mathrm m$, and $[c]=\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$; $\\epsilon$, $X$, $\\phi$, $\\mathbf e$, and $\\mathbf b$ are dimensionless. Choose a cutoff $0<\\epsilon_*\\le1$ sufficiently small in terms of the displayed uniform bounds below, as required by [[thm-emg-weak-slow-test-lorentz-limit]], and fix $0<\\epsilon\\le\\epsilon_*$. On an open $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$, let $\\mathcal K\\Subset\\Omega$ be compact and convex, use length coordinates $x^0=ct$, $x^i=LX^i$, and suppose the prescribed metric is smooth, stationary and Lorentzian on a neighborhood of $\\mathbb R_{x^0}\\times L\\mathcal K$, with $\\partial_0$ future timelike. For a smooth dimensionless $\\phi$ and some fixed finite $C_g$, assume for every spatial multi-index $\\beta$ with $|\\beta|\\le2$ that\n\n$$|\\partial_X^\\beta r_{00}^{(\\epsilon)}|+\\max_{i,j}|\\partial_X^\\beta r_{ij}^{(\\epsilon)}|\\le C_g\\epsilon^2,\\qquad \\max_i|\\partial_X^\\beta r_{0i}^{(\\epsilon)}|\\le C_g\\epsilon^{3/2},$$\n\nwhere $g_{00}^{(\\epsilon)}=-(1+2\\epsilon\\phi)+r_{00}^{(\\epsilon)}$, $g_{ij}^{(\\epsilon)}=(1-2\\epsilon\\phi)\\delta_{ij}+r_{ij}^{(\\epsilon)}$, and $g_{0i}^{(\\epsilon)}=r_{0i}^{(\\epsilon)}$. Prescribe smooth stationary dimensionless fields $\\mathbf e,\\mathbf b$ with values and first derivatives bounded on $\\mathcal K$, and a smooth real closed two-form $F^{(\\epsilon)}$ on the same background neighborhood, with $dF^{(\\epsilon)}=0$ and SI components $F_{0i}^{(\\epsilon)}=-E_i^{(\\epsilon)}/c$, $F_{ij}^{(\\epsilon)}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k^{(\\epsilon)}$ and\n\n$$\\alpha\\mathbf E^{(\\epsilon)}=\\frac{\\epsilon c^2}{L}\\mathbf e,\\qquad \\alpha\\mathbf B^{(\\epsilon)}=\\frac{\\sqrt\\epsilon c}{L}\\mathbf b.$$\n\nClosure $dF^{(\\epsilon)}=0$ ensures local potentials, not a global potential: in each sufficiently small open coordinate ball $D$ about a curve point, use the smooth radial primitive of [[lem-emg-local-potential-construction]] as the supplied $A_D$ with $dA_D=F^{(\\epsilon)}|_D$. Adopt fixed-endpoint action stationarity only on sufficiently short finite curve segments contained in such a ball, as in [[post-emg-background-maxwell-test]] and [[pthm-rpm-external-charge-action-equivalence]]. There is no assumption that the whole trajectory lies in a single potential domain, that $F^{(\\epsilon)}$ is globally exact, or that the local potentials are stationary. No inhomogeneous Maxwell or Einstein equation is imposed on this prescribed background. The dimensionless comparison trajectory satisfies $\\mathbf X_N'=\\mathbf W_N$, $\\mathbf W_N'=-\\nabla_X\\phi(\\mathbf X_N)+\\mathbf e(\\mathbf X_N)+\\mathbf W_N\\times\\mathbf b(\\mathbf X_N)$ on $[0,S_*]$, where primes denote $S$ derivatives, $S_*>0$, and $S=\\sqrt\\epsilon ct/L$. Write $\\| (\\mathbf X,\\mathbf W)\\|_1$ for the sum of the absolute values of the six components and $\\|Df\\|_{1\\to1}$ for its induced operator norm, as in [[thm-emg-weak-slow-test-lorentz-limit]]. Let the six-component comparison vector field $f$ have derivative norm at most $L_f<\\infty$ on a compact convex state set $\\mathcal Q\\subseteq\\mathcal K\\times\\mathbb R^3$ containing both scaled trajectories throughout $[0,S_*]$.\n\nSuppose a $C^2$ future-directed proper-time worldline exists through this finite interval, obeys this local action model (equivalently the external Lorentz law on each such segment), has coordinate speed $|\\mathbf w|\\le C_v\\sqrt\\epsilon c$, has its scaled state remain in $\\mathcal Q$, and has the same initial scaled data as the comparison trajectory. Set $\\mathbf X_\\epsilon=\\mathbf x/L$ and $\\mathbf W_\\epsilon=\\mathbf w/(\\sqrt\\epsilon c)$. Then for some finite $C_r$ depending only on the displayed uniform bounds, the exact scaled motion obeys\n\n$$\\mathbf X_\\epsilon'=\\mathbf W_\\epsilon,\\qquad \\mathbf W_\\epsilon'=-\\nabla_X\\phi(\\mathbf X_\\epsilon)+\\mathbf e(\\mathbf X_\\epsilon)+\\mathbf W_\\epsilon\\times\\mathbf b(\\mathbf X_\\epsilon)+\\mathbf r_\\epsilon(S),\\qquad \\|\\mathbf r_\\epsilon(S)\\|_1\\le C_r\\epsilon,$$\n\nand consequently\n\n$$\\sup_{0\\le S\\le S_*}\\bigl(\\|\\mathbf X_\\epsilon-\\mathbf X_N\\|_1+\\|\\mathbf W_\\epsilon-\\mathbf W_N\\|_1\\bigr)\\le C_r\\epsilon S_*e^{L_fS_*}.$$\n\nThis is a finite-duration conditional model prediction. It does not construct the trajectories, prove common compact containment, solve Maxwell's equations for the prescribed field, or establish a coupled Einstein\u2013Maxwell family. The magnetic scaling may violate a coupled stress hierarchy unless additional matter scales are supplied."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:170077:      "title": "Separately adopted fixed-background Maxwell and charged probes \u2014 Examples",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:170098:            "post-emg-background-maxwell-test",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:170102:            "post-emg-background-maxwell-test": "physical-assumption",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:170128:          "statement": "On Minkowski spacetime with signature $(-,+,+,+)$, time orientation $\\partial_0$, and length coordinates $x^0=ct$, let $\\phi(\\mathbf x)$ be a $C^2$ time-independent scalar potential on an open spatial region and set $A=-\\phi(\\mathbf x)\\,dx^0/c$. Use SI units $[\\phi]=\\mathrm V$, $[q]=\\mathrm C$, $[m]=\\mathrm{kg}$, and $[c]=\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$. For a $C^2$ future-timelike proper-time segment satisfying the charged-probe law of [[post-emg-background-maxwell-test]], define $\\mathbf w=d\\mathbf x/dt$ and $\\gamma=(1-|\\mathbf w|^2/c^2)^{-1/2}$. Then\n\n$$E_{\\mathrm{test}}=\\gamma mc^2+q\\phi(\\mathbf x)$$\n\nis constant along the segment. The field and metric are prescribed data; this is neither a measurement nor a coupled point-source solution."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:170178:          "statement": "On Minkowski spacetime in standard positively oriented inertial coordinates $(x^0,x,y,z)$, with signature $(-,+,+,+)$ and future unit observer $n=\\partial_0$, choose constants $\\kappa>0$ with units $\\mathrm{m}^{-1}$, $A_*\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0\\}$ with units $\\mathrm{T\\,m}$, and dimensionless $0<\\epsilon\\le1$. Define\n\n$$A_\\epsilon=\\epsilon A_*\\cos\\!\\left(\\frac{\\kappa(x^0-z)}{\\epsilon}\\right)dx.$$\n\nThe real field $F_\\epsilon=dA_\\epsilon$ is an exact source-free Maxwell wave for every such $\\epsilon$. Its nonzero components are\n\n$$F_{0x}=-\\kappa A_*\\sin\\!\\left(\\frac{\\kappa(x^0-z)}{\\epsilon}\\right),\\qquad F_{zx}=\\kappa A_*\\sin\\!\\left(\\frac{\\kappa(x^0-z)}{\\epsilon}\\right).$$\n\nFor the inertial observer $n=\\partial_0$, the SI convention gives $E_x=c\\kappa A_*\\sin(\\kappa(x^0-z)/\\epsilon)$ and $B_y=\\kappa A_*\\sin(\\kappa(x^0-z)/\\epsilon)$, with all other components zero. At points where the sine is nonzero, the field energy is positive. The field is an exact test-background wave, but it is not an exact source-free coupled Einstein\u2013Maxwell field with $\\Lambda=0$ on the unchanged Minkowski metric."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:170193:            "post-emg-background-maxwell-test",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:170202:            "post-emg-background-maxwell-test": "physical-assumption",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:170232:          "statement": "On Minkowski spacetime with signature $(-,+,+,+)$ and time orientation $\\partial_0$, set $\\phi=0$, $\\mathbf E=0$, and prescribe a constant magnetic field with $\\alpha\\mathbf B=\\sqrt\\epsilon(c/L)b_0\\mathbf e_z$, where $\\alpha=q/m\\ne0$ has units $\\mathrm{C\\,kg^{-1}}$, $b_0\\ne0$ is dimensionless, $L>0$ has units metres, $c$ has units $\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, and $0<\\epsilon\\le1$ is dimensionless. Choose initial velocity $\\mathbf w_0=\\sqrt\\epsilon c\\mathbf W_0$ with $\\mathbf W_0\\cdot\\mathbf e_z=0$ and $\\epsilon|\\mathbf W_0|^2\\le1/2$. Let $\\mathbf X=\\mathbf x/L$, $\\mathbf W=\\mathbf w/(\\sqrt\\epsilon c)$, and $S=\\sqrt\\epsilon ct/L$. With matched initial position, the exact relativistic and Newton\u2013Lorentz comparison trajectories satisfy\n\n$$|\\mathbf W(S)-\\mathbf W_N(S)|\\le |b_0|\\epsilon|\\mathbf W_0|^3S,\\qquad |\\mathbf X(S)-\\mathbf X_N(S)|\\le\\frac12|b_0|\\epsilon|\\mathbf W_0|^3S^2$$\n\non every fixed finite interval $0\\le S\\le S_*$. This is an exact ideal test-background comparison; it is not a coupled flat Einstein\u2013Maxwell solution or a backreaction estimate."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:170541:          "statement": "Let $(M,g,N,o)$ be a smooth oriented, time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold of signature $(-,+,+,+)$; $o$ is the manifold orientation, $N$ is a smooth future timelike field, and the metric volume form is chosen to agree with $o$. Let $\\nabla$ be the metric-selected connection, let $\\mathcal F$ be a smooth real two-form on $M$, and use the curvature convention\n$R(X,Y)Z=\\nabla_X\\nabla_YZ-\\nabla_Y\\nabla_XZ-\\nabla_{[X,Y]}Z$.\nDefine\n$$Q_{ab}(\\mathcal F):=\\mathcal F_{ac}\\mathcal F_b{}^c-\\frac14 g_{ab}\\mathcal F_{cd}\\mathcal F^{cd},$$\nwhere both raised indices use $g^{-1}$. A **geometric electrovac datum with zero cosmological constant** is the tuple $(M,g,N,o,\\mathcal F)$ satisfying\n$$d\\mathcal F=0,\\qquad d(\\star_g\\mathcal F)=0,\\qquad \\operatorname{Ric}_{ab}=2Q_{ab}(\\mathcal F)$$\nat every point of $M$. Here $\\star_g$ is the Lorentzian Hodge map determined by $g$ and $o$; on two-forms $\\star_g^2=-1$. No material current, matter tensor, global potential, asymptotic flatness, positive mass, horizon, or extension condition is part of this definition.\n\nFor flux integration, an **oriented embedded smooth sphere** means a compact smooth two-dimensional manifold $S$ without boundary, homeomorphic to the unit sphere $\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^3:x\\cdot x=1\\}$, with a specified orientation in the sense of [[def-oriented-smooth-manifold-and-oriented-chart]] and a smooth inclusion $i:S\\hookrightarrow M$. Here smooth embedding means an injective immersion that is a homeomorphism onto its image with the subspace topology ([[def-smooth-embedding]]); identify $S$ with that image. The sphere orientation is specified independently of the spacetime orientation; for coordinate spheres used below it is the stated outward orientation.\n\nFor a smooth two-form $\\eta$ on a neighborhood of $i(S)$, use the pullback convention of [[def-pullback-of-a-differential-form]],\n$$(i^*\\eta)_p(v,w)=\\eta_{i(p)}(di_pv,di_pw),\\qquad p\\in S,\\quad v,w\\in T_pS.$$\nBy [[prop-pullback-of-forms-is-smooth-functorial-and-preserves-wedges]], this is a smooth top form on $S$. Its support is the closure in $S$ of its nonzero locus ([[def-compactly-supported-differential-form]]), hence is closed in compact $S$ and compact by [[thm-closed-subspace-of-a-compact-space-is-compact]]. Thus $i^*\\eta\\in\\Omega_c^2(S)$.\n\nWrite $\\mathcal I_S$ for [[def-integral-of-a-compactly-supported-top-form-on-an-oriented-manifold]]: for a subordinate smooth partition $(\\rho_j)$ and connected charts $\\phi_j$, it sets $\\mathcal I_S(\\omega)=\\sum_j I_{\\phi_j}(\\rho_j\\omega)$, where $I_{\\phi_j}$ is the signed chart integral of the compactly supported coefficient. Only finitely many terms are nonzero. The value is independent of charts, partition and refinement by [[thm-global-form-integration-is-independent-of-the-atlas-partition-and-refinement]]. Define the surface notation by\n$$\\int_S\\eta:=\\mathcal I_S(i^*\\eta).$$\nThis agrees with [[prop-integration-over-an-oriented-embedded-submanifold]]; a reparametrization here means an orientation-preserving diffeomorphism $F:T\\to S$ of oriented smooth two-manifolds (a smooth bijection with smooth inverse and orientation-preserving differential). For such $F$, the same value is $\\mathcal I_T((iF)^*\\eta)$.\nThe integration definition and its independence theorem assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, already retained in the geometric background below. For $\\eta=\\mathcal F$ and $\\eta=\\star_g\\mathcal F$, smoothness of the given field and Hodge map makes both pulled-back top forms admissible; their integrals are finite real numbers. Define the geometric electric and magnetic flux parameters by\n$$q_e(S):=\\frac1{4\\pi}\\int_S\\star_g\\mathcal F,\\qquad q_m(S):=\\frac1{4\\pi}\\int_S\\mathcal F.$$\nWhen $M$ and the coordinates have length units, these parameters have units of length. A magnetic flux need not have a global potential; no flux quantization is included. The background Lorentzian geometry and Hodge conventions carry the countable-choice assumption $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ of their named suppliers; the algebraic identities in this definition require no additional choice principle.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:179795:          "strategy": "**Given:** $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, $d,N\\ge1$, the rectangular box $B$ with side lengths in metres and volume $V$ in $\\mathrm m^d$, one operationally indistinguishable species, $m>0$ kg, $h_0=a_0>0$ J s, $\\beta>0$ J$^{-1}$, and the Hamiltonian and symmetric reference measure in the Statement.\n\n1.1 By [F3], Euclidean Lebesgue measure in the position and momentum coordinates is their product measure; [F5] then factors the nonnegative normalization integral. The box-volume formula [F4] gives $\\lambda_{dN}(B^N)=V^N$. Each of the $dN$ scalar momentum factors contributes $\\int_{\\mathbb R}e^{-\\beta p^2/(2m)}dp=\\sqrt{2\\pi m/\\beta}$ by [F2] and [F7]. Including the stipulated factor $1/(N!h_0^{dN})$ from [F12] yields $Z_N=V^N(N!h_0^{dN})^{-1}(2\\pi m/\\beta)^{dN/2}=V^N/(N!\\lambda^{dN})$. [given, F1, F2, F3, F4, F5, F7, F12, algebra]\n\n1.2 For the separate massless branch, let $N_{\\rm time}$ denote the background's smooth future-timelike field $N$ from [F10] and normalize it at the event by $n=N_{\\rm time}/\\sqrt{-g(N_{\\rm time},N_{\\rm time})}$. [F8] makes $P$ future null, so $g(P,n)<0$. By [F11], $|q_n(P)|^2=g(q_n(P),q_n(P))=g(P,n)^2$, hence $E_n(P)=-cg(P,n)=c|q_n(P)|$. Identifying $q_n$ with its components in an orthonormal basis of the positive rest space gives the adopted $H_{\\rm ur}(p)=c|p|$ with the stated units. [given, A2, F8, F9, F10, F11, algebra]\n\n2.1 After normalization, the canonical density is the product of the uniform position density on $B^N$ and the $dN$ scalar Gaussian momentum densities. For measurable coordinate rectangles, [F3] and [F5] show their probabilities factor; hence the positions and all momentum components are independent, each position is uniform on $B$, and each momentum component is centered with variance $m/\\beta$ by [F2]. [given, F2, F3, F5, step 1.1]\n\n2.2 In the grand preparation of [F1], use the vacuum convention $Z_0=1$ and substitute $Z_N=V^N/(N!\\lambda^{dN})$ for all $N\\ge0$ into the sector sum. With $\\nu=zV/\\lambda^d$, [F13] gives $\\Xi=\\sum_{N\\ge0}\\nu^N/N!=\\exp(\\nu)$, and [F14] gives the normalized sector weight $\\nu^N/(N!\\exp(\\nu))=\\exp(-\\nu)\\nu^N/N!$. [A1, F1, F13, F14, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n2.3 By [F6], the momentum integral for one ultra-relativistic particle is $$\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}e^{-\\beta c|p|}\\,d\\lambda_3(p)=4\\pi\\int_0^\\infty r^2e^{-\\beta cr}dr=4\\pi\\frac{\\Gamma(3)}{(\\beta c)^3}=\\frac{8\\pi}{(\\beta c)^3},$$ using [F2]. The point $p=0$ is Lebesgue null by [F18], so the continuous extension adds no massless zero-momentum state. Product measure and Tonelli [F3, F5] give the $N$-particle product, and the stated positional/counting reference then gives $Z_{N,\\rm ur}$. [A1, A2, F1, F2, F3, F5, F6, F18, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 Each scalar momentum coordinate has second moment $m/\\beta$ and fourth moment $3m^2/\\beta^2$ from [F2]. Thus each term $p_{i\\alpha}^2/(2m)$ has mean $1/(2\\beta)$ and variance $1/(2\\beta^2)$; independence from step 2.1 makes means and variances add, giving the stated $U$ and $\\operatorname{Var}(H_N)$. [given, F2, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n3.2 For $d=3$, write $a=\\beta/(2m)$. The one-particle momentum density is $(a/\\pi)^{3/2}e^{-a|p|^2}$. By [F6], for every Borel $A\\subseteq[0,\\infty)$ the probability that $v=|p|/m$ lies in $A$ is $$4\\pi(a/\\pi)^{3/2}\\int_{mA}r^2e^{-ar^2}\\,dr.$$ The substitution $r=mv$ therefore gives the speed density $4\\pi(m\\beta/(2\\pi))^{3/2}v^2e^{-\\beta mv^2/2}$ on $v\\ge0$. [given, F2, F6, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n3.3 Shifting the index in the absolutely convergent series [F13] gives $\\sum_{j\\ge0}j\\nu^j/j!=\\nu\\exp(\\nu)$ and $\\sum_{j\\ge0}j(j-1)\\nu^j/j!=\\nu^2\\exp(\\nu)$. After normalization by [F14], $\\mathbb E N=\\nu$ and $\\mathbb E[N(N-1)]=\\nu^2$, hence $\\operatorname{Var}(N)=\\mathbb E[N(N-1)]+\\mathbb EN-(\\mathbb EN)^2=\\nu$. Conditioning on sector $N$ recovers the canonical density because its factor $z^N$ is constant within that sector. [F13, F14, step 2.2, algebra]\n\n4.1 By [F17] and [A1], $F=-\\beta^{-1}[N\\log(V/\\lambda^d)-\\log N!]$ for fixed $T,N$ and fixed box shape. The log derivative [F16] gives $-\\partial_VF=N/(\\beta V)$, with $\\mathcal P$ in J m$^{-d}$, and therefore $\\mathcal PV=N/\\beta$. The entropy definition in [F1] gives $S=k_B(\\log Z_N+\\beta U)$ with the same fixed $h_0$ and factorial convention. [A1, F1, F15, F16, F17, step 1.1, step 3.1, algebra]\n\n4.2 The speed density is proportional to $v^2e^{-av^2}$ with $a=\\beta m/2$. Its derivative has the sign of $2v(1-av^2)$ for $v>0$, so its unique positive maximum occurs at $v_{\\rm mode}=a^{-1/2}$. The second moment is $3/(2a)=3/(m\\beta)$ by the gamma recurrence in [F2]. The mean is $$\\mathbb E v=\\frac{4a^{3/2}}{\\sqrt\\pi}\\int_0^\\infty v^3e^{-av^2}dv=\\frac{4a^{3/2}}{\\sqrt\\pi}\\frac{\\Gamma(2)}{2a^2}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{\\pi a}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{\\pi\\beta m}}.$$ These are model-distribution moments, not values for an individual measured particle. [F2, step 3.2, algebra]\n\n5.1 Under the normalized radial density proportional to $r^2e^{-\\beta cr}$, gamma moments give mean energy $3/\\beta$ and variance $3/\\beta^2$ per particle. Independence from the product preparation adds these to $U_{\\rm ur}=3N/\\beta$ and $\\operatorname{Var}(H_{\\rm ur})=3N/\\beta^2$; differentiating at fixed $V,N,c,h_0$ with $\\beta^{-1}=k_BT$ gives $C_{V,\\rm ur}=3Nk_B$ J K$^{-1}$. Since $Z_{N,\\rm ur}$ is proportional to $V^N$, [F16] gives $-\\partial_VF=N/(\\beta V)$ and $\\mathcal PV=N/\\beta$, with $\\mathcal P$ in Pa for this three-dimensional branch. [A1, F1, F2, F5, F16, step 2.3, algebra] \u220e",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:179879:          "physical_scope": "Finite periodic one-dimensional nearest-neighbor Ising ring with N>=2, exactly N indexed bonds, J>=0, zero field, and finite beta>0; no general long-range or higher-dimensional claim.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:179933:          "strategy": "1.1 The single-rod momentum Lebesgue integral is $\\int_{\\mathbb R}e^{-\\beta p^2/(2m)}dp=\\sqrt{2\\pi m/\\beta}$ by [F2] and [F8]. By [F5] and Tonelli [F6], the $N$ momentum factors integrate to the product of these integrals. Dividing by $h_0^N$ gives $\\lambda^{-N}$. [given, F2, F5, F6, F8, algebra]\n\n1.2 By [F2] and the bounded Riemann-to-Lebesgue agreement in [F8], each normalized scalar momentum Gaussian has second moment $m/\\beta$. Thus the mean kinetic energy of each rod is $1/(2\\beta)$ and the total mean is $U=N/(2\\beta)$. [given, F2, F8, algebra]\n\n1.3 The allowed labelled position set is a finite union of closed bounded ordering polytopes in $[a/2,L-a/2]^N$, hence compact by [F9]. Its boundary is contained in finitely many box faces and contact hyperplanes $x_i-x_j=\\pm a$, each a compact graph over a coordinate rectangle, so [F7] makes it Jordan measurable. Fix one label ordering $\\pi$ and sort its centers as $x_{\\pi(1)}\\le\\cdots\\le x_{\\pi(N)}$. On that sector set $y_i=x_{\\pi(i)}-a/2-(i-1)a$ and $\\ell=L-Na>0$; the hard-core inequalities become the closed bounded, hence compact, ordered simplex $S_\\ell:=\\{0\\le y_1\\le\\cdots\\le y_N\\le\\ell\\}$ by [F9]. Its boundary is contained in the box faces and finitely many tie hyperplanes, each a compact graph over a coordinate rectangle; [F7] therefore makes $S_\\ell$ Jordan measurable. The affine permutation map has absolute determinant $1$, so [F3] preserves Jordan volume. The $N!$ coordinate permutations of $S_\\ell$ partition $[0,\\ell]^N$ up to the tie hyperplanes, which have content zero by [F7]; their equal volumes sum to $\\ell^N$, hence each simplex has volume $\\ell^N/N!$. The $N!$ labelled order sectors cover the allowed set and overlap only on the stated content-zero contact hyperplanes. Therefore its Jordan content is $\\ell^N$, and [F5] gives the full labelled position Lebesgue measure $\\ell^N$. [given, A1, F3, F5, F7, F9, algebra]\n\n2.1 The reference measure divides the position-momentum integral by $N!h_0^N$. Product Lebesgue measure and Tonelli [F5, F6] separate the position measure $\\ell^N$ from step 1.3 and the momentum integral from step 1.1. Thus $Z_N=\\ell^N/(N!\\lambda^N)$. [F1, F5, F6, step 1.1, step 1.3, algebra]\n\n3.1 By the product, quotient and integer-power laws in [F4], $F=-\\beta^{-1}[N\\log((L-Na)/\\lambda)-\\log N!]$, using the logarithm derivative in [F4] at fixed $N,a,\\beta$ gives $p_{1D}=N/[\\beta(L-Na)]$. Substituting $\\rho=N/L$ yields $\\beta p_{1D}=\\rho/(1-a\\rho)$. [F1, F4, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n4.1 At $a=0$, the virial series has first term $\\rho$ and all higher terms zero, so its remainder after any $M\\ge1$ terms is zero. For $0\\le a\\rho<1$, the finite geometric identity $\\sum_{j=1}^M(a\\rho)^{j-1}=(1-(a\\rho)^M)/(1-a\\rho)$ gives the stated virial partial sum and exact remainder after multiplication by $\\rho$. If $a\\rho\\le r<1$, then $(a\\rho)^M/(1-a\\rho)\\le r^M/(1-r)$, proving the uniform bound. [step 3.1, algebra]\n\n5.1 At fixed $\\rho>0$, $L-Na=N(1/\\rho-a)>0$, so the laws in [F4] give $$\\frac{\\beta F}{N}=-\\log((L-Na)/\\lambda)+\\frac{\\log N!}{N}.$$ For each integer $k=1,\\ldots,N-1$, monotonicity of $\\log$ bounds its integral on $[k,k+1]$ between $\\log k$ and $\\log(k+1)$. Summing gives $$\\int_1^N\\log x\\,dx\\le\\log N!\\le\\int_1^N\\log x\\,dx+\\log N.$$ For $N=1$ the sums and integral are empty and zero. Since [F4] gives $(x\\log x-x)'=\\log x$, the integral is $N\\log N-N+1$. Hence $\\log N!=N\\log N-N+\\epsilon_N$ with $1\\le\\epsilon_N\\le1+\\log N$. Substitution and [F4] cancel $\\log N$, yielding $$\\frac{\\beta F}{N}=\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{\\rho\\lambda}{1-a\\rho}\\right)-1+\\frac{\\epsilon_N}{N}.$$ The excess is nonnegative and at most $(1+\\log N)/N$. Finally, $\\log N=\\int_1^Ndx/x\\le\\int_1^Ndx/\\sqrt{x}=2(\\sqrt N-1)$, so that upper bound tends to zero and proves the stated thermodynamic limit. [F4, step 2.1, algebra] \u220e",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:182195:          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], let $\\mu\\ge0$, let\n$H_\\mu$ and $K_\\mu$ be as in [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]], and form\n$\\mathcal F_s(H_\\mu)$ with vacuum $\\Omega$ and finite-particle domain\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$. For $f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ define on\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$\n$$\\Phi_\\mu(f)=a(K_\\mu\\overline f)+a^\\dagger(K_\\mu f).$$\nThis is a complex-linear map of test functions into operators\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}\\to\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$. For real $f$ it is symmetric\nand equals $\\sqrt2\\,\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(K_\\mu f)|_{\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}}$, where the Segal field of\n[[lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain]] is\n$\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(h)=\\overline{(a^\\dagger(h)+a(h))/\\sqrt2}$.\nThus its Weyl unitary is\n$W_\\mu(f):=W_{\\rm Segal}(\\sqrt2 K_\\mu f)$ and, for real $f,g$,\n$$W_\\mu(f)W_\\mu(g)= e^{-i\\,\\operatorname{Im}\\langle K_\\mu f,K_\\mu g\\rangle_2}W_\\mu(f+g).$$\nFor every real $f$ the vacuum Weyl expectation is\n$$\\langle\\Omega,W_\\mu(f)\\Omega\\rangle_2 =e^{-\\|K_\\mu f\\|_2^2/2}.$$\nHere the inner product is second-variable-linear as fixed in\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. The vacuum has zero one-point function and\ntwo-point function\n$$\\langle\\Omega,\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Phi_\\mu(g)\\Omega\\rangle_2 =\\langle K_\\mu\\overline f,K_\\mu g\\rangle_2,$$\nwhich obeys $\\langle\\Omega,\\Phi_\\mu(\\overline f)\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Omega\\rangle_2=\\|K_\\mu f\\|_2^2\\ge0$ for every complex test $f$ (ordinary diagonal positivity holds for real tests); higher vacuum moments of ordered real smeared\nfields obey the CCR pairing recursion and hence the free-field Wick pairing\nrule. The field is a weak operator-valued tempered distribution on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$: every matrix coefficient $f\\mapsto\\langle\\Psi,\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Theta\\rangle_2$ for $\\Psi,\\Theta\\in\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ is a continuous complex-linear functional on Schwartz space. Its adjoint satisfies $\\Phi_\\mu(f)^*|_{\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}}=\\Phi_\\mu(\\overline f)$, and its normalized vacuum defines a positive state on the finite polynomial $*$-algebra generated by these fields.\n\nThe relative-coordinate two-point tempered distribution is\n$$W_\\mu(z)=\\int_{H_\\mu^+}e^{-ip\\circ z}\\,d\\nu_\\mu(p),$$\nunderstood by pairing with Schwartz tests. Its translation-invariant bidistribution is the shell integral of $e^{-ip\\circ x}e^{ip\\circ y}$; on product tests it is the displayed vacuum covariance.\n\nFor $g=(a,\\Lambda)$, let $f_g$ and $U_\\mu(g)$ be the affine test pullback\nand one-particle action of [[lem-qft-scalar-poincare-action]]. Their Fock\nlifts implement the same covariance for $\\Phi_\\mu$ on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$\nand for the bounded Weyl operators.\n\nFor translations, the joint spectral measure on Fock space is the\nsecond-quantized multiplier measure with one-particle vector\n$(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),-\\mathbf k)$. Its support is contained in the closed\nfuture cone $\\{(E,\\mathbf P):E\\ge|\\mathbf P|\\}$; the vacuum is an eigenvector\nat $(0,\\mathbf0)$. These are conditional mathematical model deductions.\nNo point field, empirical observation or measurement claim is made.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:182401:          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ of [[def-countable-choice]]. On $M=\\mathbb R^4$ with global coordinates $x=(x^0,x^1,x^2,x^3)$, set $\\eta_{ab}=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, orient $M$ by $o=dx^0\\wedge dx^1\\wedge dx^2\\wedge dx^3$, and use $\\partial_0$ to select the standard future cone. Coordinates have length units, derivatives have inverse-length units, and the Fourier phase and conformal factor are dimensionless, consistent with [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]]. Thus $\\Box_\\eta$, $\\Box_g$, $R_g$, and $P_g$ have inverse-length-squared units. Let $\\Omega\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^4;(0,\\infty))$ be real and dimensionless, and define the prescribed external Lorentzian metric $g_{ab}=\\Omega^2\\eta_{ab}$. No field equation for $g$ is assumed. Write $d\\mathrm{vol}_g=\\Omega^4d^4x$ for its positive integration density and $\\Box_\\eta=\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\partial_b$. Let $R_g$ be the scalar curvature with the convention of [[def-gr-curvature-tensor-and-derived-tensors]], which is also used by [[thm-emg-conformal-einstein-transformation]], and define\n$$\\Box_g h:=\\Omega^{-4}\\partial_a\\!\\left(\\Omega^2\\eta^{ab}\\partial_bh\\right),\\qquad P_g:=\\Box_g-\\frac16R_g.$$\nThe test space is $\\mathcal D=C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^4;\\mathbb C)$ with the LF test-function topology of [[def-test-function-topology]]. Let $\\Phi_0$ be the massless Minkowski Fock field and let $\\varpi_0\\in\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ be its normalized vacuum vector, written $|0\\rangle$ in Dirac notation. The common dense invariant domain and field convention are those of [[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]] and [[lem-qft-scalar-causal-commutator]]. For $f\\in\\mathcal D$, define the curved-background smeared field by\n$$\\Phi_g(f):=\\Phi_0(\\Omega^3f).$$\n\nThen, with all equalities understood on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ or as distributional equalities as appropriate:\n\n1. The exact curvature and scalar wave identities are\n$$R_g=-6\\Omega^{-3}\\Box_\\eta\\Omega,\\qquad P_g(\\Omega^{-1}u)=\\Omega^{-3}\\Box_\\eta u\\quad(u\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^4;\\mathbb C)).$$\n\n2. The map $f\\mapsto\\Phi_g(f)$ is a weak operator-valued distribution on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$, meaning that for every $\\chi,\\psi\\in\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ the map $f\\mapsto\\langle\\chi,\\Phi_g(f)\\psi\\rangle_2$ is a continuous complex-linear functional on $\\mathcal D$ with its test-function topology. It satisfies $\\Phi_g(P_gf)=0$ for every $f\\in\\mathcal D$. The normalized Minkowski vacuum defines a positive state on the finite polynomial $*$-algebra generated by the real-test fields.\n\n3. If $\\operatorname{supp}f$ and $\\operatorname{supp}h$ are spacelike separated (every pair of points in the two supports has positive $\\eta$-squared separation), then $[\\Phi_g(f),\\Phi_g(h)]=0$ on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$.\n\n4. The two-point distribution relative to $d\\mathrm{vol}_g(x)d\\mathrm{vol}_g(y)$ is\n$$W_g(x,y)=\\Omega(x)^{-1}\\Omega(y)^{-1}W_0(x,y),$$\nwhere $W_0$ is the massless Minkowski Fock-vacuum two-point distribution from [[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]], relative to $d^4x\\,d^4y$ and paired bilinearly as $W_0(f,h)=\\langle\\varpi_0,\\Phi_0(f)\\Phi_0(h)\\varpi_0\\rangle_2$. On compact tests, $P_g$ is formally symmetric for $d\\mathrm{vol}_g$.\n\nThis is a selected free-field representation obtained from one Minkowski Fock vacuum. It asserts no uniqueness of a curved-spacetime state, generic Hadamard-state existence, thermal-state result, renormalized stress-tensor transformation, massive or minimally coupled result, Einstein equation, or backreaction claim. The metric is prescribed background data, not a dynamical solution.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:183531:          "statement": "Fix $\\mu\\ge0$, $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, $\\Box=\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\partial_b$, and the Dirac matrices and adjoint of [[def-qft-dirac-projections]]; $\\mu$ is the inverse-length mathematical shell parameter of [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]]. For a smooth real scalar field $\\phi$ and smooth commuting complex spinor $\\psi:\\mathbb R^4\\to\\mathbb C^4$, use the normalized free Lagrangian densities\n$$\\mathcal L_\\phi=-\\frac12\\left(\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\phi\\,\\partial_b\\phi+\\mu^2\\phi^2\\right),\\qquad \\mathcal L_D=\\bar\\psi\\left(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu\\right)\\psi,\\qquad \\bar\\psi=\\psi^\\dagger\\beta.$$\nFor compactly supported smooth real variations $h$ and compactly supported smooth complex spinor variations $\\chi$, the action symbols are used only through the following finite, compactly supported first variations; no global integrability of the background fields is assumed:\n$$\\delta S_\\phi(\\phi;h)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}h(\\Box-\\mu^2)\\phi\\,d^4x,\\qquad \\delta S_D(\\psi;\\chi)=2\\operatorname{Re}\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\chi^\\dagger\\beta\\left(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu\\right)\\psi\\,d^4x.$$\nEach field is stationary under all such variations if and only if its corresponding Euler\u2013Lagrange equation holds pointwise:\n$$(\\Box-\\mu^2)\\phi=0,\\qquad (i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu)\\psi=0.$$\nThe adjoint Dirac equation follows from the second equation because the fixed matrices satisfy $(\\gamma^a)^\\dagger\\beta=\\beta\\gamma^a$. These are mathematical variation identities for commuting classical fields; they assert no quantization, Grassmann calculus, or physical measurement.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:186116:          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $\\Gamma=(V,E,s,t,\\mathcal P)$ be a finite oriented graph with nonempty finite vertex set $V$, nonempty finite link set $E$, endpoint maps $s,t:E\\to V$, and a finite list $\\mathcal P$ of oriented simple cycles. Parallel links and self-loops are allowed. An oriented simple cycle $p\\in\\mathcal P$ is a finite closed path using each link at most once; write $\\sigma_{p,e}\\in\\{-1,0,1\\}$ for its signed use of link $e$, so its signed incidence at every vertex is zero. Fix enumerations $E=(\\ell_1,\\ldots,\\ell_N)$ and $V=(v_1,\\ldots,v_M)$, where $N=|E|$ and $M=|V|$. Write $\\mathbb T_1=\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ and $\\mathbb T_{2\\pi}=\\mathbb R/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)$, and set $\\mathbb T=\\mathbb T_{2\\pi}$ with normalized Haar measure $d\\theta/(2\\pi)$ and product measure $m_E$ on $\\mathbb T^E$.\n\nThe coordinatewise map\n$$\\Phi_E:\\mathbb T_{2\\pi}^E\\to\\mathbb T_1^E,\\qquad ([\\theta_e])_{e\\in E}\\mapsto([\\theta_e/(2\\pi)])_{e\\in E},$$\nis a well-defined measure-preserving group isomorphism for the normalized product Haar measures. The fixed enumeration of $E$ identifies $\\mathbb T_1^E$ with $\\mathbb T_1^N$; pulling back the Fourier characters $x\\mapsto\\exp(2\\pi i\\sum_{e\\in E}n_ex_e)$ along $\\Phi_E$ gives $e_{\\mathbf n}(\\theta)=\\exp(i\\sum_{e\\in E}n_e\\theta_e)$. Thus the Fourier basis theorem [[thm-fourier-basis-and-parseval-on-the-n-torus]], whose torus is $\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ and whose characters are $\\exp(2\\pi i kx)$, gives an orthonormal basis $\\{e_{\\mathbf n}:\\mathbf n\\in\\mathbb Z^E\\}$ of $L^2(\\mathbb T^E)$. The second-variable-linear $L^2$ pairing used below is the conjugate of the first-variable-linear pairing in that theorem, so orthonormality and completeness are unchanged.\n\nFix an integer $s\\ge1$ and let $\\mathcal K=\\bigoplus_{x\\in V}\\mathbb C^s$ with orthonormal modes $e_{x\\alpha}$, $1\\le\\alpha\\le s$. This is finite-dimensional and hence separable. Fix the lexicographic order on $J=V\\times\\{1,\\ldots,s\\}$ induced by the chosen order on $V$ and the usual order on $\\{1,\\ldots,s\\}$. Use the antisymmetric Fock space and second-variable-linear inner product convention of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. The current draft supplier [[lem-qft-fock-ccr-car]] states that, assuming Countable Choice, $\\mathcal F_a(\\mathcal K)$ is a Hilbert space and its creation and annihilation operators are bounded and satisfy the CAR; that supplier is still a draft carrier, not an accepted result in this run. For $\\mathbf m=(m_{x\\alpha})\\in\\{0,1\\}^J$, put $S(\\mathbf m)=\\{(x,\\alpha):m_{x\\alpha}=1\\}$ and write $\\xi_{\\mathbf m}=\\xi_{S(\\mathbf m)}$ for the ordered wedge basis vector of [[lem-qft-finite-mode-occupation-basis]]. This is a complete orthonormal basis of $\\mathcal F_a(\\mathcal K)$; changing the chosen order changes individual wedge vectors only by signs. Write $c_{x\\alpha}=c(e_{x\\alpha})$, $c^\\dagger_{x\\alpha}=c^\\dagger(e_{x\\alpha})$, and $n_x=\\sum_{\\alpha=1}^s c^\\dagger_{x\\alpha}c_{x\\alpha}$. On $\\xi_{\\mathbf m}$, $n_x$ acts by $N_x(\\mathbf m)=\\sum_{\\alpha=1}^s m_{x\\alpha}$; it is bounded self-adjoint with spectrum contained in $\\{0,\\ldots,s\\}$.\n\nUse the second-variable-linear $L^2$ pairing $\\langle f,g\\rangle_2=\\int_{\\mathbb T^E}\\overline{f(\\theta)}g(\\theta)\\,dm_E(\\theta)$. The kinematical Hilbert space is\n$$\\mathcal H_{\\rm kin}=L^2(\\mathbb T^E)\\widehat\\otimes\\mathcal F_a(\\mathcal K),$$\nwhere $\\widehat\\otimes$ has the same completion norm as [[lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction]] and the second-variable-linear pairing obtained by conjugating its first-variable-linear tensor pairing, as in [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. Concretely, the tensor supplier is applied to the conjugate first-variable-linear pairings $(f,g)_1=\\langle g,f\\rangle_2$ and $(\\Phi,\\Psi)_1=\\langle\\Psi,\\Phi\\rangle_2$, and the resulting tensor pairing is conjugated; this preserves the norm and the product-basis assertion. Consequently\n$$\\{e_{\\mathbf n}\\otimes\\xi_{\\mathbf m}:\\mathbf n\\in\\mathbb Z^E,\\ \\mathbf m\\in\\{0,1\\}^J\\}$$\nis a complete orthonormal basis of $\\mathcal H_{\\rm kin}$ by the product-basis assertion of [[lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction]]. This is the joint basis used for all diagonal multipliers below. Write\n$\\psi=\\sum_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}\\psi_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}e_{\\mathbf n}\\otimes\\xi_{\\mathbf m}$.\nAll matter operators previously defined on $\\mathcal F_a(\\mathcal K)$ act on\n$\\mathcal H_{\\rm kin}$ as identity on the rotor factor tensor that operator.\nOn the full kinematical basis define\n$$E_e(e_{\\mathbf n}\\otimes\\xi_{\\mathbf m})=n_e(e_{\\mathbf n}\\otimes\\xi_{\\mathbf m}),\\qquad D(E_e)=\\left\\{\\psi\\in\\mathcal H_{\\rm kin}:\\sum_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}n_e^2|\\psi_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}|^2<\\infty\\right\\}.$$\nThus $E_e$ is the maximal real diagonal multiplier, self-adjoint with integer\nspectrum, and on its domain its coefficients are $n_e\\psi_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}$.\nDefine the bounded unitary $U_e$ as multiplication by $e^{i\\theta_e}$ on the\nrotor factor and identity on the matter factor. Equivalently,\n$$U_e(e_{\\mathbf n}\\otimes\\xi_{\\mathbf m})=e_{\\mathbf n+\\delta_e}\\otimes\\xi_{\\mathbf m},\\qquad (U_e\\psi)_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}=\\psi_{\\mathbf n-\\delta_e,\\mathbf m}.$$\nEach $U_f$ preserves $D(E_e)$, since\n$(n_e+\\delta_{ef})^2\\le2n_e^2+2$ and the coefficient sequence is square\nsummable; its inverse preserves that domain by the same estimate. Computing\non coefficients gives $[E_e,U_f]=\\delta_{ef}U_f$ on $D(E_e)$.\n\n\nFix background integers $b_x\\in\\{0,\\ldots,s\\}$. In the joint basis define the real integer multiplier\n$$\\gamma_x(\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m)=\\sum_{e:s(e)=x}n_e-\\sum_{e:t(e)=x}n_e-\\left(\\sum_{\\alpha=1}^s m_{x\\alpha}-b_x\\right),$$\nand let $G_x$ be its maximal diagonal operator, with domain $D(G_x)=\\{\\psi:\\sum_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}|\\gamma_x(\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m)|^2|\\psi_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}|^2<\\infty\\}$. On the common link-electric domain this is the finite sum $\\sum_{s(e)=x}E_e-\\sum_{t(e)=x}E_e-(n_x-b_xI)$. These are the Gauss operators; the operator constraint defines $\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}:=\\bigcap_{x\\in V}\\ker G_x$. For $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb T^V$ define the diagonal multiplier $U(\\alpha)=\\exp(i\\sum_x\\alpha_xG_x)$; the next lemma proves that these operators form a strongly continuous representation and that their normalized Haar average is the orthogonal projection onto $\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}$.\n\nFor each $x$, choose a Hermitian matrix $M_x\\in M_s(\\mathbb C)$, and for each link $e$ choose a matrix $T_e\\in M_s(\\mathbb C)$. Choose real coefficients $\\lambda_E>0$ and $\\lambda_p\\in\\mathbb R$ for $p\\in\\mathcal P$. Write\n$$W_p=\\prod_{e\\in E}U_e^{\\sigma_{p,e}},\\qquad V_B=-\\frac12\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal P}\\lambda_p(W_p+W_p^\\dagger),$$\nand\n$$V_F=\\sum_{x\\in V}\\sum_{\\alpha,\\beta=1}^s c^\\dagger_{x\\alpha}(M_x)_{\\alpha\\beta}c_{x\\beta}+\\sum_{e\\in E}\\left(A_e+A_e^\\dagger\\right),\\qquad A_e=\\sum_{\\alpha,\\beta=1}^s c^\\dagger_{s(e),\\alpha}(T_e)_{\\alpha\\beta}U_e c_{t(e),\\beta}.$$\nThe operator $H_0=\\lambda_E\\sum_{e\\in E}E_e^2$ is the diagonal multiplier with domain\n$$D(H_0)=\\left\\{\\psi:\\sum_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}\\left(\\lambda_E\\sum_{e\\in E}n_e^2\\right)^2|\\psi_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}|^2<\\infty\\right\\},$$\nwhere $\\mathbf m=(m_{x\\alpha})$ ranges over occupation configurations with each $m_{x\\alpha}\\in\\{0,1\\}$, and $N_x(\\mathbf m)=\\sum_{\\alpha=1}^s m_{x\\alpha}$ is the eigenvalue of $n_x$ on that basis vector. The coefficients $\\lambda_E,\\lambda_p$ and entries of $M_x,T_e$ are ordinary real or complex scalars here; no physical units are assigned. Set $H=H_0+V_B+V_F$ on $D(H_0)$. This is a mathematical finite-graph regulator definition. It asserts no physical adoption, continuum limit, Lorentz invariance, or identification with a particular continuum QED model.\n\nFor sign comparison only, if a smooth path $\\gamma_e:[0,1]\\to X$ in a smooth manifold $X$, a real $C^1$ connection one-form $a$, and a real $C^1$ function $\\lambda$ are supplied, take $U_e^{\\rm cont}=\\exp(-ig\\int_0^1a_{\\gamma_e(r)}(\\dot\\gamma_e(r))\\,dr)$ for real $g$. Under $a\\mapsto a+d\\lambda$, the fundamental theorem along the supplied path gives the endpoint factor\n$e^{ig(\\lambda(\\gamma_e(0))-\\lambda(\\gamma_e(1)))}$.\nThis is an edgewise sign comparison: it has the same source-minus-target\nangle pattern as the finite vertex-phase convention. Neither $s(e)$ nor\n$t(e)$ is evaluated by $\\lambda$; they are vertices in $V$, whereas\n$\\gamma_e(0),\\gamma_e(1)$ are points of $X$. No map $V\\to X$ or compatible\npaths for the whole graph are supplied, and no continuum limit is asserted.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:186153:          "statement": "Assume the conditional compact-rotor/CAR data of [[def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data]]. In the joint Fourier/occupation basis write\n$$\\gamma_x(\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m)=\\sum_{e:s(e)=x}n_e-\\sum_{e:t(e)=x}n_e-\\sum_{\\alpha=1}^s m_{x\\alpha}+b_x\\in\\mathbb Z.$$\nFor every vertex $x$, let $G_x$ be multiplication by $\\gamma_x$ on its maximal diagonal domain\n$$D(G_x)=\\left\\{\\psi:\\sum_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}|\\gamma_x(\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m)|^2|\\psi_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}|^2<\\infty\\right\\}.$$\nThen each $G_x$ is self-adjoint, the $G_x$ strongly commute, and $U(\\alpha)=\\exp(i\\sum_x\\alpha_xG_x)$ is a strongly continuous unitary representation of $\\mathbb T^V$. Its action obeys\n$$U(\\alpha)c_{x\\beta}U(\\alpha)^\\dagger=e^{i\\alpha_x}c_{x\\beta},\\qquad U(\\alpha)U_eU(\\alpha)^\\dagger=e^{i(\\alpha_{s(e)}-\\alpha_{t(e)})}U_e.$$\nThe normalized Haar average\n$$P=\\frac1{(2\\pi)^{|V|}}\\int_{[0,2\\pi]^{|V|}}U(\\alpha)\\,d\\alpha$$\nis the orthogonal projection onto $\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}=\\bigcap_x\\ker G_x$. This subspace is nonzero for every admissible fixed background $b$: it contains the normalized state with link Fourier labels $n_e=0$ and site occupations $m_{x\\alpha}$ chosen so $\\sum_\\alpha m_{x\\alpha}=b_x$.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:186456:          "statement": "Take two vertices $v_1,v_2$, one oriented link $e:v_1\\to v_2$, one fermion mode at each vertex, and background $b_{v_1}=1,b_{v_2}=0$. Let $\\psi$ be the tensor of the zero-electric Fourier vector and the occupation vector with the $v_1$ mode occupied and the $v_2$ mode empty. This is a concrete nonzero state in the fixed-background physical sector.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:186497:          "statement": "For every finite oriented graph and every fixed background $b$, the compact gauge model has a physical state in every total matter-occupation sector $N\\in\\{0,\\ldots,s|V|\\}$, independently of $b$.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:186540:          "statement": "Take a two-vertex graph with one link $e:v_1\\to v_2$, one fermion mode per vertex and zero backgrounds. The zero-electric, zero-occupation vector $\\Omega$ is physical. The bounded creation operator $O=c^\\dagger_{v_1,1}$ has nonzero gauge charge at $v_1$, so its expectation in any physical vector, including $\\Omega$, vanishes.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:186609:          "statement": "Fix four-dimensional affine Minkowski spacetime with metric\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, an inertial length-coordinate chart\n$x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)$, $x^0=ct$, and coordinates measured in metres, as specified\nby [[def-qft-framework-units]]. Write\n$\\Box=\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\partial_b$. The mass-shell parameter $\\mu\\ge0$ has\nunit $\\mathrm m^{-1}$ and\n$\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2}$ has the same unit. For a\nmassive species, $\\mu=m_{\\mathrm{rest,SI}}c/\\hbar$; $\\mu=0$ is the separately\nadmitted massless branch of the mathematical free-field construction. Thus\n$\\hbar c\\omega_\\mu$ is physical energy and $\\hbar\\mathbf k$ is physical\nmomentum. The natural-unit action is written $S/\\hbar$.\n\nThe scalar one-particle space is\n$\\mathcal H_\\mu=L^2(H_\\mu^+,d\\nu_\\mu;\\mathbb C)$, where\n$H_\\mu^+=\\{(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),\\mathbf k):\\mathbf k\\in\\mathbb R^3,\\ \\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)>0\\}$ and\n$d\\nu_\\mu=d^3\\mathbf k/((2\\pi)^3\\,2\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k))$; at $\\mu=0$ the\nmeasure-zero apex $\\mathbf k=0$ is omitted. These are the shell conventions\nof [[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]].\n\nFor a smooth real classical scalar $\\phi_{\\mathrm n}:\\mathbb R^4\\to\\mathbb R$\nwith the canonical free-field length degree $-1$, adopt\n$$\\frac{S_\\phi}{\\hbar}=-\\frac12\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\left(\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\phi_{\\mathrm n}\\partial_b\\phi_{\\mathrm n}+\\mu^2\\phi_{\\mathrm n}^2\\right)\\,d^4x.$$\nFor a smooth commuting classical Dirac spinor\n$\\psi_{\\mathrm n}:\\mathbb R^4\\to\\mathbb C^4$ with canonical free-field length\ndegree $-3/2$, put $\\bar\\psi_{\\mathrm n}=\\psi_{\\mathrm n}^\\dagger\\beta$ and\nuse the Clifford convention\n$\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$ from\n[[def-qft-framework-units]]. Adopt\n$$\\frac{S_D}{\\hbar}=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\bar\\psi_{\\mathrm n}(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu)\\psi_{\\mathrm n}\\,d^4x.$$\nThe smooth classical variables in these functionals commute; no Grassmann\nalgebra or path-integral construction is being adopted. These formulas specify\nlocal action densities: for arbitrary smooth global fields the total\nintegrals need not converge. Only compactly supported variations are used;\nthe action difference under such a variation is an integral over a compact\nset and is finite for smooth fields. With these variations on the fixed\nbackground, the intended Euler\u2013Lagrange equations are\n$(\\Box-\\mu^2)\\phi_{\\mathrm n}=0$ and\n$(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu)\\psi_{\\mathrm n}=0$, conditional on the exact\nmathematical supplier recorded as [L1] below.\n\nAs the physical interpretation of this selected free model, assign the real\nscalar species to the bosonic symmetric Fock representation and assign the\nDirac field to the particle/antiparticle CAR representation on\n$\\mathcal H_+\\oplus\\overline{\\mathcal H_-}$. Choose the zero-particle Fock\nvectors as the scalar and Dirac vacuum states. This is an adopted species and\nstatistics assignment. The CCR/CAR and operator properties of the specified\nrepresentations are mathematical model consequences; they do not prove a\ngeneral spin-statistics theorem or establish that nature realizes this model.\n\nThe scalar construction is the Schwartz-smeared field\n$\\Phi_\\mu(f)=a(K_\\mu\\overline f)+a^\\dagger(K_\\mu f)$ on the finite-particle\ndomain, with the future-shell phase\n$p\\circ x=\\omega_\\mu x^0-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x$, exactly as in\n[[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]]. The Dirac construction is the bounded\nspacetime-smeared CAR field of\n[[lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality]], using the positive and negative\nprojections of the specified free Dirac matrix. In that carrier,\n$\\mathcal H_D=L^2(\\mathbb R^3,d^3\\mathbf k/(2\\pi)^3;\\mathbb C^4)$,\n$h_D(\\mathbf k)=\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\cdot\\mathbf k+\\beta\\mu$, and\n$P_\\pm(\\mathbf k)=(I_4\\pm h_D(\\mathbf k)/\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k))/2$; the\nparticle and antiparticle one-particle spaces are the closed ranges\n$\\mathcal H_+$ and $\\overline{\\mathcal H_-}$. These carriers state their\nmathematical hypotheses and construction domains; their draft status is not an\nindependent physics or proof approval.\n\nTong's lecture notes and Srednicki's book draft provide independent\npresentations of the scalar and Dirac free-field formulations. Their formal\nmode notation supplies context for this adoption, not a proof of the\noperator-domain claims and not evidence that the adopted model describes\nnature.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:187378:          "statement": "Adopt as one selected regulated physical model the finite oriented-graph data\nof [[def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data]], with finite nonempty vertex and link\nsets, compact $U(1)$ rotor variables on links, finite CAR matter at vertices,\nfixed integer background $b_x\\in\\{0,\\ldots,s\\}$, and the displayed Gauss\noperators. The mathematical definition specifies\n$\\mathcal H_{\\rm kin}=L^2(\\mathbb T^E)\\widehat\\otimes\\mathcal F_a(\\mathbb C^{s|V|})$,\nthe integer-spectrum electric operators $E_e$, link unitaries $U_e$, and the\nHamiltonian $H=H_0+V_B+V_F$ on its stated maximal domain\n$D(H_0)$. This physical adoption does not add an embedding of the graph into\nspace or alter the mathematical definition's operator domains.\n\nFor the physical-unit assignment, the link angles, $E_e$, $U_e$, and finite CAR\noperators are dimensionless. Assign units joules to $\\lambda_E$, every\n$\\lambda_p$, and every entry of $M_x$ and $T_e$ in the displayed Hamiltonian;\nthen $H$ has units joules and $tH/\\hbar$ is dimensionless with $t$ in seconds\nand $\\hbar$ in joule-seconds, as in [[def-qft-framework-units]]. The graph is\nfinite regulator data, not a coordinate lattice with an assigned spacing.\n\nThe adopted reading is limited to a model whose mathematical data are actually\nconstructed, consistent with [[post-qft-constructed-model-and-measurement]].\nThe CAR construction inside [[def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data]] is\nconditional on Countable Choice and its inspected draft Fock/CAR supplier\ninterfaces; this postulate does not confer proof acceptance on those carriers.\nIt adopts no experimental result, measurement probability, continuum\napproximation, Lorentz-invariant continuum theory, or claim that the model\ndescribes nature.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:187411:          "physical_scope": "Adopted fixed finite-graph U(1) rotor and finite-CAR regulator with a fixed background and bounded interaction data. Hamiltonian coefficients are assigned energy units; no spatial embedding, continuum approximation or limit, continuum QED identification, empirical realization, or measured prediction is asserted.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:187421:          "statement": "Assume the fixed finite-graph model adopted by\n[[post-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-model]]: a finite nonempty oriented graph,\n$s\\ge1$ finite CAR modes per vertex, fixed backgrounds\n$b_x\\in\\{0,\\ldots,s\\}$, link electric operators $E_e$, the joint Gauss\noperators $G_x$, and $H=H_0+V_B+V_F$ on the exact maximal domain\n$D(H_0)$ of [[def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data]]. Assume the Axiom of Choice\nand a constant $\\hbar>0$ in joule-seconds. Then the physical sector\n$$\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}=\\bigcap_{x\\in V}\\ker G_x$$\nis nonzero. The restriction $H_{\\rm phys}:=H|_{D(H)\\cap\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}}$\nis self-adjoint, and\n$$\\mathcal U_{\\rm phys}(t)=e^{-itH_{\\rm phys}/\\hbar},\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R,$$\nis a strongly continuous unitary group on $\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}$ with the\nexact derivative domain $D(H)\\cap\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}$. This is an exact\nfixed-regulator result conditional on the stated mathematical suppliers; it\nasserts no continuum dynamics or continuum QED construction.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:187843:          "physical_scope": "A conditional compact-variation Dirac equation and formal minimal magnetic coefficient for a smooth static pure magnetic background. The result is the minimal/tree Pauli coefficient only: it proves no self-adjoint magnetic closure, full spectrum, Landau-level construction, radiative correction, or empirical equality.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:188813:          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice and the selected free-field model of\n[[post-qft-free-field-model]], using the metric, length coordinates, inverse-\nlength parameter $\\mu\\ge0$, field dimensions and Clifford convention fixed by\n[[def-qft-framework-units]]. The classical scalar field\n$\\phi_{\\mathrm n}:\\mathbb R^4\\to\\mathbb R$ is smooth and real; the classical\nDirac field $\\psi_{\\mathrm n}:\\mathbb R^4\\to\\mathbb C^4$ is smooth with\ncommuting components. Their adopted free actions are the scalar and Dirac\nfunctionals stated in [[post-qft-free-field-model]]. Action symbols are used\nonly through compactly supported smooth variations, so no global\nintegrability of the classical background fields is assumed.\n\nThe compact-variation stationarity equations are\n$$(\\Box-\\mu^2)\\phi_{\\mathrm n}=0,\\qquad (i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu)\\psi_{\\mathrm n}=0,$$\nwith $\\Box=-\\partial_0^2+\\Delta_{\\mathbf x}$. In the separately adopted\nscalar and Dirac Fock representations, the corresponding weak test equations\nare, for $f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ and\n$F\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4;\\mathbb C^4)$,\n$$\\Phi_\\mu((\\Box-\\mu^2)f)=0,\\qquad \\Psi\\!\\left((i\\partial_0-h_D(-i\\nabla))F\\right)=0.$$\nHere \u201cweak test equation\u201d means precisely that smearing the constructed field\nwith the displayed differential operator gives the zero operator on its\nstated common domain. With $h_D(\\mathbf k)=\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\cdot\\mathbf k+\\beta\\mu$,\n$\\gamma^0=\\beta$, and $\\gamma^j=\\beta\\alpha^j$ as adopted in\n[[post-qft-free-field-model]], the Dirac test operator is equivalent to\n$i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu$.\n\nThus the classical action equations and the tested equations of the selected\nfree representations have the same differential expressions at these\nnormalizations. The Fock representation, species/statistics assignments and\nvacua are additional physical model assumptions; they are not deductions from\nthe classical actions. No empirical outcome or measurement is asserted.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:189908:          "statement": "Let $d\\ge1$, $X=\\mathbb R^d$, and $n\\ge1$. Write\n$\\mathcal D(X^n)=C_c^\\infty(X^n;\\mathbb C)$ for the complex test-function\nspace of smooth compactly supported functions\n([[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]]), with its LF topology\n([[def-test-function-topology]]). The convergence criterion used below is\nthat convergent test sequences have one common compact support and converge\nuniformly with every derivative\n([[thm-sequential-convergence-in-test-function-space]]). Here $C^\\infty$\nmeans every coordinate derivative exists and is continuous\n([[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]]). All integrals are\ncompact Riemann integrals: each integrand is continuous with compact support,\nso restrict it to a compact rectangle and apply\n[[thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable]]. Let\n$\\mathcal D_{\\mathrm{sym}}(X^n)$ be the subspace of kernels invariant under\nevery permutation of their $n$ $X$-variables. Fields are real smooth\nconfigurations $\\phi\\in C^\\infty(X;\\mathbb R)$. For an integer $N\\ge0$\nand a finite family\n$f_0\\in\\mathbb C$, $f_n\\in\\mathcal D_{\\mathrm{sym}}(X^n)$ for\n$1\\le n\\le N$, define\n$$ F_f(\\phi)=f_0+\\sum_{n=1}^{N}\\int_{X^n}f_n(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)\\prod_{j=1}^{n}\\phi(x_j)\\,dx_1\\cdots dx_n. $$\nThe pairing is complex-bilinear, with no conjugation on the kernel or field.\nAn element of $\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(X)$ is this finite symmetric\ncoefficient family, evaluated on fields by $F_f$. Pointwise sums, scalar\nmultiples, and products again have finite symmetric compactly supported\ncoefficient families: for products, expand the two finite sums and\nsymmetrize each tensor product $f_m\\otimes g_n$. Thus these elements form\nan algebra; the product-integral identity follows from Riemann Fubini on a\nrectangle containing the compact supports\n([[thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles]]).\n\nFor $k=0$, set $F_f^{(0)}(\\phi)=F_f(\\phi)$. For $1\\le k\\le N$,\nthe $k$-th functional derivative kernel assigned to this coefficient\nfamily is\n$$ F_f^{(k)}(\\phi)(x_1,\\ldots,x_k)=\\sum_{n=k}^{N}\\frac{n!}{(n-k)!}\\int_{X^{n-k}}f_n(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)\\prod_{j=k+1}^{n}\\phi(x_j)\\,dx_{k+1}\\cdots dx_n. $$\nFor $k>N$ it is zero. Each displayed kernel is smooth, symmetric and compactly supported in its $k$ remaining variables: compactness follows by projection of the finite union of coefficient-kernel supports, and derivatives pass through the finite-compact integrals by [[thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign-on-a-compact-rectangle]].\nIn the term $n=k$, the empty integral and empty product mean simply\n$f_k(x_1,\\ldots,x_k)$.\n\nThe scope is exactly smooth compact coefficient kernels. A local functional\nwhose higher derivative kernels are distributions on a thin diagonal is not\nincluded merely because its field expression is polynomial.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:199415:          "statement": "Assume the Countable Choice convention $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ of\n[[def-countable-choice]] used by the smooth tangent-bundle structure in\n[[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]. Let $(M,g,N)$ be a time-oriented smooth\nLorentzian four-manifold of signature $(-,+,+,+)$ with its metric-selected\nconnection $\\nabla$, and let $(U,x)$ be a smooth chart. Write\n$w=\\sqrt{|\\det(g_{ab})|}$. Let $J$ be a $C^1$ vector field on $U$; the\nconnection derivative on such a field is defined by its usual chart formula\n$$\\nabla_bJ^a:=\\partial_bJ^a+\\Gamma^a{}_{cb}J^c,$$\nwhose coefficients are continuous. Then, throughout $U$,\n$$\\nabla_aJ^a=w^{-1}\\partial_a(wJ^a).$$\nThe right-hand side is the chart expression of the scalar divergence\n$\\nabla_aJ^a$; no higher regularity of $J$ than $C^1$ is required.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:199541:          "statement": "Assume the $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ background of\n[[def-tgr-spacelike-current-slab]]. Let $K$, $\\Sigma_t$, the $C^1$ current\n$J$, its slice charge $Q_J(t)$ and its net outward side flux $\\Phi_J(B)$ be\nas in [[def-tgr-spacelike-current-slab]]. Use the oriented metric volume form\nof [[def-gr-metric-volume-form-and-current-flux]] clause (a). Put\n$\\Pi:=\\nabla_aJ^a$, using the $C^1$ divergence of\n[[lem-tgr-c1-current-density-divergence]]. Then the exact finite-slab balance\nis\n$$Q_J(t_1)-Q_J(t_0)+\\Phi_J(B)=\\int_K\\Pi\\,\\mathrm{vol}_g.$$\nHere the four-volume integral is the Riemann integral on the bounded Jordan\nslab $K$, whose compact closure is the supplied coordinate box. If\n$\\Pi\\ge0$ everywhere on $K$, then\n$Q_J(t_1)+\\Phi_J(B)\\ge Q_J(t_0)$. In particular, nonnegative local\nproduction implies $Q_J(t_1)\\ge Q_J(t_0)$ when the **net outward side flux**\nis zero. If $\\Pi=0$, the identity instead reads\n$Q_J(t_1)-Q_J(t_0)=-\\Phi_J(B)$, so zero production alone does not imply\nconstant slice charge.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:200838:          "statement": "Use $\\mathcal M=\\mathbb R^4$ with global length coordinates $(x^0,x^1,x^2,x^3)=(ct,\\mathbf x)$, metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, future direction $\\partial_{x^0}$, and reference causal cone $\\{V\\in\\mathbb R^4:V^0\\geq0,\\ \\eta_{ab}V^aV^b\\leq0\\}$. For $V=(V^0,\\mathbf V)$ with $V^0>0$, define $\\mathbf v(V):=c\\mathbf V/V^0$; then $\\eta(V,V)=(V^0)^2(|\\mathbf v(V)|^2/c^2-1)$, so cone membership is equivalent to $|\\mathbf v(V)|\\leq c$, with equality on its nonzero null boundary. The zero vector belongs to the cone but has no coordinate velocity under this definition. Let $X\\subset(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R$ be open, $e\\in C^2(X;\\mathbb R)$, and $(v_0,\\sigma_{p0})\\in X$. Define $n_0:=1/v_0$, $\\epsilon_0:=n_0e(v_0,\\sigma_{p0})$, $p_0:=-e_v(v_0,\\sigma_{p0})$, $T_0:=e_{\\sigma_p}(v_0,\\sigma_{p0})$, and $h_0:=\\epsilon_0+p_0$. The background fields are this constant state, $u_0=\\partial_{x^0}=(1,0,0,0)$, and $\\Pi_0=0$. Assume $n_0,T_0,h_0,e_{vv},c>0$, with $e_{vv}$ evaluated at fixed $\\sigma_p$.\n\nA first variation is defined by a family $(v_\\lambda,\\sigma_{p,\\lambda},u_\\lambda,\\Pi_\\lambda)$ jointly smooth in dimensionless $\\lambda$ near $0$ and in $x$, through the displayed background, with $u_\\lambda$ future unit, meaning $\\eta(u_\\lambda,u_\\lambda)=-1$ and $u_\\lambda^0>0$. For any such field $f_\\lambda$, set $\\delta f:=\\left.\\partial_\\lambda f_\\lambda\\right|_{\\lambda=0}$. At each $\\lambda$, set $n_\\lambda=1/v_\\lambda$, $\\epsilon_\\lambda=n_\\lambda e(v_\\lambda,\\sigma_{p,\\lambda})$, $p_\\lambda=-e_v(v_\\lambda,\\sigma_{p,\\lambda})$, $T_\\lambda=e_{\\sigma_p}(v_\\lambda,\\sigma_{p,\\lambda})$, $\\Delta_\\lambda^{ab}=\\eta^{ab}+u_\\lambda^au_\\lambda^b$, $D_\\lambda=u_\\lambda^a\\partial_a$, and $\\theta_\\lambda=\\partial_a u_\\lambda^a$. The bulk stress and current are $T_\\lambda^{ab}=\\epsilon_\\lambda u_\\lambda^au_\\lambda^b+(p_\\lambda+\\Pi_\\lambda)\\Delta_\\lambda^{ab}$ and $J_\\lambda^a=n_\\lambda u_\\lambda^a$, and the corrected law of [[lem-tgr-bulk-relaxation-entropy]] is $\\beta D_\\lambda\\Pi_\\lambda+\\Pi_\\lambda/(\\zeta c)=-\\theta_\\lambda-(T_\\lambda\\Pi_\\lambda/2)\\partial_a(\\beta u_\\lambda^a/T_\\lambda)$, where $\\beta=\\tau/\\zeta$. The linearized equations mean the first variations at $\\lambda=0$ of $\\partial_aT_\\lambda^{ab}=0$, $\\partial_aJ_\\lambda^a=0$, and this corrected law, set equal to zero; no member with $\\lambda\\ne0$ is required to solve the nonlinear system. Impose $\\delta\\sigma_p=0$, put $r:=\\delta\\epsilon$, $w:=(\\delta u^1,\\delta u^2,\\delta u^3)$, and denote $\\delta\\Pi$ by $\\Pi$ in the linearized system below. Take constant coefficients $\\zeta,\\tau>0$.\n\n$$a_s:=\\left(\\frac{\\partial p}{\\partial\\epsilon}\\right)_{\\sigma_p}=\\frac{e_{vv}}{n_0h_0}>0$$\n\nas supplied by [[lem-tgr-local-gibbs-euler]]. The resulting linearized system is\n\n$$r_t+c h_0\\operatorname{div}w=0,\\qquad w_t+\\frac{c}{h_0}\\nabla(a_s r+\\Pi)=0,\\qquad \\tau\\Pi_t+\\Pi=-\\zeta c\\operatorname{div}w.$$\n\nIts positive quadratic energy density\n\n$$\\mathcal E_{\\rm bulk}=\\frac12\\left[\\frac{a_s}{h_0}r^2+h_0|w|^2+\\frac{\\tau}{\\zeta}\\Pi^2\\right]$$\n\nsatisfies $\\partial_t\\mathcal E_{\\rm bulk}+\\operatorname{div}[c(a_s r+\\Pi)w]=-\\Pi^2/\\zeta$. The normal characteristic speeds are $0$ and $\\pm v_{\\rm bulk}$, where\n\n$$v_{\\rm bulk}=c\\sqrt{a_s+\\frac{\\zeta}{\\tau h_0}}.$$\n\nBy [[lem-tgr-constant-linear-energy-causality]], initial data $U_0\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R^5)$ with the componentwise real convention of [[def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms]], or smooth periodic data on $\\mathbb T_L^3$ with $L>0$, have unique global smooth solutions, contractive in the corresponding energy and Sobolev norms, with finite propagation at $v_{\\rm bulk}$. The branch is inside the Minkowski reference cone exactly when $a_s+\\zeta/(\\tau h_0)\\le1$. Constant density and transverse velocity modes can persist, so contraction does not mean every mode decays. No nonlinear or variable-coefficient existence theorem is claimed.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:201131:          "statement": "Let $O$ be an open region of a smooth, oriented, time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold $(M,g)$ of signature $(-,+,+,+)$, with its metric-compatible torsion-free connection $\\nabla$. Use the local material-state and unit conventions of [[def-tgr-local-fluid-state]] and [[def-tgr-framework-units]]: a smooth future-directed unit timelike observer-congruence field $u$ has $g(u,u)=-1$; a $C^2$ state map $(v,\\sigma_p):O\\to X$ takes values in a nonempty open $X\\subset(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R$; and a supplied $e\\in C^2(X,\\mathbb R)$ defines $n=1/v>0$, $s=n\\sigma_p$, $\\varepsilon=ne$, $T=\\partial_{\\sigma_p}e$, and $p=-\\partial_v e$. Adopt the selected fixed-composition material branch of [[post-tgr-local-material-thermodynamics]], including its stipulated local-equilibrium regime and $T>0$.\n\nFor this branch, adopt the perfect-fluid matter model\n$$J^a:=n u^a,\\qquad \\mathsf T^{ab}:=\\varepsilon u^a u^b+p\\Delta^{ab},\\qquad \\Delta^{ab}:=g^{ab}+u^a u^b,$$\nwith local balances $\\nabla_aJ^a=0$ and $\\nabla_a\\mathsf T^{ab}=0$ on $O$. Here $J^a$ is the selected material-marker current and $\\mathsf T^{ab}$ is the symmetric stress-energy tensor; the perfect branch adopts zero spatial marker diffusion and isotropic rest-space stress. The balances are physical model assumptions in the stated smooth regime, not conclusions from the state function or from geometry. They may be used on a prescribed background or together with an explicitly specified coupled Einstein model; this postulate does not assert a solution exists for arbitrary initial data or an arbitrary equation of state.\n\nThe marker receives its material-particle interpretation from the fixed-composition premise. No microscopic equation of state, universal material applicability, nonzero external force, dissipative flux, global self-gravitating energy additivity, or empirical observation is asserted.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:201268:          "id": "ex-tgr-uniform-perfect-background",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:201272:          "title": "Uniform prescribed-background perfect material",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:201273:          "statement": "Choose Minkowski spacetime $\\mathbb R^4$ with length coordinates $x^0=ct$, metric $g=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, and its standard future cone. On a material state domain $X$, fix $(v_0,\\sigma_0)\\in X$ with the adopted state function $e$ and its derived constants $n_0=1/v_0$, $\\varepsilon_0=n_0e(v_0,\\sigma_0)$, $p_0=-e_v(v_0,\\sigma_0)$, and $T_0=e_{\\sigma_p}(v_0,\\sigma_0)>0$. Let $u=(1,0,0,0)$ be the constant future unit observer field and adopt the perfect model of [[post-tgr-perfect-fluid-model]] on this prescribed background. Then the resulting homogeneous material is an exact solution of the local particle and stress balances.\n\nFor the constant future unit observer field $w=\\gamma(1,\\boldsymbol\\beta)$, parameterize its boost relative to $u$ in this global Cartesian chart by the dimensionless vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta\\in\\mathbb R^3$ with $|\\boldsymbol\\beta|<1$, and put $\\gamma=(1-|\\boldsymbol\\beta|^2)^{-1/2}$. The observer-projected energy density is\n$$\\varepsilon_{(w)}:=\\mathsf T^{ab}w_aw_b=(\\varepsilon_0+p_0)\\gamma^2-p_0.$$\nAt zero boost this equals $\\varepsilon_0$. The nonzero-boost value is a frame-dependent stress-energy prediction within the model, not an observation. Prescribing Minkowski geometry here does not make this nonzero-stress configuration a solution of the coupled Einstein equations.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:201619:          "statement": "Use the selected local material state, perfect stress branch, bulk-only relaxation branch, and unit conventions of [[post-tgr-perfect-fluid-model]], [[post-tgr-bulk-relaxation-model]], and [[def-tgr-framework-units]]. For this effective model take prescribed Minkowski spacetime $\\mathbb R^4$ with coordinates $(x^0,\\mathbf x)=(ct,\\mathbf x)$, $t\\in\\mathbb R$, $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^3$ measured in metres, and metric $\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$. The reference material is a constant state $(v_0,\\sigma_{p0})$ in the supplier's open state domain, with supplied $e\\in C^2$, $u_0=(1,0,0,0)$, $\\Pi_0=0$, $n_0=1/v_0>0$, $\\varepsilon_0=n_0e(v_0,\\sigma_{p0})$, $p_0=-e_v(v_0,\\sigma_{p0})$, $T_0=e_{\\sigma_p}(v_0,\\sigma_{p0})>0$, and enthalpy density $h_0=\\varepsilon_0+p_0>0$. The local Gibbs supplier [[lem-tgr-local-gibbs-euler]] gives the dimensionless adiabatic response\n\n$$a_s:=\\left(\\frac{\\partial p}{\\partial\\varepsilon}\\right)_{\\sigma_p}=\\frac{e_{vv}(v_0,\\sigma_{p0})}{n_0h_0};$$\n\nrestrict this pair of effective branches to $a_s>0$. Here $e_{vv}$ is the second derivative of the supplied $C^2$ state function with respect to $v$ at fixed $\\sigma_p$. The equality between the derivative and the state-function ratio is the exact interface stated by [[lem-tgr-local-gibbs-euler]] under the reference-state hypotheses above. The derivative is the local fixed-composition response at the reference state; the equation does not assign a universal value to $a_s$.\n\nFor this postulate, a first variation means the derivative at $\\lambda=0$ of a smooth one-parameter family of field configurations through the reference state, with dimensionless $\\lambda$; no member with $\\lambda\\ne0$ is required to solve a nonlinear equation. The fields $r,\\widehat\\Pi:\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R$ and $w:\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3$ are smooth. Write $r=\\delta\\varepsilon$, $w=(\\delta u^1,\\delta u^2,\\delta u^3)$, and, in the bulk branch, $\\widehat\\Pi=\\delta\\Pi$. The adiabatic sector means $\\delta\\sigma_p=0$. These first variations have $r$ and $\\widehat\\Pi$ in Pa and $w$ and $a_s$ dimensionless. Let $\\zeta_0>0$ and $\\tau_0>0$ be the bulk viscosity and relaxation time evaluated at the reference state, with units Pa s and s. Spatial derivatives have units $\\mathrm{m^{-1}}$ and $c$ has units $\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$.\n\nAdopt the following two constant-coefficient effective systems on the stated background:\n\n$$r_t+c h_0\\operatorname{div}w=0,\\qquad w_t+\\frac{c a_s}{h_0}\\nabla r=0$$\n\nfor the perfect branch, and\n\n$$r_t+c h_0\\operatorname{div}w=0,\\qquad w_t+\\frac{c}{h_0}\\nabla(a_s r+\\widehat\\Pi)=0,\\qquad \\tau_0\\widehat\\Pi_t+\\widehat\\Pi=-\\zeta_0c\\operatorname{div}w$$\n\nfor the bulk-only branch. Derivatives $\\nabla$ and $\\operatorname{div}$ act on $\\mathbf x$; $t$ is physical time and $c$ is the speed parameter in [[def-tgr-framework-units]]. The equations define the effective linear models. No uniform nonlinear error estimate, nonlinear solution lifespan, curved-spacetime evolution result, or existence claim for the nonlinear constitutive equations is postulated. The parameters and systems are adopted model inputs; they are not measurements or claims of universal material behavior.\n\nFor later solution claims, $\\mathbb T_L^3:=(\\mathbb R/L\\mathbb Z)^3$ is the flat three-torus for $L>0$, and smooth periodic data mean $C^\\infty$ maps on that quotient. A multi-index is a triple in $\\mathbb N^3$. A Schwartz map $U_0:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^m$ means a $C^\\infty$ map such that $x^\\alpha\\partial^\\beta U_0(x)$ is bounded for every pair of multi-indices $\\alpha,\\beta$. The systems have $m=4$ and $m=5$ components, respectively.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:201637:          "physical_scope": "Two stipulated adiabatic first-variation systems about a uniform Minkowski rest state, with positive response and bulk parameters; no nonlinear remainder or lifespan bound, curved-background evolution, empirical applicability, or measurement claim.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:201648:                "locator": "Full 25-page paper; PDF pp. 7\u201311: small perturbations of a uniform state, bulk/shear relaxation parameters in high-frequency propagation, and non-equilibrium entropy-current context. Its vanishing-charge and higher-gradient model is not the exact branch adopted here."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:201664:          "statement": "Assume the adiabatic perfect-material linear branch of [[post-tgr-linearized-material-model]] on its prescribed Minkowski background, with $c>0$, $h_0>0$, and $0<a_s\\le1$, and assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. For every initial datum $U_0=(r_0,w_0)\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R^4)$ or $U_0\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb T_L^3;\\mathbb R^4)$ with $L>0$, the Cauchy problem $U(0,\\cdot)=U_0$ for the system\n\n$$r_t+c h_0\\operatorname{div}w=0,\\qquad w_t+\\frac{c a_s}{h_0}\\nabla r=0$$\n\nhas a unique global smooth solution on the corresponding space-time domain, contractive in the positive quadratic energy and its weighted Sobolev norms. Its energy density\n\n$$\\mathcal E_{\\rm pf}=\\frac12\\left[\\frac{a_s}{h_0}r^2+h_0|w|^2\\right]$$\n\nsatisfies $\\partial_t\\mathcal E_{\\rm pf}+\\operatorname{div}(c a_s r w)=0$. For each Sobolev exponent $q\\ge0$, its weighted Sobolev norm is nonincreasing for $t\\ge0$. The characteristic speeds, meaning eigenvalues of the principal matrix in a unit spatial direction, are $0$ and $\\pm c\\sqrt{a_s}$. Finite propagation means that if initial data vanish on a ball of radius $R$, then at time $t$ they vanish on the concentric ball of radius $R-c\\sqrt{a_s}\\,t$ whenever $0\\le t<R/(c\\sqrt{a_s})$; on $\\mathbb T_L^3$ use geodesic balls. Thus the exact linear propagation speed is $c\\sqrt{a_s}\\le c$. Equality is permitted at $a_s=1$. This is a conditional model deduction and makes no claim about nonlinear or curved-spacetime evolution, or about nature beyond the adopted model.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:201709:          "statement": "Assume the adiabatic bulk-only linear branch of [[post-tgr-linearized-material-model]] on its prescribed Minkowski background, with $h_0,T_0,c,a_s,\\zeta_0,\\tau_0>0$, the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, and\n\n$$a_s+\\frac{\\zeta_0}{\\tau_0h_0}\\le1.$$\n\nFor every initial datum $U_0=(r_0,w_0,\\widehat\\Pi_0)\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R^5)$ or $U_0\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb T_L^3;\\mathbb R^5)$ with $L>0$, the Cauchy problem $U(0,\\cdot)=U_0$ for the system\n\n$$r_t+c h_0\\operatorname{div}w=0,\\qquad w_t+\\frac{c}{h_0}\\nabla(a_s r+\\widehat\\Pi)=0,\\qquad \\tau_0\\widehat\\Pi_t+\\widehat\\Pi=-\\zeta_0c\\operatorname{div}w$$\n\nhas a unique global smooth solution on the corresponding space-time domain and is contractive in its positive quadratic energy and weighted Sobolev norms. Its energy density\n\n$$\\mathcal E_{\\rm bulk}=\\frac12\\left[\\frac{a_s}{h_0}r^2+h_0|w|^2+\\frac{\\tau_0}{\\zeta_0}\\widehat\\Pi^2\\right]$$\n\nsatisfies $\\partial_t\\mathcal E_{\\rm bulk}+\\operatorname{div}[c(a_s r+\\widehat\\Pi)w]=-\\widehat\\Pi^2/\\zeta_0$. For each Sobolev exponent $q\\ge0$, its weighted Sobolev norm is nonincreasing for $t\\ge0$. The characteristic speeds, meaning eigenvalues of the principal matrix in a unit spatial direction, are $0$ and $\\pm c\\sqrt{a_s+\\zeta_0/(\\tau_0h_0)}$. Finite propagation means that if initial data vanish on a ball of radius $R$, then at time $t$ they vanish on the concentric ball of radius $R-v_{\\rm bulk}t$ whenever $0\\le t<R/v_{\\rm bulk}$, where $v_{\\rm bulk}=c\\sqrt{a_s+\\zeta_0/(\\tau_0h_0)}$; on $\\mathbb T_L^3$ use geodesic balls. Hence propagation is bounded by $c$ under the stated inequality. Equality at the causal threshold is allowed. Energy contraction does not assert decay of every mode. The result is conditional on the adopted linear model; it is not a nonlinear or curved-spacetime existence claim or an empirical conclusion.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:201854:          "statement": "Use the smooth time-oriented Lorentzian geometry, metric-selected Levi-Civita connection $\\nabla$, and length-coordinate conventions of [[def-tgr-framework-units]], with signature $(-,+,+,+)$. On an open region $O$ of a smooth four-manifold without boundary, take a smooth positive temperature field $T:O\\to(0,\\infty)$ and a smooth future unit observer-congruence field $u$ satisfying $g(u,u)=-1$. Adopt the separate entropy-only state maps of [[def-tgr-entropy-only-radiation-state]] with a fixed parameter $a_R>0$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}\\,K^{-4}}$:\n\n$$\\varepsilon=a_RT^4,\\qquad p=\\frac13a_RT^4,\\qquad s=\\frac43a_RT^3,\\qquad h:=\\varepsilon+p.$$\n\nThe fields $\\varepsilon,p,h$ have units $\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}=\\mathrm{Pa}$ and $s$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}\\,m^{-3}}$. Adopt the perfect stress tensor and its source-free balance\n\n$$\\mathsf T^{ab}:=h\\,u^au^b+p\\,g^{ab},\\qquad \\nabla_a\\mathsf T^{ab}=0$$\n\non the specified smooth background. In the proper-time velocity convention $U^a:=c u^a$, this is equivalently $\\mathsf T^{ab}=(h/c^2)U^aU^b+p g^{ab}$ with $g(U,U)=-c^2$. This branch has no conserved particle-number coordinate, particle-number current, or chemical-potential field. It adopts an effective equation of state and perfect-stress balance; it does not determine $a_R$ from quantum statistics, assert that a physical system realizes these maps, report a measurement, or derive an Einstein-field equation.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:203227:          "statement": "Let $\\Omega=\\prod_{i=1}^3(0,L_i)$ with $L_i>0$, and let $U\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ be an open neighborhood of $\\overline\\Omega$. Let $N\\in C^\\infty(U,(0,\\infty))$ satisfy $0<N_{\\min}\\le N(x)\\le N_{\\max}<\\infty$ on $\\overline\\Omega$. On $M=\\mathbb R_{x^0}\\times U$, use $x^0=ct_K$ with $c>0$ in $\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, give $M$ the time orientation $\\xi=\\partial_{x^0}$, and use the metric\n$$\ng=-N(x)^2(dx^0)^2+\\sum_{i=1}^3(dx^i)^2,\n$$\nwhere $x^i$ are measured in metres, $t_K$ in seconds, and $N$ is dimensionless. By [[def-gr-killing-field]], a Killing field is one satisfying $\\mathcal L_\\xi g=0$, where $\\mathcal L_\\xi g=\\frac d{dt}|_{t=0}\\Phi_t^*g$ for the local flow $\\Phi_t$ of $\\xi$. Thus $\\xi$ is a future timelike Killing field, $N=\\sqrt{-g(\\xi,\\xi)}$, and $u=N^{-1}\\xi$ is the smooth future unit timelike observer congruence of [[def-tgr-local-fluid-state]]; here this congruence means the unit timelike vector field, not a coordinate chart. Write $\\nabla_x$, $\\operatorname{div}_x$, $\\nu$, and $dx$ for the Euclidean spatial gradient, divergence, outward unit normal, and three-dimensional Lebesgue volume on the box, and set $|\\Omega|:=\\int_\\Omega1\\,dx=L_1L_2L_3$. For scalar fields on $\\Omega$, $L^2(\\Omega)$ and its norm use this measure.\n\nFor $t_K>0$ use the open domain $O=(0,\\infty)\\times\\Omega$. Spatial vectors such as $q$ are extended to spacetime with zero $x^0$ component.\n\nFix constants $C_V,n_0,A_0,T_{\\rm ref}>0$, a reference entropy $\\sigma_{\\rm ref}\\in\\mathbb R$, and a reference energy $e_0\\in\\mathbb R$. Their units are $C_V$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}K^{-1}}$, $n_0$ in $\\mathrm{marker\\,m^{-3}}$, $A_0$ in $\\mathrm{W\\,m^{-1}K^{-1}}$, $T_{\\rm ref}$ in kelvins, $\\sigma_{\\rm ref}$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}marker^{-1}}$, and $e_0$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,marker^{-1}}$. Define the derived per-marker heat capacity $c_v=C_V/n_0$, with units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}marker^{-1}}$, and the local mathematical material state function on $X=(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R$ by\n$$\ne(v,\\sigma_p)=c_vT_{\\rm ref}\\exp\\!\\left(\\frac{\\sigma_p-\\sigma_{\\rm ref}}{c_v}\\right)+e_0,\n$$\nindependent of $v$. For a positive proper temperature $T$, set $\\sigma_p=\\sigma_{\\rm ref}+c_v\\log(T/T_{\\rm ref})$ and $v=1/n_0$. The state definitions of [[def-tgr-local-fluid-state]] give $n=n_0$, $p=0$, $\\epsilon=C_VT+n_0e_0$, and $s=n_0\\sigma_{\\rm ref}+C_V\\log(T/T_{\\rm ref})$.\n\nLet $\\vartheta_0\\in C^2(\\overline\\Omega)$ satisfy $0<m_0\\le\\vartheta_0\\le M_0$ and admit a $C^2$ extension $\\widetilde\\vartheta_0:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R$ with the same bounds, period $2L_i$ in coordinate $x^i$, and evenness under $x^i\\mapsto-x^i$ for each $i$. These conditions make the extension even about every face $x^i=0,L_i$. Set $T_0=\\vartheta_0/N$. Define the physical heat flux, conductivity, static heat balance, and insulated-face condition by\n$$\nq=-\\kappa_{\\rm th}(\\nabla_xT+T\\nabla_x\\log N),\\qquad \\kappa_{\\rm th}=A_0/N,\\qquad NC_V\\partial_{t_K}T+\\operatorname{div}_x(N^2q)=0,\\qquad N^2q\\cdot\\nu=0\\ \\text{on each face},\n$$\nwhere $q$ has units $\\mathrm{W\\,m^{-2}}$, $\\kappa_{\\rm th}$ has units $\\mathrm{W\\,m^{-1}K^{-1}}$, and $\\nu$ is the outward Euclidean unit normal to a face. In the entropy identity use zero particle diffusion and zero shear and bulk viscosity, $V=0$ and $\\eta=\\zeta_{\\rm b}=0$, so $\\Pi=0$ and $\\pi^{ab}=0$. The thermal diffusivity $A_0/C_V$ has units $\\mathrm{m^2\\,s^{-1}}$. There is a unique solution $T\\in C([0,\\infty)\\times\\overline\\Omega)\\cap C^{2,1}((0,\\infty)\\times\\overline\\Omega)$, where $C^{2,1}$ means two continuous spatial derivatives and one continuous time derivative up to the box faces, satisfying the heat balance, the face condition, and the initial condition $T(0,x)=T_0(x)=\\vartheta_0(x)/N(x)$. It is given by\n$$\\vartheta(t_K,x):=N(x)T(t_K,x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\Gamma\\!\\left(x-y,\\frac{A_0}{C_V}t_K\\right)\\widetilde\\vartheta_0(y)\\,dy\\quad(t_K>0),\\qquad \\vartheta(0,x)=\\vartheta_0(x),\\qquad \\Gamma(z,t)=(4\\pi t)^{-3/2}e^{-|z|^2/(4t)},\\quad z\\in\\mathbb R^3,\\ t>0.$$\nThus $T=\\vartheta/N$. It satisfies $C_V\\partial_{t_K}\\vartheta=A_0\\Delta_x\\vartheta$, $\\partial_\\nu\\vartheta=0$ on each face, and $m_0\\le\\vartheta\\le M_0$.\n\nFor this solution set $Q^0=0$, $Q^i=q^i/c$, $J=n_0u$, and\n$$\n\\mathsf T^{ab}=\\epsilon u^au^b+u^aQ^b+Q^au^b,\\qquad f^b:=\\nabla_a\\mathsf T^{ab}.\n$$\nHere $Q=q/c$ has units $\\mathrm{Pa}$, $\\mathsf T$ has units $\\mathrm{Pa}$, and $f$ has units $\\mathrm{N\\,m^{-3}}$. Then $\\nabla_aJ^a=0$ and $u_bf^b=0$: $f$ is an explicitly defined external support that supplies no local power. The entropy current $S^a=su^a+Q^a/T$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}m^{-3}}$ and total entropy $\\mathcal S(t_K)=\\int_\\Omega s(t_K,x)\\,dx$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$, with\n$$\n\\frac{d\\mathcal S}{dt_K}=A_0\\int_\\Omega\\frac{|\\nabla_x\\vartheta|^2}{\\vartheta^2}\\,dx\\ge0.\n$$\nThe lapse-weighted energy $\\mathcal E_K(t_K)=\\int_\\Omega N\\epsilon\\,dx$ is constant in $\\mathrm{J}$. If $\\overline\\vartheta=|\\Omega|^{-1}\\int_\\Omega\\vartheta_0\\,dx$ and $C_\\Omega=\\frac32(\\max_iL_i)^2$, then\n$$\n\\left\\|\\vartheta(t_K,\\cdot)-\\overline\\vartheta\\right\\|_{L^2(\\Omega)}\\le \\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{A_0t_K}{C_VC_\\Omega}\\right)\\left\\|\\vartheta_0-\\overline\\vartheta\\right\\|_{L^2(\\Omega)}.\n$$\nThus $T$ converges in $L^2(\\Omega)$ to $T_{\\rm eq}=\\overline\\vartheta/N$. Since $N T_{\\rm eq}=\\overline\\vartheta>0$, the inverse-temperature vector $\\beta=u/(k_BT_{\\rm eq})$, for $k_B>0$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$, is Killing by [[lem-tgr-killing-thermal-normalization]]. This is a mathematical solution of the specified fixed-background, supported first-order heat model; it asserts no Einstein equation, general dissipative-fluid initial-value theorem, or finite propagation speed.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:203316:          "statement": "Use the fixed-background box, lapse $N$, constants and material model of [[thm-tgr-supported-static-heat-evolution]]. Let $\\overline\\vartheta>0$ and $\\delta\\in\\mathbb R$ satisfy $|\\delta|<\\overline\\vartheta$, and take the initial redshifted temperature\n$$\n\\vartheta_0(x)=\\overline\\vartheta+\\delta\\cos\\!\\left(\\frac{\\pi x^1}{L_1}\\right),\\qquad x\\in\\overline\\Omega.\n$$\nIt has a positive even-reflected periodic extension, and the corresponding exact solution and proper-temperature field are\n$$\n\\vartheta(t_K,x)=\\overline\\vartheta+\\delta\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{A_0\\pi^2t_K}{C_VL_1^2}\\right)\\cos\\!\\left(\\frac{\\pi x^1}{L_1}\\right),\\qquad T(t_K,x)=\\frac{\\vartheta(t_K,x)}{N(x)}.\n$$\nThe proper temperature tends to $\\overline\\vartheta/N(x)$, which varies spatially when the lapse is nonconstant, while the redshifted temperature $NT$ tends to the constant $\\overline\\vartheta$.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:203948:          "statement": "Choose positive lengths $L_1,L_2,L_3$, the rectangular box $\\Omega=\\prod_{i=1}^3(0,L_i)$, and an open neighborhood $U\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ of $\\overline\\Omega$. Let $N\\in C^\\infty(U,(0,\\infty))$ satisfy $0<N_{\\min}\\leq N(x)\\leq N_{\\max}<\\infty$ on $\\overline\\Omega$. On $M=\\mathbb R_{x^0}\\times U$, use $x^0=ct_K$, where $c>0$ is in $\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, and the fixed metric\n\n$$g=-N(x)^2(dx^0)^2+\\sum_{i=1}^3(dx^i)^2.$$\n\nThe coordinates $x^i$ and lengths $L_i$ are measured in metres, $t_K$ in seconds, and the lapse $N$ is dimensionless. The metric is independent of $x^0$ and has no time-space cross terms; this is the static background in this model. Give $M$ time orientation $\\xi=\\partial_{x^0}$. Then $\\xi$ is a future timelike Killing field, $N=\\sqrt{-g(\\xi,\\xi)}$, and $u=N^{-1}\\xi$ is the smooth future unit timelike observer congruence in the conventions of [[def-tgr-framework-units]]. Here a frame means this vector field and its pointwise rest spaces, not a coordinate chart or tetrad. The material is held at rest in this congruence; no flow evolution is included. The scalar $T>0$ is proper temperature in kelvins, and $\\vartheta:=NT$ is the Killing-normalized (redshifted) temperature of [[def-tgr-framework-units]]; this definition alone does not assert that $\\vartheta$ is constant.\n\nAdopt the fixed-composition material branch of [[post-tgr-local-material-thermodynamics]] with constants $C_V,n_0,T_{\\rm ref}>0$, $\\sigma_{\\rm ref}\\in\\mathbb R$, and $e_0\\in\\mathbb R$. The units are $C_V$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}K^{-1}}$, $n_0$ in $\\mathrm{marker\\,m^{-3}}$, $T_{\\rm ref}$ in kelvins, $\\sigma_{\\rm ref}$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}marker^{-1}}$, and $e_0$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,marker^{-1}}$. Put $c_v=C_V/n_0$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}marker^{-1}}$ and choose the state function on $X=(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R$ to be\n\n$$e(v,\\sigma_p)=c_vT_{\\rm ref}\\exp\\!\\left(\\frac{\\sigma_p-\\sigma_{\\rm ref}}{c_v}\\right)+e_0,$$\n\nindependent of the rest volume per marker $v$. For a given positive $T$, set $v=1/n_0$ and $\\sigma_p=\\sigma_{\\rm ref}+c_v\\log(T/T_{\\rm ref})$. For this branch define $n=v^{-1}$, $s=n\\sigma_p$, $\\epsilon=ne$, $T=\\partial_{\\sigma_p}e$, and $p=-\\partial_ve$, using the units of [[def-tgr-framework-units]] and the adopted material convention of [[post-tgr-local-material-thermodynamics]]. These maps give $n=n_0$, $p=0$, $\\epsilon=C_VT+n_0e_0$, and $s=n_0\\sigma_{\\rm ref}+C_V\\log(T/T_{\\rm ref})$. These assignments describe the selected model only; they do not assert a measured equation of state.\n\nTake an admissible initial redshifted-temperature profile $\\vartheta_0\\in C^2(\\overline\\Omega)$ with constants $0<m_0\\leq M_0<\\infty$ such that $m_0\\leq\\vartheta_0\\leq M_0$, and require a $C^2$ extension $\\widetilde\\vartheta_0:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R$ with the same bounds, period $2L_i$ in each coordinate $x^i$, and evenness under each reflection $x^i\\mapsto-x^i$. Its even periodic extension makes the normal derivative vanish at both faces in each coordinate once evolved smoothly. The initial proper temperature is $T(0,x)=\\vartheta_0(x)/N(x)$.\n\nOn $O=(0,\\infty)\\times\\Omega$, identified with the subset of $M$ by $x^0=ct_K$, the admissible temperature field is a positive map $T:[0,\\infty)\\times\\overline\\Omega\\to(0,\\infty)$ continuous with initial trace $T(0,x)=\\vartheta_0(x)/N(x)$ and $C^\\infty$ on $O$. For every $0<a<b<\\infty$, it extends smoothly in all space-time variables to a neighborhood of $[a,b]\\times\\overline\\Omega$; no smoothness across $t_K=0$ is required. The state map on $O$ is\n\n$$v=1/n_0,\\qquad \\sigma_p=\\sigma_{\\rm ref}+c_v\\log(T/T_{\\rm ref}).$$\n\nWith the definition $\\mu=e+pv-T\\sigma_p$ in [[def-tgr-local-fluid-state]], this branch has $\\mu/T=c_v+e_0/T-\\sigma_p$ and $\\nabla_a(\\mu/T)=-(e_0/T^2+c_v/T)\\nabla_aT$. The state map and these derived fields are smooth on $O$.\n\nFor [[post-tgr-first-order-dissipation]], declare the regime $\\mathcal R$ as follows. Its tuples record $(v,\\sigma_p,u,T,\\mu/T)$ and the first covariant derivatives $\\nabla u$, $\\nabla T$, and $\\nabla(\\mu/T)$ generated by first derivatives of the displayed state maps and of a future unit timelike field $u$; thus $u_b\\nabla_a u^b=0$. Require $1/(2n_0)<v<3/(2n_0)$, with arbitrary $\\sigma_p\\in\\mathbb R$ and no bounds on the derivatives. This is a nonempty open regime in the relative topology of the state/first-jet space after the normalization and state-map constraints are imposed. It contains the fixed-density heat branch pointwise because $v=1/n_0$, $T>0$, and $u=N^{-1}\\xi$ is future unit timelike. In this application set the diffusion coefficient $L=0$, so $V=0$ and the supplier's number current is $J=nu$; it is future timelike throughout $\\mathcal R$ since $n=1/v>0$, and on the fixed-density branch $J=n_0u$.\n\nAdopt from [[post-tgr-first-order-dissipation]] the first-order heat closure and current/stress/entropy conventions on this regime. The physical heat-flux field $q$ is spatial, has units $\\mathrm{W\\,m^{-2}}$, and has constitutive coefficient $\\kappa_{\\rm th}=A_0/N$ for a constant $A_0>0$ in $\\mathrm{W\\,m^{-1}K^{-1}}$. With $\\nabla_x$ the Euclidean spatial gradient, its specified static heat law, local energy balance, and insulated-face condition are\n\n$$q=-\\kappa_{\\rm th}(\\nabla_xT+T\\nabla_x\\log N),\\qquad \\kappa_{\\rm th}=\\frac{A_0}{N},\\qquad NC_V\\partial_{t_K}T+\\operatorname{div}_x(N^2q)=0,\\qquad N^2q\\cdot\\nu=0\\ \\text{on each face},$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{div}_x$ is Euclidean divergence and $\\nu$ is the outward Euclidean unit normal to a box face. The adopted first-order closure specializes to this lapse-gradient form in the selected static frame; it is not inferred from an experiment. Since $N$ is time independent, $\\nabla_xT+T\\nabla_x\\log N=N^{-1}\\nabla_x\\vartheta$, so this coefficient choice gives $N^2q=-A_0\\nabla_x\\vartheta$ and diffusivity $A_0/C_V$ in $\\mathrm{m^2\\,s^{-1}}$.\n\nFor the material stress/current in this selected branch put $Q^0=0$, $Q^i=q^i/c$, $J^a=n_0u^a$, and\n\n$$\\mathsf T^{ab}=\\epsilon u^au^b+u^aQ^b+Q^au^b.$$\n\nHere $Q=q/c$ has units $\\mathrm{Pa}$ and $\\mathsf T^{ab}$ has components in $\\mathrm{Pa}$. In the first-order notation set the bulk, shear, and diffusion coefficients to zero, so $\\Pi=0$, $\\pi^{ab}=0$, and $V^a=0$; the thermal coefficient is $\\kappa=\\kappa_{\\rm th}$. These are nonnegative $C^1(O)$ coefficient fields. The source-free number balance $\\nabla_aJ^a=0$ remains part of this branch. The external support is the force-density field $f^b:=\\nabla_a\\mathsf T^{ab}$, in $\\mathrm{N\\,m^{-3}}$, required to hold the medium on the fixed background. This model adopts the first-order heat closure but replaces that supplier's source-free stress balance by this supported balance. Require $u_bf^b=0$: the support may supply spatial force but no local power. Thus the state maps, constitutive fields, currents, and stress are smooth on $O$, and $f^b$ is a classical covariant divergence; this is a supported material model, not source-free full matter dynamics. Its entropy current is $S^a=su^a+Q^a/T$, with units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}m^{-3}}$; this postulate alone does not assert an entropy inequality.\n\nThis is an adopted effective parabolic model. It asserts no Einstein equation, no general relativistic-fluid initial-value theorem, no finite propagation speed, and no universal material law. It supplies no experiment, measurement, empirical premise, or evidence that a physical medium realizes the model.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:203978:          "physical_scope": "One fixed-background, fixed-medium, supported parabolic heat branch with positive static lapse, constant heat capacity, conductivity A0/N, insulated rectangular faces, and compatible positive initial data; not a full Einstein-fluid or causal evolution model.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:204023:          "physical_scope": "Conditional deduction for the fixed-background supported parabolic heat model of post-tgr-supported-static-heat-model, with compatible positive data; no claim of empirical realization, full Einstein-fluid dynamics, causality, or finite propagation.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:204329:          "statement": "Let $I_{x^0}\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open interval and $B\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ an open bounded coordinate box with compact closure and positive volume. On a neighborhood of $I_{x^0}\\times\\overline B$, with $x^0=ct$ assigned metres and $t$ in seconds, prescribe the smooth static metric\n$$g=-\\mathcal N(x)^2(dx^0)^2+h_{ij}(x)dx^i dx^j,$$\nwhere $h_{ij}$ is positive definite with eigenvalues bounded above and away from zero, and the lapse satisfies $0<\\mathcal N_0\\le\\mathcal N(x)\\le\\mathcal N_1<\\infty$. The static unit observer is $u=\\mathcal N^{-1}\\partial_{x^0}$, so $g(u,u)=-1$; this is a prescribed background, not a solution sourced by the gas.\n\nUse SI units: particle mass $m>0$ is in $\\mathrm{kg}$, $c>0$ is in $\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, orthonormal momentum $\\hat p$ is in $\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$, and local energy $E$ is in $\\mathrm J$. In any local orthonormal coframe, $p_i=e_i{}^a\\hat p_a$ and $h_{ij}=\\sum_a e_i{}^ae_j{}^a$, so $dV_h\\,d^3\\hat p=d^3x\\,d^3p_i$; orthonormal-frame changes are rotations and preserve $d^3\\hat p$. This defines a fibre measure without assuming a global frame. Use the fixed action reference $h_*>0$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,s}$ and $\\beta_K>0$ in $\\mathrm{J^{-1}}$:\n$$E(\\hat p)=\\sqrt{m^2c^4+c^2|\\hat p|^2},\\qquad H_K(x,\\hat p)=\\mathcal N(x)E(\\hat p),\\qquad Z_1(\\beta_K)=\\int_B\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}e^{-\\beta_KH_K}\\frac{dV_h\\,d^3\\hat p}{h_*^3}.$$\nThen $0<Z_1<\\infty$, with all polynomial momentum moments and compact-positive-$\\beta_K$ derivatives finite. For each fixed particle count $M\\ge1$, the classical ideal-gas preparation with Gibbs factor $1/M!$ has $Z_M=Z_1^M/M!$; a grand fugacity preparation with dimensionless $z>0$ has $\\Xi=\\exp(zZ_1)$.\n\nDefine $f(x,\\hat p)=z h_*^{-3}e^{-\\beta_KH_K}$ and the proper local densities\n$$n(x)=\\int f\\,d^3\\hat p,\\quad \\varepsilon(x)=\\int E f\\,d^3\\hat p,\\quad P(x)=\\frac13\\int \\hat p\\cdot\\nabla_{\\hat p}E\\,f\\,d^3\\hat p.$$\nTheir units are $\\mathrm{m^{-3}}$, $\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}$, and $\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}$, respectively. With $\\beta_{\\mathrm{loc}}=\\beta_K\\mathcal N(x)$ and $T_K=(k_B\\beta_K)^{-1}$, the exact model identities are\n$$T_{\\mathrm{loc}}(x)=\\frac1{k_B\\beta_{\\mathrm{loc}}}=\\frac{T_K}{\\mathcal N(x)},\\quad P=nk_BT_{\\mathrm{loc}},\\quad \\partial_iP=-(\\varepsilon+P)\\partial_i\\log\\mathcal N.$$\nIf $z=e^{\\beta_K\\mu_K}$ with $\\mu_K$ in joules, the local chemical parameter is $\\mu_{\\mathrm{loc}}=\\mu_K/\\mathcal N$. No Einstein equation, self-consistent gas backreaction, interacting quantum trace, or arbitrary curved-space mode spectrum is asserted.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:204627:          "statement": "Under the hypotheses of [[lem-tgr-finite-gravitational-gibbs]], keep the box, particle number, mass, regulator, reference measure, and Hamiltonian fixed while varying $\\beta>0$, and set $p_\\beta=Z_N(\\beta)^{-1}e^{-\\beta H_{N,a}}$. For a measurable observable $A:\\Gamma_N\\to\\mathbb R$ with finite second moment, define $\\langle A\\rangle_\\beta:=\\int_{\\Gamma_N}A p_\\beta\\,d\\nu_N$ and $\\operatorname{Var}_\\beta(A):=\\int_{\\Gamma_N}(A-\\langle A\\rangle_\\beta)^2p_\\beta\\,d\\nu_N$. Define $T_K=(k_B\\beta)^{-1}$ and $C_K=d\\langle H_{N,a}\\rangle_\\beta/dT_K$. Then\n$$\\langle H_{N,a}\\rangle_\\beta=-\\partial_\\beta\\log Z_N,\\qquad \\partial_\\beta\\langle H_{N,a}\\rangle_\\beta=-\\operatorname{Var}_\\beta(H_{N,a}),\\qquad C_K=k_B\\beta^2\\operatorname{Var}_\\beta(H_{N,a})\\ge0.$$\nThis is a response identity for a fixed Hamiltonian and fixed regulator; it does not assert stability when the Hamiltonian or background varies with temperature.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:205778:          "physical_scope": "Conditional on the finite equal-mass softened Newtonian model with bounded positive-volume box and fixed a>0; proves normalization, the stated Gibbs variational optimum, and nonnegative fixed-Hamiltonian canonical response, not thermalization or stability of a changing/self-consistent background.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:206042:                "locator": "Complete 36-page paper retrieved; \u00a7III, PDF pp. 8\u201311, for the Maxwell\u2013J\u00fcttner law, local thermodynamic quantities, Tolman/Klein normalization and hydrostatic equation in a self-gravitating model. The present preparation prescribes its background and does not use backreaction."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:206077:          "statement": "Assume the prescribed static background in [[post-tgr-fixed-static-gas-preparation]] and consider its two alternative classical branches. Let\n$$Z_1(\\beta_K)=\\int_B\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}e^{-\\beta_K\\mathcal N(x)E(\\hat p)}\\frac{dV_h(x)\\,d^3\\hat p}{h_*^3},\\qquad dV_h=\\sqrt{\\det(h_{ij}(x))}\\,d^3x.$$\nThen $0<Z_1<\\infty$. In the fixed-particle canonical branch, for the selected integer $M\\ge1$, the normalizer is $Z_M=Z_1^M/M!$. In the grand-canonical branch, for dimensionless fugacity $z>0$, the normalizer is $\\Xi=e^{zZ_1}$.\n\nFor either classical branch, define its coefficient $\\alpha$ by $\\alpha=M/Z_1$ in the fixed-particle branch and $\\alpha=z$ in the grand-canonical branch. Its proper local expected particle phase-space density is\n$$f_\\alpha(x,\\hat p)=\\alpha h_*^{-3}e^{-\\beta_K\\mathcal N(x)E(\\hat p)}.$$\nDefine proper local number density $n=\\int f_\\alpha\\,d^3\\hat p$, energy density $\\varepsilon=\\int E f_\\alpha\\,d^3\\hat p$, and isotropic pressure $P=\\frac13\\int\\hat p\\cdot\\nabla_{\\hat p}E\\,f_\\alpha\\,d^3\\hat p$. With $T_K=(k_B\\beta_K)^{-1}$, both branches satisfy\n$$T_{\\rm loc}(x)=\\frac{T_K}{\\mathcal N(x)},\\qquad P=nk_BT_{\\rm loc},\\qquad \\partial_iP=-(\\varepsilon+P)\\partial_i\\log\\mathcal N.$$\nOnly in the grand-canonical branch, if $z=e^{\\beta_K\\mu_K}$ for a Killing-normalized chemical parameter $\\mu_K\\in\\mathbb R$ measured in joules, rewriting the local weight as $e^{-\\beta_K\\mathcal N(E-\\mu_{\\rm loc})}$ gives $\\mu_{\\rm loc}=\\mu_K/\\mathcal N$.\n\nIn the finite fermion-mode branch, let $E_j=\\mathcal N_j\\epsilon_j$ with $\\epsilon_j=\\sqrt{m^2c^4+c^2|\\hat p_j|^2}$, $\\mathcal N_j>0$, $\\beta_K>0$, and $\\mu_K\\in\\mathbb R$. The normalized diagonal state has\n$$\\langle n_j\\rangle=\\frac{1}{e^{\\beta_K(E_j-\\mu_K)}+1}=\\frac{1}{e^{\\beta_j(\\epsilon_j-\\mu_j)}+1},\\qquad \\beta_j=\\beta_K\\mathcal N_j,\\quad \\mu_j=\\frac{\\mu_K}{\\mathcal N_j}.$$\nBoth conclusions are conditional model deductions on the fixed background or finite supplied mode set. They assert no Einstein equation, gas backreaction, arbitrary curved-space spectrum, or observed equilibrium.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:206078:          "physical_scope": "Exact classical ideal-gas normalization, redshifted local thermodynamic parameters, pressure and hydrostatic identity on the prescribed bounded-lapse static background, plus the separate finite-mode trace identity. No Einstein backreaction, generic curved-space mode spectrum, self-gravitating star, or empirical equilibrium assertion.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:206092:                "locator": "Complete 36-page paper retrieved; \u00a7III, PDF pp. 8\u201311, derives the Maxwell\u2013J\u00fcttner law, local pressure/energy relations, Tolman/Klein parameters and hydrostatic condition for a self-gravitating model. The local proof here uses a prescribed background and explicitly excludes backreaction."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:206275:                "locator": "Complete paper retrieved; \u00a7III, PDF pp. 8\u201311, for local temperature/lapse and hydrostatic conventions in the self-gravitating model. This example uses a prescribed flat background only."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:214556:          "statement": "Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open time interval. For $d=2,3$, set $Q_d=[0,2\\pi]^d$ and define the flat periodic domain $\\mathbb T^d:=\\mathbb R^d/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)^d$. Let $\\lambda_d^*$ be the Lebesgue outer set function induced by elementary volume on the algebra $\\mathcal E_d$ of elementary subsets of $\\mathbb R^d$; that is, for $S\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$, $\\lambda_d^*(S)=\\inf\\{\\sum_{k=0}^\\infty\\mu_0(A_k):A_k\\in\\mathcal E_d\\text{ for all }k,\\ S\\subseteq\\bigcup_{k=0}^\\infty A_k\\}$, where $\\mu_0$ is elementary volume. Let $\\mathcal L(\\mathbb R^d)$ be the family of sets $A\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$ satisfying $\\lambda_d^*(B)=\\lambda_d^*(B\\cap A)+\\lambda_d^*(B\\setminus A)$ for every $B\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$, and let $\\lambda_d$ be the restriction of $\\lambda_d^*$ to $\\mathcal L(\\mathbb R^d)$. This is the Carath\u00e9odory definition of Lebesgue measure. Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ for the measure and integration conventions in this item; under this hypothesis $\\mathcal L(\\mathbb R^d)$ is a sigma-algebra, $\\lambda_d$ is a complete measure, and the box $Q_d$ is measurable with $\\lambda_d(Q_d)=(2\\pi)^d$. For a real $\\lambda_d$-integrable function $F$ on $Q_d$, integrability means measurability and $\\int_{Q_d}|F|\\,d\\lambda_d<\\infty$; with $F^+=\\max(F,0)$ and $F^-=\\max(-F,0)$, define $\\int_{Q_d}F\\,d\\lambda_d:=\\int_{Q_d}F^+\\,d\\lambda_d-\\int_{Q_d}F^-\\,d\\lambda_d$. In formulas, $dx$ means $d\\lambda_d(x)$ and $dt$ means $d\\lambda_1(t)$, where $\\lambda_1$ is Lebesgue measure on $\\mathbb R$. The expression $\\int_{\\mathbb T^d}F\\,dx$ means integration of the periodic lift over $Q_d$; $\\int_{t_0}^{t_1}\\int_{Q_d}F(t,x)\\,dx\\,dt$ means the iterated Lebesgue integral with the spatial integral first whenever it exists. A field on $\\mathbb T^d$ is represented by its $2\\pi$-periodic lift to the nonempty open spatial domain $\\mathbb R^d$. All fields below are classical: their displayed time and spatial derivatives are continuous, and the velocity and buoyancy fields have continuous second spatial derivatives. Each displayed equation is a pointwise equality.\n\nFor a scalar field $q$, set $\\nabla q=(\\partial_{x_1}q,\\ldots,\\partial_{x_d}q)$ and $\\Delta q=\\sum_{j=1}^d\\partial_{x_j}^2q$. For a vector field $w=(w_1,\\ldots,w_d)$, set $\\nabla\\cdot w=\\sum_{j=1}^d\\partial_{x_j}w_j$, $\\Delta w=(\\Delta w_1,\\ldots,\\Delta w_d)$, $|w|^2=\\sum_{j=1}^dw_j^2$, and $|\\nabla w|^2=\\sum_{i,j=1}^d(\\partial_{x_j}w_i)^2$. For a matrix field $A$, its divergence is rowwise: $(\\nabla\\cdot A)_i=\\sum_{j=1}^d\\partial_{x_j}A_{ij}$. In three dimensions $e_3\\times u=(-u_2,u_1,0)$, and in two dimensions $Jv=(-v_2,v_1)$.\n\nOn $\\mathbb T^3$, let $u:I\\times\\mathbb T^3\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be velocity, $b:I\\times\\mathbb T^3\\to\\mathbb R$ be a buoyancy perturbation, and $\\pi:I\\times\\mathbb T^3\\to\\mathbb R$ be specific pressure. Let $e_3=(0,0,1)$, and prescribe constant parameters $f\\in\\mathbb R$, $N>0$, and $\\nu,\\kappa\\ge0$. The rotating, linear, constant-stratification Boussinesq equation family is\n\n$$u_t+f e_3\\times u+\\nabla\\pi=b e_3+\\nu\\Delta u,\\qquad \\nabla\\cdot u=0,\\qquad b_t+N^2u_3=\\kappa\\Delta b.$$\n\nHere $\\Delta$ acts componentwise, $u_3$ is the vertical velocity component, $f$ is the Coriolis coefficient, $N$ is the constant buoyancy-frequency parameter, and $\\nu$ and $\\kappa$ are the prescribed momentum and buoyancy diffusion coefficients. The inviscid branch is the choice $\\nu=\\kappa=0$. In this mathematical family, $b$ is only the scalar acceleration variable appearing in the vertical forcing term $b e_3$ and in its evolution equation; no density field or identification such as $b=-g\\,\\delta\\rho/\\rho_0$ is part of this definition. The positive parameter $N$ has units $\\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ and its square is the prescribed coefficient coupling $u_3$ to $b$; no background buoyancy profile $b_0(z)$ or identification $N^2=\\partial_z b_0$ is included. Such density-anomaly and background-profile relations would be additional physical modeling assumptions. In the formal Boussinesq interpretation, a constant reference density is used in inertia and the divergence constraint while density-anomaly effects are retained only through the vertical buoyancy acceleration; small fractional density variation is an applicability assumption, not a theorem giving a compressible limit.\n\nFor its rotating-frame interpretation, let $\\mathbf E$ be an oriented Euclidean three-space with a fixed origin and fixed upward unit vector $\\hat{\\mathbf z}$. A rotating basis on $I$ is a triple of $C^1$ maps $\\mathbf e_a:I\\to\\mathbf E$ ($a=1,2,3$) such that $\\langle\\mathbf e_a(t),\\mathbf e_b(t)\\rangle=\\delta_{ab}$, the ordered basis is positively oriented, $\\mathbf e_3(t)=\\hat{\\mathbf z}$, and $\\dot{\\mathbf e}_a(t)=\\boldsymbol\\Omega\\times\\mathbf e_a(t)$ for $a=1,2$, with constant $\\boldsymbol\\Omega=\\Omega\\hat{\\mathbf z}$. This differential equation fixes the sign convention for the signed angular-velocity scalar $\\Omega$; for this interpretation, spatial coordinates $x$ and vector components are relative to this time-indexed basis, and $u_t$ differentiates the components at fixed rotating coordinates. The component triple $u$ represents the vector $\\sum_{a=1}^3u_a\\mathbf e_a(t)$ in $\\mathbf E$. Under this convention the Coriolis acceleration is $-2\\boldsymbol\\Omega\\times u$ on the force side, hence $+f e_3\\times u$ on the left with $f=2\\Omega$. Let $\\Phi:I\\times\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R$ be a gravitational potential per unit mass in the rotating coordinates, with continuous first spatial derivatives; $x\\in\\mathbb R^3$ denotes the coordinate vector relative to the fixed origin in the basis $\\mathbf e_a(t)$. Define the effective potential $\\Phi_{\\rm eff}:I\\times\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R$ by $\\Phi_{\\rm eff}(t,x):=\\Phi(t,x)-\\tfrac12|\\boldsymbol\\Omega\\times x|^2$, which incorporates the centrifugal contribution. These rotating-frame and effective-potential choices are assumptions; the inertial-frame balance is distinct. The periodic domain is a mathematical idealization, not a claim that the physical fluid occupies a compact torus.\n\nIndependently, on $\\mathbb T^2$, let $h:I\\times\\mathbb T^2\\to(0,\\infty)$ be total layer depth and $v:I\\times\\mathbb T^2\\to\\mathbb R^2$ be horizontal velocity. Let $g>0$ and $f\\in\\mathbb R$ be prescribed constants, define $Jv:=(-v_2,v_1)$, and take a fixed flat bottom. The inviscid one-layer shallow-water equation family is\n\n$$h_t+\\nabla\\cdot(hv)=0,\\qquad (hv)_t+\\nabla\\cdot(hv\\otimes v)+\\nabla\\!\\left(\\frac{g h^2}{2}\\right)+f h Jv=0.$$\n\nThe tensor divergence is rowwise, $(\\nabla\\cdot(hv\\otimes v))_i=\\sum_{j=1}^2\\partial_{x_j}(h v_i v_j)$. This family has one layer, positive depth, a flat bottom, and no bottom friction, wetting/drying, or interlayer coupling. These are mathematical equation systems, not assertions that a measured fluid realizes them. With SI interpretation, $t$ is in seconds, spatial coordinates in metres, $u$ and $v$ in metres per second, $b$ in metres per second squared, $\\pi$, $\\Phi$, and $\\Phi_{\\rm eff}$ in square metres per square second, $f$, $N$, and $\\Omega$ in inverse seconds, $\\nu$ and $\\kappa$ in square metres per second, $h$ in metres, and $g$ in metres per square second. The displayed coefficients are prescribed within these formulations; no empirical calibration is asserted. If a spatially integrated energy for either system is defined per unit constant reference density, multiplying that normalized energy by the specified density in kilograms per cubic metre gives joules.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:214775:          "physical_scope": "Adopted idealized local Cartesian models for rotating geophysical continuum flows: a linear, constant-stratification Boussinesq branch about a hydrostatically balanced resting background and a separate one-layer shallow-water branch. Their approximation regimes and omitted effects are explicit; no universal empirical validity or approximation-error estimate is asserted.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/preimages/research/plan-spec.json:214778:          "statement": "Use the nonrelativistic Newtonian spacetime $I\\times E$, where $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ is a nonempty open interval with absolute time $t$ in seconds and $E$ is a three-dimensional oriented Euclidean affine space with no distinguished origin. Choose a Cartesian inertial chart with an orientation-preserving affine isometry $X:E\\to\\mathbb R^3$, in metres, and the absolute-time coordinate. Its coordinate origin is a fixed inertial point; no relativistic causal structure is assumed. Fix $t_r\\in I$ and a constant $\\Omega\\in\\mathbb R$ in s$^{-1}$, set $\\theta(t)=\\Omega(t-t_r)$, and define\n\n$$R(t)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos\\theta(t)&-\\sin\\theta(t)&0\\\\\\sin\\theta(t)&\\cos\\theta(t)&0\\\\0&0&1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad x(t,q)=R(t)^{\\mathsf T}X(q)\\quad(q\\in E).$$\n\nThe map $(t,q)\\mapsto(t,x(t,q))$ is the adapted rotating Cartesian chart, a rigid observer change in the stated frame convention. Its orthonormal basis vectors have inertial components $R(t)e_i$, where $e_i$ are the standard coordinate unit vectors and $e_3$ points vertically upward; the basis is associated with the chart, rather than itself being called a frame. For a $C^2$ worldline $q(t)$ with inertial-coordinate velocity $V(t)=dX(q(t))/dt$, its rotating-coordinate velocity is\n\n$$\\frac{dx}{dt}=R(t)^{\\mathsf T}V(t)-\\Omega e_3\\times x(t,q(t)),\\qquad e_3=(0,0,1).$$\n\nThus $u$ below denotes velocity relative to the rotating chart, not merely rotated components of $V$; the shallow-water $v$ denotes the first two components of this relative velocity in its separate depth-independent reduced model. The rotation is right-handed about $e_3$, with angular-velocity vector $\\boldsymbol\\Omega=\\Omega e_3$ and Coriolis parameter $f=2\\Omega$. In this convention the modeled Coriolis acceleration is $-2\\boldsymbol\\Omega\\times u$ on the force side, hence $+f e_3\\times u$ on the left of the momentum equation. Constant rotation has no angular-acceleration term. Let $U_{\\rm bg}\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be an open local region in rotating spatial coordinates, and let $\\Phi:I\\times U_{\\rm bg}\\to\\mathbb R$ be a scalar gravitational potential per unit mass, continuous in time and twice continuously differentiable in space, with SI units $\\mathrm{m^2\\,s^{-2}}$; by convention its gravitational acceleration is $-\\nabla_x\\Phi$, where $\\nabla_x$ is the standard Cartesian spatial gradient. Define $\\Phi_{\\rm eff}:I\\times U_{\\rm bg}\\to\\mathbb R$ by\n\n$$\\Phi_{\\rm eff}(t,x)=\\Phi(t,x)-\\frac12|\\boldsymbol\\Omega\\times x|^2.$$\n\nThe model replaces the effective acceleration $-\\nabla_x\\Phi_{\\rm eff}$ locally by the constant downward vector $-g e_3$, where $g>0$ has units $\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}}$. Let $\\rho_0>0$ be the constant reference mass density in $\\mathrm{kg\\,m^{-3}}$, let $p_0\\in C^1(U_{\\rm bg},\\mathbb R)$ be the prescribed time-independent pressure of the zero-velocity reference state in Pa, and let $p:I\\times U_{\\rm bg}\\to\\mathbb R$ be the pressure field, continuously differentiable in space and measured in Pa. The perturbation pressure per reference density is $\\pi(t,x)=(p(t,x)-p_0(x))/\\rho_0$, in $\\mathrm{m^2\\,s^{-2}}$. The resting background pressure is constrained by hydrostatic balance as specified for the Boussinesq branch below; this constraint is an adopted model assumption, not a consequence of the perturbation equations. These are adopted local approximations and linearization choices, not an exact Newtonian coordinate-transformation derivation of the displayed reduced systems. Periodicity below is imposed on the perturbation fields in rotating coordinates; it does not require that $R(t)$ map a fixed periodic lattice to itself or that the background potential or pressure be periodic.\n\nThe constant-$f$ choice is a local $f$-plane idealization; no latitude variation, planetary curvature, or $\\beta$-plane term is included. The constant reference mass density $\\rho_0>0$ has units kg m$^{-3}$; $f$ and $N$ have units s$^{-1}$; $g$ has units m s$^{-2}$; velocities have units m s$^{-1}$; and normalized pressure and potential variables have units m$^2$ s$^{-2}$.\n\nAdopt the following two distinct approximation branches; they are physical-model assumptions and not conclusions derived from mathematics.\n\n1. **Linear, constant-stratification Boussinesq branch.** Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open time interval, use the flat periodic mathematical domain $\\mathbb T^3=\\mathbb R^3/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)^3$ with fundamental cell $Q_3=[0,2\\pi]^3$, and represent fields by $2\\pi$-periodic lifts with the regularity specified next. Let $u:I\\times\\mathbb T^3\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be velocity, $b:I\\times\\mathbb T^3\\to\\mathbb R$ a buoyancy-acceleration perturbation in m s$^{-2}$, and $\\pi:I\\times\\mathbb T^3\\to\\mathbb R$ pressure per reference density in m$^2$ s$^{-2}$; $u,b$ have continuous first time and spatial derivatives and continuous second spatial derivatives, and $\\pi$ has continuous first spatial derivatives. The positive constant reference density $\\rho_0$ is used in inertia and the incompressibility constraint, while density-anomaly effects are retained only through buoyancy. The buoyancy equation of state is not specified here. The Boussinesq approximation assumes fractional density anomalies small enough to neglect their effect on inertia while retaining their gravitational buoyancy effect. In addition, this branch adopts a linearization about a resting, uniformly stratified state. Write $z=x_3$ for upward height and prescribe $B_0:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ by $B_0(z)=B_*+N^2z$, where $B_*$ is a constant buoyancy acceleration in $\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}}$ and $N>0$ is the constant buoyancy-frequency parameter in $\\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. The modeled total buoyancy anomaly relative to the constant-density gravitational reference is $B_0(z)+b(t,x)$, in $\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}}$; $b$ is its perturbation. Require the resting pressure to satisfy $\\rho_0^{-1}\\nabla_xp_0(x)=(B_0(x_3)-g)e_3$ on $U_{\\rm bg}$. This is hydrostatic balance of the full stratified reference state: $-\\rho_0^{-1}\\nabla_xp_0-ge_3+B_0e_3=0$, so substituting $p=p_0+\\rho_0\\pi$ leaves exactly $-\\nabla\\pi+be_3$ in perturbation momentum. The full resting $p_0$ includes both the constant-density hydrostatic pressure, with gradient $-\\rho_0g e_3$, and the stratification pressure, with gradient $\\rho_0B_0e_3$. These background profiles are used on the local region $U_{\\rm bg}$; no globally small density anomaly at all real heights or periodic background is asserted. The identity $B_0'=N^2$ specifies uniform stratification, and advection of the perturbations is omitted. For prescribed constants $f\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\nu,\\kappa\\ge 0$ in m$^2$ s$^{-1}$, the model equations are\n\n$$u_t+f e_3\\times u+\\nabla\\pi=b e_3+\\nu\\Delta u,\\qquad \\nabla\\cdot u=0,\\qquad b_t+N^2u_3=\\kappa\\Delta b.$$\n\nHere $e_3\\times u=(-u_2,u_1,0)$, $\\nabla q=(\\partial_{x_1}q,\\partial_{x_2}q,\\partial_{x_3}q)$, $\\nabla\\cdot w=\\sum_{j=1}^3\\partial_{x_j}w_j$, and $\\Delta q=\\sum_{j=1}^3\\partial_{x_j}^2q$, with $\\Delta$ acting componentwise on $u$. The periodic domain is a mathematical boundary idealization. Neither this linear equation family nor the Boussinesq approximation includes a derived error estimate or asserts that a particular measured fluid satisfies the assumptions.\n\n2. **One-layer shallow-water branch.** On the separate flat periodic domain $\\mathbb T^2=\\mathbb R^2/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)^2$, let $h:I\\times\\mathbb T^2\\to(0,\\infty)$ be total layer depth in metres and $v:I\\times\\mathbb T^2\\to\\mathbb R^2$ be horizontal velocity in m s$^{-1}$, with continuous first time and spatial derivatives and continuous second spatial derivatives. Use constant $g>0$ in m s$^{-2}$ and constant $f\\in\\mathbb R$ in s$^{-1}$, and define $J(v_1,v_2)=(-v_2,v_1)$. Adopt a single layer with constant reference density, a flat bottom at vertical coordinate $z=0$, no bottom stress, no wetting or drying, and no interlayer coupling. Let $z$ be height positive upward in metres, $p(t,x_1,x_2,z)$ pressure in Pa, and $H,L>0$ representative vertical and horizontal length scales in metres. Assume the shallow-layer regime $H/L\\ll1$; neglect vertical acceleration so pressure is hydrostatic, $\\partial_zp=-\\rho_0g$, with negligible pressure above the free surface $z=h(t,x_1,x_2)$, and take horizontal velocity independent of depth in the reduced model. The adopted hydrostatic profile is $p(t,x_1,x_2,z)=\\rho_0g(h(t,x_1,x_2)-z)$ for $0\\le z\\le h(t,x_1,x_2)$. The resulting one-layer equations are\n\n$$h_t+\\nabla\\cdot(hv)=0,\\qquad (hv)_t+\\nabla\\cdot(hv\\otimes v)+\\nabla\\!\\left(\\frac{g h^2}{2}\\right)+f h Jv=0.$$\n\nHere $\\nabla q=(\\partial_{x_1}q,\\partial_{x_2}q)$, $\\nabla\\cdot w=\\partial_{x_1}w_1+\\partial_{x_2}w_2$ for $w=(w_1,w_2)$, and tensor divergence is rowwise: $[\\nabla\\cdot(hv\\otimes v)]_i=\\sum_{j=1}^2\\partial_{x_j}(h v_i v_j)$ for $i=1,2$.\n\nThe two-dimensional torus is again a mathematical boundary idealization, not a claim that a geophysical fluid occupies a torus. No reduction theorem or error estimate from the three-dimensional Boussinesq branch to this shallow-water branch is adopted. The branch assumptions specify when these equations are used as a model; they do not establish their empirical adequacy.\n",
